window.GATE_BANK=window.GATE_BANK||{};window.GATE_BANK["databases"]=[{"id": "DB-U1-Q01", "subject": "databases", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "er-relational-algebra", "subtopic": "er-diagrams-cardinality-weak-entities", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "In an entity–relationship diagram, a weak entity set is depicted by", "options": ["a single rectangle.", "a double rectangle.", "a single diamond.", "a double diamond."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> ER notation. A weak entity set has no key attribute of its own; its existence depends on an owner entity set, so it is drawn as a <b>double rectangle</b>. Its identifying relationship is drawn as a double diamond, its partial key is underlined with a dashed line, and its total participation in the identifying relationship is shown by a double line.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>A weak entity set cannot be identified by its own attributes, only by (owner key + partial key).</li><li>The standard Chen notation distinguishes it with the double outline: double rectangle for the set, double diamond for the identifying relationship.</li><li>Hence the correct symbol for the weak entity set itself is the double rectangle.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) — a double rectangle.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) A single rectangle denotes a strong (regular) entity set.</li><li>(C) A diamond denotes a relationship set, not an entity set.</li><li>(D) A double diamond denotes the identifying relationship of a weak entity set.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Confusing the double rectangle (weak entity set) with the double diamond (identifying relationship). They are different symbols for different things and a question may ask for either.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Faster method.</b> If the option mentions a diamond at all, it is describing a relationship, not an entity set.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Fix the four ER symbols once: rectangle = entity set, double rectangle = weak entity set, diamond = relationship, double diamond = identifying relationship.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U1-Q02", "subject": "databases", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "er-relational-algebra", "subtopic": "er-diagrams-cardinality-weak-entities", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Entity set A has a many-to-one relationship R with entity set B. The participation of A in R is total and the participation of B in R is partial. Which one of the following is necessarily TRUE?", "options": ["Every entity of B is related to at least one entity of A.", "Every entity of A is related to exactly one entity of B.", "Every entity of B is related to exactly one entity of A.", "R must be merged into B because B's participation is partial."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Cardinality ratio plus participation constraint. “Many-to-one from A to B” means each entity of A is related to at most one entity of B, while one entity of B may be related to many entities of A. Total participation of A adds “at least one”, and the two bounds together give “exactly one”.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Many-to-one A → B: an A-entity can be linked to at most one B-entity.</li><li>Total participation of A: every A-entity is linked to at least one B-entity.</li><li>At most one and at least one give exactly one B-entity for every A-entity.</li><li>Partial participation of B only says some B-entities may have no A-entity at all; it constrains nothing about A.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) — every entity of A is related to exactly one entity of B.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) B's participation is partial, so a B-entity need not appear in R at all.</li><li>(C) Many-to-one allows many A-entities to share one B-entity, so B is not related to a unique A.</li><li>(D) A relationship is merged into an entity set only for a one-to-one or one-to-many case without loss, and partial participation of B does not force it.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Students read “partial participation of B” as if it weakened the constraint on A. Participation constrains only the entity set it is attached to.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Faster method.</b> Total participation on the “many” side of a many-to-one turns “at most one” into “exactly one”.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Separate the two facts: the cardinality ratio bounds the count from above, participation bounds it from below. Combine them for the answer.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U1-Q03", "subject": "databases", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "er-relational-algebra", "subtopic": "er-diagrams-cardinality-weak-entities", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "In a Chen-notation ER diagram, which of the following are drawn with a  double  outline or a double diamond? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["A weak entity set.", "The identifying relationship of a weak entity set.", "A multivalued attribute.", "A derived attribute."], "answer_display": "Options (A) and (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> ER notation distinguishes four special constructs. Double rectangle = weak entity set, double diamond = identifying relationship, double ellipse = multivalued attribute, dashed ellipse = derived attribute.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) A weak entity set is drawn as a double rectangle — true.</li><li>(B) The identifying relationship is drawn as a double diamond — true.</li><li>(C) A multivalued attribute uses a <b>double ellipse</b>, not a double outline on a set or a relationship.</li><li>(D) A derived attribute uses a <b>dashed ellipse</b>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A) and (B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(C) uses a double ellipse, which is a different shape.</li><li>(D) uses a dashed ellipse.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> The double ellipse for a multivalued attribute is confused with the double rectangle for a weak entity set. Both are “double”, but the shape differs and so does the meaning.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Remember the pairing shape-to-meaning: double rectangle / double diamond belong together as the weak-entity pair; double ellipse is an attribute-level symbol.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U1-Q04", "subject": "databases", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "er-relational-algebra", "subtopic": "er-diagrams-cardinality-weak-entities", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Entity set E owns weak entity set W through an identifying relationship R. E has 4 entities, R permits at most 3 W-entities per E-entity, and every E-entity participates totally in R. What is the maximum possible number of entities in W? (Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "12", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A weak entity set's cardinality is bounded by the owner's cardinality times the per-owner limit. Total participation of the owner gives a lower bound, the per-owner limit gives the upper bound.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Every E-entity must own at least one W-entity (total participation), so |W| ≥ 4.</li><li>Each E-entity may own at most 3 W-entities, so |W| ≤ 4 × 3 = 12.</li><li>The upper bound 12 is attained when all 4 E-entities own the full 3 W-entities each.</li><li>So the maximum is 12.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 12.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Adding the two numbers instead of multiplying, or forgetting that a weak entity is owned by exactly one owner — which is what makes the count a product rather than a sum.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> For an identifying relationship the owner side is one-to-many, so multiply the owner count by the per-owner limit; the owner's participation decides the minimum, the limit decides the maximum.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U1-Q05", "subject": "databases", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "er-relational-algebra", "subtopic": "er-diagrams-cardinality-weak-entities", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "In ER modelling, aggregation is used to", "options": ["combine several attributes of one entity set into a single composite attribute.", "treat a relationship set together with its participating entity sets as one higher-level entity set that can then take part in another relationship.", "replace a weak entity set by a strong entity set.", "convert a ternary relationship into three binary relationships."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Aggregation is the ER construct that removes a limitation of plain ER diagrams: a relationship set cannot participate in another relationship. Aggregating a relationship plus its entity sets yields a single abstract entity that can.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Plain ER forbids a relationship set from being an endpoint of another relationship.</li><li>Example: a project is done by employees using machines. “Uses” relates the (employee, project) pair to a machine, not an employee to a machine directly.</li><li>Aggregation groups Employee–Works_On–Project into one entity set and lets that set participate in Uses.</li><li>So aggregation abstracts a relationship together with its participants into a higher-level entity set.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) describes a composite attribute, drawn as an ellipse with sub-ellipses.</li><li>(C) is not an ER operation at all; a weak entity set stays weak unless the schema is redesigned.</li><li>(D) is a modelling decision about arity, not aggregation.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Aggregation is confused with generalization (which factors common attributes of entity sets into a superclass) and with the composite-attribute notation. All three “combine” things, but at different levels.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Faster method.</b> If the sentence mentions “a relationship participating in another relationship”, the answer is aggregation.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Aggregation = relationship-as-entity. Generalization = entity-as-subclass. Composite attribute = attribute-of-attribute.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U1-Q06", "subject": "databases", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "er-relational-algebra", "subtopic": "er-diagrams-cardinality-weak-entities", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "A multivalued attribute in an ER diagram is represented by", "options": ["a single ellipse.", "a double ellipse.", "a dashed ellipse.", "a rectangle."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Attribute notation in Chen's model. A multivalued attribute can hold a set of values for one entity, and is drawn as a double ellipse. A derived attribute is drawn with a dashed ellipse; a key attribute has its name underlined.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Multivalued means several values per entity, e.g. phone numbers of a person.</li><li>The extra value needs an extra outline, so the symbol is the double ellipse.</li><li>A derived attribute (computed from others, e.g. age from date of birth) uses a dashed ellipse instead.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) — a double ellipse.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) A single ellipse is a simple, single-valued attribute.</li><li>(C) A dashed ellipse is a derived attribute.</li><li>(D) A rectangle is an entity set.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> The double ellipse is confused with the double rectangle of a weak entity set. Note that the doubling pattern is applied to the base shape: rectangle for sets, ellipse for attributes.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Learn the four attribute shapes together: single ellipse (simple), double ellipse (multivalued), dashed ellipse (derived), underlined name (key).</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U1-Q07", "subject": "databases", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "er-relational-algebra", "subtopic": "relational-model-constraints", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Relation  R(A, B, C)  has primary key  (A, B)  and a foreign key  C  that references  S(C) . Which one of the following is TRUE?", "options": ["A and B cannot be NULL, but C may be NULL.", "C cannot be NULL, but A may be NULL.", "Any of A, B, C may be NULL.", "A alone must be unique."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Entity integrity says no attribute of a primary key may be NULL; referential integrity says a foreign key must either be NULL or match an existing referenced value. The two rules apply to different attributes here, so both can hold at once.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Entity integrity: every attribute of the primary key (A, B) is NOT NULL. So A and B cannot be NULL.</li><li>Referential integrity constrains C: each C-value must be NULL or equal some S.C value.</li><li>Nothing forbids C from being NULL, because C is not part of R's primary key.</li><li>Hence (A, B) are mandatory, C is optional.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) reverses the two rules.</li><li>(C) contradicts entity integrity.</li><li>(D) A is only part of the key; A alone may repeat.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Students assume a foreign key can never be NULL. It can, provided the FK attributes are not part of the primary key — that is exactly how “optional” relationships are modelled.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Check whether the FK overlaps the primary key. No overlap → NULL is allowed. Overlap → entity integrity forbids NULL.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U1-Q08", "subject": "databases", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "er-relational-algebra", "subtopic": "relational-model-constraints", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Let  R(A, B, C, D)  satisfy the functional dependencies  {AB → C, C → D, D → A} . How many candidate keys does  R  have?", "options": ["1", "2", "3", "4"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A candidate key is a minimal set of attributes whose closure is the whole relation. Compute closures of each attribute alone, then of the promising pairs.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>A⁺ = A; B⁺ = B; C⁺ = C D A = ACD (using C → D and D → A); D⁺ = D A = AD. None is the whole relation.</li><li>AB⁺ = A B C D = ABCD. AB is a key, and neither A nor B alone is, so AB is a candidate key.</li><li>BC⁺ = B C D A = ABCD. BC is minimal (B⁺, C⁺ both fail), so BC is a candidate key.</li><li>BD⁺ = B D A = ABD, and AB → C applies, so BD⁺ = ABCD. BD is minimal, so BD is a candidate key.</li><li>AC⁺ = ACD, AD⁺ = AD, CD⁺ = ACD — none is the whole relation. The answer is 3.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C) — 3 candidate keys (AB, BC, BD).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) misses BC and BD.</li><li>(B) misses BD.</li><li>(D) over-counts; AC, AD and CD are not keys.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Stopping at AB, or counting AB and BA as different keys. Also easy to miss BD, which needs the two-step closure D → A followed by AB → C.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Faster method.</b> Here C and D never appear in a closure that contains B, so B is in every key. That forces you to test AB, BC and BD — exactly three.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Write every single-attribute closure first. Any attribute absent from all of them must appear in every candidate key; then test the minimal pairs containing it.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U1-Q09", "subject": "databases", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "er-relational-algebra", "subtopic": "relational-model-constraints", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Which of the following statements about keys of a relation are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["Every relation has at least one candidate key.", "A superkey that has no proper subset which is also a superkey is a candidate key.", "A relation can have at most one candidate key.", "A primary key attribute may contain NULL values."], "answer_display": "Options (A) and (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Keys are defined through functional determination on the whole set of attributes. The set of all attributes of a relation is always a superkey, so minimal superkeys always exist.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The full attribute set functionally determines itself, so it is a superkey; shrinking it until it is minimal yields a candidate key. Hence at least one candidate key always exists.</li><li>A candidate key is precisely a minimal superkey — that is the definition given in (B).</li><li>A relation may have several candidate keys; one of them is chosen as the primary key.</li><li>Entity integrity forbids NULL in any primary key attribute.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A) and (B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(C) is false — a relation may have several candidate keys, and exactly one is picked as primary.</li><li>(D) is false — entity integrity requires primary key attributes to be NOT NULL.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> The word “candidate” is read as “optional”, suggesting there might be none. Every relation has at least one; what varies is how many.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Two facts to keep: the full attribute set is always a superkey (so keys exist), and minimality is what separates candidate keys from superkeys.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U1-Q10", "subject": "databases", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "er-relational-algebra", "subtopic": "relational-model-constraints", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Relation  R(A, B, C)  satisfies the functional dependency  A → B  and no other non-trivial dependency. How many superkeys does  R  have? (Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "2", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A superkey is any attribute set whose closure is all of R. With only A → B, the closure of a set S is S ∪ {B} if A ∈ S, and S otherwise; a superkey must therefore contain A and C.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The candidate key: A⁺ = AB, so A alone is not a key; AC⁺ = ABC. Hence AC is the only candidate key.</li><li>Every superkey must contain the candidate key AC.</li><li>The only attributes available to add are the remaining ones, here just B, giving the supersets AC and ABC.</li><li>So R has exactly 2 superkeys.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 2.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Counting only AC and forgetting that a superkey may contain extra attributes, or counting A and C separately as keys.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Find the candidate key first; every superkey is then a superset of it, so the count is 2 raised to the number of leftover attributes.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U1-Q11", "subject": "databases", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "er-relational-algebra", "subtopic": "relational-model-constraints", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Let  R(A, B, C, D, E)  satisfy the functional dependencies  {A → BC, CD → E, B → D, E → A} . How many candidate keys does  R  have?", "options": ["2", "3", "4", "5"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Candidate keys are minimal sets with full closure. Because the dependencies form a cycle (A → B → D → E → A together with C on one edge), the relation has several minimal keys and every attribute belongs to at least one of them.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>A⁺: A → BC gives B, C; B → D gives D; CD → E gives E. So A⁺ = ABCDE. A is a candidate key.</li><li>E⁺: E → A, then A → BC, then B → D, then CD → E. So E⁺ = ABCDE. E is a candidate key.</li><li>BC⁺: B → D, CD → E, E → A, A → BC. So BC⁺ = ABCDE, and B⁺ = BD, C⁺ = C — BC is minimal.</li><li>CD⁺: CD → E, E → A, A → BC, B → D. So CD⁺ = ABCDE, and C⁺ = C, D⁺ = D — CD is minimal.</li><li>B⁺ = BD, C⁺ = C, D⁺ = D, BD⁺ = BD — none is the whole relation, and every other set contains one of A, E, BC, CD. So there are exactly 4 candidate keys.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C) — 4 candidate keys (A, E, BC, CD).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) misses BC and CD.</li><li>(B) misses one of E, BC, CD.</li><li>(D) over-counts: BD⁺ = BD is not a key.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Missing E or CD because the cycle makes closure look “too easy”. The dependency cycle A → B → D → E → A means closure from any member reaches all the others, so several attributes become keys in their own right.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Faster method.</b> Any attribute whose single-attribute closure is the whole relation is a key by itself; here that is true of A and E, and the C-bearing pairs add two more.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> When FDs form a cycle, compute closure from each single attribute first — every attribute that closes to ABCDE is already a key and needs no partner.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U1-Q12", "subject": "databases", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "er-relational-algebra", "subtopic": "relational-model-constraints", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Relation  R(A, B)  has  A  as its primary key and  B  as an ordinary nullable attribute. Which one of the following tuples, if inserted into  R , violates a constraint implied by this schema?", "options": ["(NULL, 5)", "(1, NULL)", "(1, 5)", "(2, NULL)"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Entity integrity: no component of a primary key may be NULL. B is not part of the key, so NULL is allowed there and only uniqueness would ever reject it — and NULLs do not clash with each other under this rule.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>A is the primary key, so every tuple must supply a non-NULL value for A.</li><li>(NULL, 5) supplies no key value, so it violates entity integrity.</li><li>(1, NULL) and (2, NULL) are fine: the key values 1 and 2 are present, B is nullable.</li><li>(1, 5) is fine as long as 1 is not already present.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A) — (NULL, 5).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) and (D) have a valid key value and a NULL in a nullable column.</li><li>(C) has a valid key value and no NULL.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Students reject (1, NULL) thinking no attribute may be NULL. Nullability is a schema property; only primary-key attributes are forced to be non-NULL.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Look at where NULL sits. Inside the primary key → rejected. Outside it → allowed unless the column is declared NOT NULL.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U1-Q13", "subject": "databases", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "er-relational-algebra", "subtopic": "relational-algebra-select-project-join-division", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "In relational algebra, the division operator  R ÷ S  is used to express", "options": ["universal quantification — the “for all” condition.", "aggregation of a numeric attribute over groups.", "an outer join that preserves unmatched tuples.", "elimination of duplicates from a bag."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Division is the relational-algebra counterpart of universal quantification. R ÷ S keeps those A-values of R(A, B) that are paired in R with <b>every</b> B-value appearing in S.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Write R(A, B) and S(B). R ÷ S = { a | for every b ∈ S, (a, b) ∈ R }.</li><li>The “for every b ∈ S” is exactly a ∀ condition, so division expresses queries such as “students who took all courses”.</li><li>It is not primitive: R ÷ S = π_A(R) − π_A( (π_A(R) × S) − R ).</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A) — universal quantification.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) Aggregation is not an operator of the basic algebra at all; it is added as γ in extended algebra.</li><li>(C) Outer joins are extensions of ⋈, written ⟕ / ⟖ / ⟗.</li><li>(D) Duplicate elimination is implicit in every algebra operator, not the job of ÷.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Division is mistaken for ordinary arithmetic division or for a join. It answers “for all”, not “how many”.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Read division as a nested quantifier: keep a only if nothing in S is missing from a's row.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U1-Q14", "subject": "databases", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "er-relational-algebra", "subtopic": "relational-algebra-select-project-join-division", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Let  R(A, B) = {(1, 10), (2, 20), (3, 10)}  and  S(B, C) = {(10, p), (20, q), (30, r)} . How many tuples are there in  R ⋈ S  (natural join)?", "options": ["2", "3", "4", "5"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A natural join equates the common attribute B and concatenates matching pairs. Its size is Σ_b r_b · s_b over the common values, where r_b and s_b are the multiplicities of b in R and S.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Common attribute is B.</li><li>B = 10 appears once in R and once in S → 1 × 1 = 1 tuple: (1, 10, p).</li><li>B = 20 appears once in R and once in S → 1 tuple: (2, 20, q).</li><li>B = 30 appears only in S → contributes nothing; B = 10 also occurs twice in R, so (3, 10, p) is a second tuple for B = 10.</li><li>Total = 1 + 1 + 1 = 3 tuples.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) — 3 tuples.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) counts distinct B values instead of tuples.</li><li>(C) and (D) invent matches for B = 30, which is absent from R.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Students assume one output tuple per distinct value of B (giving 2), forgetting that repeated B values in R each match. The join is a cross product restricted to equal B.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Count multiplicities: for each common value b, multiply its count in R by its count in S and sum. Here 1·1 + 1·1 = 2 for B = 10 and B = 20, plus the second B = 10 row, giving 3.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U1-Q15", "subject": "databases", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "er-relational-algebra", "subtopic": "relational-algebra-select-project-join-division", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Which of the following statements about relational algebra operators are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["Selection σ filters tuples and projection π filters attributes.", "Projection π removes duplicate tuples from its result.", "R − S and S − R are always equal.", "R ∪ S is defined only when R and S are union-compatible."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Relational algebra is set-based, so every operator produces a relation, i.e. a set with no duplicate tuples, and the binary set operators require matching schemas.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) σ keeps rows satisfying a predicate, π keeps a chosen list of columns — true.</li><li>(B) Because the result is a relation (a set), π collapses duplicates — true.</li><li>(C) Set difference is not symmetric: R − S keeps R's tuples absent from S, which is a different set from S − R.</li><li>(D) Union requires the same number of attributes with compatible domains in the same order — true.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(C) fails whenever the two relations are not identical: {1} − {2} = {1} but {2} − {1} = {2}.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> The duplicate elimination of π is the usual surprise: SQL's SELECT does not remove duplicates, but relational algebra's π does. Mixing the two semantics is a standard error.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Remember: algebra = sets, SQL = bags. Every algebra operator, including π, returns a set.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U1-Q16", "subject": "databases", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "er-relational-algebra", "subtopic": "relational-algebra-select-project-join-division", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Let  R(A, B) = {(a₁, b₁), (a₁, b₂), (a₂, b₁), (a₃, b₁), (a₃, b₂), (a₄, b₃)}  and  S(B) = {b₁, b₂} . How many tuples does  R ÷ S  contain? (Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "2", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> R ÷ S keeps an A-value only if it is paired with every B-value of S in R. Check each A-value against the full set {b₁, b₂}.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>a₁ is paired with b₁ and with b₂ → qualifies.</li><li>a₂ is paired only with b₁ → does not qualify.</li><li>a₃ is paired with b₁ and with b₂ → qualifies.</li><li>a₄ is paired only with b₃, which is not even in S → does not qualify.</li><li>So R ÷ S = {a₁, a₃}, two tuples.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 2.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Requiring only “at least one” match, which would keep a₂ and a₄ as well. Division demands every value of S, not any one of them.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Group R by A and check set-equality against S. Here a₁ and a₃ both map to exactly {b₁, b₂}.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U1-Q17", "subject": "databases", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "er-relational-algebra", "subtopic": "relational-algebra-select-project-join-division", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "GATE_PYQ_VARIATION", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Consider the schema  Doctor(did, dname, dage)  and  Patient(pid, rdid, pname, page) , where  rdid  is a foreign key referencing  did . Assume every doctor has at least one patient. Consider the relational algebra query  π_did(Doctor) − π_did( Doctor ⋈ (did = rdid) ∧ (dage ≤ page)  Patient )  The query evaluates to the set of dids of doctors whose age is greater than that of", "options": ["some patient.", "all patients.", "some of his/her patients.", "all of his/her patients."], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Read the query as a set difference: start from all doctors, then remove the doctors that have a patient whose age is at least the doctor's. What survives is exactly the doctors for whom no such patient exists.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Doctor ⋈<sub>(did = rdid) ∧ (dage ≤ page)</sub> Patient keeps pairs where the doctor's age is ≤ some patient's age, i.e. the doctor is not older than that patient.</li><li>Projecting on did gives the doctors having at least one patient who is at least as old as they are.</li><li>Subtracting this from π_did(Doctor) leaves the doctors who have no such patient.</li><li>“No patient is at least as old as the doctor” means the doctor is strictly older than every patient — all of his/her patients.</li><li>The assumption that every doctor has at least one patient matters: without it, a doctor with no patients would also survive, and the query would not mean “older than all”.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (D) — all of his/her patients.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) and (C) describe the set that is <b>removed</b>, not the set that survives.</li><li>(B) would require a ∀ condition expressed with division, not a single join.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering “some patient” by stopping at the join instead of completing the difference, or reading the join's ≤ as if it removed patients rather than doctors.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Faster method.</b> Any “−” over a join that encodes “exists a violating tuple” is the standard pattern for a universal statement: removing the violators leaves the ones that satisfy “for all”.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Translate each operator in turn: ⋈ with a condition = “there exists”, π = “the did of”, − = “but not”. Composing them gives “doctors for whom there is no patient at least as old”, which is “older than all”.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U1-Q18", "subject": "databases", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "er-relational-algebra", "subtopic": "relational-algebra-select-project-join-division", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "The relational algebra operator that returns a relation containing only a chosen subset of the attributes of its input is", "options": ["σ (select)", "π (projection)", "ρ (rename)", "⋈ (join)"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The six primitive operators act on different aspects of a relation: σ on rows, π on columns, ρ on names, and ×, ∪, − on whole relations.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>σ<sub>condition</sub>(R) keeps the rows that satisfy the condition, leaving the schema unchanged.</li><li>π<sub>attr list</sub>(R) keeps the listed columns and discards the rest, leaving the rows filtered by duplicate elimination.</li><li>ρ renames a relation or its attributes; ⋈ combines two relations on a condition.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) — π (projection).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) selects rows, not columns.</li><li>(C) only changes names.</li><li>(D) combines two relations.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> σ and π are swapped. A quick check: σ for <b>s</b>elect rows (horizontal cut), π for <b>p</b>roject columns (vertical cut).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Picture the table: a horizontal cut removes rows (σ), a vertical cut removes columns (π).</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U1-Q19", "subject": "databases", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "er-relational-algebra", "subtopic": "relational-algebra-select-project-join-division", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Let  R(A, B)  and  S(A, B)  be union-compatible relations. Which of the following are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["R ∪ S contains every tuple that appears in R or in S or in both, with duplicates removed.", "R ∩ S can be written as R − (R − S).", "R − S = S − R for all R and S.", "R × S contains |R| + |S| tuples."], "answer_display": "Options (A) and (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Set semantics. Union, intersection and difference behave as ordinary set operations once the schemas match; the Cartesian product multiplies cardinalities.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) is the definition of set union; since the result is a relation, duplicates collapse.</li><li>(B) R − S removes from R everything shared with S; removing those again from R leaves exactly the common part, so R − (R − S) = R ∩ S.</li><li>(C) fails for any unequal pair, e.g. R = {t₁}, S = {t₂}.</li><li>(D) is wrong: |R × S| = |R| · |S|.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A) and (B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(C) Difference is directional.</li><li>(D) The Cartesian product multiplies, it does not add.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Intersection is thought to need its own operator. It does not: ∩ is derivable from − (and from ⋈). Similarly ∪ is derivable from − and ×, which is why only six operators are primitive.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Memorise the derivations: R ∩ S = R − (R − S), and |R × S| = |R| × |S|.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U1-Q20", "subject": "databases", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "er-relational-algebra", "subtopic": "relational-algebra-select-project-join-division", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Relation  R(A, B, C)  has 10 tuples and relation  S(C, D)  has 8 tuples, with  C  the only common attribute. The tuples of  R  use exactly 4 distinct values of  C , and the tuples of  S  use exactly 5 distinct values of  C . What is the maximum possible number of tuples in  R ⋈ S ? (Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "31", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The size of a natural join is Σ<sub>c</sub> r_c · s_c over the common attribute, where r_c and s_c are the multiplicities of c in R and S. Under fixed totals the sum of products is largest when the two distributions are aligned and as concentrated as the distinct-value counts allow.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Let r_c ≥ 1 for 4 values of C with Σ r_c = 10, and s_c ≥ 1 for 5 values of C with Σ s_c = 8. Then |R ⋈ S| = Σ_c r_c · s_c.</li><li>Because there are 4 and 5 distinct values, at least 1 tuple must be spent on each of them. That leaves 10 − 4 = 6 extra R-tuples and 8 − 5 = 3 extra S-tuples to concentrate on one shared value c₀.</li><li>Put all the extras on c₀: r = (7, 1, 1, 1) and s = (4, 1, 1, 1, 1).</li><li>Matching the largest counts together gives 7 × 4 = 28 on c₀, and the three remaining shared values contribute 1 × 1 each: 28 + 3 = 31.</li><li>No other distribution does better — by the rearrangement inequality the best alignment pairs the largest r_c with the largest s_c, and concentrating mass maximises the largest product.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 31.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Multiplying the totals, 10 × 8 = 80, or the distinct counts, 4 × 5 = 20. Both ignore the hard constraint that each of the distinct values must carry at least one tuple.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Subtract the compulsory one tuple per distinct value first; only the leftovers can be concentrated. Here 6 and 3 leftovers give 7 × 4 = 28 plus 3 forced unit products.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U1-Q21", "subject": "databases", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "er-relational-algebra", "subtopic": "tuple-and-domain-relational-calculus", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "The tuple relational calculus query  { t | t ∈ Student ∧ t[age] > 20 }  is equivalent to", "options": ["π_age(σ_age&gt;20(Student))", "σ_age&gt;20(Student)", "σ_age&gt;20(π_age(Student))", "Student ⋈ σ_age&gt;20(Student)"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> In tuple calculus the free tuple variable ranges over all tuples of the relation named in the membership clause; a conjunctive condition on its attributes corresponds exactly to selection, and no projection is involved because t carries all attributes.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>t ∈ Student fixes the range of t to the tuples of Student.</li><li>t[age] &gt; 20 is the selection predicate.</li><li>The result keeps whole tuples, so the algebra translation is σ_age&gt;20(Student) with the same schema.</li><li>Projection would be needed only if the query returned t[some attributes], not t itself.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) — σ_age&gt;20(Student).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) changes the schema to a single column, which the query does not ask for.</li><li>(C) projects before selecting, which may drop attributes the predicate needs.</li><li>(D) joins Student with itself; the calculus query has one variable.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Students add a projection because the predicate mentions age. The predicate filters; it does not change the output schema. Only an explicit attribute list on t changes the schema.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Membership clause → which relation. Conditions on attributes → σ. t[list] on the left of the bar → π of that list.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U1-Q22", "subject": "databases", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "er-relational-algebra", "subtopic": "tuple-and-domain-relational-calculus", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "In tuple relational calculus, the expression  { t | ¬(t ∈ R) }  is rejected as unsafe because", "options": ["it is syntactically invalid.", "it can denote an infinite relation, since t may take values outside the active domain.", "negation is not permitted in relational calculus.", "t is a free variable."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A calculus expression is safe when every value it can produce comes from the active domain — the set of values actually occurring in the database. { t | ¬(t ∈ R) } ranges over the infinite universe of all possible tuples, so it is not a finite relation and cannot be evaluated.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The comprehension { t | ¬(t ∈ R) } admits every tuple not in R, over every possible attribute domain.</li><li>That set is infinite (or at least unbounded), so it is not a relation in the database sense.</li><li>Safety requires each answer tuple to be drawn from dom(R) extended by constants in the query.</li><li>Negation itself is perfectly legal — only its unrestricted use is the problem.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) — it can denote an infinite relation.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) The syntax is well formed.</li><li>(C) Negation is allowed; unsafe negation is not.</li><li>(D) t is free by design — the free variable names the answer tuples.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Negation is blamed. The calculus allows ¬ freely; what it forbids is a query whose result is not confined to the active domain. { t | t ∈ R ∧ ¬P(t) } is safe, { t | ¬(t ∈ R) } is not.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Ask of every calculus query: which relation bounds the free variable? If nothing does, the query is unsafe.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U1-Q23", "subject": "databases", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "er-relational-algebra", "subtopic": "tuple-and-domain-relational-calculus", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Which of the following statements about relational calculus are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["Tuple relational calculus and domain relational calculus have the same expressive power.", "Every relational algebra expression can be written as a safe relational calculus expression.", "Relational calculus is procedural: it states how the answer is to be computed.", "The safety condition confines a query's values to the active domain of the database."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Relational algebra and the two calculi are all <b>relationally complete</b>: the algebra is procedural, the calculi are declarative, and all three express exactly the same class of queries once safety is imposed.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) TRC and DRC are inter-translatable, so neither is more expressive — true.</li><li>(B) Codd's theorem gives translations both ways between algebra and safe calculus — true.</li><li>(C) Calculus says <b>what</b> the answer is (a set of tuples satisfying a formula); the algebra says <b>how</b>. So calculus is declarative, not procedural.</li><li>(D) Safety restricts quantifiers and free variables to the active domain, which is what makes the expression finite — true.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(C) reverses the definitions — calculus is declarative, not procedural.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Calculus is assumed to be the “stronger” formalism. In fact it has exactly the same power as the algebra; what it lacks is a fixed evaluation order, which is why SQL compilers translate to algebra.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Two things to keep straight: procedural = algebra, declarative = calculus, equal in power; and safety is the condition that makes calculus finite.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U1-Q24", "subject": "databases", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "er-relational-algebra", "subtopic": "tuple-and-domain-relational-calculus", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Let  R(A, B) = {(1, 5), (2, 5), (2, 7), (3, 5), (4, 9)} . The domain relational calculus query  { ⟨a⟩ | ∃b (⟨a, b⟩ ∈ R ∧ b = 5) }  returns how many distinct values of  a ? (Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "3", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A domain-calculus query projects the free variables of its formula. The existential quantifier on b makes b a bound (projected-away) variable, so the answer is the set of a-values that co-occur with b = 5.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The formula requires a pair (a, b) in R with b = 5.</li><li>Pairs with b = 5 are (1, 5), (2, 5) and (3, 5).</li><li>Their a-values are 1, 2, 3 — three distinct values.</li><li>(2, 7) and (4, 9) are excluded because their b is not 5.</li><li>In algebra this is π_A(σ_B=5(R)), which has 3 tuples.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 3.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Counting tuples rather than distinct a-values, or being distracted by the repeated a = 2. The result of a calculus query is a set, so a = 2 contributes once.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Free variables = output columns. Here only a is free, so translate to π_A(σ_B=5(R)) and count.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U1-Q25", "subject": "databases", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "er-relational-algebra", "subtopic": "tuple-and-domain-relational-calculus", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Which one of the following queries  cannot  be expressed in relational algebra, nor in safe relational calculus?", "options": ["Names of employees who earn more than their manager.", "Names of all employees who are, directly or indirectly, subordinates of a given employee.", "Names of employees who work on every project.", "Names of employees who work on no project."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Relational algebra and safe calculus are relationally complete but not Turing complete: they can express any finite first-order query over the stored relations, and nothing that needs an unbounded number of iteration steps. Transitive closure is the standard query outside that class.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) is a self-join of Employee with itself on manager = eid plus a comparison — first-order, expressible.</li><li>(C) is division: π_eid(Works) ÷ π_pid(Project) — expressible.</li><li>(D) is a difference: π_eid(Employee) − π_eid(Works) — expressible.</li><li>(B) asks for the transitive closure of the “manager of” relation. Its length is unbounded, so no fixed finite expression of the algebra or of first-order calculus can capture it.</li><li>This is the classic reason SQL needed recursion (WITH RECURSIVE) to compute closure.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) needs one join and one comparison.</li><li>(C) is a textbook division query.</li><li>(D) is a set difference.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> “Indirectly” is read as “one more join”. One extra join only reaches depth two; no finite number of joins reaches every depth, which is precisely why closure is not first-order expressible.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Faster method.</b> Whenever a query says “directly or indirectly”, “any number of levels”, or “shortest path”, suspect transitive closure — outside relational algebra.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Distinguish the quantifier patterns. “For all projects” is a bounded ∀ and division handles it; “any number of steps” is an unbounded iteration and needs recursion.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U1-Q26", "subject": "databases", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "er-relational-algebra", "subtopic": "er-diagrams-cardinality-weak-entities", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "In Chen notation, a relationship set of  degree three  is drawn as", "options": ["a single diamond joined by three lines, one to each of the three participating entity sets.", "three diamonds, each joined to two entity sets.", "a double diamond joined to three entity sets.", "a rectangle joined to three diamonds."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The <i>degree</i> of a relationship set is the number of entity sets that take part in it. Chen notation draws one relationship as one diamond, with one line per participating entity set — so degree three is one diamond with three lines.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Count the entity sets that take part: three.</li><li>Draw one diamond — the relationship itself is a single object, not one object per pair.</li><li>Draw one connecting line from the diamond to each of the three entity sets.</li><li>Label each line with that entity set's cardinality contribution if the diagram shows it.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A) — one diamond with three lines.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reading a ternary relationship as three binary diamonds. Three binary relationships record three independent pairwise facts and cannot express the single triple fact that a ternary relationship records.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U1-Q27", "subject": "databases", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "er-relational-algebra", "subtopic": "er-diagrams-cardinality-weak-entities", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Entity sets  A  ( m  entities) and  B  ( n  entities), with  m, n ≥ 1 , take part in a many-to-many relationship  R , and  both  participations are total. The number of tuples in the relation that represents  R  lies", "options": ["exactly at m · n.", "between max(m, n) and m · n, inclusive.", "between m + n and m · n, inclusive.", "exactly at m + n."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Two independent constraints bound a many-to-many relationship: the ratio gives the upper bound and total participation gives the lower bound.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Upper bound: each of the <span class=\"mm\">m</span> A-entities may pair with each of the <span class=\"mm\">n</span> B-entities, so at most <span class=\"mm\">m · n</span> distinct pairs.</li><li>Lower bound from A's side: total participation means every A-entity appears in at least one pair, so at least <span class=\"mm\">m</span> pairs.</li><li>Lower bound from B's side: every B-entity appears in at least one pair, so at least <span class=\"mm\">n</span> pairs.</li><li>Pairs are distinct tuples, so the count is at least the larger of the two bounds, i.e. at least <span class=\"mm\">max(m, n)</span>. Both bounds are attainable.</li><li>Hence <span class=\"mm\">max(m, n) ≤ |R| ≤ m · n</span>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Adding the cardinalities (<span class=\"mm\">m + n</span>) or assuming the maximum is forced. The maximum <span class=\"mm\">m · n</span> needs every A-entity to pair with <i>every</i> B-entity, which the m:n ratio permits but does not require.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U1-Q28", "subject": "databases", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "er-relational-algebra", "subtopic": "er-diagrams-cardinality-weak-entities", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": " W  is a weak entity set owned by entity set  E  through the identifying relationship  R . Which of the following statements are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["W has a partial key (discriminator) that distinguishes W-entities owned by the same E-entity.", "The participation of W in R is total.", "The primary key of the relation built from W is the primary key of E together with W's partial key.", "A W-entity may exist without being related to any E-entity."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B), (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A weak entity set has no key of its own. It is identified by its owner, and its existence is dependent on that owner — existence dependency is exactly what 'weak' means.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Because <span class=\"mm\">W</span> has no key of its own, it carries a partial key (drawn with a dashed underline) that only has to be unique <i>within</i> one owner's set of W-entities — so (A) is true.</li><li>A weak entity cannot be identified without its owner, so it must always take part in the identifying relationship — so (B) is true.</li><li>The identifying relationship is one-to-many from <span class=\"mm\">E</span> to <span class=\"mm\">W</span>, so the owner's key together with the partial key is unique over all of <span class=\"mm\">W</span> — so (C) is true.</li><li>(D) contradicts existence dependency: an owner-less weak entity has no identity at all — so (D) is false.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Thinking the partial key alone identifies a weak entity. It does not: two different owners may each own a W-entity with the same partial-key value, which is why the owner's key must be prefixed.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U1-Q29", "subject": "databases", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "er-relational-algebra", "subtopic": "er-diagrams-cardinality-weak-entities", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "An ER schema contains entity sets  A, B, C, D ; a weak entity set  W  owned by  A ; a multivalued attribute  phone  on  B ; a 1:N relationship from  A  to  C ; a many-to-many relationship between  B  and  D ; and a ternary relationship among  A, B  and  C . Applying the standard ER-to-relational mapping — one relation per strong or weak entity set, a separate relation per multivalued attribute, a 1:N relationship absorbed into the relation on its many side, and a separate relation per m:n or n-ary relationship — how many relations does the schema produce?", "options": [], "answer_display": "8", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The ER-to-relational mapping is a fixed recipe; the question is which constructs cost a relation and which are absorbed into an existing one.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Strong entity sets <span class=\"mm\">A, B, C, D</span> → 4 relations.</li><li>Weak entity set <span class=\"mm\">W</span> → 1 more relation (it becomes a relation whose key is A's key plus W's partial key) → 5.</li><li>Multivalued attribute <span class=\"mm\">phone</span> → 1 more relation (owner key + phone value) → 6.</li><li>1:N relationship <span class=\"mm\">A → C</span> → 0 new relations; it is absorbed as a foreign key in <span class=\"mm\">C</span> → still 6.</li><li>m:n relationship <span class=\"mm\">B–D</span> → 1 more relation → 7.</li><li>Ternary relationship among <span class=\"mm\">A, B, C</span> → 1 more relation → 8.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 8</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Charging a relation for the 1:N relationship, or forgetting that a weak entity set still gets its own relation. The 1:N side is the free one; the weak entity set is not.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Only relationships whose cardinality cannot be carried by a foreign key — m:n and n-ary — need a relation of their own.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U1-Q30", "subject": "databases", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "er-relational-algebra", "subtopic": "er-diagrams-cardinality-weak-entities", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Which one of the following statements about a ternary relationship  R  among entity sets  A, B  and  C  is TRUE?", "options": ["R is always equivalent to three binary relationships among A, B and C.", "R can record a combination of an A-entity, a B-entity and a C-entity that no collection of binary relationships can record.", "R must be many-to-many in all three directions.", "R requires every entity to take part exactly once."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A relationship of degree three is an association among three entities <i>simultaneously</i>, not three associations among pairs.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>A ternary relationship instance is a triple <span class=\"mm\">(a, b, c)</span>.</li><li>Three binary relationships would instead store <span class=\"mm\">(a, b)</span>, <span class=\"mm\">(b, c)</span> and <span class=\"mm\">(a, c)</span> separately. From those three pairs you cannot tell which triples actually occurred: the pairs <span class=\"mm\">(a1, b1), (b1, c1), (a1, c1)</span> are consistent with both <span class=\"mm\">(a1, b1, c1)</span> and with <span class=\"mm\">(a1, b1, c2), (a1, b2, c1), …</span></li><li>So the ternary relationship carries information the binary decomposition loses — (B) is true and (A) is false.</li><li>Nothing forces the ratio to be m:n in every direction, and nothing forces single participation — (C) and (D) are false.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming a ternary relationship is just 'three binary ones glued together'. It is not, and that is precisely why the ER-to-relational mapping gives an n-ary relationship its own relation.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U1-Q31", "subject": "databases", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "er-relational-algebra", "subtopic": "er-diagrams-cardinality-weak-entities", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Entity set  A  has a one-to-many relationship  R  with entity set  B  — that is, one A-entity is related to many B-entities. Which statement is TRUE?", "options": ["Each B-entity is related to at most one A-entity.", "Each A-entity is related to at most one B-entity.", "Each A-entity must be related to at least one B-entity.", "Each B-entity must be related to at least one A-entity."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A cardinality ratio constrains <i>how many</i> entities on one side may be associated with an entity on the other. It says nothing about whether an entity must be associated at all — that is participation, a separate constraint.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>'One A to many B' means the function runs from B to A: each B-entity picks at most one A-entity.</li><li>So (A) is true.</li><li>The same ratio allows an A-entity to have any number of B-entities, including zero, so (B) is false.</li><li>(C) and (D) assert total participation, which the question never states. Both are false.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reading 'one-to-many from A to B' as 'one B per A'. The 'one' side is the side that is unique in the other direction: one A per B.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U1-Q32", "subject": "databases", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "er-relational-algebra", "subtopic": "relational-model-constraints", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Relation  R(A, B, C, D)  has exactly one candidate key,  {A} . The number of superkeys of  R  is", "options": ["4", "8", "15", "16"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A superkey is any attribute set that contains a candidate key. With a single candidate key <span class=\"mm\">{A}</span>, a set is a superkey exactly when it contains <span class=\"mm\">A</span>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Any superkey must contain <span class=\"mm\">A</span> — otherwise it cannot determine the whole tuple.</li><li>Each of the remaining three attributes <span class=\"mm\">B, C, D</span> is then free to be in or out.</li><li>That gives <span class=\"mm\">2³ = 8</span> superkeys.</li><li>Check: <span class=\"mm\">{A}, {AB}, {AC}, {AD}, {ABC}, {ABD}, {ACD}, {ABCD}</span> — eight.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) — 8.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Counting <span class=\"mm\">2⁴ = 16</span> by letting <span class=\"mm\">A</span> vary too. <span class=\"mm\">A</span> is compulsory in every superkey, so only the other three attributes are free.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> With one candidate key of size <span class=\"mm\">k</span> in a relation of <span class=\"mm\">n</span> attributes, the superkey count is <span class=\"mm\">2^(n − k)</span>.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U1-Q33", "subject": "databases", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "er-relational-algebra", "subtopic": "relational-model-constraints", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Relation  R(A, B, C, D, E)  satisfies the functional dependencies  F = {A → B, BC → D, D → E, E → C} . The number of candidate keys of  R  is", "options": ["1", "2", "3", "4"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A candidate key is a minimal attribute set whose closure is all five attributes. Enumerate closures, then drop any set that contains a smaller key.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><span class=\"mm\">A⁺ = {A, B}</span> — <span class=\"mm\">BC → D</span> needs <span class=\"mm\">C</span>, so nothing further fires. <span class=\"mm\">A</span> alone is not a key.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">{A, C}⁺</span>: <span class=\"mm\">A → B</span> adds B; <span class=\"mm\">BC → D</span> adds D; <span class=\"mm\">D → E</span> adds E. Closure is all five — <span class=\"mm\">{A, C}</span> is a key.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">{A, D}⁺</span>: <span class=\"mm\">A → B</span>; <span class=\"mm\">D → E</span>; <span class=\"mm\">E → C</span>; then <span class=\"mm\">BC → D</span>. All five — <span class=\"mm\">{A, D}</span> is a key.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">{A, E}⁺</span>: <span class=\"mm\">A → B</span>; <span class=\"mm\">E → C</span>; <span class=\"mm\">BC → D</span>; <span class=\"mm\">D → E</span>. All five — <span class=\"mm\">{A, E}</span> is a key.</li><li>No set without <span class=\"mm\">A</span> closes: from <span class=\"mm\">{B, C, D, E}</span> the rules <span class=\"mm\">BC → D, D → E, E → C</span> never produce <span class=\"mm\">A</span>.</li><li>So the candidate keys are <span class=\"mm\">{A, C}, {A, D}, {A, E}</span> — three, all minimal.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C) — 3.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Stopping at the first key found, or counting <span class=\"mm\">{A, C, D}</span> as a fourth key. A superset of a key is a superkey, never a candidate key.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U1-Q34", "subject": "databases", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "er-relational-algebra", "subtopic": "relational-model-constraints", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Which of the following statements about functional dependencies are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["A → B is trivial exactly when B ⊆ A.", "If A → B and B → C, then A → C.", "If A → B, then AC → BC for every attribute set C.", "If A → B and C → B, then A → C."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B), (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Armstrong's axioms: reflexivity, augmentation and transitivity generate every dependency implied by a set, and nothing else does.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Reflexivity: <span class=\"mm\">A → B</span> holds automatically when <span class=\"mm\">B ⊆ A</span>, because a set always determines its own subsets. Such a dependency is called trivial — (A) is true.</li><li>Transitivity: <span class=\"mm\">A → B</span> and <span class=\"mm\">B → C</span> give <span class=\"mm\">A → C</span> — (B) is true.</li><li>Augmentation: adding the same attributes to both sides preserves the dependency, so <span class=\"mm\">A → B</span> yields <span class=\"mm\">AC → BC</span> — (C) is true.</li><li>(D) is the converse of transitivity and is not a valid rule. Two different sets may each determine <span class=\"mm\">B</span> without determining each other — (D) is false.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Treating <span class=\"mm\">A → B, C → B ⊢ A → C</span> as transitivity. Transitivity needs the same set on the right of one dependency and the left of the next; here the shared attribute is on the right of both.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U1-Q35", "subject": "databases", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "er-relational-algebra", "subtopic": "relational-model-constraints", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Relation  R(A, B, C, D)  has exactly one candidate key,  {A, B} . The number of superkeys of  R  is", "options": [], "answer_display": "4", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Every superkey must contain the whole candidate key; the attributes outside it are free.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>A superkey must contain <span class=\"mm\">{A, B}</span>.</li><li>The two remaining attributes <span class=\"mm\">C, D</span> are each free to be included or excluded.</li><li>That gives <span class=\"mm\">2² = 4</span> superkeys: <span class=\"mm\">{AB}, {ABC}, {ABD}, {ABCD}</span>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 4</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering 8 by treating the key as size 1, or 16 by letting <span class=\"mm\">A</span> and <span class=\"mm\">B</span> vary independently. Neither can be dropped.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U1-Q36", "subject": "databases", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "er-relational-algebra", "subtopic": "relational-model-constraints", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Relation  R(A, B, C)  has exactly two candidate keys,  {A}  and  {B} . Which statement is TRUE?", "options": ["R has exactly 8 superkeys.", "{A, B} is a superkey but not a candidate key.", "Every attribute of R is prime.", "R has exactly two superkeys."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Candidate keys are the <i>minimal</i> superkeys. Any larger set containing a key is still a superkey, but minimality is what a candidate key adds.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><span class=\"mm\">{A, B}</span> determines every attribute (it contains the key <span class=\"mm\">{A}</span>), so it is a superkey.</li><li>It is not minimal, because the proper subset <span class=\"mm\">{A}</span> is already a superkey. So it is not a candidate key — (B) is true.</li><li>Superkeys are the sets containing <span class=\"mm\">A</span> or containing <span class=\"mm\">B</span>: <span class=\"mm\">{A}, {B}, {AB}, {AC}, {BC}, {ABC}</span> — six, so (A) and (D) are both false.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">C</span> belongs to no candidate key, so it is non-prime — (C) is false.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Adding 4 + 4 = 8 superkeys by counting the supersets of each key separately. The set <span class=\"mm\">{A, B, C}</span> is counted twice that way; inclusion–exclusion gives 4 + 4 − 2 = 6.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U1-Q37", "subject": "databases", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "er-relational-algebra", "subtopic": "relational-model-constraints", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Which one of the following violates  entity integrity ?", "options": ["A tuple whose primary-key attribute is NULL.", "A tuple whose foreign key is NULL.", "A tuple whose non-key attribute is NULL.", "Two tuples that agree on a non-key attribute."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Entity integrity: no component of a primary key may be NULL, because the key is what identifies the tuple. Referential integrity, by contrast, is about foreign keys and explicitly allows NULL.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>A primary key identifies a tuple; a NULL is 'unknown', and an unknown value cannot identify anything — so (A) violates entity integrity.</li><li>A NULL foreign key means 'this tuple refers to nothing', which referential integrity permits — (B) is legal.</li><li>Non-key attributes may be NULL unless a NOT NULL constraint says otherwise — (C) is legal.</li><li>Only key attributes must be unique; duplicates in a non-key attribute are ordinary data — (D) is legal.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Confusing entity integrity with referential integrity, and so rejecting a NULL foreign key. That is exactly the case referential integrity allows.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U1-Q38", "subject": "databases", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "er-relational-algebra", "subtopic": "relational-algebra-select-project-join-division", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Relation  R  has 4 tuples and 3 attributes; relation  S  has 5 tuples and 2 attributes. They share exactly one attribute  B , which is a key of  R  and a foreign key in  S  that is never NULL. Every tuple of  S  matches exactly one tuple of  R . The number of tuples in  R ⋈ S  is", "options": ["4", "5", "9", "20"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A natural join pairs tuples that agree on the common attribute. When the common attribute is a key on one side, each tuple on the other side has at most one partner.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Each of the 5 tuples of <span class=\"mm\">S</span> has a <span class=\"mm\">B</span>-value that is a key value of <span class=\"mm\">R</span>, so it matches exactly one <span class=\"mm\">R</span>-tuple.</li><li>That produces exactly one joined tuple per <span class=\"mm\">S</span>-tuple: 5.</li><li>Some <span class=\"mm\">R</span>-tuples may match nothing and simply do not appear — the join is not an outer join, so <span class=\"mm\">R</span>'s 4 tuples do not add to the count.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) — 5.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Multiplying 4 × 5 = 20, which is the size of the Cartesian product and only an upper bound for a join. Because <span class=\"mm\">B</span> is a key of <span class=\"mm\">R</span>, no <span class=\"mm\">S</span>-tuple can have two partners.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U1-Q39", "subject": "databases", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "er-relational-algebra", "subtopic": "relational-algebra-select-project-join-division", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "For union-compatible relations  R  and  S , which expression is equivalent to  R ∩ S ?", "options": ["R − (R − S)", "(R − S) − S", "(R ∪ S) − R", "πall(R) − πall(S)"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Intersection is not primitive in the core algebra; it is defined from set difference.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><span class=\"mm\">R − S</span> keeps the tuples of <span class=\"mm\">R</span> that are not in <span class=\"mm\">S</span>.</li><li>Removing those from <span class=\"mm\">R</span> leaves exactly the tuples of <span class=\"mm\">R</span> that <i>are</i> in <span class=\"mm\">S</span>: <span class=\"mm\">R − (R − S) = R ∩ S</span>.</li><li>(B) simplifies to <span class=\"mm\">R − S</span>; (C) is <span class=\"mm\">S − R</span>; (D) is just <span class=\"mm\">R − S</span> again.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Choosing <span class=\"mm\">(R ∪ S) − R</span>, which looks like it 'removes the overlap' but actually returns <span class=\"mm\">S − R</span> — the opposite set.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U1-Q40", "subject": "databases", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "er-relational-algebra", "subtopic": "relational-algebra-select-project-join-division", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Consider  Works(eid, pid)  and  Projects(pid) . Which expressions return exactly the  eid s of employees who work on  every  project? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["πeid(Works) ÷ πpid(Projects)", "πeid(Works) − πeid((πeid(Works) × πpid(Projects)) − Works)", "πeid(Works) − πeid(πeid(Works) × πpid(Projects))", "πeid(Works ⋈ Projects)"], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> 'For all' is division. Equivalently, it is 'difference of difference': take everyone, subtract those who miss at least one project.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) is the division operator applied directly: it returns the <span class=\"mm\">eid</span>s whose set of projects contains the whole of <span class=\"mm\">π<sub>pid</sub>(Projects)</span> — true.</li><li>(B) builds the set of <i>missing</i> pairs — every (eid, pid) combination that is not in <span class=\"mm\">Works</span> — projects it to <span class=\"mm\">eid</span>, and subtracts it from all employees. What survives misses no project — true.</li><li>(C) subtracts all employees who work on anything at all, which returns only employees with no project — false.</li><li>(D) returns every employee who works on at least one project — that is 'exists', not 'for all' — false.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A) and (B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reading (D) as the answer. A join with the projects relation answers 'works on <i>some</i> project'; the universal quantifier needs division or the double difference.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U1-Q41", "subject": "databases", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "er-relational-algebra", "subtopic": "relational-algebra-select-project-join-division", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Let  R(A) = {1, 2, 3}  and  S(A) = {2, 3, 4} . The number of tuples in  R ⋈ R.A < S.A  S  is", "options": [], "answer_display": "6", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A theta join pairs every tuple of one relation with every tuple of the other that satisfies the condition — here a strict inequality, not an equality on a common attribute.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>List the pairs <span class=\"mm\">(r, s)</span> with <span class=\"mm\">r ∈ R</span>, <span class=\"mm\">s ∈ S</span> and <span class=\"mm\">r &lt; s</span>.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">r = 1</span>: <span class=\"mm\">s = 2, 3, 4</span> → 3 pairs.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">r = 2</span>: <span class=\"mm\">s = 3, 4</span> → 2 pairs.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">r = 3</span>: <span class=\"mm\">s = 4</span> → 1 pair.</li><li>Total <span class=\"mm\">3 + 2 + 1 = 6</span>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 6</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Joining on equality of <span class=\"mm\">A</span>, which would give only the pairs <span class=\"mm\">(2, 2)</span> and <span class=\"mm\">(3, 3)</span>. The condition is <span class=\"mm\">&lt;</span>, and both relations are kept whole — the attribute is not merged.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U1-Q42", "subject": "databases", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "er-relational-algebra", "subtopic": "relational-algebra-select-project-join-division", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Let  S(B)  be a relation with  zero  tuples. For a relation  R(A, B) , the division  R ÷ S  is", "options": ["the empty relation.", "πA(R).", "undefined, because division needs a non-empty divisor.", "R."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Division asks: which <span class=\"mm\">A</span>-values are paired with <i>every</i> <span class=\"mm\">B</span>-value in <span class=\"mm\">S</span>? With an empty <span class=\"mm\">S</span> the requirement is vacuously satisfied by every value.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>By definition, <span class=\"mm\">R ÷ S = { a | for every b ∈ S, (a, b) ∈ R }</span>.</li><li>When <span class=\"mm\">S = ∅</span> there is no <span class=\"mm\">b</span> to check, so the condition holds for every <span class=\"mm\">a</span> that appears in <span class=\"mm\">R</span>.</li><li>The values of <span class=\"mm\">a</span> that appear in <span class=\"mm\">R</span> are exactly <span class=\"mm\">π<sub>A</sub>(R)</span>, so <span class=\"mm\">R ÷ ∅ = π<sub>A</sub>(R)</span>.</li><li>Confirm with the derived formula: <span class=\"mm\">π<sub>A</sub>(R) − π<sub>A</sub>((π<sub>A</sub>(R) × ∅) − R) = π<sub>A</sub>(R) − π<sub>A</sub>(∅) = π<sub>A</sub>(R)</span>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering 'the empty relation' by pattern-matching 'empty input, empty output'. Universally quantified conditions are vacuously true over an empty range, so the result is the <i>largest</i> possible set, not the smallest.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> This vacuous-truth case is the same reason a universal statement over an empty domain is true, and it is a favourite GATE trap.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U1-Q43", "subject": "databases", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "er-relational-algebra", "subtopic": "relational-algebra-select-project-join-division", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Which of the following is  not  a unary relational algebra operator?", "options": ["σ (selection)", "π (projection)", "⋈ (natural join)", "ρ (rename)"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Arity of an operator = number of relations it consumes. Selection, projection and rename each transform one relation; a join combines two.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><span class=\"mm\">σ<sub>cond</sub>(R)</span> filters the tuples of a single relation — unary.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">π<sub>attrs</sub>(R)</span> keeps a subset of the attributes of a single relation — unary.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">ρ</span> renames a relation or its attributes — unary.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">R ⋈ S</span> needs both <span class=\"mm\">R</span> and <span class=\"mm\">S</span> — it is binary, so it is the answer.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Classifying the join as unary because 'it acts on the attributes in common'. It consumes two relations, which is what arity measures.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U1-Q44", "subject": "databases", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "er-relational-algebra", "subtopic": "relational-algebra-select-project-join-division", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Let  R(A, B)  and  S(B, C) , where  B  is a key of  R  and a foreign key in  S  that is never NULL. Which of the following statements are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["R ⋈ S has exactly as many tuples as S.", "R ⋈ S has attributes A, B and C.", "R ⋈ S can contain more tuples than |R| × |S|.", "The natural join is associative."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B), (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A natural join equates the common attributes and keeps one copy of each. When the common attribute is a key on one side, the join is a lookup, not a product.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Every tuple of <span class=\"mm\">S</span> has a non-NULL <span class=\"mm\">B</span>-value, and referential integrity guarantees a matching <span class=\"mm\">R</span>-tuple. Since <span class=\"mm\">B</span> is a key of <span class=\"mm\">R</span>, that partner is unique — one joined tuple per <span class=\"mm\">S</span>-tuple, so the counts are equal — (A) is true.</li><li>The common attribute appears once, and the remaining attributes of both sides are retained — (B) is true.</li><li>A join can never produce more than the Cartesian product <span class=\"mm\">|R| × |S|</span>; it selects a subset of those pairs — (C) is false.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">(R ⋈ S) ⋈ T = R ⋈ (S ⋈ T)</span> for natural joins, and it is commutative too — (D) is true.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Believing a natural join can blow up the row count. Duplication on the common attribute is what multiplies rows, and a key on one side makes that impossible.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U1-Q45", "subject": "databases", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "er-relational-algebra", "subtopic": "relational-algebra-select-project-join-division", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Relations  R(A, B)  and  S(A, B)  are union-compatible, with  |R| = 12 ,  |S| = 9  and  |R − S| = 5 . The number of tuples in  R ∩ S  is", "options": [], "answer_display": "7", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> <span class=\"mm\">R</span> splits into two disjoint parts: the tuples it shares with <span class=\"mm\">S</span>, and the tuples it does not.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><span class=\"mm\">R = (R − S) ∪ (R ∩ S)</span>, and the two parts are disjoint.</li><li>So <span class=\"mm\">|R| = |R − S| + |R ∩ S|</span>.</li><li>Substituting: <span class=\"mm\">12 = 5 + |R ∩ S|</span>, hence <span class=\"mm\">|R ∩ S| = 7</span>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 7</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Using <span class=\"mm\">|R ∪ S|</span> instead: that would give <span class=\"mm\">12 + 9 − 7 = 14</span>, a different quantity. The stated set is the difference, not the union.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U1-Q46", "subject": "databases", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "er-relational-algebra", "subtopic": "tuple-and-domain-relational-calculus", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "In the tuple relational calculus formula  { t | ∃s ∈ R (t[A] = s[A]) } , the variable  t  is", "options": ["bound by the existential quantifier.", "free.", "quantified over the domain of A.", "unsafe."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A variable is free when it is not in the scope of a quantifier. The free variables of a formula are exactly the columns of the result.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><span class=\"mm\">s</span> is introduced by <span class=\"mm\">∃s ∈ R</span>, so <span class=\"mm\">s</span> is bound.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">t</span> is never introduced by a quantifier — it appears on the left of the bar and inside the formula as a free variable.</li><li>Free variables supply the output columns, which is why the result is a set of <span class=\"mm\">t</span> tuples — so (B) is true.</li><li>Quantification over the domain of <span class=\"mm\">A</span> is domain calculus syntax; here the range is a relation, so (C) is false. Nothing about safety is violated, so (D) is false.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming the variable on the left of the bar is bound because it 'belongs to' the query. Being on the left of the bar is precisely what makes it free.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U1-Q47", "subject": "databases", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "er-relational-algebra", "subtopic": "tuple-and-domain-relational-calculus", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Which of the following statements about the domain relational calculus are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["A query has the form { ⟨x₁, …, xₙ⟩ | P(x₁, …, xₙ) }.", "Its variables range over domain values rather than over whole tuples.", "Safety confines the result to values drawn from the active domain.", "It is strictly more expressive than relational algebra."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B), (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Domain calculus is the value-based counterpart of tuple calculus: the variables are single values, and the result is a set of value-tuples.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The result is a set of value-tuples <span class=\"mm\">⟨x₁, …, xₙ⟩</span> satisfying <span class=\"mm\">P</span> — (A) is true.</li><li>Each <span class=\"mm\">xᵢ</span> ranges over the domain of an attribute, not over tuples — that is the distinction from tuple calculus — (B) is true.</li><li>Safety restricts every variable to the active domain — the set of values actually occurring in the database — which is what makes the result finite — (C) is true.</li><li>Domain calculus, tuple calculus and relational algebra are all equivalent in expressive power (Codd's theorem), so (D) is false.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Believing one calculus is stronger than the other. Both are exactly as strong as relational algebra — and all three fall short of transitive closure.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U1-Q48", "subject": "databases", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "er-relational-algebra", "subtopic": "tuple-and-domain-relational-calculus", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Let  R(A, B) = {(1, 2), (2, 3), (3, 1), (4, 2), (5, 3)} . The number of tuples in  { ⟨a₁, a₂⟩ | ∃b (R(a₁, b) ∧ R(a₂, b) ∧ a₁ ≠ a₂) }  is", "options": [], "answer_display": "4", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The shared bound variable <span class=\"mm\">b</span> links two occurrences of the same relation — a self-join expressed in calculus. The condition <span class=\"mm\">a₁ ≠ a₂</span> removes the diagonal.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Group the tuples by <span class=\"mm\">b</span>.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">b = 2</span>: <span class=\"mm\">a ∈ {1, 4}</span> → the ordered pairs <span class=\"mm\">(1, 4)</span> and <span class=\"mm\">(4, 1)</span> → 2.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">b = 3</span>: <span class=\"mm\">a ∈ {2, 5}</span> → <span class=\"mm\">(2, 5)</span> and <span class=\"mm\">(5, 2)</span> → 2.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">b = 1</span>: <span class=\"mm\">a ∈ {3}</span> only → no pair with <span class=\"mm\">a₁ ≠ a₂</span> → 0.</li><li>Total <span class=\"mm\">2 + 2 + 0 = 4</span>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 4</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Forgetting that the answer is ordered. <span class=\"mm\">⟨a₁, a₂⟩</span> and <span class=\"mm\">⟨a₂, a₁⟩</span> are different tuples, so <span class=\"mm\">{1, 4}</span> contributes two, not one. Dropping the <span class=\"mm\">a₁ ≠ a₂</span> condition would add the three diagonal tuples.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U1-Q49", "subject": "databases", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "er-relational-algebra", "subtopic": "tuple-and-domain-relational-calculus", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "For union-compatible  R(A, B)  and  S(A, B) , which tuple calculus expression is equivalent to  R − S ?", "options": ["{ t | t ∈ R ∧ ¬(t ∈ S) }", "{ t | ¬(t ∈ R) ∧ t ∈ S }", "{ t | t ∈ R ∨ t ∈ S }", "{ t | ¬(t ∈ R ∧ t ∈ S) }"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Translation is compositional: <span class=\"mm\">∧</span> for 'and', <span class=\"mm\">∨</span> for 'or', <span class=\"mm\">¬</span> for 'not', and a membership clause for each relation.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>'In <span class=\"mm\">R</span> and not in <span class=\"mm\">S</span>' translates to <span class=\"mm\">t ∈ R ∧ ¬(t ∈ S)</span> — (A).</li><li>(B) is <span class=\"mm\">S − R</span>, the other difference.</li><li>(C) is <span class=\"mm\">R ∪ S</span>.</li><li>(D) is the complement of the intersection — everything except the shared tuples.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Swapping the operands: <span class=\"mm\">t ∈ R ∧ ¬(t ∈ S)</span> and <span class=\"mm\">¬(t ∈ R) ∧ t ∈ S</span> look symmetric but are different sets. Order matters in difference.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U1-Q50", "subject": "databases", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "er-relational-algebra", "subtopic": "tuple-and-domain-relational-calculus", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Which one of the following tuple calculus expressions is  safe ?", "options": ["{ t | ¬(t ∈ R) }", "{ t | t ∈ R ∨ t[A] &gt; 5 }", "{ t | t ∈ S ∧ ¬(t ∈ R) }", "{ t | t[A] = 3 }"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Safety is a syntactic test: every free variable must be confined by a positive membership clause in a relation, so the result is drawn from the finite active domain.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) has a free <span class=\"mm\">t</span> under a negation with no relation bounding it — it admits every tuple not in <span class=\"mm\">R</span>, an infinite set. Unsafe.</li><li>(B) is a disjunction whose second branch, <span class=\"mm\">t[A] &gt; 5</span>, is unbounded — a <span class=\"mm\">∨</span> is safe only if both branches are. Unsafe.</li><li>(C) bounds <span class=\"mm\">t</span> by the positive clause <span class=\"mm\">t ∈ S</span>; the negation then only filters within that finite set. Safe.</li><li>(D) constrains one attribute and leaves the rest unconstrained — infinitely many tuples satisfy it. Unsafe.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming any formula with a negation is unsafe, or that a <span class=\"mm\">∨</span> is safe as long as one branch is bounded. A negated or disjunctive subformula is safe only when something positive in the formula still confines the variable.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> The practical rule: a variable is confined if some positive membership <span class=\"mm\">t ∈ R</span> appears in the same conjunction. Filtering inside a confined range is always safe.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U2-Q01", "subject": "databases", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "sql-querying", "subtopic": "ddl-dml-dcl", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Which of the following sets contains  only  Data Definition Language (DDL) statements?", "options": ["INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE, MERGE", "CREATE, ALTER, DROP, TRUNCATE", "GRANT, REVOKE, DENY", "SELECT, COMMIT, ROLLBACK"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> SQL statements are grouped by what they act on. <b>DDL</b> defines and changes the <i>schema</i> (<code class=\"mm\">CREATE</code>, <code class=\"mm\">ALTER</code>, <code class=\"mm\">DROP</code>, <code class=\"mm\">TRUNCATE</code>, <code class=\"mm\">RENAME</code>). <b>DML</b> changes the <i>data</i> inside the objects (<code class=\"mm\">SELECT</code>, <code class=\"mm\">INSERT</code>, <code class=\"mm\">UPDATE</code>, <code class=\"mm\">DELETE</code>, <code class=\"mm\">MERGE</code>). <b>DCL</b> controls privileges (<code class=\"mm\">GRANT</code>, <code class=\"mm\">REVOKE</code>). <b>TCL</b> controls transactions (<code class=\"mm\">COMMIT</code>, <code class=\"mm\">ROLLBACK</code>, <code class=\"mm\">SAVEPOINT</code>).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Check each word against the four groups: definition, manipulation, control, transaction.</li><li>Option (B) lists four schema-changing statements only — all DDL.</li><li>Option (A) is the DML family; (C) is DCL; (D) mixes a DML query with two transaction-control statements.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) — CREATE, ALTER, DROP, TRUNCATE are all DDL.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE and MERGE act on rows, so they are DML.</li><li>(C) GRANT and REVOKE hand out or take back privileges, so they are DCL (DENY is the same family).</li><li>(D) SELECT reads rows (DML/DQL), while COMMIT and ROLLBACK end or undo a transaction (TCL).</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> <code class=\"mm\">TRUNCATE</code> looks like a row operation, so it is often filed under DML. In the SQL standard it is a DDL statement: it drops and re-creates the table's storage rather than deleting rows one at a time, which is why it is far faster than an unqualified <code class=\"mm\">DELETE</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> DDL = definition, DML = data, DCL = privileges, TCL = transactions.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U2-Q02", "subject": "databases", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "sql-querying", "subtopic": "ddl-dml-dcl", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Table  enroll  holds 8 rows. Compare  DELETE FROM enroll;  with  DROP TABLE enroll; . Which statement is TRUE?", "options": ["DELETE removes the table definition but leaves the rows; DROP removes the rows.", "Both statements remove the rows and the table definition.", "DELETE removes every row but keeps the table definition; DROP removes the rows together with the table definition.", "DROP removes only the rows and leaves the definition; DELETE removes the definition."], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The row set and the schema object are separate things. <code class=\"mm\">DELETE</code> is DML: it removes rows from an existing table, and the table keeps its columns, constraints, indexes and privileges. <code class=\"mm\">DROP</code> is DDL: it removes the table object itself from the catalogue, so the rows go with it and any later reference to the name fails.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">DELETE FROM enroll;</code> with no <code class=\"mm\">WHERE</code> deletes all 8 rows; the table still exists and accepts new inserts.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">DROP TABLE enroll;</code> deletes the definition; the 8 rows disappear because the object that held them is gone.</li><li>A re-created table has no rows and no old constraints — nothing is restored.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) is wrong because <code class=\"mm\">DELETE</code> never touches the definition.</li><li>(A) and (D) invert the two statements.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Treating <code class=\"mm\">DROP</code> as \"delete the rows\". The distinction is testable: after <code class=\"mm\">DELETE</code>, <code class=\"mm\">SELECT * FROM enroll</code> returns an empty result set; after <code class=\"mm\">DROP</code>, the same query raises a \"no such table\" error.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> DELETE empties, DROP erases.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U2-Q03", "subject": "databases", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "sql-querying", "subtopic": "ddl-dml-dcl", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "In the schema below,  enroll.roll  is declared  REFERENCES student(roll) ON DELETE SET NULL . Student 4 has two rows in  enroll . After  DELETE FROM student WHERE roll = 4;  what happens to those two rows? student(roll, name, dept, cgpa)   -- roll is the primary key\ncourse(cid, title, credits)       -- cid is the primary key\nenroll(roll, cid, marks)          -- marks is NULL while a course is ungraded ", "options": ["Both enroll rows are deleted with the student.", "One row is deleted and the other is set to NULL.", "The delete is rejected, because two rows still reference student 4.", "Both enroll rows remain, with their roll value set to NULL."], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A foreign key with a referential action decides what happens to <i>referencing</i> rows when the <i>referenced</i> row is deleted. The four standard actions are <code class=\"mm\">CASCADE</code> (delete the children), <code class=\"mm\">SET NULL</code> (keep the children, null out the key), <code class=\"mm\">SET DEFAULT</code>, and <code class=\"mm\">NO ACTION</code>/<code class=\"mm\">RESTRICT</code> (reject the delete).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">ON DELETE SET NULL</code> keeps every referencing row and overwrites only the foreign-key column.</li><li>Both of student 4's enrollment rows are referencing rows, so both are kept.</li><li>Their <code class=\"mm\">roll</code> becomes NULL; the <code class=\"mm\">cid</code> and <code class=\"mm\">marks</code> values are untouched.</li><li>The student row itself is removed, so the two orphaned enrollments now have no owner.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) describes <code class=\"mm\">ON DELETE CASCADE</code>.</li><li>(C) describes <code class=\"mm\">NO ACTION</code> or <code class=\"mm\">RESTRICT</code>, the default when no action is written.</li><li>(B) is not an action any standard system performs.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Confusing \"the delete is refused\" with \"the children are removed\". A foreign key by itself does not prevent a delete; it only prevents leaving a <i>dangling</i> reference. <code class=\"mm\">SET NULL</code> avoids the dangling reference by erasing the value, so the delete is allowed and the child rows survive. Note the column must be nullable for this action to be legal.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Foreign key actions: CASCADE deletes children, SET NULL orphans them, RESTRICT refuses the parent delete.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U2-Q04", "subject": "databases", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "sql-querying", "subtopic": "ddl-dml-dcl", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": " student  holds 6 rows and  enroll  holds 8 rows, as below. Two DML statements are then executed, in this order: DELETE FROM enroll WHERE marks IS NULL;\nDELETE FROM student WHERE roll NOT IN (SELECT roll FROM enroll); How many rows remain in  student ? student: (1,Aarav,CSE,9.1)  (2,Bhavna,CSE,8.4)  (3,Chirag,ECE,7.8)\n         (4,Divya,CSE,8.9)  (5,Esha,MECH,6.9)  (6,Farhan,ECE,7.2)\ncourse:  (CS101,DBMS,4)  (CS102,OS,4)  (EC201,Signals,3)\nenroll:  (1,CS101,88)  (1,CS102,74)  (2,CS101,NULL)  (2,CS102,65)\n         (3,EC201,91)  (4,CS101,95)  (4,CS102,80)  (5,EC201,NULL) ", "options": [], "answer_display": "4", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> This is a two-step DML trace. The first statement is a filtered delete on the child table; the second deletes parent rows that no longer have any child. Because <code class=\"mm\">roll</code> is the primary key of <code class=\"mm\">student</code> it can never be NULL, so the <code class=\"mm\">NOT IN</code> subquery contains no NULL and behaves like an ordinary anti-join.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Step 1 removes the two ungraded enrollments: (2,CS101,NULL) and (5,EC201,NULL). Six enrollment rows remain.</li><li>The rolls still present in <code class=\"mm\">enroll</code> are 1, 2, 3 and 4 — note that roll 2 survives through its CS102 row, and roll 5 has now lost its only row.</li><li>Step 2 deletes student rows whose roll is not in {1, 2, 3, 4}: that removes rolls 5 and 6.</li><li>6 − 2 = 4 students remain: rolls 1, 2, 3 and 4.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 4 students remain (rolls 1, 2, 3, 4).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Deleting roll 5 in step 1 by mistake, because its enrollment was ungraded. The first statement deletes <i>enrollment</i> rows, not students; roll 5 is removed only in step 2, and only because it has no enrollment left. Also note that a NULL in the <code class=\"mm\">NOT IN</code> subquery would have made step 2 delete nothing at all — it is safe here only because <code class=\"mm\">roll</code> is a key.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Trace each statement on the table it names, then re-evaluate the next statement against the new state.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U2-Q05", "subject": "databases", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "sql-querying", "subtopic": "ddl-dml-dcl", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Which of the following statements about SQL constraints and privileges are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["A CHECK constraint rejects a row whose checked expression evaluates to UNKNOWN.", "GRANT SELECT ON student TO analyst WITH GRANT OPTION; lets analyst pass that same privilege on to other users.", "In standard SQL a UNIQUE constraint permits several rows that have NULL in the unique column.", "A column declared PRIMARY KEY may contain NULL provided the table also has a UNIQUE constraint on the same column."], "answer_display": "Options (B), (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Three separate rules are being tested: how <code class=\"mm\">CHECK</code> treats UNKNOWN, what <code class=\"mm\">WITH GRANT OPTION</code> adds, and how the standard treats NULL inside <code class=\"mm\">UNIQUE</code> and <code class=\"mm\">PRIMARY KEY</code>. A constraint is violated only when its predicate is <i>false</i>; UNKNOWN (from a NULL operand) satisfies it. NULL is never equal to NULL, so two NULLs cannot duplicate each other in a unique column. A primary key, by definition, may not be NULL.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) A <code class=\"mm\">CHECK (marks &gt;= 0)</code> on a NULL <code class=\"mm\">marks</code> evaluates to UNKNOWN, which is accepted — so the row is inserted. The statement is false.</li><li>(B) <code class=\"mm\">WITH GRANT OPTION</code> is exactly the right to re-grant. True.</li><li>(C) The unique constraint is violated only when two rows agree on every constrained column with non-NULL values; NULLs are distinct, so many NULLs may coexist. True (SQL Server is the well-known deviation, allowing only one).</li><li>(D) Entity integrity forbids NULL in any primary-key column, whatever other constraints exist. False.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (B) and (C) are true.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reading a <code class=\"mm\">CHECK</code> as \"must be true\". SQL uses three-valued logic, so a constraint fires only on FALSE; UNKNOWN passes. This is why <code class=\"mm\">CHECK (marks IN (0,1))</code> does not stop a NULL from being inserted, and why a \"not equal\" check such as <code class=\"mm\">CHECK (a &lt;&gt; b)</code> still admits rows where either side is NULL.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Constraints reject FALSE, not UNKNOWN; NULLs are distinct in UNIQUE; a primary key is never NULL.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U2-Q06", "subject": "databases", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "sql-querying", "subtopic": "nested-and-correlated-subqueries", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Using the schema below, which query returns exactly the students who have  not  enrolled in any course? student(roll, name, dept, cgpa)   -- roll is the primary key\ncourse(cid, title, credits)       -- cid is the primary key\nenroll(roll, cid, marks)          -- marks is NULL while a course is ungraded  student: (1,Aarav,CSE,9.1)  (2,Bhavna,CSE,8.4)  (3,Chirag,ECE,7.8)\n         (4,Divya,CSE,8.9)  (5,Esha,MECH,6.9)  (6,Farhan,ECE,7.2)\ncourse:  (CS101,DBMS,4)  (CS102,OS,4)  (EC201,Signals,3)\nenroll:  (1,CS101,88)  (1,CS102,74)  (2,CS101,NULL)  (2,CS102,65)\n         (3,EC201,91)  (4,CS101,95)  (4,CS102,80)  (5,EC201,NULL) ", "options": ["SELECT roll FROM student s WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM enroll e WHERE e.roll = s.roll);", "SELECT roll FROM student s WHERE EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM enroll e WHERE e.roll = s.roll);", "SELECT roll FROM student WHERE roll NOT IN (SELECT roll FROM enroll WHERE marks IS NOT NULL);", "SELECT roll FROM student WHERE roll &lt;&gt; ALL (SELECT roll FROM enroll WHERE marks IS NULL);"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> <code class=\"mm\">NOT EXISTS</code> is the standard \"for no matching row\" test. The inner query is correlated on <code class=\"mm\">s.roll</code>, so for each student the database asks whether any enrollment row exists; <code class=\"mm\">NOT EXISTS</code> keeps the student only when the answer is \"no row\". Unlike <code class=\"mm\">NOT IN</code> it is safe in the presence of NULLs, because it never compares values — it only tests whether a row was produced.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>For roll 1 the inner query returns two rows, so <code class=\"mm\">NOT EXISTS</code> is false and roll 1 is dropped.</li><li>For roll 6 the inner query returns no row at all, so <code class=\"mm\">NOT EXISTS</code> is true and roll 6 is kept.</li><li>Applying this to every student leaves exactly roll 6.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A) — only roll 6 (Farhan) has no enrollment.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) is the complement: it returns the five students who <i>are</i> enrolled.</li><li>(C) filters the subquery to graded rows, so the enrolled rolls it sees are {1, 2, 3, 4}. Roll 5 is absent from that set even though it <i>is</i> enrolled, so the query wrongly returns rolls 5 and 6.</li><li>(D) asks for rolls that differ from every <i>ungraded</i> enrollment roll ({2, 5}), which returns 1, 3, 4 and 6 — the opposite of the intended set.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming <code class=\"mm\">NOT IN</code> and <code class=\"mm\">NOT EXISTS</code> are interchangeable. They agree only when the inner column is declared NOT NULL. When a NULL can appear, <code class=\"mm\">NOT IN</code> silently returns the empty set while <code class=\"mm\">NOT EXISTS</code> still answers the intended question.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> For \"has no related row\", prefer NOT EXISTS: it is NULL-safe and reads as a universal test.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U2-Q07", "subject": "databases", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "sql-querying", "subtopic": "nested-and-correlated-subqueries", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "A subquery refers to a column of the table named in the enclosing query. Which statement about such a subquery is TRUE?", "options": ["It must appear in the FROM clause; a correlated reference is illegal in WHERE.", "It is an independent subquery, evaluated exactly once and then reused for every outer row.", "It is a correlated subquery, and conceptually it is evaluated again for each candidate row of the outer query.", "It cannot contain an aggregate function."], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A subquery that mentions an outer column is <b>correlated</b>. Its result depends on the current outer row, so the conceptual model is: for each candidate outer row, bind the outer columns, run the inner query, then test the predicate. An <b>uncorrelated</b> subquery mentions only its own tables, so it can be computed once and its result reused — which is why optimisers try to decorrelate.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Spot the outer reference: here <code class=\"mm\">s.roll</code> inside the inner query.</li><li>Because the inner result changes with each outer row, it is re-evaluated per row in the conceptual model.</li><li>That is the definition of a correlated subquery.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) describes an uncorrelated (independent) subquery.</li><li>(A) is false: correlation is legal in <code class=\"mm\">WHERE</code>, in the <code class=\"mm\">SELECT</code> list and in <code class=\"mm\">HAVING</code>.</li><li>(D) is false: a correlated aggregate such as <code class=\"mm\">(SELECT AVG(marks) FROM enroll e WHERE e.roll = s.roll)</code> is perfectly legal.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Believing a correlated subquery always costs one full scan per row. That is the naive plan; most engines rewrite it into a semi-join or an index lookup, so the practical cost can be close to a single join.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Outer reference present = correlated = per-row semantics; absent = uncorrelated = compute once.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U2-Q08", "subject": "databases", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "sql-querying", "subtopic": "nested-and-correlated-subqueries", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Suppose the  roll  column of  enroll  is nullable and the subquery below returns at least one NULL. SELECT roll FROM student\n WHERE roll NOT IN (SELECT roll FROM enroll); What does the outer query return?", "options": ["Every student roll that is absent from the non-NULL values of the subquery.", "No rows at all.", "Every student roll, including those present in the subquery.", "A syntax error, because NULL cannot appear in an IN list."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> <code class=\"mm\">x NOT IN (a, b, NULL)</code> expands to <code class=\"mm\">x &lt;&gt; a AND x &lt;&gt; b AND x &lt;&gt; NULL</code>. The last conjunct is UNKNOWN for every <code class=\"mm\">x</code>, and <code class=\"mm\">TRUE AND UNKNOWN = UNKNOWN</code>. A <code class=\"mm\">WHERE</code> clause keeps a row only when the predicate is TRUE, so no row qualifies — even a roll that matches nothing in the list.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Rewrite <code class=\"mm\">NOT IN</code> as a chain of <code class=\"mm\">&lt;&gt;</code> comparisons joined by <code class=\"mm\">AND</code>.</li><li>One comparison is against NULL, which yields UNKNOWN.</li><li>UNKNOWN <code class=\"mm\">AND</code> anything is never TRUE, so the whole predicate can never be TRUE.</li><li>The result set is therefore empty.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) — the query returns no rows.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) is what the query would return if the subquery contained no NULL; the NULL destroys it.</li><li>(C) is the opposite of what happens: UNKNOWN fails, it does not pass.</li><li>(D) is false: NULL is a legal value in a subquery result; the failure is semantic, not syntactic.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reading <code class=\"mm\">NOT IN</code> as set difference. Set difference ignores NULLs; <code class=\"mm\">NOT IN</code> propagates them. The fix is either to add <code class=\"mm\">WHERE roll IS NOT NULL</code> to the subquery, or to rewrite the query as <code class=\"mm\">NOT EXISTS</code>, which never compares values.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> NOT IN + a NULL in the subquery = empty result. Use NOT EXISTS or filter the NULL.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U2-Q09", "subject": "databases", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "sql-querying", "subtopic": "nested-and-correlated-subqueries", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Which of the following statements about SQL subqueries and quantified comparisons are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["x = ANY (S) returns the same truth value as x IN (S) for every subquery S whose result is not NULL-valued.", "x &gt; ALL (S) is TRUE when the subquery S returns no rows at all.", "A correlated subquery written in the SELECT list is conceptually evaluated once per row of the result.", "NOT IN and NOT EXISTS always return the same rows."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B), (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Quantified comparisons are shorthand for a fold: <code class=\"mm\">= ANY</code> is an OR over equality (that is <code class=\"mm\">IN</code>), and <code class=\"mm\">&gt; ALL</code> is an AND over the comparisons. An OR over an empty set is FALSE, so <code class=\"mm\">= ANY</code> on an empty subquery is false; an AND over an empty set is TRUE, so <code class=\"mm\">&gt; ALL</code> on an empty subquery is vacuously true.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) <code class=\"mm\">= ANY</code> ≡ <code class=\"mm\">IN</code>, by definition of the quantifier. True.</li><li>(B) <code class=\"mm\">x &gt; ALL (empty)</code> has no counterexample, so it holds vacuously. True — this is the \"every student scored more than the best student of an empty class\" case.</li><li>(C) a <code class=\"mm\">SELECT</code>-list subquery correlated on the outer row must be recomputed for each output row. True.</li><li>(D) false: they diverge as soon as the inner column can be NULL, where <code class=\"mm\">NOT IN</code> collapses to the empty set and <code class=\"mm\">NOT EXISTS</code> does not.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (C) are true; (D) is false.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming <code class=\"mm\">&gt; ALL</code> needs a non-empty subquery. The empty case is exactly where GATE-style questions aim: universal quantification over an empty domain is TRUE, existential quantification over it is FALSE.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> = ANY is IN, <> ALL is NOT IN, > ALL over an empty set is vacuously true.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U2-Q10", "subject": "databases", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "sql-querying", "subtopic": "nested-and-correlated-subqueries", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Using the schema and data below, how many students does the following query return? SELECT COUNT(*) FROM student s\n WHERE (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM enroll e\n         WHERE e.roll = s.roll\n           AND e.marks > (SELECT AVG(marks) FROM enroll e2 WHERE e2.cid = e.cid)) >= 2;  student(roll, name, dept, cgpa)   -- roll is the primary key\ncourse(cid, title, credits)       -- cid is the primary key\nenroll(roll, cid, marks)          -- marks is NULL while a course is ungraded  student: (1,Aarav,CSE,9.1)  (2,Bhavna,CSE,8.4)  (3,Chirag,ECE,7.8)\n         (4,Divya,CSE,8.9)  (5,Esha,MECH,6.9)  (6,Farhan,ECE,7.2)\ncourse:  (CS101,DBMS,4)  (CS102,OS,4)  (EC201,Signals,3)\nenroll:  (1,CS101,88)  (1,CS102,74)  (2,CS101,NULL)  (2,CS102,65)\n         (3,EC201,91)  (4,CS101,95)  (4,CS102,80)  (5,EC201,NULL) ", "options": [], "answer_display": "1", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The query is a doubly nested correlated count. For a given student, the middle query walks that student's enrollments; for each of them the innermost query recomputes the average marks of <i>that course</i>. A NULL <code class=\"mm\">marks</code> never satisfies <code class=\"mm\">&gt;</code>, so ungraded courses simply do not contribute.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Course averages over the non-NULL marks: CS101 = (88 + 95)/2 = 91.5; CS102 = (74 + 65 + 80)/3 = 73; EC201 = 91/1 = 91.</li><li>Roll 1: 88 &gt; 91.5 is false, 74 &gt; 73 is true → count 1.</li><li>Roll 2: CS101 is NULL (not counted), 65 &gt; 73 is false → count 0.</li><li>Roll 3: 91 &gt; 91 is false → count 0.</li><li>Roll 4: 95 &gt; 91.5 is true, 80 &gt; 73 is true → count 2.</li><li>Roll 5: its only mark is NULL → count 0. Roll 6 has no enrollment rows, so the inner count is 0.</li><li>Only roll 4 reaches the threshold of 2, so the outer <code class=\"mm\">COUNT(*)</code> is 1.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 1 student (roll 4, Divya).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Two traps sit here. First, <code class=\"mm\">AVG</code> ignores NULLs rather than treating them as 0, so CS101 averages 91.5 and not 61. Second, the threshold is \"at least 2 courses\", so roll 1 with a single qualifying course must be excluded — a count of 1 is not a pass.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Resolve innermost first: per-course average, then per-student comparison, then the outer count.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U2-Q11", "subject": "databases", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "sql-querying", "subtopic": "aggregate-functions-group-by-having", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Using the data below, what does the query return? SELECT COUNT(*), COUNT(marks) FROM enroll;  student: (1,Aarav,CSE,9.1)  (2,Bhavna,CSE,8.4)  (3,Chirag,ECE,7.8)\n         (4,Divya,CSE,8.9)  (5,Esha,MECH,6.9)  (6,Farhan,ECE,7.2)\ncourse:  (CS101,DBMS,4)  (CS102,OS,4)  (EC201,Signals,3)\nenroll:  (1,CS101,88)  (1,CS102,74)  (2,CS101,NULL)  (2,CS102,65)\n         (3,EC201,91)  (4,CS101,95)  (4,CS102,80)  (5,EC201,NULL) ", "options": ["8 and 2", "8 and 8", "6 and 6", "8 and 6"], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> <code class=\"mm\">COUNT(*)</code> counts <i>rows</i> and never looks at column values, so NULLs are included. <code class=\"mm\">COUNT(expr)</code> counts only the rows where <code class=\"mm\">expr</code> is not NULL. Every other aggregate (<code class=\"mm\">SUM</code>, <code class=\"mm\">AVG</code>, <code class=\"mm\">MIN</code>, <code class=\"mm\">MAX</code>) also ignores NULLs; only <code class=\"mm\">COUNT(*)</code> counts them.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">enroll</code> holds 8 rows, so <code class=\"mm\">COUNT(*)</code> = 8.</li><li>Two rows have <code class=\"mm\">marks</code> NULL — (2,CS101) and (5,EC201) — so <code class=\"mm\">COUNT(marks)</code> = 8 − 2 = 6.</li><li>The pair is therefore (8, 6).</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (D) — 8 and 6.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) would need every row to be graded.</li><li>(C) would be the answer if <code class=\"mm\">COUNT(*)</code> also skipped NULL rows.</li><li>(A) reports the number of NULL marks instead of the number of non-NULL ones.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming <code class=\"mm\">COUNT(marks)</code> and <code class=\"mm\">COUNT(*)</code> are the same. The gap between them is precisely the number of NULLs, which is why <code class=\"mm\">COUNT(*) - COUNT(col)</code> is a standard idiom for counting missing values.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> COUNT(*) counts rows; COUNT(col) counts non-NULL values of col.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U2-Q12", "subject": "databases", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "sql-querying", "subtopic": "aggregate-functions-group-by-having", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Which statement about  WHERE  and  HAVING  is TRUE?", "options": ["WHERE filters individual rows before grouping; HAVING filters whole groups after aggregation and may contain aggregate functions.", "HAVING filters individual rows before grouping; WHERE filters groups after aggregation.", "Both clauses may contain aggregate functions and are interchangeable.", "WHERE may contain aggregate functions but HAVING may not."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> SQL evaluates a grouped query in a fixed order: <code class=\"mm\">FROM</code> → <code class=\"mm\">WHERE</code> → <code class=\"mm\">GROUP BY</code> → <code class=\"mm\">HAVING</code> → <code class=\"mm\">SELECT</code> → <code class=\"mm\">ORDER BY</code>. Because <code class=\"mm\">WHERE</code> runs before the groups exist, it cannot refer to an aggregate; because <code class=\"mm\">HAVING</code> runs after grouping, it can.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">WHERE</code> sees one row at a time, so its predicates use plain columns.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">GROUP BY</code> then collapses rows into groups.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">HAVING</code> tests the group, so <code class=\"mm\">COUNT(*)</code>, <code class=\"mm\">SUM(marks)</code> and friends are legal there.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) reverses the order of the two clauses.</li><li>(C) is false: an aggregate in <code class=\"mm\">WHERE</code> is a syntax error in every major system.</li><li>(D) inverts the rule.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Trying to filter on a group with <code class=\"mm\">WHERE COUNT(*) &gt; 2</code>. The engine rejects it; the same condition belongs in <code class=\"mm\">HAVING</code>. A useful memory hook: <b>HAVING</b> is to groups what <b>WHERE</b> is to rows.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Row filter → WHERE; group filter → HAVING.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U2-Q13", "subject": "databases", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "sql-querying", "subtopic": "aggregate-functions-group-by-having", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Using the data below, what does the query return (averages shown to one decimal place)? SELECT dept, AVG(cgpa) FROM student\n GROUP BY dept\nHAVING COUNT(*) > 1;  student: (1,Aarav,CSE,9.1)  (2,Bhavna,CSE,8.4)  (3,Chirag,ECE,7.8)\n         (4,Divya,CSE,8.9)  (5,Esha,MECH,6.9)  (6,Farhan,ECE,7.2)\ncourse:  (CS101,DBMS,4)  (CS102,OS,4)  (EC201,Signals,3)\nenroll:  (1,CS101,88)  (1,CS102,74)  (2,CS101,NULL)  (2,CS102,65)\n         (3,EC201,91)  (4,CS101,95)  (4,CS102,80)  (5,EC201,NULL) ", "options": ["Three rows: CSE, ECE and MECH", "Two rows: CSE 8.8 and ECE 7.5", "One row: CSE 8.8", "Two rows: CSE 9.1 and ECE 7.8"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The query groups by department, computes each group's average, and then keeps only the groups whose <code class=\"mm\">COUNT(*)</code> exceeds 1. The <code class=\"mm\">HAVING</code> test is applied to the group, so a department with a single student disappears even though its average is perfectly well defined.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Group and count: CSE has 3 students, ECE has 2, MECH has 1.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">HAVING COUNT(*) &gt; 1</code> keeps CSE and ECE and drops MECH.</li><li>CSE average = (9.1 + 8.4 + 8.9)/3 = 26.4/3 = 8.8.</li><li>ECE average = (7.8 + 7.2)/2 = 15.0/2 = 7.5.</li><li>Two rows are returned.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) — CSE 8.8 and ECE 7.5.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) keeps MECH, whose group has only one row and therefore fails the <code class=\"mm\">HAVING</code> test.</li><li>(C) drops ECE, whose count is 2 and so satisfies <code class=\"mm\">&gt; 1</code>.</li><li>(D) reports the first value of each group instead of the group average.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Thinking <code class=\"mm\">HAVING</code> filters rows. It filters groups: a department with one student is removed as a group, not the student removed from the average.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> GROUP BY partitions, aggregates summarise, HAVING keeps or drops whole partitions.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U2-Q14", "subject": "databases", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "sql-querying", "subtopic": "aggregate-functions-group-by-having", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Using the data below, what single value does the following query return? SELECT AVG(marks) FROM enroll WHERE cid = 'CS101';  student: (1,Aarav,CSE,9.1)  (2,Bhavna,CSE,8.4)  (3,Chirag,ECE,7.8)\n         (4,Divya,CSE,8.9)  (5,Esha,MECH,6.9)  (6,Farhan,ECE,7.2)\ncourse:  (CS101,DBMS,4)  (CS102,OS,4)  (EC201,Signals,3)\nenroll:  (1,CS101,88)  (1,CS102,74)  (2,CS101,NULL)  (2,CS102,65)\n         (3,EC201,91)  (4,CS101,95)  (4,CS102,80)  (5,EC201,NULL) Give the exact value (one decimal place).", "options": [], "answer_display": "91.5", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> <code class=\"mm\">AVG(expr)</code> is defined as <code class=\"mm\">SUM(expr) / COUNT(expr)</code>. Both aggregates skip NULL, so the denominator is the number of <i>non-NULL</i> values, not the number of rows. That is the whole point of the question: the ungraded row is left out of both the numerator and the denominator.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">cid = 'CS101'</code> selects three rows: (1,CS101,88), (2,CS101,NULL) and (4,CS101,95).</li><li>The NULL row is excluded, so <code class=\"mm\">SUM(marks)</code> = 88 + 95 = 183 and <code class=\"mm\">COUNT(marks)</code> = 2.</li><li>AVG = 183 / 2 = 91.5.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 91.5</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Dividing by the row count, 183/3 = 61. That is the value you get if NULL is treated as 0 — SQL never does that for <code class=\"mm\">AVG</code>. If the intent really is \"treat ungraded as zero\", the question must say <code class=\"mm\">AVG(COALESCE(marks,0))</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> AVG ignores NULLs in both the sum and the count; the denominator is COUNT(col).</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U2-Q15", "subject": "databases", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "sql-querying", "subtopic": "aggregate-functions-group-by-having", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Which of the following statements about grouping and aggregate functions are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["SELECT dept, name FROM student GROUP BY dept; is invalid in standard SQL, because name is neither grouped nor aggregated.", "A WHERE clause may not contain an aggregate function, but a HAVING clause may.", "COUNT(*) ignores rows in which some column is NULL.", "An aggregate query with no GROUP BY returns exactly one row even when the table is empty."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B), (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> After <code class=\"mm\">GROUP BY</code>, the <code class=\"mm\">SELECT</code> list may contain only (i) grouped columns, (ii) aggregates and (iii) expressions built from those two. Anything else has no single value per group. Separately, an aggregate query with no <code class=\"mm\">GROUP BY</code> defines one implicit group covering the whole table — and a group over zero rows still exists, so one row is produced.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) <code class=\"mm\">name</code> varies inside a <code class=\"mm\">dept</code> group, so the standard rejects the query. True. (MySQL with <code class=\"mm\">ONLY_FULL_GROUP_BY</code> disabled returns an arbitrary name, which is a vendor extension, not the standard.)</li><li>(B) <code class=\"mm\">WHERE</code> is evaluated before grouping, so an aggregate there is meaningless; <code class=\"mm\">HAVING</code> is evaluated after grouping. True.</li><li>(C) <code class=\"mm\">COUNT(*)</code> counts rows regardless of NULLs anywhere in them. False.</li><li>(D) the implicit single group exists even for an empty table: <code class=\"mm\">COUNT(*)</code> = 0 and <code class=\"mm\">SUM</code>/<code class=\"mm\">AVG</code> return NULL, but one row is returned. True.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (D) are true; (C) is false.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Expecting an empty aggregate query to return no rows. It returns one row — the empty-group row — which is why <code class=\"mm\">SELECT MAX(marks) FROM enroll WHERE 1=0</code> yields a single NULL rather than nothing.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Grouped SELECT may only use grouped columns and aggregates; the no-GROUP-BY aggregate always emits one row.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U2-Q16", "subject": "databases", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "sql-querying", "subtopic": "joins-outer-joins", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Using the data below, how many rows does the following query return? SELECT * FROM student s LEFT JOIN enroll e ON s.roll = e.roll;  student: (1,Aarav,CSE,9.1)  (2,Bhavna,CSE,8.4)  (3,Chirag,ECE,7.8)\n         (4,Divya,CSE,8.9)  (5,Esha,MECH,6.9)  (6,Farhan,ECE,7.2)\ncourse:  (CS101,DBMS,4)  (CS102,OS,4)  (EC201,Signals,3)\nenroll:  (1,CS101,88)  (1,CS102,74)  (2,CS101,NULL)  (2,CS102,65)\n         (3,EC201,91)  (4,CS101,95)  (4,CS102,80)  (5,EC201,NULL) ", "options": ["6", "8", "9", "14"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A <b>left outer join</b> emits one row for every matching pair, plus one <i>NULL-extended</i> row for each left row that found no match. So the count is (number of matching pairs) + (number of unmatched left rows).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Matching pairs: roll 1 contributes 2, roll 2 contributes 2, roll 3 contributes 1, roll 4 contributes 2, roll 5 contributes 1 — 8 pairs in total, the whole of <code class=\"mm\">enroll</code>.</li><li>Unmatched left rows: roll 6 (Farhan) has no enrollment, so it contributes 1 row with <code class=\"mm\">cid</code> and <code class=\"mm\">marks</code> NULL.</li><li>8 + 1 = 9 rows.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C) — 9 rows.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) is the inner-join count: an inner join returns only the 8 matched pairs and silently drops roll 6.</li><li>(A) is the number of students, not the number of joined rows.</li><li>(D) would be the count if the join were many-to-many across a larger table; here the total is bounded by 8 + 6 = 14 only in the degenerate case where every enrollment failed to match.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming an outer join adds one row per left row. It adds one row only for <i>unmatched</i> left rows; matched rows are duplicated exactly as an inner join would duplicate them.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> LEFT JOIN row count = matched pairs + unmatched left rows.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U2-Q17", "subject": "databases", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "sql-querying", "subtopic": "joins-outer-joins", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Using the data below, how many rows does the following query return? SELECT * FROM student, course;  student: (1,Aarav,CSE,9.1)  (2,Bhavna,CSE,8.4)  (3,Chirag,ECE,7.8)\n         (4,Divya,CSE,8.9)  (5,Esha,MECH,6.9)  (6,Farhan,ECE,7.2)\ncourse:  (CS101,DBMS,4)  (CS102,OS,4)  (EC201,Signals,3)\nenroll:  (1,CS101,88)  (1,CS102,74)  (2,CS101,NULL)  (2,CS102,65)\n         (3,EC201,91)  (4,CS101,95)  (4,CS102,80)  (5,EC201,NULL) ", "options": ["18", "9", "6", "3"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A comma-separated <code class=\"mm\">FROM</code> list with no join condition is a <b>Cartesian product</b>: every row of the first table is paired with every row of the second. The size is the product of the two row counts.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">student</code> has 6 rows and <code class=\"mm\">course</code> has 3 rows.</li><li>No <code class=\"mm\">WHERE</code> clause removes any pairing.</li><li>6 × 3 = 18 rows.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A) — 18 rows.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) is 6 + 3, the sum of the row counts rather than the product.</li><li>(C) and (D) are the individual table sizes.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Confusing the Cartesian product with a natural join. The product has <code class=\"mm\">|R| × |S|</code> rows; a natural join on a common attribute collapses the pairs that agree, and here the two tables share no attribute name at all, so even <code class=\"mm\">NATURAL JOIN</code> would degenerate to the product.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> No join condition = cross product = product of the cardinalities.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U2-Q18", "subject": "databases", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "sql-querying", "subtopic": "joins-outer-joins", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Using the data below, what does the following query return? SELECT COUNT(*) FROM student s\n  LEFT JOIN enroll e ON s.roll = e.roll\n WHERE e.roll IS NULL;  student: (1,Aarav,CSE,9.1)  (2,Bhavna,CSE,8.4)  (3,Chirag,ECE,7.8)\n         (4,Divya,CSE,8.9)  (5,Esha,MECH,6.9)  (6,Farhan,ECE,7.2)\ncourse:  (CS101,DBMS,4)  (CS102,OS,4)  (EC201,Signals,3)\nenroll:  (1,CS101,88)  (1,CS102,74)  (2,CS101,NULL)  (2,CS102,65)\n         (3,EC201,91)  (4,CS101,95)  (4,CS102,80)  (5,EC201,NULL) ", "options": ["8", "0", "6", "1"], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> This is the standard <b>anti-join</b> idiom: left-join, then keep only the NULL-extended rows. After the outer join, <code class=\"mm\">e.roll</code> is NULL exactly for the left rows that found no match, so the <code class=\"mm\">WHERE</code> predicate isolates the students with no enrollment.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The left join produces 9 rows, of which 8 are matched and 1 is the NULL-extended row for roll 6.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">e.roll IS NULL</code> is true only on that single row.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">COUNT(*)</code> = 1.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (D) — 1 (only roll 6).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) would be the answer if every student had at least one enrollment.</li><li>(C) counts all students, ignoring the join.</li><li>(A) is the size of <code class=\"mm\">enroll</code>.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Writing <code class=\"mm\">WHERE e.roll IS NULL</code> inside an <i>inner</i> join and expecting the same answer. With an inner join the NULL-extended rows never exist, so the predicate can never be true and the result is always empty — the outer join is what makes the test meaningful.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Anti-join = LEFT JOIN … WHERE right.key IS NULL.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U2-Q19", "subject": "databases", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "sql-querying", "subtopic": "joins-outer-joins", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Which of the following statements about outer joins are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["The ON condition of a left outer join also filters out left rows that fail to match.", "A predicate on a right-table column placed in the WHERE clause of a left outer join can remove the NULL-extended rows and so reduce the result to that of an inner join.", "A FULL OUTER JOIN returns unmatched rows from both sides, padding the other side with NULLs.", "A LEFT JOIN B and B RIGHT JOIN A produce the same set of result rows (with the same column pairs) when written with the same join condition."], "answer_display": "Options (B), (C), (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> An outer join preserves one or both sides. <code class=\"mm\">LEFT</code> preserves the left input, <code class=\"mm\">RIGHT</code> preserves the right, <code class=\"mm\">FULL</code> preserves both. Swapping the operands therefore swaps which side is preserved, and the two results describe the same pairs. The <code class=\"mm\">ON</code> condition decides <i>matching</i>; the <code class=\"mm\">WHERE</code> clause is applied <i>after</i> the NULL extension, so a WHERE predicate can undo the preservation.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(D) swapping the operands of a left join turns it into the mirror-image right join with identical pairs. True.</li><li>(B) if the WHERE clause requires, say, <code class=\"mm\">e.marks &gt; 70</code>, the NULL-extended rows evaluate to UNKNOWN and are discarded, leaving exactly the inner-join rows. True — this is the classic \"outer join that is really an inner join\" bug.</li><li>(C) is the definition of a full outer join. True.</li><li>(A) false: a left row that fails the <code class=\"mm\">ON</code> condition is precisely the row that is kept, padded with NULLs.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (D), (B) and (C) are true; (A) is false.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Putting a right-table predicate in <code class=\"mm\">WHERE</code> and expecting NULL-padded rows to survive. They cannot: a NULL never satisfies a comparison. To filter the <i>matching</i> without losing the unmatched rows, the predicate must move into the <code class=\"mm\">ON</code> clause.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> ON decides matches, WHERE prunes the final rows — and that is how a left join silently becomes an inner join.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U2-Q20", "subject": "databases", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "sql-querying", "subtopic": "joins-outer-joins", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Using the schema and data below, what does the following query return? SELECT COUNT(*) FROM student s\n  JOIN enroll e ON e.roll = s.roll\n  JOIN course c ON c.cid = e.cid\n WHERE s.dept = 'CSE' AND c.credits = 4;  student(roll, name, dept, cgpa)   -- roll is the primary key\ncourse(cid, title, credits)       -- cid is the primary key\nenroll(roll, cid, marks)          -- marks is NULL while a course is ungraded  student: (1,Aarav,CSE,9.1)  (2,Bhavna,CSE,8.4)  (3,Chirag,ECE,7.8)\n         (4,Divya,CSE,8.9)  (5,Esha,MECH,6.9)  (6,Farhan,ECE,7.2)\ncourse:  (CS101,DBMS,4)  (CS102,OS,4)  (EC201,Signals,3)\nenroll:  (1,CS101,88)  (1,CS102,74)  (2,CS101,NULL)  (2,CS102,65)\n         (3,EC201,91)  (4,CS101,95)  (4,CS102,80)  (5,EC201,NULL) ", "options": [], "answer_display": "6", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A three-table equi-join is a chain of matches: the student must match an enrollment, and that enrollment must match a course. <code class=\"mm\">COUNT(*)</code> then counts <i>rows</i> of the joined result, so a student with two qualifying enrollments contributes two, not one.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>CSE students are rolls 1, 2 and 4.</li><li>Their enrollment rows: roll 1 has CS101 and CS102; roll 2 has CS101 and CS102; roll 4 has CS101 and CS102 — six rows in total.</li><li>All six reference CS101 or CS102, and both of those courses have <code class=\"mm\">credits = 4</code>, so the third join and the <code class=\"mm\">credits</code> predicate keep all six.</li><li>No EC201 enrollment belongs to a CSE student, so nothing is filtered further. The answer is 6.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 6</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering 3 (the number of CSE students) or 2 (the number of courses). The query counts joined rows, so the grain is (student, enrollment, course) — one row per enrollment.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> COUNT(*) after a join counts the joined rows; check the grain before you answer.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U2-Q21", "subject": "databases", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "sql-querying", "subtopic": "views-triggers-constraints", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Which statement about an SQL view is TRUE?", "options": ["A view always stores a private copy of the rows it returns, refreshed on every insert into the base table.", "A view is a named query: it stores no rows of its own (unless it is a materialised view) and its rows are recomputed from the base tables when the view is queried.", "A view must be defined over exactly one base table.", "Dropping a view deletes the rows of the underlying table."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> In standard SQL a view is a <i>derived</i> table: the catalogue stores only the defining query. Every reference to the view expands to that query, so the view always reflects the current base tables. A <b>materialised</b> view is the exception — it does keep a stored copy and must be refreshed — but that is an optimisation feature, not what a plain <code class=\"mm\">CREATE VIEW</code> gives you.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The definition is stored as text; no tuples are copied.</li><li>Querying the view runs (or rewrites) the defining query against the base tables.</li><li>Therefore the view is always consistent with the base data, with no refresh step.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) describes a materialised view, and even then the refresh is explicit or scheduled, not automatic on every insert.</li><li>(C) is false: a view may join any number of tables, as the aggregate view in this unit does.</li><li>(D) is false: <code class=\"mm\">DROP VIEW</code> removes only the definition; the base tables and their rows are untouched.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Believing a view is a snapshot. Because it is recomputed, a view over <code class=\"mm\">student</code> immediately shows a newly inserted student — there is nothing to refresh.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> A view is stored logic, not stored data.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U2-Q22", "subject": "databases", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "sql-querying", "subtopic": "views-triggers-constraints", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Which statement about an SQL trigger is TRUE?", "options": ["A trigger fires only for SELECT statements.", "A trigger must be called explicitly, for example with EXECUTE TRIGGER, after the statement it is meant to guard.", "A trigger fires automatically when its specified event (such as an INSERT, UPDATE or DELETE) occurs; it cannot be invoked directly by a statement.", "A table that has triggers may not also have constraints."], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A trigger is an <b>event–condition–action</b> rule attached to a table or view. The database fires it as a side effect of the triggering statement; there is no SQL statement that calls a trigger by name. Triggers are declared <code class=\"mm\">BEFORE</code> or <code class=\"mm\">AFTER</code> the event, and <code class=\"mm\">FOR EACH ROW</code> or <code class=\"mm\">FOR EACH STATEMENT</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The event is a DML change (in standard SQL), optionally restricted with <code class=\"mm\">WHEN</code> to specific columns.</li><li>The action is a block of SQL executed by the engine when the event occurs.</li><li>No user statement names the trigger, so it cannot be called directly.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) there is no such statement in standard SQL.</li><li>(A) triggers are defined on data-changing events; some systems add <code class=\"mm\">INSTEAD OF</code> triggers on views, but a plain <code class=\"mm\">SELECT</code> does not fire one.</li><li>(D) triggers and constraints are independent mechanisms and routinely coexist; constraints are usually preferred for declarative rules because they are cheaper.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Thinking a trigger replaces a foreign key. Triggers can enforce rules that constraints cannot express, but they are procedural, harder to reason about, and a well-modelled schema uses constraints first.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Triggers are fired by the engine, never called by the user.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U2-Q23", "subject": "databases", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "sql-querying", "subtopic": "views-triggers-constraints", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Using the schema below, which of the following views is updatable in the standard sense — that is,  INSERT ,  UPDATE  and  DELETE  through the view can be mapped onto the single base table it reads? student(roll, name, dept, cgpa)   -- roll is the primary key\ncourse(cid, title, credits)       -- cid is the primary key\nenroll(roll, cid, marks)          -- marks is NULL while a course is ungraded ", "options": ["CREATE VIEW v AS SELECT roll, name, dept FROM student WHERE dept = 'CSE';", "CREATE VIEW v AS SELECT dept, COUNT(*) AS n FROM student GROUP BY dept;", "CREATE VIEW v AS SELECT DISTINCT dept FROM student;", "CREATE VIEW v AS SELECT s.roll, AVG(e.marks) AS avgmarks FROM student s JOIN enroll e ON e.roll = s.roll GROUP BY s.roll;"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A view is updatable when each of its rows corresponds to exactly one row of exactly one base table. Selection and projection preserve that correspondence; aggregation, <code class=\"mm\">DISTINCT</code>, <code class=\"mm\">GROUP BY</code> and joins do not, because one view row can then be derived from many base rows (or from none).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) reads one table, keeps its key column <code class=\"mm\">roll</code> and only filters rows, so every view row maps back to one student row. Updatable.</li><li>(B) produces one row per department; no single student row corresponds to it. Not updatable.</li><li>(C) collapses duplicates, so a view row has no unique source row. Not updatable.</li><li>(D) aggregates over a join; again one row summarises many. Not updatable.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B), (C) and (D) are all <b>read-only</b> views under the standard updatability rules. A system may still let you modify them through an <code class=\"mm\">INSTEAD OF</code> trigger, but that is a workaround, not updatability.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming a view is read-only whenever it has a <code class=\"mm\">WHERE</code> clause. A selection predicate is harmless — in fact, with <code class=\"mm\">WITH CHECK OPTION</code> it becomes an enforced constraint on what may be written through the view.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> One view row ↔ one base row is what makes a view updatable; aggregation and DISTINCT break it.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U2-Q24", "subject": "databases", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "sql-querying", "subtopic": "views-triggers-constraints", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "The view below is created over the data shown. How many rows does  SELECT * FROM v WHERE n > 1;  return? CREATE VIEW v AS\n  SELECT dept, COUNT(*) AS n FROM student GROUP BY dept;  student: (1,Aarav,CSE,9.1)  (2,Bhavna,CSE,8.4)  (3,Chirag,ECE,7.8)\n         (4,Divya,CSE,8.9)  (5,Esha,MECH,6.9)  (6,Farhan,ECE,7.2)\ncourse:  (CS101,DBMS,4)  (CS102,OS,4)  (EC201,Signals,3)\nenroll:  (1,CS101,88)  (1,CS102,74)  (2,CS101,NULL)  (2,CS102,65)\n         (3,EC201,91)  (4,CS101,95)  (4,CS102,80)  (5,EC201,NULL) ", "options": [], "answer_display": "2", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The view materialises the grouping as an ordinary table of two columns, which can then be filtered like any other table. The <code class=\"mm\">WHERE</code> on the view is applied <i>after</i> the view's own <code class=\"mm\">GROUP BY</code>, so it filters groups, not students.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The view has three rows: CSE with n = 3, ECE with n = 2 and MECH with n = 1.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">n &gt; 1</code> keeps CSE and ECE.</li><li>The result has 2 rows.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 2 rows (CSE with 3, ECE with 2).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reading the query as \"count the students in departments with more than one student\", which would give 5. The outer query counts nothing — it returns the view's rows, so the answer is the number of qualifying groups.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> A view over an aggregate is a table of groups; filtering it filters groups.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U2-Q25", "subject": "databases", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "sql-querying", "subtopic": "views-triggers-constraints", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Which of the following statements about views, triggers and constraints are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["With WITH CHECK OPTION on a view, an insert or update performed through the view is rejected if the new row would not satisfy the view's defining condition.", "Dropping a base table that a view references is always rejected by the DBMS.", "A trigger declared FOR EACH ROW fires once for every row that the triggering statement affects.", "A CHECK constraint may be declared directly on a view in standard SQL."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Three different mechanisms are being separated. <code class=\"mm\">WITH CHECK OPTION</code> makes the view's predicate a write-time constraint. Triggers are declared at row granularity or statement granularity. <code class=\"mm\">CHECK</code> is a table constraint; the standard mechanism for a cross-table rule is an assertion, which no mainstream product implements.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) is the definition of <code class=\"mm\">WITH CHECK OPTION</code>: a row that would vanish from the view cannot be written through it. True.</li><li>(B) false: the standard permits the drop and leaves the view invalid (\"inoperative\") until it is re-created or the table returns; some systems require <code class=\"mm\">CASCADE</code>, others simply fail at the next use of the view. It is not universally rejected.</li><li>(C) <code class=\"mm\">FOR EACH ROW</code> means one firing per affected row; <code class=\"mm\">FOR EACH STATEMENT</code> fires once for the whole statement. True.</li><li>(D) false: <code class=\"mm\">CHECK</code> is declared on a table or column, not on a view.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A) and (C) are true; (B) and (D) are false.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Two traps. First, assuming <code class=\"mm\">WITH CHECK OPTION</code> only affects <code class=\"mm\">SELECT</code> — it affects writes. Second, assuming a trigger fires once per statement by default; the row granularity has to be read from the declaration, and <code class=\"mm\">FOR EACH ROW</code> is the more common form.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> CHECK OPTION guards writes through a view; FOR EACH ROW sets trigger granularity; CHECK belongs to tables.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U2-Q26", "subject": "databases", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "sql-querying", "subtopic": "ddl-dml-dcl", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Table  orders  is declared as  orders(oid INT PRIMARY KEY, qty INT NOT NULL DEFAULT 5, note VARCHAR(20)) . The statement  INSERT INTO orders (oid, note) VALUES (7, NULL);  is then executed. Which statement is TRUE?", "options": ["It is rejected, because qty is NOT NULL and no value was supplied for it.", "It is accepted: qty receives the declared default 5, and note is stored as NULL.", "It is accepted, and qty is stored as NULL because the column was left out of the column list.", "It is rejected, because note may not be given a NULL."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A column that is omitted from an <code class=\"mm\">INSERT</code> column list takes its declared <code class=\"mm\">DEFAULT</code>, and a declared default satisfies <code class=\"mm\">NOT NULL</code>. <code class=\"mm\">NOT NULL</code> forbids only an explicit NULL or an omitted column that has no default; it never forbids a default value.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The statement names two columns, <code class=\"mm\">oid</code> and <code class=\"mm\">note</code>; <code class=\"mm\">qty</code> is not named.</li><li>An unnamed column is filled from its <code class=\"mm\">DEFAULT</code> clause, here 5, so <code class=\"mm\">NOT NULL</code> is satisfied.</li><li>NULL is written explicitly into <code class=\"mm\">note</code>, which is nullable because no constraint forbids it.</li><li>The row (7, 5, NULL) is inserted.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) — the row is accepted with qty = 5 and note = NULL.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) confuses an omitted column with a missing value; the default supplies one.</li><li>(C) would hold only if <code class=\"mm\">qty</code> had no <code class=\"mm\">DEFAULT</code>, in which case the insert would be rejected rather than storing NULL.</li><li>(D) invents a constraint on <code class=\"mm\">note</code>; the declaration makes it nullable.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reading <code class=\"mm\">NOT NULL</code> as \"every insert must name the column\". It only says the stored value may not be NULL, and the default guarantees that.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Omitted column with a DEFAULT → default value, never NULL and never an error.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U2-Q27", "subject": "databases", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "sql-querying", "subtopic": "ddl-dml-dcl", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "With the schema and data below, the statement  UPDATE enroll SET marks = marks + 5 WHERE cid = 'C3';  is executed. Which statement is TRUE? student(roll, name, dept, cgpa)   -- roll is the primary key\ncourse(cid, title, credits)       -- cid is the primary key\nenroll(roll, cid, marks)          -- marks is NULL while a course is ungraded  student: (11,Ira,CSE,8.6)   (12,Kabir,ECE,7.4)  (13,Lata,CSE,9.2)\n         (14,Manoj,MECH,6.8) (15,Nisha,ECE,8.0)  (16,Omar,CSE,7.9)\ncourse:  (C1,DBMS,4)  (C2,OS,3)  (C3,Networks,4)\nenroll:  (11,C1,82)  (11,C2,91)  (12,C1,74)  (12,C3,68)  (13,C1,95)\n         (13,C2,88)  (15,C1,74)  (15,C2,60)  (16,C1,88)  (16,C3,NULL) ", "options": ["Both rows of course C3 change: 68 becomes 73 and the NULL becomes 5.", "The statement is rejected, because one row of C3 has a NULL mark.", "The graded row becomes 73, and the row whose mark is NULL stays NULL.", "The row whose mark is NULL is deleted, because NULL + 5 cannot be computed."], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Any arithmetic with NULL yields NULL, so <code class=\"mm\">NULL + 5</code> is NULL. An <code class=\"mm\">UPDATE</code> writes that NULL back, which is a legal value in a nullable column. The <code class=\"mm\">WHERE</code> clause tests only <code class=\"mm\">cid</code>, so both C3 rows are still selected.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Rows selected by <code class=\"mm\">WHERE cid = 'C3'</code>: (12,C3,68) and (16,C3,NULL).</li><li>(12,C3,68) → 68 + 5 = 73.</li><li>(16,C3,NULL) → NULL + 5 = NULL, so the column is set to NULL again.</li><li>Two rows are updated; one value changes, the other is unchanged.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C) — 73 and NULL.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) treats NULL as 0, which SQL never does for arithmetic.</li><li>(B) is wrong: a NULL operand is not an error, only an UNKNOWN value.</li><li>(D) is wrong: no statement deletes rows unless it is a <code class=\"mm\">DELETE</code>.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Treating NULL as zero. <code class=\"mm\">marks + 5</code> on a NULL mark is NULL, not 5 — a value is only turned into 0 by an explicit <code class=\"mm\">COALESCE(marks,0)</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> NULL propagates through arithmetic; the row is still updated, to NULL.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U2-Q28", "subject": "databases", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "sql-querying", "subtopic": "ddl-dml-dcl", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": " u1  holds  SELECT  on  student   WITH GRANT OPTION  and has granted  SELECT  on it to  u2 . The DBA now runs  REVOKE SELECT ON student FROM u1 RESTRICT; . Which statement is TRUE?", "options": ["The statement succeeds, and u2 keeps the privilege.", "The statement succeeds, and u2 loses the privilege as well.", "The statement fails, but u1 still loses the privilege.", "The statement fails, because u1 has passed the privilege on; REVOKE ... CASCADE would succeed and take it away from both u1 and u2."], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A privilege that has been granted onward creates a dependency. <code class=\"mm\">RESTRICT</code> refuses to revoke a privilege that others still hold through it; <code class=\"mm\">CASCADE</code> revokes the privilege from the grantee and, transitively, from everyone who received it from that grantee.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">u1</code> was granted <code class=\"mm\">SELECT</code> <code class=\"mm\">WITH GRANT OPTION</code>, so its grant to <code class=\"mm\">u2</code> is a dependent grant.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">RESTRICT</code> is the default: it fails rather than silently changing <code class=\"mm\">u2</code>'s rights.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">CASCADE</code> removes the privilege from <code class=\"mm\">u1</code> and follows the dependency to <code class=\"mm\">u2</code>.</li><li>Nothing is revoked when the <code class=\"mm\">RESTRICT</code> form fails, so <code class=\"mm\">u1</code> keeps the privilege.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (D) — RESTRICT fails; CASCADE removes it from both users.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) and (B) both assume <code class=\"mm\">RESTRICT</code> succeeds, which it does not when a dependent grant exists.</li><li>(C) invents a partial effect; a failed <code class=\"mm\">REVOKE</code> changes nothing at all.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Expecting <code class=\"mm\">RESTRICT</code> to revoke <code class=\"mm\">u1</code> and leave <code class=\"mm\">u2</code> dangling. The statement is atomic: it either succeeds completely or not at all.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> RESTRICT protects dependent grants by failing; CASCADE is the deliberate recursive revoke.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U2-Q29", "subject": "databases", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "sql-querying", "subtopic": "ddl-dml-dcl", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "A schema declares both foreign keys with  ON DELETE CASCADE : dept(dno)          -- primary key\nemp(eno, dno)      -- dno REFERENCES dept(dno) ON DELETE CASCADE\ndependent(did, eno) -- eno REFERENCES emp(eno) ON DELETE CASCADE and holds the data dept:      1, 2, 3\nemp:       (101,1)  (102,1)  (103,2)  (104,3)\ndependent: (1,101)  (2,101)  (3,103) The statement  DELETE FROM dept WHERE dno = 1;  is executed. How many rows are removed from  emp  and  dependent  together? (Count only those two tables.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "4", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A cascade chain is followed transitively. Deleting a <code class=\"mm\">dept</code> row cascades to its <code class=\"mm\">emp</code> rows, and deleting an <code class=\"mm\">emp</code> row in turn cascades to its <code class=\"mm\">dependent</code> rows.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Delete dept 1 → cascade to emp rows with dno = 1: 101 and 102. That is 2 rows of <code class=\"mm\">emp</code>.</li><li>Each deleted emp row cascades to its dependents: emp 101 has dependents 1 and 2; emp 102 has none. That is 2 rows of <code class=\"mm\">dependent</code>.</li><li>Dept 2 and dept 3 are untouched, so emp 103, 104 and dependent 3 survive.</li><li>2 + 2 = 4 rows removed.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 4</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Counting only the direct children (2 emp rows) and forgetting that the cascade continues to the grandchildren.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> CASCADE is transitive; follow the chain until no referencing row remains.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U2-Q30", "subject": "databases", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "sql-querying", "subtopic": "ddl-dml-dcl", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Which of the following statements about DML, DDL and privileges are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["If an UPDATE would violate a CHECK constraint for even one of the rows it touches, none of those rows is updated — the statement is atomic.", "An INSERT INTO t SELECT ... that fails on the last of five rows leaves the first four rows inserted.", "DELETE FROM t; with no WHERE removes every row but keeps the table definition and the privileges granted on it.", "DROP TABLE t; also removes the privileges that were granted on t."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (C), (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Two separate principles are at work. A single SQL statement is atomic: it either has its full effect or none. And privileges are attached to the object, so destroying the object destroys the privileges that depended on it.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) True. Constraint checking is part of the statement, so a violation anywhere rolls the whole statement back.</li><li>(B) False. Statement atomicity means the four earlier rows are undone with the fifth.</li><li>(C) True. <code class=\"mm\">DELETE</code> is DML: the rows go, the object and its grants stay.</li><li>(D) True. A grant is a privilege on a named object; once the object no longer exists the grant has nothing to attach to.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (C) and (D) are true; (B) is false.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) is the classic partial-effect trap: only an explicit loop of separate statements can leave earlier rows behind.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Believing that a multi-row DML statement commits row by row. It does not; the whole statement succeeds or the whole statement is undone.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> One statement = all or nothing; dropping the object drops the grants on it.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U2-Q31", "subject": "databases", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "sql-querying", "subtopic": "nested-and-correlated-subqueries", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "With the schema and data below, what does the following query produce for roll 14, the student with no row in  enroll ? student(roll, name, dept, cgpa)   -- roll is the primary key\ncourse(cid, title, credits)       -- cid is the primary key\nenroll(roll, cid, marks)          -- marks is NULL while a course is ungraded  student: (11,Ira,CSE,8.6)   (12,Kabir,ECE,7.4)  (13,Lata,CSE,9.2)\n         (14,Manoj,MECH,6.8) (15,Nisha,ECE,8.0)  (16,Omar,CSE,7.9)\ncourse:  (C1,DBMS,4)  (C2,OS,3)  (C3,Networks,4)\nenroll:  (11,C1,82)  (11,C2,91)  (12,C1,74)  (12,C3,68)  (13,C1,95)\n         (13,C2,88)  (15,C1,74)  (15,C2,60)  (16,C1,88)  (16,C3,NULL)  SELECT s.roll,\n       (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM enroll e WHERE e.roll = s.roll) AS n\nFROM student s; ", "options": ["Roll 14 is left out of the result entirely.", "Roll 14 appears with n set to NULL.", "Roll 14 appears with n = 0, because COUNT(*) over no rows is 0.", "The query is rejected, because a correlated subquery may not appear in the SELECT list."], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> An aggregate without <code class=\"mm\">GROUP BY</code> always returns exactly one row, even when it sees no input rows. <code class=\"mm\">COUNT(*)</code> over an empty set is 0, so the correlated subquery yields 0 rather than NULL or no row. A scalar subquery in the <code class=\"mm\">SELECT</code> list is legal as long as it returns at most one row.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>For roll 14 the inner query is <code class=\"mm\">SELECT COUNT(*) FROM enroll e WHERE e.roll = 14</code>, which matches nothing.</li><li>An aggregate query over zero rows still produces one row: the count of an empty set is 0.</li><li>The outer row for roll 14 therefore survives, with <code class=\"mm\">n</code> = 0.</li><li>Rolls 11, 13 and 16 get 2, 2 and 2; rolls 12 and 15 get 2 each as well — every enrolled student has exactly two rows.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C) — roll 14 appears with n = 0.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) describes a join or an <code class=\"mm\">EXISTS</code> filter, not a scalar subquery in the select list.</li><li>(B) would be right for <code class=\"mm\">SUM</code> or <code class=\"mm\">MAX</code>, which return NULL over no rows; <code class=\"mm\">COUNT</code> does not.</li><li>(D) is false: correlation in the <code class=\"mm\">SELECT</code> list is standard and is how per-row aggregates are written.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming every aggregate over an empty input returns NULL. Only <code class=\"mm\">COUNT</code> returns 0; <code class=\"mm\">SUM</code>, <code class=\"mm\">AVG</code>, <code class=\"mm\">MIN</code> and <code class=\"mm\">MAX</code> return NULL.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> COUNT over nothing is 0; every other aggregate over nothing is NULL.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U2-Q32", "subject": "databases", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "sql-querying", "subtopic": "nested-and-correlated-subqueries", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "On the data below, the subquery in SELECT s.roll FROM student s\nWHERE s.cgpa = (SELECT e.marks FROM enroll e WHERE e.cid = 'C1'); returns five rows. Which statement is TRUE?", "options": ["The statement fails at run time, because a scalar subquery used with = must return at most one row.", "The statement returns every student whose cgpa equals any of those five marks.", "The statement returns no rows, because a comparison against a multi-row subquery is always UNKNOWN.", "The statement is a syntax error, because a subquery may not stand on the right of = in a WHERE clause."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A subquery in a position that expects one value — the right operand of <code class=\"mm\">=</code>, or a <code class=\"mm\">SELECT</code> list item — is a scalar subquery and must produce at most one row. Producing more is a run-time cardinality error, not a silent set comparison.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The right side of <code class=\"mm\">=</code> expects a single value, so the subquery is scalar by position.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">WHERE e.cid = 'C1'</code> matches five rows, so the subquery returns five values.</li><li>A scalar subquery that returns more than one row raises an error when it is evaluated.</li><li>To compare against a set, the query must say so: <code class=\"mm\">IN</code> or <code class=\"mm\">= ANY</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A) — the statement fails at run time with a cardinality error.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) describes <code class=\"mm\">= ANY</code> (equivalently <code class=\"mm\">IN</code>), which is a different operator.</li><li>(C) confuses a cardinality error with three-valued logic; no comparison is even attempted.</li><li>(D) is wrong: the position is legal, only the number of rows is not.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Expecting <code class=\"mm\">=</code> followed by a subquery to behave like <code class=\"mm\">IN</code>. It does not; <code class=\"mm\">=</code> demands exactly one value, and an empty subquery would give NULL rather than an error.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Use IN or = ANY for a set; = is for one value and fails loudly on many.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U2-Q33", "subject": "databases", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "sql-querying", "subtopic": "nested-and-correlated-subqueries", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "With the schema and data below, which students does the following query return? student(roll, name, dept, cgpa)   -- roll is the primary key\ncourse(cid, title, credits)       -- cid is the primary key\nenroll(roll, cid, marks)          -- marks is NULL while a course is ungraded  student: (11,Ira,CSE,8.6)   (12,Kabir,ECE,7.4)  (13,Lata,CSE,9.2)\n         (14,Manoj,MECH,6.8) (15,Nisha,ECE,8.0)  (16,Omar,CSE,7.9)\ncourse:  (C1,DBMS,4)  (C2,OS,3)  (C3,Networks,4)\nenroll:  (11,C1,82)  (11,C2,91)  (12,C1,74)  (12,C3,68)  (13,C1,95)\n         (13,C2,88)  (15,C1,74)  (15,C2,60)  (16,C1,88)  (16,C3,NULL)  SELECT s.roll FROM student s\nWHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM enroll e\n                  WHERE e.roll = s.roll AND e.marks > 80); ", "options": ["Only roll 14, the student with no row in enroll at all.", "Rolls 12, 14 and 15 — every student with no enrollment above 80, including the student who has no enrollment at all.", "Rolls 12 and 15 only, because roll 14 has no enroll row for the inner query to test.", "Rolls 12, 15 and 16."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The extra condition belongs <code class=\"mm\">inside</code> the <code class=\"mm\">EXISTS</code> subquery, so the inner query asks \"does this student have an enrollment above 80?\" and <code class=\"mm\">NOT EXISTS</code> keeps the students for whom the answer is no. A student with no enrollment trivially has none above 80, so that student is kept too.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Enrollments above 80: 82 and 91 (roll 11), 95 and 88 (roll 13), 88 (roll 16).</li><li>Rolls with such an enrollment: 11, 13, 16 — these are dropped.</li><li>Roll 12 has 74 and 68; roll 15 has 74 and 60; roll 14 has no row at all. None of them has an enrollment above 80.</li><li>The result is rolls 12, 14 and 15.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) — rolls 12, 14 and 15.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) reads the query as \"has no enrollment\", which is what you get if the <code class=\"mm\">marks > 80</code> condition is dropped.</li><li>(C) wrongly assumes an empty inner result is not a valid answer; it is exactly what <code class=\"mm\">NOT EXISTS</code> is built to detect.</li><li>(D) keeps roll 16, whose 88 is above 80, so <code class=\"mm\">EXISTS</code> is true and the row is removed.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Moving <code class=\"mm\">marks > 80</code> out of the subquery into the outer <code class=\"mm\">WHERE</code>. That changes the question from \"no enrollment above 80\" to \"has no enrollment, and separately scored above 80\", and it also silently drops every student with no enrollment.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Conditions that define the thing being searched for go inside the EXISTS subquery.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U2-Q34", "subject": "databases", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "sql-querying", "subtopic": "nested-and-correlated-subqueries", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Which of the following statements about subqueries are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["In the conceptual model, a correlated subquery in the SELECT list is evaluated once for each row the outer query produces.", "NOT EXISTS is TRUE for an outer row whenever the inner query produces no rows, even when the inner column is nullable.", "IN and EXISTS always return the same rows, so one may be substituted for the other without care.", "A subquery in the FROM clause may refer to a column of another table in the same FROM clause."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Correlation means the inner query depends on the current outer row, so conceptually it is re-evaluated per row. <code class=\"mm\">EXISTS</code> tests only whether a row was produced, which makes it immune to NULLs — unlike <code class=\"mm\">IN</code>, whose comparison against a NULL yields UNKNOWN. Tables in one <code class=\"mm\">FROM</code> list are combined as a set, so one cannot see the other's columns without a lateral construct, which standard SQL does not have.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) True by the definition of correlation.</li><li>(B) True: <code class=\"mm\">EXISTS</code> never compares values, only counts rows, so a NULL in the inner column cannot break it.</li><li>(C) False: they diverge as soon as the inner column can be NULL (<code class=\"mm\">NOT IN</code> collapses to the empty set) and when duplicate rows matter.</li><li>(D) False: standard SQL has no <code class=\"mm\">LATERAL</code>; a derived table sees only its own tables and the enclosing query.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A) and (B) are true; (C) and (D) are false.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(C) fails on NULLs: <code class=\"mm\">x NOT IN (…, NULL)</code> is never TRUE, while <code class=\"mm\">NOT EXISTS</code> is unaffected.</li><li>(D) describes a lateral join, a vendor extension rather than standard SQL.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming <code class=\"mm\">IN</code> and <code class=\"mm\">EXISTS</code> are interchangeable. They agree only when the inner column is declared <code class=\"mm\">NOT NULL</code> and the outer query needs no duplicate elimination.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> EXISTS counts rows, IN compares values; only one of the two survives NULLs.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U2-Q35", "subject": "databases", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "sql-querying", "subtopic": "nested-and-correlated-subqueries", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "With the schema and data below, how many students does the following query return? student(roll, name, dept, cgpa)   -- roll is the primary key\ncourse(cid, title, credits)       -- cid is the primary key\nenroll(roll, cid, marks)          -- marks is NULL while a course is ungraded  student: (11,Ira,CSE,8.6)   (12,Kabir,ECE,7.4)  (13,Lata,CSE,9.2)\n         (14,Manoj,MECH,6.8) (15,Nisha,ECE,8.0)  (16,Omar,CSE,7.9)\ncourse:  (C1,DBMS,4)  (C2,OS,3)  (C3,Networks,4)\nenroll:  (11,C1,82)  (11,C2,91)  (12,C1,74)  (12,C3,68)  (13,C1,95)\n         (13,C2,88)  (15,C1,74)  (15,C2,60)  (16,C1,88)  (16,C3,NULL)  SELECT COUNT(*) FROM student s\nWHERE EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM enroll e\n              WHERE e.roll = s.roll\n                AND e.marks > (SELECT AVG(e2.marks) FROM enroll e2\n                               WHERE e2.cid = e.cid)); ", "options": [], "answer_display": "3", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> This is a doubly nested correlated query. For a candidate student the middle query walks that student's enrollments; for each of them the innermost query recomputes the average marks of <code class=\"mm\">that course</code> over its non-NULL marks. A NULL mark never satisfies <code class=\"mm\">&gt;</code>, so ungraded rows contribute nothing.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Course averages over non-NULL marks: C1 = (82+74+95+74+88)/5 = 413/5 = 82.6; C2 = (91+88+60)/3 = 239/3 ≈ 79.67; C3 = 68/1 = 68.</li><li>Roll 11: 82 &gt; 82.6 is false, 91 &gt; 79.67 is true → the student qualifies.</li><li>Roll 12: 74 &gt; 82.6 false, 68 &gt; 68 false → does not qualify.</li><li>Roll 13: 95 &gt; 82.6 true → qualifies.</li><li>Roll 14: no enrollment rows, so the middle query produces nothing → does not qualify.</li><li>Roll 15: 74 &gt; 82.6 false, 60 &gt; 79.67 false → does not qualify.</li><li>Roll 16: 88 &gt; 82.6 true, and the C3 NULL is never above 68 → qualifies.</li><li>Qualifying students: rolls 11, 13 and 16, so the count is 3.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 3</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Two traps. Computing one global average instead of a per-course average, and treating the NULL mark as 0 — which would make the C3 average 34 and let roll 12's 68 clear it.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Resolve the innermost aggregate per group first, then compare each row against its own group's value.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U2-Q36", "subject": "databases", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "sql-querying", "subtopic": "aggregate-functions-group-by-having", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "With the schema and data below, what does  SELECT COUNT(cid), COUNT(DISTINCT cid), COUNT(*) FROM enroll;  return? student(roll, name, dept, cgpa)   -- roll is the primary key\ncourse(cid, title, credits)       -- cid is the primary key\nenroll(roll, cid, marks)          -- marks is NULL while a course is ungraded  student: (11,Ira,CSE,8.6)   (12,Kabir,ECE,7.4)  (13,Lata,CSE,9.2)\n         (14,Manoj,MECH,6.8) (15,Nisha,ECE,8.0)  (16,Omar,CSE,7.9)\ncourse:  (C1,DBMS,4)  (C2,OS,3)  (C3,Networks,4)\nenroll:  (11,C1,82)  (11,C2,91)  (12,C1,74)  (12,C3,68)  (13,C1,95)\n         (13,C2,88)  (15,C1,74)  (15,C2,60)  (16,C1,88)  (16,C3,NULL) ", "options": ["10, 3, 10", "3, 3, 10", "10, 10, 10", "10, 3, 7"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> <code class=\"mm\">COUNT(col)</code> counts the rows where <code class=\"mm\">col</code> is not NULL, <code class=\"mm\">COUNT(DISTINCT col)</code> counts the distinct non-NULL values, and <code class=\"mm\">COUNT(*)</code> counts rows regardless of any value.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>All ten <code class=\"mm\">enroll</code> rows have a non-NULL <code class=\"mm\">cid</code>, so <code class=\"mm\">COUNT(cid)</code> = 10.</li><li>The distinct <code class=\"mm\">cid</code> values are C1, C2 and C3, so <code class=\"mm\">COUNT(DISTINCT cid)</code> = 3.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">COUNT(*)</code> counts rows, so it is 10.</li><li>The answer is (10, 3, 10).</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A) — 10, 3, 10.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) applies <code class=\"mm\">DISTINCT</code> to the first count as well.</li><li>(C) drops <code class=\"mm\">DISTINCT</code> from the second count.</li><li>(D) reports 7, which is the number of rows for the courses with at least two enrollments — irrelevant here.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming <code class=\"mm\">COUNT(DISTINCT cid)</code> equals <code class=\"mm\">COUNT(cid)</code> because there are only three courses. Distinctness and row count are different questions.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> COUNT(col) counts values, COUNT(DISTINCT col) counts distinct values, COUNT(*) counts rows.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U2-Q37", "subject": "databases", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "sql-querying", "subtopic": "aggregate-functions-group-by-having", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "With the data below, what happens to  SELECT COUNT(*) FROM enroll HAVING COUNT(*) > 5; ? student: (11,Ira,CSE,8.6)   (12,Kabir,ECE,7.4)  (13,Lata,CSE,9.2)\n         (14,Manoj,MECH,6.8) (15,Nisha,ECE,8.0)  (16,Omar,CSE,7.9)\ncourse:  (C1,DBMS,4)  (C2,OS,3)  (C3,Networks,4)\nenroll:  (11,C1,82)  (11,C2,91)  (12,C1,74)  (12,C3,68)  (13,C1,95)\n         (13,C2,88)  (15,C1,74)  (15,C2,60)  (16,C1,88)  (16,C3,NULL) ", "options": ["It is rejected, because HAVING requires a GROUP BY clause.", "It is legal: the whole table is one implicit group, so it returns the single value 10.", "It is legal, and it returns all ten rows.", "It is legal, and it returns one row per course that has more than five enrollments."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A query with aggregates and no <code class=\"mm\">GROUP BY</code> has exactly one group — the whole table. <code class=\"mm\">HAVING</code> is a filter on groups, so it is legal without <code class=\"mm\">GROUP BY</code> and is applied to that single group. The result is one row when the condition holds and zero rows when it does not.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>No <code class=\"mm\">GROUP BY</code> → one group containing all ten rows.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">COUNT(*)</code> for that group is 10.</li><li>10 &gt; 5 is true, so the group survives and one row is produced.</li><li>The result is the single value 10 — not ten rows, because the row count is fixed by the number of groups.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) — one row containing 10.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) is a common belief but not the standard: <code class=\"mm\">HAVING</code> without <code class=\"mm\">GROUP BY</code> is well defined.</li><li>(C) confuses filtering groups with filtering rows; <code class=\"mm\">WHERE</code> filters rows.</li><li>(D) would need a <code class=\"mm\">GROUP BY cid</code>.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Thinking <code class=\"mm\">HAVING</code> is only meaningful with <code class=\"mm\">GROUP BY</code>. It filters whatever groups exist, and without <code class=\"mm\">GROUP BY</code> exactly one group exists.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> No GROUP BY + aggregate = one implicit group; HAVING tests that one group.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U2-Q38", "subject": "databases", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "sql-querying", "subtopic": "aggregate-functions-group-by-having", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Table  t(g, v)  holds  (A,1), (A,2), (NULL,3), (NULL,4), (B,5) . What does  SELECT g, COUNT(*) FROM t GROUP BY g;  return?", "options": ["4 rows, because each NULL is its own group.", "2 rows, because groups whose key is NULL are discarded.", "3 rows: A with 2, B with 1, and one NULL group with 2.", "3 rows: A with 2, B with 1, and one NULL group with 1."], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> <code class=\"mm\">GROUP BY</code> treats NULLs as equal to each other for the purpose of forming groups. All rows with a NULL grouping key fall into a single group, which is then reported with a NULL key. This is a special case of grouping, not of <code class=\"mm\">=</code>, under which NULL = NULL is UNKNOWN.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Partition by <code class=\"mm\">g</code>: {A,A}, {NULL,NULL}, {B}.</li><li>Counts: A → 2, NULL → 2, B → 1.</li><li>Three groups, hence three rows; the NULL group is reported, not discarded.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C) — 3 rows, the NULL group holding 2.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) applies the <code class=\"mm\">=</code> semantics of NULL to grouping, which does not use it.</li><li>(B) confuses <code class=\"mm\">GROUP BY</code> with an equality predicate that could fail.</li><li>(D) counts only one of the two NULL rows.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Believing NULL groups vanish, or that each NULL forms its own group. Grouping collapses all NULL keys into one group.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> All NULL keys form one group; COUNT(*) then reports every row in it.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U2-Q39", "subject": "databases", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "sql-querying", "subtopic": "aggregate-functions-group-by-having", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "With the schema and data below, what single value does the following query return? student(roll, name, dept, cgpa)   -- roll is the primary key\ncourse(cid, title, credits)       -- cid is the primary key\nenroll(roll, cid, marks)          -- marks is NULL while a course is ungraded  student: (11,Ira,CSE,8.6)   (12,Kabir,ECE,7.4)  (13,Lata,CSE,9.2)\n         (14,Manoj,MECH,6.8) (15,Nisha,ECE,8.0)  (16,Omar,CSE,7.9)\ncourse:  (C1,DBMS,4)  (C2,OS,3)  (C3,Networks,4)\nenroll:  (11,C1,82)  (11,C2,91)  (12,C1,74)  (12,C3,68)  (13,C1,95)\n         (13,C2,88)  (15,C1,74)  (15,C2,60)  (16,C1,88)  (16,C3,NULL)  SELECT COUNT(*) FROM (\n  SELECT dept FROM student\n  GROUP BY dept\n  HAVING AVG(cgpa) > 8.0\n) x; ", "options": [], "answer_display": "1", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The derived table groups students by department and keeps only the departments whose average <code class=\"mm\">cgpa</code> exceeds 8.0. The outer <code class=\"mm\">COUNT(*)</code> then counts the surviving groups — one row per department.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>CSE: (8.6 + 9.2 + 7.9)/3 = 25.7/3 ≈ 8.567, which is above 8.0 → kept.</li><li>ECE: (7.4 + 8.0)/2 = 7.7, which is not above 8.0 → dropped.</li><li>MECH: 6.8/1 = 6.8, not above 8.0 → dropped.</li><li>One group survives, so <code class=\"mm\">COUNT(*)</code> = 1.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 1</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Counting students rather than departments, or rounding CSE's 8.567 down to 8.0 and then applying a strict <code class=\"mm\">&gt;</code>. The comparison is on the exact average, and 25.7/3 is strictly greater than 8.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> HAVING filters groups, so the outer count is a count of groups.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U2-Q40", "subject": "databases", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "sql-querying", "subtopic": "aggregate-functions-group-by-having", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Which of the following statements about grouping and aggregate functions are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["SELECT dept, COUNT(*) FROM student; is rejected in standard SQL, because dept is neither grouped nor aggregated.", "SELECT cid, COUNT(*) FROM enroll GROUP BY cid HAVING marks > 80; is legal, because HAVING may reference any column of the table.", "AVG(marks) and SUM(marks) / COUNT(*) agree when no mark is NULL, and differ when some mark is NULL.", "MIN and MAX ignore NULLs, so a group in which every mark is NULL yields NULL for MAX(marks)."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (C), (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> After <code class=\"mm\">GROUP BY</code>, each output row stands for a whole group, so the select list may contain only grouped columns, aggregates and expressions built from those two. <code class=\"mm\">HAVING</code> is itself a condition on the group and follows the same rule. Every aggregate except <code class=\"mm\">COUNT(*)</code> ignores NULL, so an all-NULL group aggregates to NULL.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) True: <code class=\"mm\">dept</code> varies inside the single implicit group, so it has no single value per group.</li><li>(B) False: <code class=\"mm\">marks</code> is neither grouped nor aggregated, so it has no value per group either — the engine rejects it.</li><li>(C) True: without NULLs the two expressions are identical; with k NULLs out of n rows, <code class=\"mm\">AVG</code> divides by n − k while <code class=\"mm\">SUM/COUNT(*)</code> divides by n, so they differ.</li><li>(D) True: <code class=\"mm\">MAX</code> over an empty set of non-NULL values is NULL.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (C) and (D) are true; (B) is false.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) is the trap: HAVING may use aggregates and grouped columns, not arbitrary columns.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Thinking HAVING relaxes the grouping rule because it runs after grouping. It filters groups; it does not let an ungrouped column through.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Grouped SELECT and HAVING may only use grouped columns and aggregates.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U2-Q41", "subject": "databases", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "sql-querying", "subtopic": "joins-outer-joins", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "With the schema and data below, what does  SELECT * FROM student NATURAL JOIN enroll;  return? student(roll, name, dept, cgpa)   -- roll is the primary key\ncourse(cid, title, credits)       -- cid is the primary key\nenroll(roll, cid, marks)          -- marks is NULL while a course is ungraded  student: (11,Ira,CSE,8.6)   (12,Kabir,ECE,7.4)  (13,Lata,CSE,9.2)\n         (14,Manoj,MECH,6.8) (15,Nisha,ECE,8.0)  (16,Omar,CSE,7.9)\ncourse:  (C1,DBMS,4)  (C2,OS,3)  (C3,Networks,4)\nenroll:  (11,C1,82)  (11,C2,91)  (12,C1,74)  (12,C3,68)  (13,C1,95)\n         (13,C2,88)  (15,C1,74)  (15,C2,60)  (16,C1,88)  (16,C3,NULL) ", "options": ["Six columns and 60 rows, because the two roll columns are both kept.", "One roll column (not two) and 10 rows, because roll is the only column name the two tables share.", "One roll column and 6 rows, one per student.", "It is rejected, because a natural join needs a declared foreign key."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A natural join is an equi-join on every column name the two tables share, with the duplicate columns merged into one. The row count is the number of matching pairs, exactly as for any inner join.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Shared column name: <code class=\"mm\">roll</code> only. So the join condition is <code class=\"mm\">s.roll = e.roll</code>.</li><li>The merged result has 4 + 3 − 1 = 6 columns.</li><li>Every one of the 10 <code class=\"mm\">enroll</code> rows has a matching student, so there are 10 matching pairs.</li><li>Roll 14 has no enrollment, so it does not appear — this is an inner join.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) — one roll column and 10 rows.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) describes a cross join that keeps both copies of <code class=\"mm\">roll</code>.</li><li>(C) counts students rather than matching pairs, and would silently drop the multi-enrollment students.</li><li>(D) is false: a natural join is driven by column names, not by declared keys.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Forgetting that NATURAL JOIN merges the shared column, and that it is an inner join, so the unenrolled student disappears.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> NATURAL JOIN = equi-join on shared names + one merged copy of each shared column.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U2-Q42", "subject": "databases", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "sql-querying", "subtopic": "joins-outer-joins", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Which statement about  ON  and  WHERE  in an inner join is TRUE?", "options": ["ON carries only equality conditions; an inequality belongs in WHERE.", "ON is evaluated after WHERE.", "They give different results whenever a joined column contains a NULL.", "For an inner join, moving a predicate from ON to WHERE gives the same result."], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> For an inner join the two clauses are interchangeable, because both simply discard rows that fail the predicate. The distinction only becomes visible with an outer join, where <code class=\"mm\">ON</code> decides which pairs match but <code class=\"mm\">WHERE</code> runs after the NULL-extension and can remove the preserved rows.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>An inner join keeps exactly the pairs for which the <code class=\"mm\">ON</code> predicate is TRUE.</li><li>A <code class=\"mm\">WHERE</code> predicate is also applied to those same pairs, before the result is produced.</li><li>Conjunction is commutative, so applying either predicate first leaves the same set of pairs.</li><li>Hence the two placements are equivalent for an inner join — and only for an inner join.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) is false: <code class=\"mm\">ON</code> accepts any boolean expression, including inequalities and conjunctions.</li><li>(B) reverses the order; <code class=\"mm\">ON</code> determines the pairs, <code class=\"mm\">WHERE</code> filters afterwards.</li><li>(C) is false for inner joins; NULL behaviour is the same under either placement.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Generalising the outer-join rule to inner joins. The placement matters only when there are NULL-extended rows to lose.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Inner join: ON and WHERE are equivalent. Outer join: ON matches, WHERE prunes.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U2-Q43", "subject": "databases", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "sql-querying", "subtopic": "joins-outer-joins", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "With the data below, what does the following query return? student: (11,Ira,CSE,8.6)   (12,Kabir,ECE,7.4)  (13,Lata,CSE,9.2)\n         (14,Manoj,MECH,6.8) (15,Nisha,ECE,8.0)  (16,Omar,CSE,7.9)\ncourse:  (C1,DBMS,4)  (C2,OS,3)  (C3,Networks,4)\nenroll:  (11,C1,82)  (11,C2,91)  (12,C1,74)  (12,C3,68)  (13,C1,95)\n         (13,C2,88)  (15,C1,74)  (15,C2,60)  (16,C1,88)  (16,C3,NULL)  SELECT COUNT(*) FROM student a JOIN student b\n  ON a.dept = b.dept AND a.roll  ", "options": ["4", "6", "9", "15"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> This is a self-join that pairs students within a department. The strict inequality <code class=\"mm\">a.roll &lt; b.roll</code> keeps each unordered pair once, so the count is the sum over departments of <code class=\"mm\">C(k,2)</code> for a department of k students.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>CSE: rolls 11, 13, 16 → C(3,2) = 3 pairs.</li><li>ECE: rolls 12, 15 → C(2,2) = 1 pair.</li><li>MECH: roll 14 alone → 0 pairs.</li><li>3 + 1 + 0 = 4.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A) — 4.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) 6 counts the CSE pairs twice, i.e. it uses <code class=\"mm\">a.roll &lt;&gt; b.roll</code> instead of a strict order.</li><li>(C) 9 is 3 × 3, the CSE rows of the unrestricted product.</li><li>(D) 15 is C(6,2), the number of pairs ignoring the department.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Using <code class=\"mm\">&lt;&gt;</code> instead of <code class=\"mm\">&lt;</code>, which returns each unordered pair twice.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Self-join for unordered pairs: use a strict inequality so each pair appears once.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U2-Q44", "subject": "databases", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "sql-querying", "subtopic": "joins-outer-joins", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Which of the following statements about joins are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["NATURAL JOIN joins on every column name the two tables share and keeps one copy of each shared column.", "CROSS JOIN and INNER JOIN ... ON TRUE produce the same row multiset.", "A LEFT JOIN whose ON condition matches nothing still returns every row of the left table, NULL-extended.", "A FULL OUTER JOIN never returns more rows than the sum of the two tables' row counts."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B), (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A join with no effective condition is a Cartesian product, whether it is written <code class=\"mm\">CROSS JOIN</code> or <code class=\"mm\">INNER JOIN ... ON TRUE</code>. An outer join guarantees the preserved side is represented: an unmatched preserved row still appears, padded with NULLs. The number of rows can therefore far exceed the sum of the inputs.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) True — that is the definition of a natural join.</li><li>(B) True: a condition that is always TRUE restricts nothing, so the inner join degenerates to the product.</li><li>(C) True — that is exactly what the preserved side means.</li><li>(D) False: if every left row matches every right row the result has m × n rows, which exceeds m + n as soon as both sides have at least two rows.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (C) are true; (D) is false.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(D) fails on a complete many-to-many match: 3 × 3 = 9 rows from tables of 3 and 3.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming an outer join bounds the output by the input sizes. Only the unmatched rows are added; matched pairs are duplicated exactly as an inner join would duplicate them.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> No condition = product; outer join guarantees the preserved side, and nothing else.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U2-Q45", "subject": "databases", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "sql-querying", "subtopic": "joins-outer-joins", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "With the schema and data below, how many rows does the following query return? student(roll, name, dept, cgpa)   -- roll is the primary key\ncourse(cid, title, credits)       -- cid is the primary key\nenroll(roll, cid, marks)          -- marks is NULL while a course is ungraded  student: (11,Ira,CSE,8.6)   (12,Kabir,ECE,7.4)  (13,Lata,CSE,9.2)\n         (14,Manoj,MECH,6.8) (15,Nisha,ECE,8.0)  (16,Omar,CSE,7.9)\ncourse:  (C1,DBMS,4)  (C2,OS,3)  (C3,Networks,4)\nenroll:  (11,C1,82)  (11,C2,91)  (12,C1,74)  (12,C3,68)  (13,C1,95)\n         (13,C2,88)  (15,C1,74)  (15,C2,60)  (16,C1,88)  (16,C3,NULL)  SELECT COUNT(*) FROM student s\nLEFT JOIN enroll e ON s.roll = e.roll AND e.marks > 80; ", "options": [], "answer_display": "8", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> In a <code class=\"mm\">LEFT JOIN</code> the <code class=\"mm\">ON</code> condition decides which pairs match; the left table is preserved regardless. The row count is (matching pairs) + (left rows with no match). Placing the right-table predicate in <code class=\"mm\">ON</code> is what keeps the unmatched students in the result.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Enrollments above 80: (11,C1,82), (11,C2,91), (13,C1,95), (13,C2,88), (16,C1,88) — 5 matching pairs.</li><li>Students with at least one such enrollment: 11, 13, 16. They contribute their 5 pairs and nothing extra.</li><li>Students 12, 14 and 15 have no enrollment above 80, so each contributes one NULL-extended row: 3 rows.</li><li>5 + 3 = 8 rows.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 8</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Two traps. Putting <code class=\"mm\">e.marks &gt; 80</code> in <code class=\"mm\">WHERE</code> instead of <code class=\"mm\">ON</code>, which deletes the three NULL-extended rows and leaves 5; or counting students (6) instead of joined rows.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> LEFT JOIN row count = matching pairs + unmatched left rows, with the right-side filter kept in ON.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U2-Q46", "subject": "databases", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "sql-querying", "subtopic": "views-triggers-constraints", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": " CREATE VIEW high AS SELECT roll, cid, marks FROM enroll WHERE marks > 80 WITH CHECK OPTION;  is created, and then  INSERT INTO high VALUES (17, 'C1', 50);  is executed. What happens?", "options": ["The row is inserted into enroll and is simply invisible through high.", "The row is inserted into enroll with marks raised to 81 by the check option.", "The insert is rejected, because the new row would not satisfy the view's WHERE predicate.", "The insert is rejected, because high is not updatable."], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> <code class=\"mm\">WITH CHECK OPTION</code> turns the view's predicate into a condition on writes made through the view: a row that would fall outside the view is refused rather than inserted invisibly. Without the clause the insert would be accepted and the row would simply not appear through the view.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The view is updatable: it is a selection and projection of a single base table.</li><li>The check option requires every inserted or updated row to satisfy <code class=\"mm\">marks &gt; 80</code>.</li><li>50 does not satisfy it, so the insert fails with an error.</li><li>Nothing is written to <code class=\"mm\">enroll</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C) — the insert is rejected.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) is what happens <code class=\"mm\">without</code> the check option.</li><li>(B) invents a coercion; SQL never adjusts data to make a constraint pass.</li><li>(D) is false: this view is updatable, which is why the check option is meaningful.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Confusing <code class=\"mm\">WITH CHECK OPTION</code> with <code class=\"mm\">WITH READ ONLY</code>. The first permits the write if the predicate holds and rejects it otherwise; the second forbids writes altogether.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> WITH CHECK OPTION makes the view's predicate a write-time constraint.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U2-Q47", "subject": "databases", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "sql-querying", "subtopic": "views-triggers-constraints", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Which statement about an  INSTEAD OF  trigger on a view is TRUE?", "options": ["It fires after the row has been written to the base table.", "It runs in place of the triggering statement, which is how a non-updatable view is made writable.", "It may be declared only for DELETE on a view.", "It can be attached to a base table as well as to a view."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> An <code class=\"mm\">INSTEAD OF</code> trigger replaces the triggering DML statement on the view. Because the view has no storage of its own, the trigger body performs the real writes on the base tables — which is exactly what makes an otherwise non-updatable view writable. It is the one trigger form that is defined on views rather than on tables.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The statement targets the view, and the view cannot store rows.</li><li>The trigger intercepts the statement and supplies its own actions on the base tables.</li><li>No automatic write happens, which is why the trigger body must perform it.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) describes a normal <code class=\"mm\">AFTER</code> trigger, which assumes the write already happened.</li><li>(C) is false: <code class=\"mm\">INSERT</code>, <code class=\"mm\">UPDATE</code> and <code class=\"mm\">DELETE</code> are all allowed.</li><li>(D) is false: <code class=\"mm\">INSTEAD OF</code> is defined on views; base tables use <code class=\"mm\">BEFORE</code> and <code class=\"mm\">AFTER</code>.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming an INSTEAD OF trigger also performs the original write. It replaces it, so the base-table write must be written by hand.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> INSTEAD OF = on views, replaces the statement, body does the real work.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U2-Q48", "subject": "databases", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "sql-querying", "subtopic": "views-triggers-constraints", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "In standard SQL, a  CHECK  constraint whose condition contains a subquery over another table …", "options": ["is allowed, and is re-evaluated whenever the other table changes.", "is allowed, but evaluated only on INSERT, never on UPDATE.", "is not allowed: a CHECK condition may reference only columns of the row being tested.", "is allowed, but silently ignored."], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A <code class=\"mm\">CHECK</code> constraint is a row-level predicate: it is tested against each candidate row and may mention only columns of that row. A subquery would make the constraint depend on other rows, which the standard excludes; cross-row conditions belong in a foreign key, an assertion or a trigger.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The constraint is evaluated per row, at insert and update time, with no wider context.</li><li>A subquery would need the rest of the database, including rows being changed concurrently.</li><li>The standard therefore forbids subqueries in <code class=\"mm\">CHECK</code>; most engines reject the DDL outright.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C) — not permitted.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) would require re-checking every row of the table whenever the other table changes — not part of the model.</li><li>(B) invents a partial enforcement.</li><li>(D) is false: a rejected statement is not a silent ignore.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Believing a CHECK can compare against another table because the syntax accepts a general condition. The permitted scope is the current row only.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> CHECK sees one row; anything cross-row needs a trigger, a key or an assertion.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U2-Q49", "subject": "databases", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "sql-querying", "subtopic": "views-triggers-constraints", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": " CREATE VIEW cs AS SELECT roll, cgpa FROM student WHERE dept = 'CSE';  is created on the schema and data below, and then  UPDATE cs SET cgpa = cgpa + 0.5;  is executed. How many rows of  student  change? student(roll, name, dept, cgpa)   -- roll is the primary key\ncourse(cid, title, credits)       -- cid is the primary key\nenroll(roll, cid, marks)          -- marks is NULL while a course is ungraded  student: (11,Ira,CSE,8.6)   (12,Kabir,ECE,7.4)  (13,Lata,CSE,9.2)\n         (14,Manoj,MECH,6.8) (15,Nisha,ECE,8.0)  (16,Omar,CSE,7.9)\ncourse:  (C1,DBMS,4)  (C2,OS,3)  (C3,Networks,4)\nenroll:  (11,C1,82)  (11,C2,91)  (12,C1,74)  (12,C3,68)  (13,C1,95)\n         (13,C2,88)  (15,C1,74)  (15,C2,60)  (16,C1,88)  (16,C3,NULL) ", "options": [], "answer_display": "3", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> This view is updatable: it selects a subset of rows and a subset of columns of one base table, so each view row maps to exactly one base row. An <code class=\"mm\">UPDATE</code> through the view therefore reaches the underlying rows, and the <code class=\"mm\">WHERE</code> in the view definition limits it to the CSE students.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The view exposes only rows with <code class=\"mm\">dept = 'CSE'</code>: rolls 11, 13 and 16.</li><li>Selection and projection of a single table preserve updatability, so the update is propagated.</li><li>The statement names no rows, so every row in the view is updated.</li><li>3 rows of <code class=\"mm\">student</code> change.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 3</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Adding up all six students, or assuming a view is never updatable. Only the CSE rows are visible through the view, so only they can be reached.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Updatable view = one view row ↔ one base row; an update through it hits exactly the rows the view exposes.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U2-Q50", "subject": "databases", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "sql-querying", "subtopic": "views-triggers-constraints", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Which of the following statements about views, triggers and constraints are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["A view whose definition uses GROUP BY is not updatable in standard SQL.", "WITH CHECK OPTION turns the view's predicate into a condition on every write made through the view.", "An INSTEAD OF trigger fires only after the row has been written to the base table.", "A view may rename base columns, so the same base column can appear under different names in different views."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B), (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Updatability requires a one-to-one map from view rows to base rows. Grouping destroys that map because one view row summarises many base rows, so such a view is read-only. <code class=\"mm\">WITH CHECK OPTION</code> adds a write-time predicate. A view is a named query, so it may rename and reorder columns freely — the name in the view is what users see.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) True: a grouped row is an aggregate over many base rows, so an update has no single target row.</li><li>(B) True — that is its definition.</li><li>(C) False: <code class=\"mm\">INSTEAD OF</code> replaces the statement; nothing has been written when it runs.</li><li>(D) True: <code class=\"mm\">SELECT roll AS id, cgpa AS score FROM student</code> is a legal view definition.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (D) are true; (C) is false.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(C) inverts the firing semantics: an INSTEAD OF trigger runs in place of the write, so no base-table row exists yet.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming any view can be written through. Grouping, DISTINCT and unions all break the one-to-one mapping and make the view read-only.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Grouped view = read-only; WITH CHECK OPTION = write-time predicate; views may rename columns.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U3-Q01", "subject": "databases", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "normalization", "subtopic": "functional-dependencies-armstrong-axioms", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Relation R(A, B, C) is subject to no constraint other than its schema. Which one of the following functional dependencies is trivial?", "options": ["A → B", "AB → C", "A → AB", "ABC → A"], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A functional dependency <code class=\"mm\">X &rarr; Y</code> is <i>trivial</i> when <code class=\"mm\">Y &sube; X</code>: the right-hand side is already part of the left-hand side, so the dependency holds in <i>every</i> relation with that schema, whatever the data. Triviality is a syntactic test on the two attribute sets, not a claim about what the data happens to satisfy.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; B</code>: <code class=\"mm\">B &sube; A</code> is false (B is a different attribute), so this dependency constrains the data and is non-trivial.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">AB &rarr; C</code>: <code class=\"mm\">C &sube; AB</code> is false, so it is non-trivial.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; AB</code>: the right-hand side contains B, which is not in <code class=\"mm\">A</code>, so it is non-trivial. It happens to be true whenever <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; B</code> holds, but being implied is not the same as being trivial.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">ABC &rarr; A</code>: <code class=\"mm\">A &sube; ABC</code> is true, so every tuple satisfies it automatically. This one is trivial.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (D) &mdash; <code class=\"mm\">ABC &rarr; A</code>.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; B</code> forbids two tuples that agree on A and differ on B &mdash; a real constraint.</li><li>(B) <code class=\"mm\">AB &rarr; C</code> is the ordinary kind of dependency a schema is built on.</li><li>(C) <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; AB</code> is valid only when <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; B</code> is, so it is not automatic.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Calling <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; AB</code> trivial because it looks obviously true. It is true only under an extra assumption; a trivial dependency needs no assumption at all. The other frequent slip is testing the left-hand side against the right-hand side instead of the other way round.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Ask one question only: is every attribute on the right already on the left? If yes, the dependency is trivial and tells you nothing about the data.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U3-Q02", "subject": "databases", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "normalization", "subtopic": "1nf-2nf-3nf-bcnf", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Which one of the following is the exact condition for a relation to be in first normal form (1NF)?", "options": ["Every attribute of every tuple holds a single atomic value from the attribute's domain.", "No non-prime attribute is partially dependent on a candidate key.", "No non-prime attribute is transitively dependent on a candidate key.", "Every determinant of a non-trivial functional dependency is a superkey."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> 1NF is the weakest of the normal forms and the only one that talks about the <i>shape</i> of a value rather than about dependencies. It requires the relational model's basic assumption: every cell holds one atomic value from the column's domain, so there are no repeating groups, no sets, and no nested relations.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>1NF = atomic values only. A cell such as <code class=\"mm\">{98111, 98122}</code> in a <code class=\"mm\">phone</code> column breaks it; the fix is one row per phone number.</li><li>Option (B) is the definition of 2NF, which presupposes 1NF and adds a constraint on partial dependencies.</li><li>Option (C) is the definition of 3NF, one step further up.</li><li>Option (D) is the definition of BCNF.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) is 2NF, which is a statement about non-prime attributes and keys, not about atomic values.</li><li>(C) is 3NF.</li><li>(D) is BCNF.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Treating 1NF as a trivial form that \"every table satisfies anyway\". A table with a comma-separated list, a repeating group, or an array column is not in 1NF, and every higher form is then undefined for it.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Learn the ladder in one line each: 1NF atomic values, 2NF no partial dependency, 3NF no transitive dependency, BCNF every determinant is a superkey.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U3-Q03", "subject": "databases", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "normalization", "subtopic": "closure-of-attribute-sets-keys", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Relation R(A, B, C, D) satisfies F = {A -> B, B -> C, C -> D}. Which one of the following sets is the only candidate key of R?", "options": ["{A, B}", "{A, C}", "{A}", "{A, B, C}"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A candidate key is a <i>minimal</i> attribute set whose closure is the whole relation. Compute the closure by repeatedly applying the dependencies, then check minimality: no proper subset may close to <code class=\"mm\">R</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">A&#8314; = {A}</code>. Apply <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; B</code> to get B; then <code class=\"mm\">B &rarr; C</code> gives C; then <code class=\"mm\">C &rarr; D</code> gives D.</li><li>So <code class=\"mm\">A&#8314; = {A, B, C, D}</code>: the whole relation is determined by A alone.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">{A}</code> is therefore a superkey, and it has no non-empty proper subset, so it is minimal.</li><li>Every set containing A, such as <code class=\"mm\">{A, B}</code> or <code class=\"mm\">{A, B, C}</code>, is a superkey but not minimal. No set without A closes: B, C and D each determine only attributes further down the chain.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C) &mdash; <code class=\"mm\">{A}</code>.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) and (D) contain A, so they are superkeys; they are not minimal, and a candidate key must be minimal.</li><li>(B) contains A as well, and C adds nothing: <code class=\"mm\">A</code> alone already reaches every attribute.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering <code class=\"mm\">{A, B}</code> because the schema \"looks like\" it needs two attributes, or missing the chain because only one dependency is applied per pass. Keep applying the rules until nothing new appears.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> In a chain <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; B &rarr; C &rarr; D</code> the head of the chain is the only key. Chains always collapse to their first attribute.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U3-Q04", "subject": "databases", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "normalization", "subtopic": "multivalued-dependencies-4nf", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Relation R(X, Y, Z) satisfies the multivalued dependency X ->> Y. Which one of the following best states what this means?", "options": ["Each value of X is associated with exactly one value of Y.", "For each value of X, the set of Y values that occurs with it does not depend on which Z values occur with it.", "Within each group of equal X values, Y functionally determines Z.", "X, Y and Z must together form the primary key."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A multivalued dependency <code class=\"mm\">X &rarr;&rarr; Y</code> in <code class=\"mm\">R(X, Y, Z)</code> says that Y and Z are <i>independent</i> given X. Formally, whenever <code class=\"mm\">(x, y&#8321;, z&#8321;)</code> and <code class=\"mm\">(x, y&#8322;, z&#8322;)</code> are both in the relation, so are <code class=\"mm\">(x, y&#8321;, z&#8322;)</code> and <code class=\"mm\">(x, y&#8322;, z&#8321;)</code>. The Y values repeat for every Z value and vice versa.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Take one X value, say a student. The set of courses that student takes and the set of hobbies that student has are two independent facts about that student.</li><li>If the two facts are stored in one relation, every course must be paired with every hobby, or the table would wrongly suggest that a particular course goes with a particular hobby.</li><li>That forced cross-product is exactly the independence statement: given X, the Y set is the same whichever Z you look at.</li><li>So the meaning is (B); nothing here says a single Y per X, which would be the functional dependency <code class=\"mm\">X &rarr; Y</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) describes a functional dependency, a strictly stronger statement.</li><li>(C) invents a dependency inside the group that the MVD does not assert.</li><li>(D) confuses the MVD with a key constraint; keys are about uniqueness, not about independence.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reading <code class=\"mm\">&rarr;&rarr;</code> as \"many values, therefore many-to-many\". An MVD is about two <i>independent</i> multi-valued facts sharing one determinant, which is why it needs a decomposition into two relations and not a junction table of pairs.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> FD = one value per determinant. MVD = a set of values per determinant, independent of the remaining attributes.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U3-Q05", "subject": "databases", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "normalization", "subtopic": "lossless-join-dependency-preservation", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Relation R(A, B, C) is decomposed into R1(A, B) and R2(B, C). The decomposition is lossless if and only if", "options": ["A → B or C → B holds in R.", "A → C or C → A holds in R.", "B → A or B → C holds in R.", "no dependency need hold: a binary decomposition is always lossless."], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> For a decomposition into two relations, the lossless-join test is: the common attributes must functionally determine all of at least one part, i.e. the common set must be a superkey of <code class=\"mm\">R&#8321;</code> or of <code class=\"mm\">R&#8322;</code>. Here the common attributes are <code class=\"mm\">{B}</code>, so the condition is <code class=\"mm\">B &rarr; A</code> (B is a key of <code class=\"mm\">R&#8321;</code>) or <code class=\"mm\">B &rarr; C</code> (B is a key of <code class=\"mm\">R&#8322;</code>).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Compute the intersection: <code class=\"mm\">R&#8321; &cap; R&#8322; = {B}</code>.</li><li>Ask whether the intersection determines the whole of <code class=\"mm\">R&#8321;</code>, that is <code class=\"mm\">B &rarr; A</code>.</li><li>Ask whether the intersection determines the whole of <code class=\"mm\">R&#8322;</code>, that is <code class=\"mm\">B &rarr; C</code>.</li><li>If either holds, every tuple of the join is a tuple of the original relation, so the join loses nothing. If neither holds, the join can manufacture spurious tuples.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) puts the dependencies on the wrong side: A and C are not the common attributes, so they cannot reconstruct a tuple across the join.</li><li>(B) names attributes that do not even appear together in one part.</li><li>(D) is false: <code class=\"mm\">R&#8321;(A, B)</code> and <code class=\"mm\">R&#8322;(B, C)</code> with no dependencies at all can join into extra tuples.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Testing the common attribute in the wrong direction, for example accepting <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; B</code>. That dependency is about A, but A is in only one part; the join is stitched together on B.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Two-way rule: intersect the two schemas, then ask whether that intersection is a key of either side. One side is enough.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U3-Q06", "subject": "databases", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "normalization", "subtopic": "functional-dependencies-armstrong-axioms", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Let F = {A -> B, B -> C, C -> D} be the set of dependencies of R(A, B, C, D). Which one of the following dependencies is NOT in F+?", "options": ["D → A", "A → C", "B → D", "A → D"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> <code class=\"mm\">F&#8314;</code> is the closure of F: every dependency implied by F. A dependency <code class=\"mm\">X &rarr; Y</code> is implied exactly when <code class=\"mm\">Y &sube; X&#8314;</code>, so the whole question reduces to computing attribute closures.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">D&#8314; = {D}</code>: no dependency has D on its left-hand side, so nothing can be derived. <code class=\"mm\">D &rarr; A</code> is therefore not implied.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">A&#8314; = {A, B, C, D}</code>: <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; B</code>, then <code class=\"mm\">B &rarr; C</code>, then <code class=\"mm\">C &rarr; D</code>. So <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; C</code> and <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; D</code> are both implied.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">B&#8314; = {B, C, D}</code>: <code class=\"mm\">B &rarr; C</code> then <code class=\"mm\">C &rarr; D</code>. So <code class=\"mm\">B &rarr; D</code> is implied.</li><li>Only the first candidate fails, so (A) is the answer.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A) &mdash; <code class=\"mm\">D &rarr; A</code> is not implied.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) follows by transitivity through B.</li><li>(C) follows by transitivity through C.</li><li>(D) is the transitive chain of all three dependencies.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming dependencies run backwards as well as forwards. Transitivity is one-directional: <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; D</code> says nothing about <code class=\"mm\">D &rarr; A</code>. Reversing an arrow is the single most common error in closure questions.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> To test any dependency <code class=\"mm\">X &rarr; Y</code>, compute <code class=\"mm\">X&#8314;</code> and look for Y inside it. Never reason from the right-hand side.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U3-Q07", "subject": "databases", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "normalization", "subtopic": "1nf-2nf-3nf-bcnf", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Relation R(A, B, C, D) has the single candidate key {A, B} and satisfies A -> C. Which one of the following correctly explains why R is not in 2NF?", "options": ["C is a prime attribute, and prime attributes may not be functionally determined by anything.", "The dependency A → C is transitive, and 2NF forbids transitive dependencies.", "C is not determined by {A, B} at all, so the key cannot identify a tuple.", "C is a non-prime attribute that is determined by A alone, a proper subset of the candidate key {A, B}."], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> 2NF forbids <i>partial</i> dependencies: a non-prime attribute must not depend on a proper subset of a candidate key. Here the key is <code class=\"mm\">{A, B}</code>, so both A and B are prime and both C and D are non-prime.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Prime attributes: A and B, because they belong to the candidate key <code class=\"mm\">{A, B}</code>.</li><li>Non-prime attributes: C and D.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; C</code> has a left-hand side <code class=\"mm\">{A}</code> that is a proper subset of the key, and its right-hand side C is non-prime.</li><li>That is precisely a partial dependency, so the relation violates 2NF. The fix is to split off <code class=\"mm\">R&#8321;(A, C)</code> and keep <code class=\"mm\">R&#8322;(A, B, D)</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) is false: a prime attribute may perfectly well be determined by something, including by a proper subset of another key.</li><li>(B) confuses 2NF with 3NF; transitivity is the 3NF concern, and it is measured against non-prime attributes determining other non-prime attributes.</li><li>(C) is false: C <i>is</i> determined by <code class=\"mm\">{A, B}</code>, since <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; C</code> implies <code class=\"mm\">AB &rarr; C</code> by augmentation.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Checking whether \"a part of the key determines something\" without checking that the something is non-prime. If the right-hand side is prime, the dependency is not a 2NF violation at all.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Two conditions must both hold for a 2NF violation: the left-hand side is a proper subset of a candidate key, and the right-hand side is non-prime.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U3-Q08", "subject": "databases", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "normalization", "subtopic": "closure-of-attribute-sets-keys", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Relation R(A, B, C, D) satisfies F = {AB -> C, C -> D, D -> A}. How many candidate keys does R have?", "options": [], "answer_display": "3", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> An attribute that never appears on the right-hand side of any dependency can never be derived, so it must be present in <i>every</i> candidate key. Here only B has that property, which narrows the search to sets containing B.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Right-hand sides are C, D and A, so B never appears there: B is in every key.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">B&#8314; = {B}</code>, so B alone is not a key; test the pairs that contain B.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">(AB)&#8314;</code>: <code class=\"mm\">AB &rarr; C</code>, then <code class=\"mm\">C &rarr; D</code>, then <code class=\"mm\">D &rarr; A</code> gives <code class=\"mm\">{A, B, C, D}</code>. <code class=\"mm\">AB</code> is a candidate key.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">(BC)&#8314;</code>: <code class=\"mm\">C &rarr; D</code>, <code class=\"mm\">D &rarr; A</code>, and then <code class=\"mm\">AB &rarr; C</code> is already satisfied &mdash; the closure is <code class=\"mm\">{A, B, C, D}</code>. <code class=\"mm\">BC</code> is a candidate key.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">(BD)&#8314;</code>: <code class=\"mm\">D &rarr; A</code> gives A, and now <code class=\"mm\">AB &rarr; C</code> gives C, so the closure is <code class=\"mm\">{A, B, C, D}</code>. <code class=\"mm\">BD</code> is a candidate key.</li><li>No larger set is minimal, and the three keys found are not supersets of one another. The count is 3.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 3 &mdash; the candidate keys are <code class=\"mm\">AB</code>, <code class=\"mm\">BC</code> and <code class=\"mm\">BD</code>.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Stopping at the first key found, or missing <code class=\"mm\">BD</code> because it needs a two-step derivation: <code class=\"mm\">D &rarr; A</code> first, and only then <code class=\"mm\">AB &rarr; C</code>. Closure computation must be run to exhaustion.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Find the attributes that appear on no right-hand side &mdash; they are in every key &mdash; then grow each of them with one more attribute at a time until the closure is the full relation.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U3-Q09", "subject": "databases", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "normalization", "subtopic": "multivalued-dependencies-4nf", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Which of the following statements about multivalued dependencies are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["Every functional dependency X → Y is also a multivalued dependency X →→ Y.", "Every multivalued dependency X →→ Y is also a functional dependency X → Y.", "X →→ Y is trivial exactly when Y ⊆ X or X ∪ Y = R.", "A relation is in 4NF exactly when, for every non-trivial multivalued dependency X →→ Y that holds in it, X is a superkey."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (C) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Functional dependencies are a special case of multivalued dependencies, so the MVD class is strictly larger. Triviality and the 4NF condition are the two definitions the form is built on.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) If every X value pairs with exactly one Y value, then certainly the Y values do not vary with Z. So <code class=\"mm\">X &rarr; Y</code> implies <code class=\"mm\">X &rarr;&rarr; Y</code> &mdash; true.</li><li>(B) The converse fails: a student may take several courses and have several hobbies, which is an MVD but certainly not an FD &mdash; false.</li><li>(C) <code class=\"mm\">X &rarr;&rarr; Y</code> is trivial when Y is inside X (then it says nothing) or when X and Y together exhaust the relation (then the leftover part is empty and again nothing is constrained) &mdash; true.</li><li>(D) That is exactly the definition of 4NF: every non-trivial MVD must have a superkey on its left &mdash; true.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (C) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) reverses the containment: MVDs are the weaker, more general form.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Treating <code class=\"mm\">&rarr;&rarr;</code> as a notational variant of <code class=\"mm\">&rarr;</code>. The arrow direction of the implication matters: FD implies MVD, never the other way.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> 4NF is to MVDs what BCNF is to FDs: every non-trivial dependency of the relevant kind must have a superkey on the left.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U3-Q10", "subject": "databases", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "normalization", "subtopic": "functional-dependencies-armstrong-axioms", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Armstrong's axiom system for functional dependencies consists of three inference rules. Which of the following are among those three axioms? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["Reflexivity: if Y ⊆ X then X → Y.", "Augmentation: if X → Y then XZ → YZ for every attribute set Z.", "Pseudotransitivity: if X → Y and YW → Z then XW → Z.", "Transitivity: if X → Y and Y → Z then X → Z."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Armstrong's three axioms are reflexivity, augmentation and transitivity. They are <i>sound</i> (they never derive a dependency that fails in some legal instance) and <i>complete</i> (every implied dependency can be derived from them). Everything else in the literature is a derived rule.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Reflexivity: a set determines its own subsets &mdash; an axiom.</li><li>Augmentation: adding the same attributes to both sides preserves the dependency &mdash; an axiom.</li><li>Transitivity: chaining two dependencies &mdash; the third axiom.</li><li>Pseudotransitivity is sound, and it can be proved from augmentation and transitivity, but it is not one of the three axioms. So (C) is not an axiom, while (A), (B) and (D) are.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(C) is a derived rule: it follows from (A), (B) and (D) rather than being assumed.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Listing pseudotransitivity, union, decomposition or additivity as axioms. They are all valid rules, but a valid rule is not necessarily an axiom &mdash; the system needs only three assumptions to generate all the rest.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Three axioms, three words: reflexivity, augmentation, transitivity. Any other named rule is derived.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U3-Q11", "subject": "databases", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "normalization", "subtopic": "1nf-2nf-3nf-bcnf", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Relation R(A, B, C, D) satisfies F = {AB -> C, C -> D}. Which one of the following is the highest normal form that R satisfies?", "options": ["1NF only", "2NF", "3NF", "BCNF"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Test the forms bottom-up. Each one adds a condition, so the highest form satisfied is the last test that passes before the first that fails.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Find the keys. <code class=\"mm\">A&#8314; = {A}</code>, <code class=\"mm\">B&#8314; = {B}</code>, <code class=\"mm\">C&#8314; = {C, D}</code>, <code class=\"mm\">D&#8314; = {D}</code>; <code class=\"mm\">(AB)&#8314; = {A, B, C, D}</code>. So <code class=\"mm\">AB</code> is the only candidate key, A and B are prime, C and D are non-prime.</li><li>1NF: every value is atomic &mdash; satisfied.</li><li>2NF: is any non-prime attribute determined by a proper subset of <code class=\"mm\">AB</code>? The only dependency with a smaller left-hand side is <code class=\"mm\">C &rarr; D</code>, and C is not a subset of the key. So there is no partial dependency &mdash; 2NF holds.</li><li>3NF: for <code class=\"mm\">C &rarr; D</code>, C is not a superkey and D is not prime, so the 3NF condition fails. The relation is not in 3NF.</li><li>BCNF fails for the same dependency, since C is not a superkey. The highest form is therefore 2NF.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) &mdash; 2NF, and not 3NF.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) is too low: 2NF is satisfied, so the relation is better than 1NF only.</li><li>(C) fails because <code class=\"mm\">C &rarr; D</code> is a transitive dependency on a non-prime attribute.</li><li>(D) fails for the same reason, and BCNF is stricter than 3NF anyway.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Declaring the relation to be in 3NF because \"the key determines everything\". A relation can be 2NF and still have a non-prime attribute determining another non-prime attribute, which is exactly the 3NF violation here.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Compute the keys first, then label every attribute prime or non-prime, then walk the ladder one form at a time. Skip no rung.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U3-Q12", "subject": "databases", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "normalization", "subtopic": "lossless-join-dependency-preservation", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "The tableau (chase) test is applied to a decomposition of R into R1, ..., Rn. The test declares the decomposition lossless when", "options": ["some row of the final tableau consists entirely of distinguished (a) symbols.", "every row of the final tableau contains at least one distinguished symbol.", "no row of the tableau can be changed by any further application of the dependencies.", "the union of the parts is R and the intersection of all the parts is empty."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The chase builds a tableau with one row per decomposed relation. A cell holds the distinguished symbol <code class=\"mm\">a&#7522;</code> when the attribute <code class=\"mm\">A&#7522;</code> belongs to that relation, and a subscripted <code class=\"mm\">b</code> symbol otherwise. Dependencies are then applied until nothing changes. The decomposition is lossless exactly when one row becomes all-distinguished, because that row represents a tuple of the original relation that the join reproduces.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Write one row per <code class=\"mm\">R&#7522;</code>, marking <code class=\"mm\">a&#7522;</code> in the columns that <code class=\"mm\">R&#7522;</code> contains.</li><li>Apply each dependency <code class=\"mm\">X &rarr; Y</code>: if two rows agree on all of X, make them agree on all of Y, preferring an <code class=\"mm\">a</code> symbol over a <code class=\"mm\">b</code> symbol.</li><li>Repeat to a fixed point.</li><li>If some row is all <code class=\"mm\">a</code> symbols, the join is lossless; otherwise it is lossy.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A) &mdash; one row must become entirely distinguished.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) is too weak: scattered <code class=\"mm\">a</code> symbols across different rows prove nothing.</li><li>(C) merely says the chase has finished, which is the stopping rule, not the verdict.</li><li>(D) describes a union and an empty intersection, which is a property of the decomposition, not a lossless guarantee; it is in fact the classic lossy shape.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming that a decomposition into relations whose union is <code class=\"mm\">R</code> is automatically lossless. That condition is necessary for the decomposition to make sense at all; it says nothing about whether the join reconstructs the original tuples.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Chase verdict in one line: one all-<code class=\"mm\">a</code> row means lossless, no such row means lossy.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U3-Q13", "subject": "databases", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "normalization", "subtopic": "closure-of-attribute-sets-keys", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Relation R(A, B, C, D) satisfies F = {A -> B, B -> C, C -> D}. How many attributes are there in the closure A+?", "options": [], "answer_display": "4", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> <code class=\"mm\">X&#8314;</code> is the set of all attributes determined by X. It is computed by starting with X and repeatedly adding the right-hand side of every dependency whose left-hand side is already inside the set.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Start with <code class=\"mm\">A&#8314; = {A}</code>.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; B</code> applies, so add B: <code class=\"mm\">{A, B}</code>.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">B &rarr; C</code> applies, so add C: <code class=\"mm\">{A, B, C}</code>.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">C &rarr; D</code> applies, so add D: <code class=\"mm\">{A, B, C, D}</code>.</li><li>No further dependency applies, so the closure holds all 4 attributes.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 4.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Counting only the attributes reached in one pass, or forgetting that the starting attribute itself belongs to its closure. <code class=\"mm\">A&#8314;</code> always contains A.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> A chain <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; B &rarr; C &rarr; D</code> sweeps up every attribute downstream, so the closure of the head is the whole relation.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U3-Q14", "subject": "databases", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "normalization", "subtopic": "functional-dependencies-armstrong-axioms", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Let F = {A -> B, B -> C, A -> C} be a set of dependencies on R(A, B, C). How many functional dependencies are there in a minimal cover of F?", "options": [], "answer_display": "2", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A minimal (canonical) cover is an equivalent set of dependencies in which every right-hand side is a single attribute, no dependency is redundant, and no left-hand side can be shrunk. Here every right-hand side is already single, and every left-hand side is a single attribute that cannot be shrunk further.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Right-hand sides are already singletons, so there is nothing to split.</li><li>Is <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; B</code> redundant? Remove it and compute <code class=\"mm\">A&#8314;</code> from <code class=\"mm\">{B &rarr; C, A &rarr; C}</code>: the closure is <code class=\"mm\">{A, C}</code>, which does not contain B. So it is needed.</li><li>Is <code class=\"mm\">B &rarr; C</code> redundant? Remove it and the remaining set gives <code class=\"mm\">B&#8314; = {B}</code>, so it is needed.</li><li>Is <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; C</code> redundant? Remove it: from <code class=\"mm\">{A &rarr; B, B &rarr; C}</code> we get <code class=\"mm\">A&#8314; = {A, B, C}</code>, which contains C, so it is redundant and can be dropped.</li><li>The minimal cover is <code class=\"mm\">{A &rarr; B, B &rarr; C}</code>: two dependencies.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 2.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reporting three because the original set had three. A minimal cover is not a subset of the original in general &mdash; it is an equivalent set with no redundancy &mdash; and here the transitive dependency must go.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> To minimise: make right-hand sides single, then remove each dependency in turn and test whether the rest still derives it.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U3-Q15", "subject": "databases", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "normalization", "subtopic": "multivalued-dependencies-4nf", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Relation R(A, B, C) satisfies the two multivalued dependencies A ->> B and A ->> C, and no functional dependency. Which one of the following statements is TRUE?", "options": ["R is in BCNF, and therefore also in 4NF.", "R is in 4NF because A →→ B and A →→ C are its only dependencies.", "R is not in 4NF, because A is not a superkey of R; the lossless 4NF decomposition is R₁(A, B) and R₂(A, C).", "R cannot be decomposed into 4NF without loss of information."], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> 4NF asks that every non-trivial MVD have a superkey on its left. With no functional dependency at all, the only candidate key of <code class=\"mm\">R(A, B, C)</code> is the full set <code class=\"mm\">ABC</code>, so <code class=\"mm\">A</code> is not a superkey and both MVDs violate 4NF.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Keys: no FD holds, so no proper subset determines the relation; the candidate key is <code class=\"mm\">ABC</code>.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">A &rarr;&rarr; B</code> is non-trivial (B is not inside A, and <code class=\"mm\">A &cup; B = AB &ne; ABC</code>) and <code class=\"mm\">A</code> is not a superkey, so 4NF fails.</li><li>The standard 4NF decomposition of <code class=\"mm\">X &rarr;&rarr; Y</code> in <code class=\"mm\">R(X, Y, Z)</code> is <code class=\"mm\">(X, Y)</code> and <code class=\"mm\">(X, Z)</code>. Applying it gives <code class=\"mm\">R&#8321;(A, B)</code> and <code class=\"mm\">R&#8322;(A, C)</code>, and the second MVD is then trivially satisfied inside those parts.</li><li>The decomposition is lossless: the common attribute A is a key of both parts (each part is a two-attribute relation in which A determines the other attribute by the MVD), so the join restores exactly the original tuples.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) is false: BCNF speaks only about FDs, so a relation with no FDs is trivially in BCNF &mdash; and that is exactly why BCNF alone cannot detect this redundancy. It is 4NF that fails.</li><li>(B) inverts the 4NF condition: having MVDs is precisely what must be checked, not a reason to pass.</li><li>(D) is false: the MVD-based decomposition above is lossless.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Concluding \"in BCNF, therefore in 4NF\". BCNF is defined on functional dependencies only; a relation can be in BCNF and still be in 3NF-violating shape for MVDs. Every 4NF relation is in BCNF, never the reverse.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> An MVD with no FD on the same determinant is invisible to BCNF and fatal to 4NF. Split <code class=\"mm\">X &rarr;&rarr; Y</code> into <code class=\"mm\">(X, Y)</code> and <code class=\"mm\">(X, Z)</code>.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U3-Q16", "subject": "databases", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "normalization", "subtopic": "1nf-2nf-3nf-bcnf", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "A relation R is in Boyce-Codd normal form (BCNF) exactly when", "options": ["every attribute is atomic and no attribute holds a set of values.", "no non-prime attribute is transitively dependent on a candidate key.", "every candidate key is a superkey.", "for every non-trivial functional dependency X → Y that holds in R, X is a superkey of R."], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> BCNF strengthens 3NF by dropping 3NF's escape clause. 3NF allows a dependency whose left-hand side is not a superkey provided the right-hand side is a prime attribute; BCNF allows no such exception. Every non-trivial dependency must have a superkey on its left.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Take every non-trivial FD <code class=\"mm\">X &rarr; Y</code> that holds in the relation.</li><li>Ask whether <code class=\"mm\">X</code> is a superkey &mdash; that is, whether <code class=\"mm\">X&#8314;</code> is the whole relation.</li><li>If the answer is yes for all of them, the relation is in BCNF; a single no means it is not.</li><li>Option (D) states exactly this. (A) is 1NF, (B) is 3NF stated for non-prime attributes, and (C) is a consequence of the definition rather than the definition itself.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) describes 1NF.</li><li>(B) describes 3NF, which is weaker than BCNF.</li><li>(C) is true of every relation by definition &mdash; a candidate key is a minimal superkey &mdash; so it cannot be the BCNF test.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Quoting BCNF as \"no transitive dependency\". That is 3NF. The difference shows up precisely when a non-superkey determinant has a prime attribute on its right-hand side: 3NF permits it, BCNF does not.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> BCNF = every determinant of a non-trivial FD is a superkey. No exceptions, no prime-attribute escape.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U3-Q17", "subject": "databases", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "normalization", "subtopic": "lossless-join-dependency-preservation", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Relation R(A, B, C, D) satisfies F = {A -> B, B -> C, C -> D}. Which of the following decompositions of R are lossless? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["R₁(A, C) and R₂(B, D)", "R₁(A, B) and R₂(B, C, D)", "R₁(A, B) and R₂(A, C, D)", "R₁(A, B, C) and R₂(C, D)"], "answer_display": "Options (B), (C) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> For a two-way decomposition the test is short: intersect the two schemas and check whether the intersection determines one of the parts. The chase test gives the same verdict.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) <code class=\"mm\">R&#8321;(A, C) &cap; R&#8322;(B, D) = &empty;</code>. With no common attribute the join is a cartesian product, so the decomposition is lossy. (A) is out.</li><li>(B) common attribute B. <code class=\"mm\">B&#8314; = {B, C, D}</code>, so B is a key of <code class=\"mm\">R&#8322;(B, C, D)</code>. Lossless.</li><li>(C) common attribute A. <code class=\"mm\">A&#8314; = {A, B, C, D}</code>, so A is a key of <code class=\"mm\">R&#8321;(A, B)</code>. Lossless.</li><li>(D) common attribute C. <code class=\"mm\">C&#8314; = {C, D}</code>, so C is a key of <code class=\"mm\">R&#8322;(C, D)</code>. Lossless.</li><li>Three of the four pass: (B), (C) and (D).</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (B), (C) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) shares no attribute between the two parts, which is the textbook lossy case.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Judging losslessness by \"does the decomposition look natural\" or by counting attributes. Only the intersection test decides, and a decomposition can look perfectly reasonable and still be lossy.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Intersect, then ask \"is the intersection a key of either part?\" For a chain <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; B &rarr; C &rarr; D</code>, any split whose two parts share a point of the chain is lossless.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U3-Q18", "subject": "databases", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "normalization", "subtopic": "closure-of-attribute-sets-keys", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Relation R(A, B, C, D, E) satisfies F = {AB -> C, C -> D, D -> E, E -> A}. How many candidate keys does R have?", "options": [], "answer_display": "4", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> B appears on no right-hand side, so B is in every candidate key. Every key is therefore B together with the smallest extra attribute or attributes needed to close the cycle <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; C &rarr; D &rarr; E &rarr; A</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">B&#8314; = {B}</code>, so B alone is not a key; B needs a partner.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">(AB)&#8314;</code>: <code class=\"mm\">AB &rarr; C</code>, <code class=\"mm\">C &rarr; D</code>, <code class=\"mm\">D &rarr; E</code>, <code class=\"mm\">E &rarr; A</code> &mdash; the closure is all five. <code class=\"mm\">AB</code> is a key.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">(BC)&#8314;</code>: <code class=\"mm\">C &rarr; D</code>, <code class=\"mm\">D &rarr; E</code>, <code class=\"mm\">E &rarr; A</code>, then <code class=\"mm\">AB &rarr; C</code> is already satisfied &mdash; all five. <code class=\"mm\">BC</code> is a key.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">(BD)&#8314;</code>: <code class=\"mm\">D &rarr; E</code>, <code class=\"mm\">E &rarr; A</code>, then <code class=\"mm\">AB &rarr; C</code> &mdash; all five. <code class=\"mm\">BD</code> is a key.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">(BE)&#8314;</code>: <code class=\"mm\">E &rarr; A</code>, then <code class=\"mm\">AB &rarr; C</code>, <code class=\"mm\">C &rarr; D</code> &mdash; all five. <code class=\"mm\">BE</code> is a key.</li><li>None of the four contains another, so all four are minimal. The count is 4.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 4 &mdash; the candidate keys are <code class=\"mm\">AB</code>, <code class=\"mm\">BC</code>, <code class=\"mm\">BD</code> and <code class=\"mm\">BE</code>.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming the cycle <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; C &rarr; D &rarr; E &rarr; A</code> produces one key. A cycle lets you enter it at any point, and each entry point gives a different minimal key: that is exactly why there are four here and not one.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Locate the attributes that appear on no right-hand side, pair them with each possible entry into the cycle, and keep only the pairs whose closure is the whole relation.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U3-Q19", "subject": "databases", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "normalization", "subtopic": "functional-dependencies-armstrong-axioms", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Relation R(A, B, C) satisfies F = {A -> B, B -> C}. Which of the following functional dependencies are in F+? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["A → C", "AB → C", "A → BC", "C → A"], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> <code class=\"mm\">X &rarr; Y</code> belongs to <code class=\"mm\">F&#8314;</code> exactly when <code class=\"mm\">Y &sube; X&#8314;</code>. Compute the closure of each left-hand side and test containment.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">A&#8314; = {A, B, C}</code>: <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; B</code> then <code class=\"mm\">B &rarr; C</code>. So <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; C</code> holds, and <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; BC</code> holds too.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">(AB)&#8314; = {A, B, C}</code>, so <code class=\"mm\">AB &rarr; C</code> holds. It also follows directly by augmentation from <code class=\"mm\">B &rarr; C</code>.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">C&#8314; = {C}</code>: C is on the right-hand side only, so nothing can be derived from it and <code class=\"mm\">C &rarr; A</code> fails.</li><li>Hence (A), (B) and (C) are in <code class=\"mm\">F&#8314;</code>; (D) is not.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(D) reverses the direction of the chain; a dependency never implies its own converse.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Missing <code class=\"mm\">AB &rarr; C</code> because \"AB is bigger than the left-hand side of the original dependency\". Augmentation makes any left-hand side bigger and keeps the dependency valid.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Closure test, every time: grow the left-hand side until it stops growing, then check that the right-hand side is inside.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U3-Q20", "subject": "databases", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "normalization", "subtopic": "multivalued-dependencies-4nf", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Relation R(A, B, C) satisfies A ->> B and A ->> C, with candidate key ABC. Which one of the following is a correct 4NF decomposition of R?", "options": ["R₁(A, B, C) — R is already in 4NF, so no decomposition is needed.", "R₁(A, B) and R₂(A, C)", "R₁(A, B) and R₂(B, C)", "R₁(A, C) and R₂(B, C)"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> When <code class=\"mm\">X &rarr;&rarr; Y</code> holds in <code class=\"mm\">R(X, Y, Z)</code>, the 4NF decomposition is <code class=\"mm\">(X, Y)</code> and <code class=\"mm\">(X, Z)</code>. The determinant X is kept in both parts, which is what makes the join lossless.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">A</code> is not a superkey of <code class=\"mm\">ABC</code>, so the relation is not in 4NF and must be decomposed.</li><li>Applying the rule to <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr;&rarr; B</code> with the leftover attribute C gives <code class=\"mm\">(A, B)</code> and <code class=\"mm\">(A, C)</code>.</li><li>Check the parts: in <code class=\"mm\">(A, B)</code> the MVD <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr;&rarr; B</code> is trivial because <code class=\"mm\">A &cup; B</code> is the whole relation; the same holds for <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr;&rarr; C</code> in <code class=\"mm\">(A, C)</code>. Both parts are in 4NF.</li><li>Check losslessness: the common attribute is A, and each part is determined by A, so the join restores exactly the original tuples.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) leaves a non-trivial MVD with a non-superkey determinant in place, so 4NF is still violated.</li><li>(C) and (D) both drop A from one part. Joining on B or on C cannot reconstruct the pairing of Y values with Z values, so the decomposition loses information.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Decomposing like a binary many-to-many relationship, into <code class=\"mm\">(A, B)</code> and <code class=\"mm\">(B, C)</code>. An MVD split is not a relationship split: the determinant must appear in both parts.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> MVD split: keep X on both sides, put Y in one part and the remaining attributes in the other.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U3-Q21", "subject": "databases", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "normalization", "subtopic": "1nf-2nf-3nf-bcnf", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Which of the following statements about 3NF and BCNF decompositions are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["Every relation has a decomposition into 3NF that is both lossless and dependency preserving.", "Every relation has a decomposition into BCNF that is lossless.", "Every relation has a decomposition into BCNF that is both lossless and dependency preserving.", "Every relation that is in BCNF is also in 3NF."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The two guarantees are not symmetric. 3NF can always deliver both properties at once, by the synthesis algorithm. BCNF can always deliver losslessness, but may be forced to give up dependency preservation.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) 3NF synthesis from a minimal cover produces relations that are lossless and preserve every dependency, adding a relation over a candidate key if none of the synthesised relations contains one &mdash; true.</li><li>(B) The BCNF decomposition algorithm splits on any violating dependency and always yields a lossless join &mdash; true.</li><li>(C) is false. The classic counterexample is <code class=\"mm\">R(A, B, C)</code> with <code class=\"mm\">{AB &rarr; C, C &rarr; B}</code>: the BCNF decomposition into <code class=\"mm\">(A, C)</code> and <code class=\"mm\">(B, C)</code> loses <code class=\"mm\">AB &rarr; C</code>.</li><li>(D) BCNF is strictly stronger than 3NF: its condition implies the 3NF condition, so every BCNF relation is in 3NF &mdash; true.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(C) overstates BCNF. When the two goals conflict, BCNF wins on redundancy and loses on dependency preservation, which is why 3NF is often the practical stopping point.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Believing BCNF is always preferable because it is \"higher\". A BCNF decomposition that drops a dependency forces every update to be checked by an expensive join, which is often worse than the redundancy 3NF leaves behind.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> 3NF = lossless + dependency preserving, always. BCNF = lossless, always; dependency preserving, only sometimes.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U3-Q22", "subject": "databases", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "normalization", "subtopic": "lossless-join-dependency-preservation", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Relation R(A, B, C, D) satisfies F = {A -> B, BC -> D} and is decomposed into R1(A, B) and R2(A, C, D). Which one of the following statements is TRUE?", "options": ["The decomposition is lossless and dependency preserving.", "The decomposition is dependency preserving but lossy.", "The decomposition is lossless but not dependency preserving.", "The decomposition is both lossy and not dependency preserving."], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Losslessness and dependency preservation are independent properties and must be tested separately. Losslessness is about the join reconstructing the original tuples; preservation is about being able to check every original dependency inside a single decomposed relation.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Losslessness: <code class=\"mm\">R&#8321; &cap; R&#8322; = {A}</code>, and <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; B</code> holds, so A is a key of <code class=\"mm\">R&#8321;(A, B)</code>. The join is lossless.</li><li>Dependency preservation: <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; B</code> is checkable inside <code class=\"mm\">R&#8321;</code>.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">BC &rarr; D</code> needs B and C together, but B appears only in <code class=\"mm\">R&#8321;</code> and C only in <code class=\"mm\">R&#8322;</code>. Neither part contains both, and no part implies it: the projection of F on <code class=\"mm\">R&#8322;</code> is empty, and on <code class=\"mm\">R&#8321;</code> it is just <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; B</code>.</li><li>So <code class=\"mm\">BC &rarr; D</code> is lost. The decomposition is lossless but not dependency preserving.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) fails on preservation, as shown above.</li><li>(B) fails on losslessness: the common attribute A is a key of <code class=\"mm\">R&#8321;</code>, so no spurious tuples can appear.</li><li>(D) is wrong on both counts.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming a lossless decomposition automatically preserves dependencies. It does not, and this example is the smallest one that shows it: the two properties pull in different directions.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Run two tests, not one. Lossless: does the intersection determine a part? Preserving: does every dependency live inside some single part?</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U3-Q23", "subject": "databases", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "normalization", "subtopic": "closure-of-attribute-sets-keys", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Relation R(A, B, C, D) satisfies F = {A -> B, B -> C, C -> D}. How many superkeys does R have?", "options": [], "answer_display": "8", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A superkey is any attribute set that contains a candidate key. Once the candidate keys are known, counting superkeys is a counting problem on the remaining attributes.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Keys: <code class=\"mm\">A&#8314; = {A, B, C, D}</code>, and no attribute alone or in a pair without A closes, so the only candidate key is <code class=\"mm\">{A}</code>.</li><li>Every superkey must contain A; the other three attributes B, C, D are free to be in or out.</li><li>The number of superkeys is therefore <code class=\"mm\">2&#179; = 8</code>.</li><li>Check by listing: <code class=\"mm\">{A}, {AB}, {AC}, {AD}, {ABC}, {ABD}, {ACD}, {ABCD}</code> &mdash; eight.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 8.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Counting <code class=\"mm\">2&#8308; = 16</code> by letting A vary as well, or counting only the four subsets that are not supersets. A superkey must contain the whole candidate key, so A is not free.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> With a single candidate key of size k in an n-attribute relation the superkey count is <code class=\"mm\">2^(n &minus; k)</code>; here <code class=\"mm\">2&#179; = 8</code>.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U3-Q24", "subject": "databases", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "normalization", "subtopic": "multivalued-dependencies-4nf", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "In R(A, B, C), which one of the following multivalued dependencies is trivial?", "options": ["A →→ B", "A →→ BC", "B →→ C", "C →→ A"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> <code class=\"mm\">X &rarr;&rarr; Y</code> is trivial when <code class=\"mm\">Y &sube; X</code> or when <code class=\"mm\">X &cup; Y</code> is the whole relation. In either case nothing is left for Y to be independent of, so the dependency imposes no condition on the data.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">A &rarr;&rarr; B</code>: <code class=\"mm\">B &sube; A</code> is false and <code class=\"mm\">A &cup; B = AB &ne; ABC</code>, so it is non-trivial.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">A &rarr;&rarr; BC</code>: <code class=\"mm\">BC &sube; A</code> is false, but the second clause holds, because <code class=\"mm\">A &cup; BC = ABC = R</code>. There is no leftover attribute, so the dependency is trivial.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">B &rarr;&rarr; C</code>: <code class=\"mm\">C &sube; B</code> is false and <code class=\"mm\">B &cup; C = BC &ne; ABC</code>, so it is non-trivial.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">C &rarr;&rarr; A</code>: <code class=\"mm\">A &sube; C</code> is false and <code class=\"mm\">C &cup; A = AC &ne; ABC</code>, so it is non-trivial.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) &mdash; <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr;&rarr; BC</code>.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A), (C) and (D) all leave a genuinely undetermined remainder, so each one constrains the data.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Using only the functional-dependency rule <code class=\"mm\">Y &sube; X</code> and forgetting the second clause <code class=\"mm\">X &cup; Y = R</code>. That second clause is what makes <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr;&rarr; BC</code> trivial, and it is also why a dependency can be trivial without looking obviously so.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Two triviality clauses for MVDs, one for FDs. If nothing remains outside <code class=\"mm\">X &cup; Y</code>, the dependency cannot bite.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U3-Q25", "subject": "databases", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "normalization", "subtopic": "lossless-join-dependency-preservation", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Relation R(A, B, C, D) is decomposed into R1(A, B, C) and R2(C, D). The decomposition is lossless if", "options": ["C → D holds in R.", "A → C holds in R.", "D → C holds in R.", "B → C holds in R."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The common attribute here is C. The decomposition is lossless when C is a superkey of at least one of the two parts: of <code class=\"mm\">R&#8322;(C, D)</code>, which needs <code class=\"mm\">C &rarr; D</code>, or of <code class=\"mm\">R&#8321;(A, B, C)</code>, which would need C to determine A and B.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">R&#8321; &cap; R&#8322; = {C}</code>.</li><li>If <code class=\"mm\">C &rarr; D</code> holds, C is a key of <code class=\"mm\">R&#8322;</code>, so every tuple of the join is a tuple of the original relation &mdash; lossless.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; C</code> constrains A and C, both of which live inside <code class=\"mm\">R&#8321;</code>; it never relates the two parts, so it cannot make the join lossless.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">D &rarr; C</code> makes D a key of <code class=\"mm\">R&#8322;</code>, not C. The common attribute must itself be the key, so this does not help.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">B &rarr; C</code> likewise lives inside <code class=\"mm\">R&#8321;</code> and does not involve D at all.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) and (D) are dependencies entirely inside one part; they say nothing about how the parts join.</li><li>(C) points the arrow the wrong way: it makes D determine C, so C is not the key of <code class=\"mm\">R&#8322;</code>.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Accepting any dependency that mentions the common attribute. The dependency must have the common attribute on the <i>left</i> and must reach across the split.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Common attribute C, split into <code class=\"mm\">R&#8321;</code> and <code class=\"mm\">R&#8322;</code>: lossless if <code class=\"mm\">C &rarr; (R&#8321; &minus; C)</code> or <code class=\"mm\">C &rarr; (R&#8322; &minus; C)</code>. Here <code class=\"mm\">C &rarr; D</code> is the second form.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U3-Q26", "subject": "databases", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "normalization", "subtopic": "functional-dependencies-armstrong-axioms", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "Which one of the following is NOT a valid inference rule for functional dependencies?", "options": ["Union: if X → Y and X → Z then X → YZ.", "Decomposition: if X → YZ then X → Y.", "Converse: if X → Y then Y → X.", "Pseudotransitivity: if X → Y and YW → Z then XW → Z."], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A functional dependency is a one-way statement about values: equal values on the left force equal values on the right. It never licenses the reverse. Reversing the arrow is the single most common invalid inference in this unit.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Union and decomposition are derived rules that follow from Armstrong's augmentation and transitivity; both are sound.</li><li>Pseudotransitivity follows from augmentation and transitivity; it is also sound.</li><li>The converse claims that <code class=\"mm\">X &rarr; Y</code> implies <code class=\"mm\">Y &rarr; X</code>. Take <code class=\"mm\">R(A, B)</code> with the single tuple <code class=\"mm\">(1, 1)</code> against <code class=\"mm\">(2, 1)</code>: <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; B</code> holds but <code class=\"mm\">B &rarr; A</code> fails, because <code class=\"mm\">B = 1</code> maps to two different <code class=\"mm\">A</code> values.</li><li>So the converse is the invalid rule.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) Union is valid: from <code class=\"mm\">X &rarr; Y</code> and <code class=\"mm\">X &rarr; Z</code>, augment and combine to get <code class=\"mm\">X &rarr; YZ</code>.</li><li>(B) Decomposition is valid because a set of attributes determines each of its members.</li><li>(D) Pseudotransitivity is valid: augment <code class=\"mm\">X &rarr; Y</code> to <code class=\"mm\">XW &rarr; YW</code>, then transitively compose with <code class=\"mm\">YW &rarr; Z</code>.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Treating <code class=\"mm\">&rarr;</code> as if it were an equation. Augmentation adds attributes on both sides, which looks like reversibility, but it is a completely different operation from swapping the two sides.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> You may add attributes to both sides, or chain arrows, but you may never reverse an arrow.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U3-Q27", "subject": "databases", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "normalization", "subtopic": "functional-dependencies-armstrong-axioms", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "Which one of the following statements about Armstrong's three axioms is TRUE?", "options": ["The axioms are sound but not complete, so some dependencies in F⁺ cannot be derived from them.", "The axioms are both sound and complete: every dependency they derive holds in every relation satisfying F, and every dependency that holds can be derived.", "The axioms generate exactly the dependencies listed in F and never anything new.", "Completeness means the axioms never derive a dependency that does not actually hold."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Two separate properties are at stake. <b>Soundness</b> means every derived dependency is genuinely implied by <code class=\"mm\">F</code>. <b>Completeness</b> means every dependency implied by <code class=\"mm\">F</code> can be derived. Armstrong's reflexivity, augmentation and transitivity are sound and complete.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Soundness: each of the three axioms is easily checked against the definition of a functional dependency.</li><li>Completeness is the harder half; it is what guarantees that closure computation by repeated application of the axioms reaches <i>all</i> of <code class=\"mm\">F&#8314;</code>.</li><li>Together they mean the closure <code class=\"mm\">F&#8314;</code> is exactly the set of derivable dependencies.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) Denies completeness, which Armstrong's system has.</li><li>(C) Denies that anything new is derived, which would make transitivity useless.</li><li>(D) Describes <i>soundness</i>, not completeness — the two words are swapped.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Using the two words interchangeably. Soundness is \"nothing wrong gets derived\"; completeness is \"nothing right is left underived\".</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Sound + complete is what lets a closure computation stand in for reasoning about <code class=\"mm\">F&#8314;</code> directly.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U3-Q28", "subject": "databases", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "normalization", "subtopic": "functional-dependencies-armstrong-axioms", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "Relation R(A, B, C) satisfies F = {AB → C, C → A}. Which one of the following functional dependencies is in F⁺?", "options": ["B → C", "A → C", "C → B", "BC → A"], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A dependency <code class=\"mm\">X &rarr; Y</code> is in <code class=\"mm\">F&#8314;</code> exactly when <code class=\"mm\">Y &sube; X&#8314;</code>. So the whole question is four small closure computations.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">B&#8314; = {B}</code> — no dependency has <code class=\"mm\">B</code> alone on its left, so <code class=\"mm\">B &rarr; C</code> is out.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">A&#8314; = {A}</code> — <code class=\"mm\">AB &rarr; C</code> needs <code class=\"mm\">B</code> as well, so <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; C</code> is out.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">C&#8314; = {C, A}</code> — <code class=\"mm\">C &rarr; A</code> applies, then <code class=\"mm\">AB &rarr; C</code> needs <code class=\"mm\">B</code>. So <code class=\"mm\">C &rarr; B</code> is out.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">(BC)&#8314; = {B, C, A}</code> — <code class=\"mm\">C &rarr; A</code> gives <code class=\"mm\">A</code>, and now <code class=\"mm\">AB &rarr; C</code> is already satisfied. Hence <code class=\"mm\">BC &rarr; A</code> holds.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) and (B) have left-hand sides that determine nothing beyond themselves.</li><li>(C) would need <code class=\"mm\">B</code> in <code class=\"mm\">C&#8314;</code>; the only dependency producing <code class=\"mm\">B</code> is <code class=\"mm\">AB &rarr; C</code>, which produces <code class=\"mm\">C</code>, not <code class=\"mm\">B</code>.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reading <code class=\"mm\">AB &rarr; C</code> backwards as \"<code class=\"mm\">C</code> determines <code class=\"mm\">A</code> and <code class=\"mm\">B</code>\". The given <code class=\"mm\">C &rarr; A</code> supplies only <code class=\"mm\">A</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> To test membership, compute the closure of the left-hand side only — never reason about the arrow by analogy.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U3-Q29", "subject": "databases", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "normalization", "subtopic": "functional-dependencies-armstrong-axioms", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "Relation R(A, B, C) satisfies F = {A → B, B → C}. Which of the following statements are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["F is a minimal cover of itself.", "A → C is in F⁺.", "C → A is in F⁺.", "The dependency A → B is redundant in F."], "answer_display": "Options (A) and (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A minimal cover is a set of dependencies with singleton right-hand sides, no extraneous attribute on any left-hand side, and no dependency that is implied by the rest. A dependency is redundant when it survives in the closure of the others.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Both right-hand sides are already singletons, and both left-hand sides are single attributes, so no attribute can be extraneous.</li><li>Drop <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; B</code>: with only <code class=\"mm\">B &rarr; C</code> left, <code class=\"mm\">A&#8314; = {A}</code>, so <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; B</code> is not recoverable — it is needed.</li><li>Drop <code class=\"mm\">B &rarr; C</code>: with only <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; B</code> left, <code class=\"mm\">B&#8314; = {B}</code>, so <code class=\"mm\">B &rarr; C</code> is needed too.</li><li>Hence <code class=\"mm\">F</code> is already minimal, and by transitivity <code class=\"mm\">A&#8314; = {A, B, C}</code>, so <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; C</code> holds.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">C&#8314; = {C}</code>, so <code class=\"mm\">C &rarr; A</code> does not hold.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A) and (B) are true; (C) and (D) are false.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(C) inverts the chain: the arrows run <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; B &rarr; C</code>, never back.</li><li>(D) removing <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; B</code> leaves nothing that produces <code class=\"mm\">B</code>, so the dependency is essential.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Believing a minimal cover must be unique. Several minimal covers can exist for the same <code class=\"mm\">F</code>; here there happens to be only one.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Minimal means no redundancy, not minimum cardinality over all possible equivalent sets in every case.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U3-Q30", "subject": "databases", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "normalization", "subtopic": "functional-dependencies-armstrong-axioms", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "Relation R(A, B, C, D) satisfies F = {A → BC, B → D, C → D}. How many functional dependencies are there in a minimal cover of F?(Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "4", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Building a minimal cover has three passes: split every right-hand side into singletons, remove extraneous left-hand attributes, then remove redundant dependencies.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Split: <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; BC</code> becomes <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; B</code> and <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; C</code>. The set is now <code class=\"mm\">{A &rarr; B, A &rarr; C, B &rarr; D, C &rarr; D}</code>.</li><li>No left-hand side has more than one attribute, so there is no extraneous attribute to remove.</li><li>Test <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; B</code>: without it, <code class=\"mm\">A&#8314; = {A, C, D}</code> (via <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; C</code> and <code class=\"mm\">C &rarr; D</code>), which does not contain <code class=\"mm\">B</code>. Needed.</li><li>Test <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; C</code>: without it, <code class=\"mm\">A&#8314; = {A, B, D}</code> (via <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; B</code> and <code class=\"mm\">B &rarr; D</code>), which does not contain <code class=\"mm\">C</code>. Needed.</li><li>Test <code class=\"mm\">B &rarr; D</code>: without it, <code class=\"mm\">B&#8314; = {B}</code>. Needed.</li><li>Test <code class=\"mm\">C &rarr; D</code>: without it, <code class=\"mm\">C&#8314; = {C}</code>. Needed.</li><li>Nothing is redundant, so the minimal cover has <b>4</b> dependencies.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 4</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering 3 by counting the three original dependencies. Splitting <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; BC</code> increases the count before any redundancy removal can reduce it.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Split first, then prune — the two passes change the count in opposite directions, so do them in order and never skip one.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U3-Q31", "subject": "databases", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "normalization", "subtopic": "closure-of-attribute-sets-keys", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "Relation R(A, B, C) has exactly two candidate keys, {A} and {B, C}. Which attributes of R are prime?", "options": ["A only", "A and B", "A and C", "A, B and C"], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> An attribute is <b>prime</b> when it belongs to at least one candidate key. It need not belong to every key, and it need not be a key on its own.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">A</code> is in the candidate key <code class=\"mm\">{A}</code>, so <code class=\"mm\">A</code> is prime.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">B</code> is in the candidate key <code class=\"mm\">{B, C}</code>, so <code class=\"mm\">B</code> is prime.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">C</code> is in the same candidate key, so <code class=\"mm\">C</code> is prime.</li><li>All three attributes are prime.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) counts only the single-attribute key and forgets the composite one.</li><li>(B) and (C) each drop one member of <code class=\"mm\">{B, C}</code>, but membership of a key is enough on its own.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming a prime attribute must be able to identify a tuple by itself. Prime means \"part of some key\", nothing more; <code class=\"mm\">B</code> alone determines nothing.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Collect every candidate key first, then take their union — that union is exactly the set of prime attributes.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U3-Q32", "subject": "databases", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "normalization", "subtopic": "closure-of-attribute-sets-keys", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "Relation R(A, B, C, D) satisfies F = {AB → C, C → D, D → B}. Which one of the following is a candidate key of R?", "options": ["{A, B, C}", "{A, C}", "{B, D}", "{C, D}"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A candidate key is a <i>minimal</i> superkey. First find the attributes that can never be produced by any dependency — they must appear in every key — then close each smallest candidate.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">A</code> appears on no right-hand side, so every key must contain <code class=\"mm\">A</code>. <code class=\"mm\">A&#8314; = {A}</code>, so <code class=\"mm\">A</code> alone is not a key.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">(AB)&#8314; = {A, B, C, D}</code>: <code class=\"mm\">AB &rarr; C</code>, then <code class=\"mm\">C &rarr; D</code>. So <code class=\"mm\">{A, B}</code> is a key.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">(AC)&#8314; = {A, C, D, B}</code>: <code class=\"mm\">C &rarr; D</code>, then <code class=\"mm\">D &rarr; B</code>. So <code class=\"mm\">{A, C}</code> is a key.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">(AD)&#8314; = {A, D, B, C}</code>: <code class=\"mm\">D &rarr; B</code>, then <code class=\"mm\">AB &rarr; C</code>. So <code class=\"mm\">{A, D}</code> is a key.</li><li>The candidate keys are therefore <code class=\"mm\">{A, B}</code>, <code class=\"mm\">{A, C}</code> and <code class=\"mm\">{A, D}</code>; <code class=\"mm\">{A, C}</code> is the only option among them.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) <code class=\"mm\">{A, B, C}</code> is a superkey but not minimal — <code class=\"mm\">C</code> is unnecessary because <code class=\"mm\">{A, B}</code> already determines everything.</li><li>(C) <code class=\"mm\">(BD)&#8314; = {B, D}</code>: nothing determines <code class=\"mm\">A</code> or <code class=\"mm\">C</code> from <code class=\"mm\">B</code> and <code class=\"mm\">D</code>.</li><li>(D) <code class=\"mm\">(CD)&#8314; = {C, D, B}</code>: <code class=\"mm\">A</code> is still missing.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Stopping at the first superkey found. <code class=\"mm\">{A, B, C}</code> closes to everything but fails minimality, which is the whole difference between a superkey and a key.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Start from the attributes that appear in no right-hand side; they are forced into every key and cut the search down immediately.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U3-Q33", "subject": "databases", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "normalization", "subtopic": "closure-of-attribute-sets-keys", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "Relation R(A, B, C, D) satisfies F = {A → B, B → C, C → D, D → A}. How many superkeys does R have?(Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "15", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A superkey is any attribute set whose closure is the whole relation. Count keys by finding the minimal ones first, then count every set that contains at least one of them.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The dependencies form a single cycle <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; B &rarr; C &rarr; D &rarr; A</code>, so from any one attribute the closure grows to all four.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">A&#8314; = {A, B, C, D}</code>, <code class=\"mm\">B&#8314; = {B, C, D, A}</code>, <code class=\"mm\">C&#8314; = {C, D, A, B}</code>, <code class=\"mm\">D&#8314; = {D, A, B, C}</code>.</li><li>Every single attribute is therefore a candidate key, and no proper subset of a singleton exists.</li><li>A superkey is any non-empty set containing at least one attribute — which is every non-empty subset of a four-attribute relation.</li><li>Count: <code class=\"mm\">2&#8308; &minus; 1 = 15</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 15</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering 4 by counting only the candidate keys. The question asks for superkeys, and every set that contains a key is one.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> When every attribute is a key, every non-empty subset is a superkey — the count is <code class=\"mm\">2&#8319; &minus; 1</code>.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U3-Q34", "subject": "databases", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "normalization", "subtopic": "closure-of-attribute-sets-keys", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "Relation R(A, B, C, D) satisfies F = {AB → C, C → D, D → A}. Which of the following statements are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["{A, B} is a candidate key of R.", "{B, C} is a candidate key of R.", "{A} is a superkey of R.", "D is a prime attribute of R."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Candidate keys here are all pairs containing <code class=\"mm\">B</code>, because <code class=\"mm\">B</code> appears on no right-hand side and so must be in every key. Prime attributes are the union of all candidate keys.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">B</code> is never determined, so every key contains <code class=\"mm\">B</code>. <code class=\"mm\">B&#8314; = {B}</code>, so <code class=\"mm\">{B}</code> is not a key.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">(AB)&#8314; = {A, B, C, D}</code> — a candidate key.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">(BC)&#8314; = {B, C, D, A}</code> — a candidate key.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">(BD)&#8314; = {B, D, A, C}</code> — a candidate key.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">A&#8314; = {A}</code>, so <code class=\"mm\">{A}</code> is not even a superkey.</li><li>Prime attributes = <code class=\"mm\">{A, B}</code> &cup; <code class=\"mm\">{B, C}</code> &cup; <code class=\"mm\">{B, D}</code> = <code class=\"mm\">{A, B, C, D}</code>; in particular <code class=\"mm\">D</code> is prime, through the key <code class=\"mm\">{B, D}</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (D) are true; (C) is false.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other option is wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(C) <code class=\"mm\">A</code> determines nothing at all: the only dependency with <code class=\"mm\">A</code> on the left is <code class=\"mm\">AB &rarr; C</code>, which needs <code class=\"mm\">B</code>.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Concluding that <code class=\"mm\">D</code> is non-prime because it sits on the right-hand side of <code class=\"mm\">C &rarr; D</code>. Being determined by something says nothing about prime status; only key membership does.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Enumerate candidate keys, take their union for the prime attributes, and answer superkey questions from the key list rather than from the arrows.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U3-Q35", "subject": "databases", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "normalization", "subtopic": "closure-of-attribute-sets-keys", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "Relation R(A, B, C, D, E) satisfies F = {AB → C, BC → D, CD → E, DE → A}. How many superkeys does R have?", "options": ["10", "12", "13", "16"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Count candidate keys first, then count the attribute sets that contain at least one of them. Where keys overlap, inclusion&ndash;exclusion removes the double counts.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>No single attribute determines all five: <code class=\"mm\">A&#8314; = {A}</code>, <code class=\"mm\">B&#8314; = {B}</code>, <code class=\"mm\">C&#8314; = {C}</code>, <code class=\"mm\">D&#8314; = {D}</code>, <code class=\"mm\">E&#8314; = {E}</code>.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">(AB)&#8314; = {A, B, C, D, E}</code>: <code class=\"mm\">AB &rarr; C</code>, <code class=\"mm\">BC &rarr; D</code>, <code class=\"mm\">CD &rarr; E</code>. So <code class=\"mm\">{A, B}</code> is a key.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">(BC)&#8314; = {B, C, D, E, A}</code>: <code class=\"mm\">BC &rarr; D</code>, <code class=\"mm\">CD &rarr; E</code>, <code class=\"mm\">DE &rarr; A</code>. So <code class=\"mm\">{B, C}</code> is a key.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">(BDE)&#8314; = {B, D, E, A, C}</code>: <code class=\"mm\">DE &rarr; A</code>, then <code class=\"mm\">AB &rarr; C</code>. So <code class=\"mm\">{B, D, E}</code> is a third key — easy to miss.</li><li>No other set closes to all five: <code class=\"mm\">(CD)&#8314; = {C, D, E, A}</code> is missing <code class=\"mm\">B</code>, and <code class=\"mm\">(AC)&#8314; = {A, C}</code>.</li><li>Count the supersets of each key: <code class=\"mm\">{A, B}</code> gives <code class=\"mm\">2&#179; = 8</code>, <code class=\"mm\">{B, C}</code> gives 8, <code class=\"mm\">{B, D, E}</code> gives <code class=\"mm\">2&#178; = 4</code>.</li><li>Remove the double counts: sets containing both <code class=\"mm\">{A, B}</code> and <code class=\"mm\">{B, C}</code> number 4, both <code class=\"mm\">{A, B}</code> and <code class=\"mm\">{B, D, E}</code> number 2, both <code class=\"mm\">{B, C}</code> and <code class=\"mm\">{B, D, E}</code> number 2, and the set containing all three is counted back once.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">8 + 8 + 4 &minus; 4 &minus; 2 &minus; 2 + 1 = 13</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C) — 13 superkeys.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) 10 ignores the third key <code class=\"mm\">{B, D, E}</code> altogether.</li><li>(B) 12 is what you get if the two overlapping keys are treated as disjoint and the triple overlap is dropped.</li><li>(D) 16 is every non-empty subset of the five attributes, which would need every attribute to be a key.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Stopping after the first two keys found. <code class=\"mm\">{B, D, E}</code> only closes to everything through the chain <code class=\"mm\">DE &rarr; A &rarr; C</code>, and missing it changes the answer.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Superkey counts need the complete key list plus inclusion&ndash;exclusion; never count from the number of dependencies.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U3-Q36", "subject": "databases", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "normalization", "subtopic": "1nf-2nf-3nf-bcnf", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "A relation R is in third normal form (3NF) exactly when, for every non-trivial functional dependency X → A that holds in R,", "options": ["X is a superkey of R.", "A is a prime attribute of R.", "X is a superkey of R, or A is a prime attribute of R.", "X contains no prime attribute of R."], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> 3NF relaxes BCNF with one escape clause. BCNF demands that every non-trivial determinant be a superkey; 3NF allows a determinant that is not a superkey provided the determined attribute is prime.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The strict condition alone is the BCNF definition, so option (A) describes BCNF, not 3NF.</li><li>The prime-attribute condition alone is too weak on its own and does not state 3NF either.</li><li>The correct statement is the disjunction: for each non-trivial <code class=\"mm\">X &rarr; A</code>, either <code class=\"mm\">X</code> is a superkey or <code class=\"mm\">A</code> is prime.</li><li>Because a superkey condition implies the prime condition is irrelevant once satisfied, 3NF is a strict weakening of BCNF.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) is exactly BCNF and is therefore too strong.</li><li>(B) ignores the superkey route entirely and would wrongly reject relations whose determinants are keys.</li><li>(D) is not part of any normal-form definition.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reciting 3NF as \"no transitive dependency\" and then applying it literally. The usable test is the disjunction above; the transitive-dependency phrasing is only an informal description.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> 3NF = BCNF + the prime-attribute escape. Remember the escape, because every 3NF-but-not-BCNF question turns on it.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U3-Q37", "subject": "databases", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "normalization", "subtopic": "1nf-2nf-3nf-bcnf", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "A relation R is in 2NF but not in 3NF. Which one of the following must be TRUE?", "options": ["Some non-prime attribute is transitively dependent on a candidate key.", "Some non-prime attribute is partially dependent on a candidate key.", "Some determinant of a non-trivial dependency is not a superkey, and its right-hand side is a prime attribute.", "R has at least two candidate keys."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> 2NF removes partial dependencies of non-prime attributes on part of a key. 3NF additionally removes transitive dependencies of non-prime attributes on a key. Being in 2NF but not 3NF therefore means the remaining defect is transitive, not partial.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Since <code class=\"mm\">R</code> is in 2NF, no non-prime attribute depends on a proper subset of a candidate key.</li><li>Since <code class=\"mm\">R</code> is not in 3NF, some non-trivial <code class=\"mm\">X &rarr; A</code> has <code class=\"mm\">X</code> not a superkey and <code class=\"mm\">A</code> non-prime.</li><li>Because <code class=\"mm\">A</code> is non-prime and determined by a non-superkey, it reaches <code class=\"mm\">A</code> only through another attribute set — that is precisely a transitive dependency on a candidate key.</li><li>Hence option (A) must hold.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) describes a violation of 2NF, which contradicts the premise.</li><li>(C) describes 3NF-but-not-BCNF: there the right-hand side is prime, so 3NF still holds.</li><li>(D) is not forced; a relation with a single candidate key can be in 2NF and not 3NF.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reading \"not in 3NF\" as \"not in 2NF\". The forms are nested — every 3NF relation is in 2NF — so a 3NF failure leaves the 2NF guarantee intact.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Read a normal-form gap as a diagnosis: 2NF fixes partial dependencies, 3NF fixes transitive ones, BCNF fixes everything else.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U3-Q38", "subject": "databases", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "normalization", "subtopic": "1nf-2nf-3nf-bcnf", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "Relation R(A, B, C, D, E) satisfies F = {A → BC, CD → E, B → D, E → A}. How many of the five attributes of R are non-prime?(Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "0", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> An attribute is prime when it belongs to at least one candidate key. So the only way to count non-prime attributes is to enumerate candidate keys and take the complement of their union.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">A&#8314; = {A, B, C, D, E}</code>: <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; BC</code>, <code class=\"mm\">B &rarr; D</code>, <code class=\"mm\">CD &rarr; E</code>. So <code class=\"mm\">{A}</code> is a candidate key.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">E&#8314; = {E, A, B, C, D}</code>: <code class=\"mm\">E &rarr; A</code>, then <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; BC</code>, <code class=\"mm\">B &rarr; D</code>. So <code class=\"mm\">{E}</code> is a candidate key.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">(BC)&#8314; = {B, C, D, E, A}</code>: <code class=\"mm\">B &rarr; D</code>, <code class=\"mm\">CD &rarr; E</code>, <code class=\"mm\">E &rarr; A</code>. So <code class=\"mm\">{B, C}</code> is a candidate key.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">(CD)&#8314; = {C, D, E, A, B}</code>, so <code class=\"mm\">{C, D}</code> is a candidate key.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">(CE)&#8314; = {C, E, A, B, D}</code>, so <code class=\"mm\">{C, E}</code> is a candidate key.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">(BE)&#8314; = {B, E, A, C, D}</code>, so <code class=\"mm\">{B, E}</code> is a candidate key.</li><li>Union of all candidate keys = <code class=\"mm\">{A, B, C, D, E}</code>. Every attribute is prime, so <b>0</b> attributes are non-prime.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 0</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming every relation has some non-prime attribute, so answering 1 or more without checking. Here the dependency structure is so tightly connected that each attribute sits inside some key.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> When every attribute is prime, the 3NF prime-attribute escape applies to every dependency, so the relation is automatically in 3NF — a useful shortcut worth checking before a long normal-form analysis.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U3-Q39", "subject": "databases", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "normalization", "subtopic": "1nf-2nf-3nf-bcnf", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "Which of the following statements about second normal form are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["2NF forbids a non-prime attribute from depending on a proper subset of any candidate key.", "A relation in 1NF whose only candidate key is a single attribute is automatically in 2NF.", "Every relation in BCNF is also in 2NF.", "Every relation in 2NF is also in 3NF."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> 2NF is about <i>partial</i> dependencies: a non-prime attribute must depend on a whole candidate key, never on part of one. Partial dependency can only arise when a candidate key has at least two attributes.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) is the definition of 2NF, stated for every candidate key rather than just the primary key.</li><li>(B) follows because a single-attribute key has no non-empty proper subset, so no partial dependency is possible.</li><li>(C) follows because BCNF forbids every non-superkey determinant, which includes the partial ones; BCNF is the strongest of the three forms and implies the weaker two.</li><li>(D) is false: a relation can be in 2NF and still carry a transitive dependency, which is exactly what 3NF removes.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (C) are true; (D) is false.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other option is wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(D) reverses the containment: 3NF &sub; 2NF &sub; 1NF, so 3NF implies 2NF and never the other way round.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Checking partial dependency against the primary key only. 2NF is defined against <i>every</i> candidate key, and a relation can be clean on one key and dirty on another.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Nested order 1NF &sub; 2NF &sub; 3NF &sub; BCNF; each step removes one specific defect.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U3-Q40", "subject": "databases", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "normalization", "subtopic": "1nf-2nf-3nf-bcnf", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "Relation R(A, B, C, D, E) satisfies F = {A → B, BC → D, D → E, E → C}. Which one of the following is the highest normal form that R satisfies?", "options": ["1NF only", "2NF", "3NF", "BCNF"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Test the forms from the strongest down, but you need the candidate keys first: whether a dependency is partial depends on which attributes form a key.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">A</code> appears on no right-hand side, so <code class=\"mm\">A</code> is in every key. <code class=\"mm\">A&#8314; = {A, B}</code>, so <code class=\"mm\">{A}</code> is not a key.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">(AC)&#8314; = {A, B, C, D, E}</code>: <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; B</code>, <code class=\"mm\">BC &rarr; D</code>, <code class=\"mm\">D &rarr; E</code>. So <code class=\"mm\">{A, C}</code> is a key.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">(AD)&#8314; = {A, B, D, E, C}</code>: <code class=\"mm\">D &rarr; E</code>, <code class=\"mm\">E &rarr; C</code>, then <code class=\"mm\">BC &rarr; D</code>. So <code class=\"mm\">{A, D}</code> is a key. Likewise <code class=\"mm\">{A, E}</code> is a key.</li><li>Candidate keys are <code class=\"mm\">{A, C}</code>, <code class=\"mm\">{A, D}</code> and <code class=\"mm\">{A, E}</code>; the non-prime attribute is <code class=\"mm\">B</code> alone.</li><li>2NF: <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; B</code> determines the non-prime <code class=\"mm\">B</code> from <code class=\"mm\">A</code>, a proper subset of the key <code class=\"mm\">{A, C}</code>. That is a partial dependency, so 2NF fails.</li><li>Since 2NF fails, 3NF and BCNF fail too, and the highest form satisfied is 1NF.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A) — 1NF only.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) would require <code class=\"mm\">B</code> to depend on the whole of every key, but <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; B</code> gives it from <code class=\"mm\">A</code> alone.</li><li>(C) and (D) cannot hold once 2NF has failed, because the forms are nested.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Spotting that all three keys contain <code class=\"mm\">A</code> and concluding the relation is fine. A shared attribute in every key makes partial dependencies <i>more</i> likely, not less.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Find the candidate keys first; without them \"partial dependency\" is not even a well-defined test.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U3-Q41", "subject": "databases", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "normalization", "subtopic": "lossless-join-dependency-preservation", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "A decomposition of R into R₁, …, Rₙ is dependency preserving when", "options": ["the union of the projections of F onto the parts is equivalent to F.", "every pair of parts shares at least one attribute.", "the union of the parts is R, so no attribute is lost.", "every part is in BCNF."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Dependency preservation is about whether the constraints can still be enforced without joining the parts back together. Each part <code class=\"mm\">R&#7522;</code> can only enforce the dependencies whose attributes all lie inside it — its projection <code class=\"mm\">F&#7522;</code> of <code class=\"mm\">F&#8314;</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Project <code class=\"mm\">F</code> onto each part: <code class=\"mm\">F&#7522;</code> contains every dependency of <code class=\"mm\">F&#8314;</code> whose attributes are all in <code class=\"mm\">R&#7522;</code>.</li><li>Take the union <code class=\"mm\">G = F&#8321; &cup; &hellip; &cup; F&#8345;</code>.</li><li>The decomposition preserves dependencies exactly when <code class=\"mm\">G&#8314; = F&#8314;</code>, i.e. when <code class=\"mm\">G</code> is equivalent to <code class=\"mm\">F</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) describes a condition sometimes used for losslessness, not for dependency preservation; two parts can share an attribute and still lose a dependency.</li><li>(C) is the basic sanity condition for any decomposition at all, and is satisfied by decompositions that lose dependencies.</li><li>(D) is a normal-form property and is unrelated to whether dependencies survive.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Confusing dependency preservation with losslessness. They are independent: a decomposition can be lossless and not preserve dependencies, or preserve them and be lossy.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Lossless = no spurious tuples on join; dependency preserving = constraints still enforceable part by part.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U3-Q42", "subject": "databases", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "normalization", "subtopic": "lossless-join-dependency-preservation", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "Relation R(A, B, C, D) satisfies F = {A → B, C → D} and is decomposed into R₁(A, B) and R₂(C, D). Which one of the following statements is TRUE?", "options": ["The decomposition is lossless and dependency preserving.", "The decomposition is lossless but not dependency preserving.", "The decomposition is lossy but dependency preserving.", "The decomposition is both lossy and not dependency preserving."], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Two independent tests. Losslessness for a binary decomposition: the common attributes must be a superkey of at least one part. Dependency preservation: the union of the projected dependencies must cover <code class=\"mm\">F</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">R&#8321; &cap; R&#8322; = &empty;</code>. An empty intersection is never a superkey of either part, so the join test fails and the decomposition is <b>lossy</b>.</li><li>Concretely, the join of the two parts produces every combination of the <code class=\"mm\">A, B</code> pairs with the <code class=\"mm\">C, D</code> pairs, including tuples that were never in <code class=\"mm\">R</code>.</li><li>Projection onto <code class=\"mm\">R&#8321;(A, B)</code> gives <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; B</code>; projection onto <code class=\"mm\">R&#8322;(C, D)</code> gives <code class=\"mm\">C &rarr; D</code>.</li><li>Their union is exactly <code class=\"mm\">F</code>, so both dependencies survive: the decomposition is <b>dependency preserving</b>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) and (B) both claim losslessness, which an empty intersection rules out.</li><li>(D) claims the dependencies are lost, but each dependency lies entirely inside one part.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming a decomposition that keeps every attribute and every dependency must be lossless. Losing no attribute is not the same as losing no information.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Check the intersection first — if <code class=\"mm\">R&#8321; &cap; R&#8322;</code> is empty, the binary decomposition is lossy no matter how the dependencies fall.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U3-Q43", "subject": "databases", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "normalization", "subtopic": "lossless-join-dependency-preservation", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "Relation R(A, B, C, D) satisfies F = {A → B, B → C, C → D}. Which of the following decompositions of R are dependency preserving? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["R₁(A, B), R₂(B, C), R₃(C, D)", "R₁(A, B), R₂(B, D)", "R₁(A, B), R₂(A, C, D)", "R₁(A, B, C), R₂(C, D)"], "answer_display": "Options (A) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> For each dependency of <code class=\"mm\">F</code>, compute the closure of its left-hand side under the union of the projected dependencies. The decomposition preserves dependencies only if every dependency passes.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) Projections give <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; B</code>, <code class=\"mm\">B &rarr; C</code> and <code class=\"mm\">C &rarr; D</code> — exactly <code class=\"mm\">F</code>. Preserved.</li><li>(B) Projections give <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; B</code> and <code class=\"mm\">B &rarr; D</code> (valid since <code class=\"mm\">B&#8314; = {B, C, D}</code>). Under these, <code class=\"mm\">B&#8314; = {B, D}</code>, so <code class=\"mm\">B &rarr; C</code> is lost.</li><li>(C) Projections give <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; B</code> and, on <code class=\"mm\">A, C, D</code>, <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; C</code>, <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; D</code>, <code class=\"mm\">C &rarr; D</code>. Under these, <code class=\"mm\">B&#8314; = {B}</code>, so <code class=\"mm\">B &rarr; C</code> is lost.</li><li>(D) Projections give <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; B</code>, <code class=\"mm\">B &rarr; C</code>, <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; C</code> and <code class=\"mm\">C &rarr; D</code>. All three dependencies of <code class=\"mm\">F</code> are recovered. Preserved.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A) and (D) are dependency preserving; (B) and (C) are not.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) and (C) both split <code class=\"mm\">B</code> away from <code class=\"mm\">C</code>, so nothing inside either part can express <code class=\"mm\">B &rarr; C</code>.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Judging preservation by whether the original dependencies \"appear\" in the parts. A dependency can be implied by the projections even when it is not written down, as <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; C</code> in part (D) shows.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Preservation is a closure test on the union of projections, not a visual check for identical arrows.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U3-Q44", "subject": "databases", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "normalization", "subtopic": "lossless-join-dependency-preservation", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "Relation R(A, B, C, D, E) satisfies F = {AB → C, C → D, D → E} and is decomposed into R₁(A, B, C), R₂(A, B, D) and R₃(D, E). How many of the three dependencies of F are preserved by this decomposition?(Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "2", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Project <code class=\"mm\">F&#8314;</code> onto each part, union the projections into <code class=\"mm\">G</code>, then test each dependency of <code class=\"mm\">F</code> by computing the left-hand closure under <code class=\"mm\">G</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Projection onto <code class=\"mm\">R&#8321;(A, B, C)</code>: <code class=\"mm\">AB &rarr; C</code> (attributes <code class=\"mm\">A, B, C</code> all present).</li><li>Projection onto <code class=\"mm\">R&#8322;(A, B, D)</code>: <code class=\"mm\">AB &rarr; D</code>, which is valid because <code class=\"mm\">(AB)&#8314; = {A, B, C, D, E}</code> in <code class=\"mm\">F</code>.</li><li>Projection onto <code class=\"mm\">R&#8323;(D, E)</code>: <code class=\"mm\">D &rarr; E</code>.</li><li>So <code class=\"mm\">G = {AB &rarr; C, AB &rarr; D, D &rarr; E}</code>.</li><li>Test <code class=\"mm\">AB &rarr; C</code>: <code class=\"mm\">(AB)&#8314;</code> under <code class=\"mm\">G</code> = <code class=\"mm\">{A, B, C, D, E}</code>. Preserved.</li><li>Test <code class=\"mm\">C &rarr; D</code>: <code class=\"mm\">C&#8314;</code> under <code class=\"mm\">G</code> = <code class=\"mm\">{C}</code>, because no dependency in <code class=\"mm\">G</code> has <code class=\"mm\">C</code> on its left. Lost.</li><li>Test <code class=\"mm\">D &rarr; E</code>: <code class=\"mm\">D&#8314;</code> under <code class=\"mm\">G</code> = <code class=\"mm\">{D, E}</code>. Preserved.</li><li>Two of the three are preserved.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 2</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Counting <code class=\"mm\">C &rarr; D</code> as preserved because <code class=\"mm\">C</code> and <code class=\"mm\">D</code> both survive somewhere in the schema. They never appear together in a single part, so no part can enforce that dependency.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> A dependency survives only if some single part contains all its attributes and the union of projections still implies it.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U3-Q45", "subject": "databases", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "normalization", "subtopic": "lossless-join-dependency-preservation", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "Relation R(A, B, C, D) satisfies F = {A → B, C → D}. Which one of the following decompositions of R is lossless?", "options": ["R₁(A, B), R₂(C, D)", "R₁(A, C), R₂(B, D)", "R₁(A, B), R₂(A, C, D)", "R₁(A, C), R₂(B, C)"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> For a binary decomposition into <code class=\"mm\">R&#8321;</code> and <code class=\"mm\">R&#8322;</code>, the decomposition is lossless exactly when <code class=\"mm\">R&#8321; &cap; R&#8322;</code> determines every attribute of at least one of the two parts — that is, the intersection is a superkey of <code class=\"mm\">R&#8321;</code> or of <code class=\"mm\">R&#8322;</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) <code class=\"mm\">R&#8321; &cap; R&#8322; = &empty;</code>, which is never a superkey. Lossy.</li><li>(B) <code class=\"mm\">R&#8321; &cap; R&#8322; = &empty;</code> again. Lossy.</li><li>(C) <code class=\"mm\">R&#8321; &cap; R&#8322; = {A}</code>, and <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; B</code> holds, so <code class=\"mm\">{A}</code> determines all of <code class=\"mm\">R&#8321;(A, B)</code>. <b>Lossless.</b></li><li>(D) <code class=\"mm\">R&#8321; &cap; R&#8322; = {C}</code>. Neither <code class=\"mm\">C &rarr; A</code> (for <code class=\"mm\">R&#8321;(A, C)</code>) nor <code class=\"mm\">C &rarr; B</code> (for <code class=\"mm\">R&#8322;(B, C)</code>) holds. Lossy.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) and (B) have empty intersections, so the join reconstructs the full cross product of the parts.</li><li>(D) shares <code class=\"mm\">C</code> but <code class=\"mm\">C</code> determines only <code class=\"mm\">D</code>, which is in neither part, so nothing is pinned down.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming any two parts that share an attribute join losslessly. The shared attributes must actually be a key of one of the parts.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Binary losslessness = \"the intersection is a superkey of one side\". Test it with the closure of the intersection.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U3-Q46", "subject": "databases", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "normalization", "subtopic": "multivalued-dependencies-4nf", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "Relation R(A, B, C) satisfies the multivalued dependency A →→ B. Which one of the following multivalued dependencies is guaranteed to hold in R as well?", "options": ["A →→ C", "A → B", "B →→ A", "C →→ B"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Multivalued dependencies come with a <b>complementation</b> rule: if <code class=\"mm\">X &rarr;&rarr; Y</code> holds in <code class=\"mm\">R</code>, then <code class=\"mm\">X &rarr;&rarr; (R &minus; X &minus; Y)</code> holds too. The two right-hand sides partition what is left of <code class=\"mm\">R</code> once <code class=\"mm\">X</code> is removed.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Here <code class=\"mm\">X = A</code> and <code class=\"mm\">Y = B</code> in <code class=\"mm\">R(A, B, C)</code>.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">R &minus; X &minus; Y = {A, B, C} &minus; {A} &minus; {B} = {C}</code>.</li><li>So <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr;&rarr; C</code> follows immediately.</li><li>Intuitively, \"the set of <code class=\"mm\">B</code> values under an <code class=\"mm\">A</code> is independent of the <code class=\"mm\">C</code> values\" is exactly the same statement as \"the set of <code class=\"mm\">C</code> values is independent of the <code class=\"mm\">B</code> values\".</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) is the converse of the implication <code class=\"mm\">X &rarr; Y &rArr; X &rarr;&rarr; Y</code>; an MVD does not give a functional dependency.</li><li>(C) reverses the left and right sides, which no rule permits.</li><li>(D) moves the determinant to <code class=\"mm\">C</code>, which nothing licenses.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Treating an MVD as a weaker functional dependency. It is a different statement — about independent sets of values — and the only guaranteed consequences are complementation, augmentation, transitivity and the trivial cases.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> For every <code class=\"mm\">X &rarr;&rarr; Y</code> there is a matching <code class=\"mm\">X &rarr;&rarr; (R &minus; X &minus; Y)</code>; always check the complement before concluding anything.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U3-Q47", "subject": "databases", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "normalization", "subtopic": "multivalued-dependencies-4nf", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "Relation R(X, Y, Z) is decomposed into R₁(X, Y) and R₂(X, Z). This decomposition is lossless whenever", "options": ["X →→ Y holds in R.", "X → Y holds in R.", "Y →→ Z holds in R.", "no condition is needed: every binary decomposition is lossless."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A multivalued dependency <code class=\"mm\">X &rarr;&rarr; Y</code> in <code class=\"mm\">R(X, Y, Z)</code> says the <code class=\"mm\">Y</code> values and the <code class=\"mm\">Z</code> values under an <code class=\"mm\">X</code> vary independently. Storing them in separate relations <code class=\"mm\">(X, Y)</code> and <code class=\"mm\">(X, Z)</code> therefore loses nothing: the cross product on join regenerates exactly the original tuples.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>If <code class=\"mm\">X &rarr;&rarr; Y</code> holds, then for each <code class=\"mm\">X</code> value the relation contains every combination of its <code class=\"mm\">Y</code> values with its <code class=\"mm\">Z</code> values.</li><li>Joining <code class=\"mm\">R&#8321;(X, Y)</code> with <code class=\"mm\">R&#8322;(X, Z)</code> on <code class=\"mm\">X</code> regenerates exactly those combinations.</li><li>No tuple that was absent can appear, so the join is exactly <code class=\"mm\">R</code> — lossless.</li><li>This is the standard MVD decomposition used by 4NF.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) <code class=\"mm\">X &rarr; Y</code> is stronger than needed and would also work, but it is not the condition; the MVD is what justifies the split.</li><li>(C) <code class=\"mm\">Y &rarr;&rarr; Z</code> would justify a different split, on <code class=\"mm\">Y</code>, not this one.</li><li>(D) is false: <code class=\"mm\">R&#8321;(X, Y)</code>, <code class=\"mm\">R&#8322;(Z)</code> is a binary decomposition and is lossy.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Believing losslessness requires a functional dependency. An MVD is enough, and the 4NF decomposition depends on exactly that fact.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Lossless binary rule: the intersection is a superkey of one part <i>or</i> an MVD holds across the split.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U3-Q48", "subject": "databases", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "normalization", "subtopic": "multivalued-dependencies-4nf", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "Which of the following statements about fourth normal form are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["Every relation in 4NF is also in BCNF.", "Every relation in BCNF is also in 4NF.", "Decomposing R on a multivalued dependency X →→ Y into (X ∪ Y) and (X ∪ (R − X − Y)) is lossless.", "4NF is defined using functional dependencies alone."], "answer_display": "Options (A) and (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> 4NF strengthens BCNF: for every non-trivial multivalued dependency <code class=\"mm\">X &rarr;&rarr; Y</code>, <code class=\"mm\">X</code> must be a superkey. Since every functional dependency is also a multivalued dependency, the 4NF condition subsumes the BCNF condition.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) True: if every non-trivial MVD has a superkey determinant, then in particular every non-trivial FD does, because <code class=\"mm\">X &rarr; Y &rArr; X &rarr;&rarr; Y</code>. So 4NF implies BCNF.</li><li>(B) False: a relation can have a non-trivial MVD with a non-superkey determinant while having no non-trivial FD at all.</li><li>(C) True: that split is exactly the MVD decomposition, and joining the two parts on <code class=\"mm\">X</code> regenerates <code class=\"mm\">R</code>.</li><li>(D) False: 4NF is defined in terms of multivalued dependencies; using only FDs would just restate BCNF.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A) and (C) are true; (B) and (D) are false.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) reverses the containment: 4NF &sub; BCNF, not the other way round.</li><li>(D) removes the very concept that distinguishes 4NF from BCNF.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Thinking 4NF and BCNF are incomparable. They are nested, and the nesting direction matters: a relation can be in BCNF and fail 4NF, never the reverse.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Order: 1NF &sub; 2NF &sub; 3NF &sub; BCNF &sub; 4NF, each defined by a strictly stronger condition.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U3-Q49", "subject": "databases", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "normalization", "subtopic": "multivalued-dependencies-4nf", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "Relation R(A, B, C, D) satisfies the multivalued dependencies A →→ B and A →→ C. Applying the 4NF decomposition repeatedly until no non-trivial multivalued dependency with a non-superkey left-hand side remains, how many relations result?(Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "3", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The 4NF decomposition on <code class=\"mm\">X &rarr;&rarr; Y</code> replaces <code class=\"mm\">R</code> with <code class=\"mm\">(X &cup; Y)</code> and <code class=\"mm\">(X &cup; (R &minus; X &minus; Y))</code>, and the process repeats on each part until every remaining MVD is trivial or has a superkey left-hand side.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">A</code> is not a superkey of <code class=\"mm\">R(A, B, C, D)</code> and <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr;&rarr; B</code> is non-trivial, so split on it: <code class=\"mm\">(A, B)</code> and <code class=\"mm\">(A, C, D)</code>.</li><li>In <code class=\"mm\">(A, B)</code>, the restriction of <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr;&rarr; B</code> is trivial because <code class=\"mm\">A &cup; B</code> is the whole part. Done.</li><li>In <code class=\"mm\">(A, C, D)</code>, the restriction of <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr;&rarr; C</code> is non-trivial and <code class=\"mm\">A</code> is not a superkey. Split: <code class=\"mm\">(A, C)</code> and <code class=\"mm\">(A, D)</code>.</li><li>Both remaining parts have two attributes, so every MVD in them is trivial. The process stops.</li><li>Resulting relations: <code class=\"mm\">(A, B)</code>, <code class=\"mm\">(A, C)</code>, <code class=\"mm\">(A, D)</code> — three relations.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 3</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering 2 by splitting only once on <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr;&rarr; B</code> and stopping. The second MVD survives inside <code class=\"mm\">(A, C, D)</code> and must be applied again.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Keep decomposing until every part is either two attributes wide or has only trivial MVDs — the count comes from the recursion, not from the number of given dependencies.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U3-Q50", "subject": "databases", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "normalization", "subtopic": "multivalued-dependencies-4nf", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "Which one of the following relations is in BCNF but not in 4NF?", "options": ["R(A, B, C) with A →→ B and no non-trivial functional dependency.", "R(A, B, C) with A → B and B → C.", "R(A, B, C) with AB → C and C → B.", "R(A, B, C, D) with A → B and C → D."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> BCNF constrains functional dependencies; 4NF constrains multivalued dependencies. A relation escapes BCNF only through a non-trivial FD with a non-superkey determinant, and escapes 4NF through a non-trivial MVD with a non-superkey determinant. To be in BCNF but not 4NF, the relation must have no offending FD but at least one offending MVD.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) With no non-trivial FD, BCNF is satisfied vacuously. But <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr;&rarr; B</code> is non-trivial (<code class=\"mm\">B &not&sube; A</code> and <code class=\"mm\">A &cup; B &ne; R</code>) and <code class=\"mm\">A</code> is not a superkey. So 4NF fails. <b>This is the answer.</b></li><li>(B) <code class=\"mm\">B &rarr; C</code> has <code class=\"mm\">B</code> as a non-superkey determinant, so BCNF already fails.</li><li>(C) <code class=\"mm\">C &rarr; B</code> has <code class=\"mm\">C</code> as a non-superkey determinant (the keys are <code class=\"mm\">AB</code> and <code class=\"mm\">AC</code>), so BCNF fails.</li><li>(D) <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; B</code> has <code class=\"mm\">A</code> as a non-superkey determinant, so BCNF fails.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B), (C) and (D) each contain a non-trivial FD whose determinant is not a superkey, so none of them is even in BCNF.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming that a relation with no functional dependencies must be fully normalized. With no FDs it is in BCNF automatically, but an MVD alone can still leave it outside 4NF — which is precisely why 4NF exists.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> BCNF looks only at FDs; 4NF looks at MVDs. A relation with MVDs and no FDs is the classic BCNF-but-not-4NF case.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U4-Q01", "subject": "databases", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "transactions-concurrency", "subtopic": "acid-properties", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "A transaction debits &#8377;5,000 from account  A  and credits &#8377;5,000 to account  B . The debit is written to disk, then the system crashes before the credit is written. On restart the debit is undone. Which ACID property is being enforced?", "options": ["Atomicity: either all of the transaction's updates are reflected in the database or none of them is.", "Consistency: the sum of the two balances is unchanged.", "Isolation: no other transaction observed the intermediate state.", "Durability: the committed debit survives the crash."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Atomicity requires that a transaction is an indivisible unit of work: its effects are either all applied or all removed. A partially applied transaction that is then rolled back is exactly what atomicity forbids, and undoing the debit restores the all-or-nothing guarantee.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The transaction consists of two updates that must travel together: debit <code class=\"mm\">A</code> and credit <code class=\"mm\">B</code>.</li><li>The debit reached the database, the credit did not, so the database held a state in which money had disappeared.</li><li>Recovery used the before-image of <code class=\"mm\">A</code> to undo the debit, leaving the database as if the transaction had never run.</li><li>Removing the partial effect is the definition of atomicity; consistency and durability are properties of the end states and of committed data respectively, and neither is what the undo enforces.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) Consistency is about the integrity constraints holding before and after a complete transaction; the undo is not what preserves the sum, the completed pair of updates would be.</li><li>(C) Isolation concerns interference between concurrent transactions; no second transaction appears here.</li><li>(D) Durability concerns committed data surviving a crash. This transaction never committed, so durability has nothing to protect.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reading 'the system crashed and recovery fixed it' and answering durability. Durability protects work that has committed; atomicity removes work that has not.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Undo of a partial transaction is the atomicity mechanism; redo of a committed transaction is the durability mechanism.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U4-Q02", "subject": "databases", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "transactions-concurrency", "subtopic": "acid-properties", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "Which ACID property guarantees that, once a transaction has committed, its updates survive a subsequent system crash?", "options": ["Isolation", "Consistency", "Atomicity", "Durability"], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Durability is the guarantee that the effects of a committed transaction are permanent: once the commit is acknowledged, a later failure cannot erase them.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The moment of interest is the commit point: before it, the transaction may be undone; after it, it must never be undone.</li><li>Durability is implemented by forcing the log records of the transaction to stable (non-volatile) storage before the commit is acknowledged, and by redoing committed work during recovery.</li><li>Hence the property that speaks about survival after a crash is durability.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) Isolation is about the visibility of intermediate states to concurrent transactions.</li><li>(B) Consistency is about integrity constraints being preserved by a complete transaction.</li><li>(C) Atomicity is about all-or-nothing execution; it undoes aborted work but says nothing about committed work surviving a crash.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Confusing atomicity with durability because both involve recovery. Recovery performs undo for atomicity and redo for durability; they are opposite halves of the same log.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Undo = atomicity, redo = durability, locks/timestamps = isolation, constraints = consistency.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U4-Q03", "subject": "databases", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "transactions-concurrency", "subtopic": "acid-properties", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "Which one of the following statements about the consistency property (the C of ACID) is TRUE?", "options": ["The DBMS can enforce consistency entirely on its own, without being told the application's integrity constraints.", "Consistency and isolation are the same requirement, because both concern concurrent execution.", "A transaction that starts from a state satisfying the declared integrity constraints must leave the database in a state that again satisfies them.", "A schedule is consistent only when no two transactions execute concurrently."], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Consistency is a statement about states, not about execution: if a transaction runs to completion on a consistent database, the database is consistent again afterwards. The constraints themselves come from the application; the DBMS can only enforce the ones it is told about (keys, foreign keys, CHECK assertions).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Consistency is preserved by the transaction's own logic together with the declared constraints: every intermediate state may be inconsistent, but the final state must satisfy all constraints.</li><li>The other three ACID properties are mechanisms the DBMS supplies; consistency is the property the application is responsible for stating.</li><li>Therefore (C) is the accurate statement.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) The DBMS cannot enforce constraints it does not know; a transfer of funds between accounts keeps the total constant only because the application says so.</li><li>(B) Isolation is about concurrent execution being equivalent to a serial order; consistency is about the end state satisfying constraints. A serializable schedule can still break a constraint if the transaction's logic is wrong.</li><li>(D) Serial execution is a scheduling notion; consistency does not require it.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Treating consistency as something the concurrency-control protocol delivers. A perfectly serializable schedule can still violate a business rule.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Isolation, atomicity and durability are DBMS mechanisms; consistency is the application's contract, checked with the DBMS's constraint machinery.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U4-Q04", "subject": "databases", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "transactions-concurrency", "subtopic": "acid-properties", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "Which of the following statements about the ACID properties and the way a DBMS implements them are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["Atomicity is implemented by undoing, from the log, the updates of a transaction that aborts.", "Durability is obtained by forcing the log records of a committed transaction to stable storage before the commit is acknowledged.", "Isolation is obtained by a concurrency-control protocol whose effect is that concurrent execution is equivalent to some serial order.", "Consistency is guaranteed by the log manager, because replaying the log restores every integrity constraint."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Each of the first three letters of ACID has a standard mechanism: undo logging for atomicity, force-log-at-commit plus redo for durability, and a serializability-enforcing protocol (locking, timestamps, validation) for isolation. Consistency has no such mechanism of its own.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) True. An aborted transaction is rolled back with the before-images in the undo part of the log.</li><li>(B) True. A commit is acknowledged only after its log records are on stable storage; otherwise a crash immediately after commit could lose the transaction.</li><li>(C) True. Isolation is defined as equivalence to a serial order, and that is precisely what 2PL or timestamp ordering enforces.</li><li>(D) False. Replaying the log restores states, not constraints: if a transaction's logic broke a constraint, replaying it reproduces the violation. Consistency needs the constraints to be declared and checked.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(D) is the odd one out: the log manager has no knowledge of integrity constraints, and redo/undo are value-level operations.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Believing that recovery 'repairs' an inconsistent database. Recovery restores the last committed state; if that state was inconsistent, recovery faithfully restores the inconsistency.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Map each letter to its mechanism: A = undo, D = force + redo, I = serializability protocol, C = declared constraints plus correct transaction logic.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U4-Q05", "subject": "databases", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "transactions-concurrency", "subtopic": "acid-properties", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "A DBMS uses the deferred-update technique, in which a transaction's updates reach the database only after its commit record has been written. Transaction  T1  updates 6 data items and commits; its log records are on stable storage, but none of its pages has been written to the database yet. Transaction  T2  updates 3 data items and aborts before commit, and none of its updates ever reached the database. After a crash and restart, how many data items must recovery process in total (redo plus undo)? (Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "6", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Deferred update is a NO-UNDO/REDO scheme: updates are buffered and applied only after commit, so an aborted or uncommitted transaction leaves nothing in the database and needs no undo. A committed transaction whose pages were not yet written does need redo.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>T1 committed, so its effects are permanent, but its 6 pages are not yet in the database: recovery must redo all 6.</li><li>T2 aborted before commit under deferred update, so none of its 3 updates ever reached the database: there is nothing to undo, so undo contributes 0.</li><li>Total items processed = redo 6 + undo 0 = 6.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 6.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Adding 3 for T2 out of habit. Under deferred update an abort costs nothing at recovery because the update never touched the database; undo would only be needed under a steal policy or immediate update.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> NO-UNDO/REDO (deferred update) means aborts are free and only committed work is redone; UNDO/NO-REDO (immediate update, no-force) means the reverse.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U4-Q06", "subject": "databases", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "transactions-concurrency", "subtopic": "schedules-serializability-conflict-view", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "Two operations conflict when they belong to different transactions, access the same data item, and at least one of them is a write. Which one of the following pairs of operations does  NOT  conflict?", "options": ["r1(X) and w2(X)", "w1(X) and r2(X)", "w1(X) and w2(X)", "r1(X) and r2(X)"], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Conflict is defined on three conditions together: different transactions, same data item, and at least one write. Two reads satisfy the first two conditions but fail the third, so their order can never be observed.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) read-write on X from different transactions: conflicts.</li><li>(B) write-read on X: conflicts.</li><li>(C) write-write on X: conflicts.</li><li>(D) read-read on X: both operations see the same value whatever their order, so swapping them changes nothing. No conflict.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A), (B) and (C) each contain a write on the same item from two transactions, so all three are conflicting pairs.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming any two operations on the same item conflict. Read-read pairs are the single exception, and that exception is what lets the precedence graph be sparse.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Three conflict types: read-write, write-read, write-write. Read-read never conflicts.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U4-Q07", "subject": "databases", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "transactions-concurrency", "subtopic": "schedules-serializability-conflict-view", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "Which one of the following statements about conflict serializability and view serializability is TRUE?", "options": ["Every view-serializable schedule is conflict-serializable.", "Every conflict-serializable schedule is view-serializable.", "The two notions coincide for every schedule.", "A schedule whose precedence graph contains a cycle can still be conflict-serializable."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Conflict equivalence implies view equivalence, because if two schedules agree on the order of every conflicting pair they necessarily agree on who reads which version and on the final writer. The reverse implication fails, and blind writes are the standard counterexample.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Conflict equivalence compares every conflicting pair; view equivalence compares only the reads-from relation and the final write of each item.</li><li>Agreeing on more (conflicts) forces agreement on less (views), so conflict-serializable implies view-serializable.</li><li>A concrete counterexample is <code class=\"mm\">S = r1(X); w2(X); w1(X); w3(X)</code>. The conflict graph has <code class=\"mm\">T1 &rarr; T2</code> (from <code class=\"mm\">r1(X)</code> and <code class=\"mm\">w2(X)</code>) and <code class=\"mm\">T2 &rarr; T1</code> (from <code class=\"mm\">w2(X)</code> and <code class=\"mm\">w1(X)</code>), a cycle, so S is not conflict-serializable. Yet S is view-equivalent to the serial order <code class=\"mm\">T1, T2, T3</code>: there too <code class=\"mm\">T1</code> reads the initial value of <code class=\"mm\">X</code> and <code class=\"mm\">T3</code> performs the final write.</li><li>Hence (B) is true and (A) is false.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) reverses the implication; view serializability is the weaker, more permissive notion.</li><li>(C) fails because of blind-write schedules.</li><li>(D) is false by definition: an acyclic precedence graph is exactly the test for conflict serializability.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Treating the two definitions as interchangeable because both are 'serializable'. Conflict serializability is the practical notion (testable in polynomial time); view serializability is the theoretical superset (NP-complete to test).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Conflict-serializable ⊂ view-serializable, strictly. Precedence graph acyclic ⇔ conflict-serializable.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U4-Q08", "subject": "databases", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "transactions-concurrency", "subtopic": "schedules-serializability-conflict-view", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "Consider the schedule  S = r1(A); r2(A); w1(A); w2(A)  of two transactions on a single data item  A . Which one of the following is TRUE?", "options": ["S is conflict-serializable.", "S is view-serializable but not conflict-serializable.", "S is neither conflict-serializable nor view-serializable.", "S is both conflict-serializable and view-serializable."], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A schedule is conflict-serializable exactly when its precedence graph is acyclic. View serializability needs the same reads-from relation and the same final writer as some serial schedule; with no blind writes involved, a cycle in the conflict graph is fatal to both.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Conflicts: <code class=\"mm\">r2(A)</code> vs <code class=\"mm\">w1(A)</code> gives the edge <code class=\"mm\">T2 &rarr; T1</code>; <code class=\"mm\">r1(A)</code> vs <code class=\"mm\">w2(A)</code> gives <code class=\"mm\">T1 &rarr; T2</code>; <code class=\"mm\">w1(A)</code> vs <code class=\"mm\">w2(A)</code> gives <code class=\"mm\">T1 &rarr; T2</code>. The graph has a two-cycle, so S is not conflict-serializable.</li><li>Try the serial order <code class=\"mm\">T1, T2</code>: there <code class=\"mm\">T2</code> reads the value written by <code class=\"mm\">T1</code>, whereas in S both transactions read the initial value. Different reads-from relation.</li><li>Try the serial order <code class=\"mm\">T2, T1</code>: there <code class=\"mm\">T1</code> reads the value written by <code class=\"mm\">T2</code>, again different from S.</li><li>The final writer in S is <code class=\"mm\">T2</code>, which both serial orders also give, but the reads-from relation already rules out both, so S is not view-serializable either.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) and (D) fail on the two-cycle in the precedence graph.</li><li>(B) fails because no serial schedule reproduces the reads-from relation; view serializability is not rescued by the final write agreeing.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Checking only the final writer and declaring the schedule view-serializable. View equivalence requires the reads-from relation as well.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> With no blind writes, view serializability coincides with conflict serializability, so a cycle settles both at once.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U4-Q09", "subject": "databases", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "transactions-concurrency", "subtopic": "schedules-serializability-conflict-view", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "Which of the following statements about serializability are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["A schedule is conflict-serializable if and only if its precedence graph is acyclic.", "Every conflict-serializable schedule is view-serializable, and every view-serializable schedule is conflict-serializable.", "Blind writes can make a schedule view-serializable even though it is not conflict-serializable.", "Every serial schedule is conflict-serializable."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (C) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The precedence (serialization) graph is the decision procedure for conflict serializability: nodes are transactions, an edge <code class=\"mm\">Ti &rarr; Tj</code> means Ti must precede Tj, and the schedule is conflict-serializable exactly when the graph is acyclic. View serializability is strictly weaker because blind writes may be reordered harmlessly.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) True. This is the serialization-graph theorem; a topological order of an acyclic graph is the equivalent serial schedule.</li><li>(B) False. The forward implication holds, the reverse does not; view serializability is the strictly larger class.</li><li>(C) True. Example: <code class=\"mm\">w1(X) w2(X) w3(X)</code> where T1 and T2 both write blindly. The conflict graph has a cycle, yet the schedule is view-equivalent to the serial order T3, T1, T2 once the overwritten blind write of T2 is disregarded.</li><li>(D) True. A serial schedule has no conflicting operations in an interleaved order at all, so its graph has no edges and is trivially acyclic.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (C) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) is the only false statement: it asserts the two notions coincide, which blind-write schedules disprove.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming the two definitions are equivalent because every textbook example is conflict-serializable. View serializability is a strictly larger, NP-complete-to-test class.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Acyclic precedence graph is the examinable test; blind writes are the reason view serializability is larger.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U4-Q10", "subject": "databases", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "transactions-concurrency", "subtopic": "schedules-serializability-conflict-view", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "Consider the schedule  S = r1(X); w1(X); r2(X); w2(X); r3(Y); w3(Y)  of three transactions over data items  X  and  Y . How many of the 3! = 6 serial schedules of T1, T2 and T3 are conflict-equivalent to S? (Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "3", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Two schedules are conflict-equivalent when they order every conflicting pair of operations the same way. Equivalently, the set of serial schedules equivalent to S is exactly the set of topological orders of S's precedence graph.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Conflicts on <code class=\"mm\">X</code>: <code class=\"mm\">r1(X)</code> vs <code class=\"mm\">w2(X)</code>, <code class=\"mm\">w1(X)</code> vs <code class=\"mm\">r2(X)</code>, and <code class=\"mm\">w1(X)</code> vs <code class=\"mm\">w2(X)</code>. All three give the edge <code class=\"mm\">T1 &rarr; T2</code>.</li><li>Conflicts on <code class=\"mm\">Y</code>: none, because <code class=\"mm\">T3</code> touches only <code class=\"mm\">Y</code>.</li><li>The precedence graph is the single edge <code class=\"mm\">T1 &rarr; T2</code>; T3 is isolated, so it may be placed anywhere relative to the pair.</li><li>Topological orders: <code class=\"mm\">T1 T2 T3</code>, <code class=\"mm\">T1 T3 T2</code> and <code class=\"mm\">T3 T1 T2</code>. That is 3 of the 6 serial schedules.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 3.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Counting 2 by keeping T3 in its original position, or counting 6 by forgetting that T1 must precede T2. The count is the number of linear extensions of the partial order, which is 3!/(the constraints imposed) = 3 here.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Number of conflict-equivalent serial schedules = number of topological orders of the precedence graph. An isolated node multiplies the count by its number of insertion slots.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U4-Q11", "subject": "databases", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "transactions-concurrency", "subtopic": "recoverability-cascadeless", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "A schedule is recoverable if, for every pair of transactions  Ti  and  Tj , whenever  Tj  reads a data item previously written by  Ti , the commit of  Ti  appears before the commit of  Tj . Which one of the following is TRUE about a schedule in which  T1  writes  X ,  T2  then reads  X , and  T2  commits before  T1  does?", "options": ["The schedule is recoverable, because T2 read a value that T1 had already written.", "The schedule is not recoverable, because if T1 aborts, T2 has already committed on the strength of a value that never existed and cannot be undone.", "The schedule is strict but not cascadeless.", "The schedule is cascadeless but not recoverable."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Recoverability exists so that a commit is never based on data that later turns out never to have existed. If a reader commits before its writer, the writer's abort forces an undo of committed work, which is impossible; such a schedule is therefore not recoverable.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>T2 read <code class=\"mm\">X</code> written by the uncommitted T1: this is a dirty read.</li><li>T2 then committed. T1 has not committed.</li><li>If T1 now aborts, T2's committed read is invalidated, and no rollback can repair a committed transaction.</li><li>The commit order required by recoverability (writer before reader) is violated, so the schedule is not recoverable.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) misreads the definition: reading a written value is not enough, the writer must commit first.</li><li>(C) A strict schedule forbids even reading or writing an uncommitted item, so this schedule is not strict.</li><li>(D) Cascadelessness is stronger than recoverability, so 'cascadeless but not recoverable' is impossible.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Checking only that the read happened after the write. Recoverability is about the order of the two commits, not the order of the operations.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Recoverable ⇔ no reader commits before the transaction it read from. Violating it means committed data may have to be undone, which no recovery manager can do.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U4-Q12", "subject": "databases", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "transactions-concurrency", "subtopic": "recoverability-cascadeless", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "Which one of the following best describes a cascadeless (avoids-cascading-rollback) schedule?", "options": ["No transaction reads a data item written by a transaction that has not yet committed.", "No transaction overwrites a data item written by a transaction that has not yet committed.", "Every transaction acquires all of its locks before releasing any lock.", "Transactions read uncommitted data only when the writer commits immediately afterwards."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A cascadeless schedule forbids dirty reads: a read is allowed only on committed data. That single restriction means an abort can never force another transaction to roll back, because nobody depends on uncommitted values.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Cascading rollback happens when a reader of uncommitted data must itself be undone because the writer aborted.</li><li>Removing dirty reads removes the dependency, hence the cascade.</li><li>So cascadeless means: reads only of committed values, which is statement (A).</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) describes a strict schedule, which forbids both reading and writing uncommitted data. Strict implies cascadeless, not the reverse.</li><li>(C) describes two-phase locking, a protocol rather than the schedule property.</li><li>(D) is not a property of schedules at all: 'commits immediately afterwards' does not remove the window in which the reader depends on an uncommitted writer.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Swapping (A) and (B). Cascadeless restricts reads only; strict restricts reads and writes.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Recoverable ⊂ cascadeless ⊂ strict. Each step adds one restriction: commit order, then no dirty reads, then no dirty reads or writes.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U4-Q13", "subject": "databases", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "transactions-concurrency", "subtopic": "recoverability-cascadeless", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "Which one of the following implications is TRUE for all schedules?", "options": ["Every recoverable schedule is cascadeless.", "Every conflict-serializable schedule is recoverable.", "Every recoverable schedule is serializable.", "Every cascadeless schedule is recoverable."], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The three recovery classes form a chain of strict inclusions: strict ⊂ cascadeless ⊂ recoverable. Serializability is an orthogonal notion: it constrains the order of conflicting operations, not the order of commits.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Cascadeless forbids dirty reads. If nobody reads uncommitted data, then whenever Tj reads from Ti, Ti has already committed, which satisfies the recoverability condition. So cascadeless ⇒ recoverable.</li><li>The converse fails: a schedule can allow a dirty read and still order the commits correctly, so recoverable does not imply cascadeless.</li><li>Serializability says nothing about commit order, so neither implies the other; a conflict-serializable schedule with T2 committing before T1 can be non-recoverable.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) reverses the implication; recoverable is the weaker class.</li><li>(B) is false: conflict serializability constrains conflicting operations, not commits. Take T1 writing X, T2 reading X, T2 committing, then T1 aborting: the conflict order is T1 before T2, serializable, yet not recoverable.</li><li>(C) is false: a serializable interleaving may still be non-recoverable, and a recoverable schedule need not be serializable.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming serializable schedules are automatically recoverable. They are not; serializability and recoverability are independent axes.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Memorise the chain strict ⊂ cascadeless ⊂ recoverable, and keep serializability on a separate axis.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U4-Q14", "subject": "databases", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "transactions-concurrency", "subtopic": "recoverability-cascadeless", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "Which of the following statements are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["Every strict schedule is cascadeless.", "Every cascadeless schedule is strict.", "A schedule in which no transaction performs a dirty read cannot suffer cascading rollbacks.", "In a recoverable schedule, no committed transaction has read a value written by a transaction that later aborts."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (C) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Strict forbids both reading and writing uncommitted data; cascadeless forbids only reading it. The extra prohibition can only shrink the class, so strict is a subset of cascadeless. The purpose of both is to prevent one abort from forcing other transactions to roll back.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) True. Strict forbids more, so it is contained in cascadeless.</li><li>(B) False. A schedule that allows overwriting an uncommitted value but never reads one is cascadeless and not strict.</li><li>(C) True. Cascading rollback is caused exactly by dirty reads; remove them and no abort can propagate.</li><li>(D) True. That is the definition of recoverability read contrapositively: if Tj read from Ti and committed, then Ti committed first, so Ti never aborts after Tj's commit.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (C) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) asserts the reverse containment, which the overwrite-without-read example refutes.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Confusing the direction of the inclusion. Strict is the strongest condition, so it is the smallest class.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> strict ⊂ cascadeless ⊂ recoverable; the extra power of strict comes from also banning writes over uncommitted values, which is what strict 2PL provides.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U4-Q15", "subject": "databases", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "transactions-concurrency", "subtopic": "recoverability-cascadeless", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "Consider the schedule:  T1  writes  X ;  T2  reads  X  and writes  Y ;  T3  reads  Y ;  T4  reads  Z . No transaction has committed when  T1  aborts. How many transactions in total must be rolled back, counting  T1  itself? (Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "3", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Cascading rollback follows the reads-from relation transitively: every transaction that read a value produced by an aborted transaction must be undone, and so must every transaction that read a value produced by those, and so on.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">T1</code> wrote <code class=\"mm\">X</code> and aborts: <code class=\"mm\">T1</code> must be rolled back.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">T2</code> read <code class=\"mm\">X</code> from the uncommitted <code class=\"mm\">T1</code>, so <code class=\"mm\">T2</code> must be rolled back too (first-level cascade).</li><li><code class=\"mm\">T3</code> read <code class=\"mm\">Y</code>, which <code class=\"mm\">T2</code> wrote, and <code class=\"mm\">T2</code> is now being undone, so <code class=\"mm\">T3</code> must be rolled back as well (second-level cascade).</li><li><code class=\"mm\">T4</code> read only <code class=\"mm\">Z</code>, which no undone transaction wrote, so <code class=\"mm\">T4</code> is unaffected.</li><li>Total rolled back: <code class=\"mm\">T1</code>, <code class=\"mm\">T2</code>, <code class=\"mm\">T3</code> = 3.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 3.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Stopping at the first level and answering 2, or including T4 because it ran concurrently. Only the transitive closure of the reads-from relation from the aborted transaction is rolled back.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Cascading rollback = transitive closure over reads-from edges. A schedule with no dirty reads (cascadeless) has no such closure beyond the aborting transaction itself.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U4-Q16", "subject": "databases", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "transactions-concurrency", "subtopic": "two-phase-locking-2pl-strict-rigorous", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "In the two-phase locking protocol, which one of the following statements is TRUE?", "options": ["A transaction may release a lock and later acquire a new lock, provided it still holds at least one lock.", "Two-phase locking prevents deadlock.", "A transaction has a growing phase in which it only acquires locks and a shrinking phase in which it only releases locks.", "A transaction may acquire a shared lock during its shrinking phase."], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Two-phase locking splits each transaction's life into a growing (lock-acquisition) phase followed by a shrinking (lock-release) phase; the boundary is the lock point. Once the first unlock happens, no further lock may be granted to that transaction.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Phase 1, growing: the transaction acquires locks and holds all of them; the number of locks held is non-decreasing.</li><li>Phase 2, shrinking: the transaction releases locks and acquires none; the number of locks held is non-increasing.</li><li>The instant at which the transaction holds its maximum number of locks is the lock point, and it separates the two phases.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) contradicts the protocol: any acquisition after the first release destroys the two-phase structure and the serializability guarantee.</li><li>(B) is false; 2PL can deadlock because two transactions may each hold a lock the other needs.</li><li>(D) is false for the same reason as (A): no lock of any kind may be acquired in the shrinking phase.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Believing 2PL removes deadlock. It guarantees conflict serializability and nothing about deadlock; deadlock avoidance needs ordering, wait-die or wound-wait.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> 2PL gives serializability, not deadlock-freedom; the lock point is the moment of maximum held locks.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U4-Q17", "subject": "databases", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "transactions-concurrency", "subtopic": "two-phase-locking-2pl-strict-rigorous", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "Which one of the following is guaranteed by the  basic  two-phase locking protocol?", "options": ["Freedom from deadlock.", "Recoverability of the schedule.", "Freedom from starvation.", "Conflict serializability of the schedule."], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Basic 2PL guarantees exactly one thing: every schedule it produces is conflict-serializable. It does not order commits, so it does not give recoverability; it does not order lock requests, so it can deadlock; it does not prevent repeated restarts, so it can starve a transaction.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Under 2PL the lock point of each transaction defines a total order that every conflicting pair respects.</li><li>Conflicting operations are therefore ordered as they would be in the serial order of the lock points.</li><li>That is precisely conflict serializability, and it is the whole of what basic 2PL promises.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) 2PL can deadlock; classic example: T1 locks X then requests Y while T2 locks Y then requests X.</li><li>(B) basic 2PL allows a transaction to release a lock before commit, so another transaction may read uncommitted data and commit first: not recoverable.</li><li>(C) a transaction may be repeatedly chosen as victim and never finish.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Crediting 2PL with more than it delivers. Strict 2PL adds cascadelessness and recoverability; rigorous 2PL adds more; neither adds deadlock-freedom.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Basic 2PL = conflict serializability only. Strict 2PL = + cascadeless/recoverable. Rigorous 2PL = + serial order by commit time.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U4-Q18", "subject": "databases", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "transactions-concurrency", "subtopic": "two-phase-locking-2pl-strict-rigorous", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "A transaction performs the operations  LS(X), LX(Y), U(X), LS(Z), U(Y), U(Z) , where  LS  is a shared lock,  LX  an exclusive lock and  U  an unlock. Which one of the following is TRUE?", "options": ["The sequence obeys basic two-phase locking.", "The sequence violates two-phase locking, because a lock is acquired after a lock has been released.", "The sequence obeys basic two-phase locking but not strict two-phase locking.", "The sequence violates two-phase locking only because it holds two locks at the same time."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The test for 2PL is purely structural: scan the operation sequence and check that no lock acquisition follows any release. Holding several locks simultaneously is not a violation; it is what the growing phase looks like.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">T1</code> acquires <code class=\"mm\">X</code> and <code class=\"mm\">Y</code>, then releases <code class=\"mm\">X</code> at the third operation: the shrinking phase has begun.</li><li>The fourth operation acquires <code class=\"mm\">Z</code>, a lock taken after a release, which is exactly what 2PL forbids.</li><li>Hence the sequence is not two-phase, and therefore is neither strict nor rigorous two-phase.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) and (C) claim the sequence obeys basic 2PL, which the lock-after-unlock step contradicts.</li><li>(D) Holding several locks at once is legal and expected during the growing phase; the violation is the re-acquisition.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Looking only at how many locks are held. 2PL is about the shape of the sequence (all acquisitions, then all releases), not the number held at any instant.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> To test 2PL, find the first unlock and check that no lock request follows it.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U4-Q19", "subject": "databases", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "transactions-concurrency", "subtopic": "two-phase-locking-2pl-strict-rigorous", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "Which of the following statements about two-phase locking are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["Basic two-phase locking produces only conflict-serializable schedules.", "Strict two-phase locking holds every exclusive lock until the transaction commits or aborts.", "Rigorous two-phase locking holds every lock, shared as well as exclusive, until the transaction commits or aborts.", "Two-phase locking guarantees that no deadlock can occur."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The three variants differ only in when locks are released. Basic 2PL releases anywhere after the lock point; strict 2PL keeps all exclusive locks to the end; rigorous 2PL keeps all locks to the end. The later the release, the stronger the recovery guarantee, and none of them addresses deadlock.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) True. That is the serialization theorem for 2PL.</li><li>(B) True. Holding exclusive locks to commit is what makes strict 2PL cascadeless: no other transaction can read or overwrite uncommitted data.</li><li>(C) True. Rigorous 2PL extends the rule to shared locks, which makes the serial order agree with the commit order.</li><li>(D) False. 2PL is deadlock-prone by construction, since locks are held while further locks are requested.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(D) is the only false statement: deadlock is prevented by lock ordering, wait-die, wound-wait or timestamp ordering, not by 2PL.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming the strongest variant of 2PL also removes deadlock. Stronger release rules strengthen recoverability only.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> basic ⊂ strict ⊂ rigorous in terms of what they guarantee, and deadlock-freedom is in none of them.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U4-Q20", "subject": "databases", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "transactions-concurrency", "subtopic": "two-phase-locking-2pl-strict-rigorous", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "Three transactions are shown with the full sequence of lock and unlock operations each performs on data items  X  and  Y , where  LS  is a shared lock,  LX  an exclusive lock and  U  an unlock.  T1: LS(X), U(X), LX(X), U(X)   T2: LX(X), U(X)   T3: LX(X), LX(Y), U(X), U(Y)  How many of the three transactions obey two-phase locking? (Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "2", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A transaction obeys 2PL when, scanning its operation sequence from left to right, no lock acquisition occurs after the first release. A transaction that locks once and unlocks once, or that locks everything and then releases everything, is in two-phase form.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">T1</code>: acquires <code class=\"mm\">X</code>, releases <code class=\"mm\">X</code>, then acquires <code class=\"mm\">X</code> again. A lock is requested after a release, so the growing phase is restarted: violates 2PL.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">T2</code>: acquires <code class=\"mm\">X</code> and releases it. There is a growing phase (one lock) and a shrinking phase (one unlock) with no re-acquisition: obeys 2PL.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">T3</code>: acquires <code class=\"mm\">X</code> and <code class=\"mm\">Y</code>, then releases <code class=\"mm\">X</code> and <code class=\"mm\">Y</code>. All acquisitions precede all releases: obeys 2PL.</li><li>Two of the three transactions are in two-phase form, so the answer is 2.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 2.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Judging T3 by how many locks it holds, or judging T1 by its last operation only. The test is positional: the first release closes the growing phase for good.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> 2PL is checked by the position of the first unlock relative to every later lock request.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U4-Q21", "subject": "databases", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "transactions-concurrency", "subtopic": "timestamp-ordering-and-deadlocks", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "In basic timestamp ordering, the read operation  r(X)  of a transaction  T  is rejected when", "options": ["TS(T) < RTS(X).", "TS(T) > WTS(X).", "TS(T) < WTS(X).", "T holds no lock on X."], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Timestamp ordering keeps, for every data item, the largest timestamp of any transaction that has read it (RTS) and of any that has written it (WTS). A read by T must behave as though it happened at time TS(T): if a younger transaction has already written X, the read cannot be placed before that write, so T is aborted and restarted.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>For a read <code class=\"mm\">r(X)</code>, the rule compares <code class=\"mm\">TS(T)</code> with <code class=\"mm\">WTS(X)</code>.</li><li>If <code class=\"mm\">TS(T) &lt; WTS(X)</code>, some younger transaction has already written <code class=\"mm\">X</code>, so a read at time <code class=\"mm\">TS(T)</code> would have to see that write; the schedule cannot be rearranged to allow it, so T is rejected.</li><li>Otherwise the read proceeds and <code class=\"mm\">RTS(X)</code> is updated to the maximum of its old value and <code class=\"mm\">TS(T)</code>.</li><li>Hence the rejecting condition is <code class=\"mm\">TS(T) &lt; WTS(X)</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) <code class=\"mm\">TS(T) &lt; RTS(X)</code> is not a rejection condition: a reader older than the last reader is always allowed.</li><li>(B) a read older than nothing in particular is irrelevant; the comparison that matters is against the last write.</li><li>(D) timestamp ordering uses no locks at all; that is precisely why it cannot deadlock.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Mixing up the read rule with the write rule. Read compares against WTS; write compares against both WTS and RTS (and Thomas's rule modifies the write case only).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Read rule: reject if TS(T) < WTS(X). Write rule: reject if TS(T) < WTS(X) or TS(T) < RTS(X), with Thomas's rule replacing the first rejection by ignoring the write.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U4-Q22", "subject": "databases", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "transactions-concurrency", "subtopic": "timestamp-ordering-and-deadlocks", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "Under basic timestamp ordering, a write  w(X)  by  T  with  TS(T) < WTS(X)  is rejected. Under  Thomas's write rule  the same operation is", "options": ["always rejected.", "ignored when RTS(X) ≤ TS(T), because the value it would write is already obsolete.", "executed after waiting for the younger writer to commit.", "ignored whenever TS(T) < WTS(X), whatever RTS(X) is."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Thomas's write rule observes that a write which is older than the current value of the item and which nobody has read in the meantime can never be observed: the value it produces is overwritten by the younger write before any read can see it. Discarding such a write keeps the schedule view-equivalent to a serial one and avoids an unnecessary abort.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Basic rule: if <code class=\"mm\">TS(T) &lt; WTS(X)</code>, a younger transaction has already written <code class=\"mm\">X</code>, so T is aborted.</li><li>Thomas's refinement adds a second condition: if additionally <code class=\"mm\">RTS(X) &le; TS(T)</code>, no transaction has read the younger version, so T's write would be invisible anyway.</li><li>In that case the write is simply skipped (an obsolete write) and T continues; only when <code class=\"mm\">RTS(X) &gt; TS(T)</code> must T be aborted, because a reader has already seen a version that T's write would have to precede.</li><li>Hence Thomas's rule ignores the write when <code class=\"mm\">RTS(X) &le; TS(T)</code>, which is option (B).</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) is the basic rule, which Thomas's rule relaxes.</li><li>(C) timestamp ordering never waits, so no operation is ever delayed for a commit.</li><li>(D) drops the RTS condition, which would discard a write that a reader has already observed.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Applying Thomas's rule to reads as well. It refines the write rule only; reads are still rejected whenever TS(T) < WTS(X).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Thomas's write rule = ignore obsolete writes. It makes timestamp ordering tolerate some out-of-order writes without aborting, and it is the reason the resulting schedules are view-serializable rather than conflict-serializable.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U4-Q23", "subject": "databases", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "transactions-concurrency", "subtopic": "timestamp-ordering-and-deadlocks", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "Which one of the following statements about deadlock detection is TRUE?", "options": ["A cycle in the wait-for graph means that a deadlock exists, and the cycle may involve any number of transactions.", "A cycle in the wait-for graph is necessary but not sufficient for a deadlock.", "Timestamp ordering can deadlock, so it needs a wait-for graph.", "Wait-die and wound-wait can deadlock."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The wait-for graph has one node per active transaction and an edge <code class=\"mm\">Ti &rarr; Tj</code> when Ti is waiting for a lock held by Tj. A cycle is both necessary and sufficient for deadlock, because each transaction in the cycle waits for the next and none can proceed.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>A transaction blocked on a lock is waiting for the holder to release it.</li><li>If the wait-for edges form a cycle, every member of the cycle waits for another member, so none can ever proceed: deadlock.</li><li>If there is no cycle, the waits form a partial order and the transaction at the end of the longest chain will eventually finish.</li><li>The cycle may be of length 2 (the common case) or longer; nothing limits it to two transactions.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) reverses necessity and sufficiency: a cycle is exactly the criterion, not merely a symptom.</li><li>(C) timestamp ordering never makes a transaction wait, so it cannot deadlock and needs no wait-for graph.</li><li>(D) wait-die and wound-wait are deadlock-prevention schemes based on timestamps; one of the two transactions is always aborted rather than allowed to wait cyclically.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Thinking only two-transaction deadlocks matter. Cycles of any length occur, and a cycle of length 3 is common with three-way lock orders.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Cycle in wait-for graph ⇔ deadlock. Detection means finding a cycle; resolution means aborting a victim in it.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U4-Q24", "subject": "databases", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "transactions-concurrency", "subtopic": "timestamp-ordering-and-deadlocks", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "Which of the following statements are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["Basic timestamp ordering is free of deadlock because no transaction ever waits for another.", "Thomas's write rule ignores an obsolete write instead of aborting the writing transaction.", "In wait-die, an older transaction waits for a younger one, while a younger transaction that requests an item held by an older one is aborted.", "In wound-wait, an older transaction that requests an item held by a younger one is aborted."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Timestamp-based protocols avoid deadlock by never blocking: a transaction either proceeds, has its operation skipped, or is aborted and restarted. The two prevention schemes, wait-die and wound-wait, decide by age who waits and who dies, always in the same direction, which is what rules out cycles.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) True. Timestamp ordering decides every operation immediately against RTS and WTS; a transaction is aborted rather than queued, so no wait-for edge ever exists.</li><li>(B) True. That is the definition of Thomas's write rule.</li><li>(C) True. Wait-die: the older transaction is allowed to wait for the younger; the younger is aborted (dies) if it asks for an item held by an older one.</li><li>(D) False. Wound-wait is the mirror image: the older transaction wounds (aborts) the younger holder, and the younger waits for the older.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(D) reverses wound-wait: in wound-wait the older preempts the younger, so an older requester is never the one aborted.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Swapping the two schemes. Mnemonic: wait-die is 'old waits, young dies'; wound-wait is 'old wounds, young waits'.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Both schemes are deadlock-free because waits always go in one direction of age; both can starve, which restarts with the original timestamp are meant to bound.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U4-Q25", "subject": "databases", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "transactions-concurrency", "subtopic": "timestamp-ordering-and-deadlocks", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "Transactions  T1 ,  T2  and  T3  start with timestamps 10, 20 and 30 respectively. Basic timestamp ordering (not Thomas's write rule) is used, and the operations below are executed in the order shown:  1. r1(X) 2. w3(X) 3. r2(X) 4. w2(Y) 5. r3(Y) 6. w1(Y) . How many transactions are aborted during this execution? (Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "2", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Every operation is checked against the read and write timestamps of its item. A read is rejected when TS(T) < WTS(X); a write is rejected when TS(T) < WTS(X) or TS(T) < RTS(X). A rejected operation aborts its transaction, whose later operations are then not executed.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">1. r1(X)</code>: <code class=\"mm\">WTS(X)</code> is empty, so the read is granted and <code class=\"mm\">RTS(X) = 10</code>.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">2. w3(X)</code>: <code class=\"mm\">TS(T3) = 30 &ge; WTS(X)</code> and <code class=\"mm\">TS(T3) = 30 &ge; RTS(X) = 10</code>, so the write is granted and <code class=\"mm\">WTS(X) = 30</code>.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">3. r2(X)</code>: <code class=\"mm\">TS(T2) = 20 &lt; WTS(X) = 30</code>, so the read is rejected and <code class=\"mm\">T2</code> is aborted. That is 1 abort.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">4. w2(Y)</code>: <code class=\"mm\">T2</code> has already been aborted, so this operation is not executed.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">5. r3(Y)</code>: <code class=\"mm\">TS(T3) = 30 &ge; WTS(Y) = 0</code>, so the read is granted and <code class=\"mm\">RTS(Y) = 30</code>.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">6. w1(Y)</code>: <code class=\"mm\">TS(T1) = 10 &ge; WTS(Y) = 0</code> but <code class=\"mm\">TS(T1) = 10 &lt; RTS(Y) = 30</code>, so the write is rejected and <code class=\"mm\">T1</code> is aborted. That is 2 aborts.</li><li>Total aborts = 2, one at step 3 and one at step 6.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 2.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Checking only WTS and forgetting the read timestamp, which is what rejects the write at step 6; or executing step 4 for an already aborted T2.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Simulate timestamp ordering with a table of RTS and WTS per item and check every operation against both timestamps; a rejected operation removes the transaction from the rest of the schedule.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U4-Q26", "subject": "databases", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "transactions-concurrency", "subtopic": "acid-properties", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-31", "text": "A transaction debits ₹2,000 from account A and credits ₹2,000 to account B. The debit reaches the database, the credit does not, and the system then crashes. Recovery undoes the debit. Which ACID property is the one that demands this undo?", "options": ["Consistency: the invariant that the sum of the two balances is preserved.", "Atomicity: every update of the transaction is applied, or none of them is.", "Isolation: no other transaction observes the intermediate state.", "Durability: an update of a committed transaction survives a crash."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Atomicity makes a transaction an all-or-nothing unit. A transaction that has applied half of its updates must be rolled back to the state it started from, and the mechanism that does that rollback is what atomicity requires.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The transaction has two updates that must travel together: debit <code class=\"mm\">A</code>, credit <code class=\"mm\">B</code>.</li><li>The debit reached the database and the credit did not, so the transaction is only partly applied.</li><li>Recovery finds no commit record for the transaction in the log and undoes the debit, restoring the before-image.</li><li>The requirement that a partly applied transaction be removed completely, rather than left half done, is exactly atomicity.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) &mdash; atomicity demands the undo.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) Consistency is about the transaction taking the database from one valid state to another; it is the property the application programmer preserves by writing a correct transaction, not the mechanism that undoes a half-finished one.</li><li>(C) Isolation is about what concurrent transactions may observe, and no second transaction appears in this example.</li><li>(D) Durability protects a <em>committed</em> transaction; this one never committed.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering \"consistency\" because the sum of the balances changed. The sum changed only because the transaction was left half applied, and the undo that fixes it is atomicity&#8217;s job.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Half-applied and then removed is atomicity; half-applied and left there would be the failure atomicity exists to prevent.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U4-Q27", "subject": "databases", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "transactions-concurrency", "subtopic": "acid-properties", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-31", "text": "Which one of the following is the mechanism through which a DBMS guarantees the durability of a committed transaction?", "options": ["Forcing the transaction&#8217;s log records to stable storage before the commit is acknowledged, so that redo can reapply the updates after a crash.", "Keeping the transaction&#8217;s updated pages in the buffer pool until the next checkpoint.", "Holding every lock of the transaction until the transaction terminates.", "Taking a full backup of the database after every commit."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Durability says that once the DBMS acknowledges a commit, the transaction&#8217;s effects survive a crash. The acknowledgement is therefore tied to the log reaching stable storage, not to the data pages reaching it: a redo record on disk is enough, because recovery can replay it.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>At commit the DBMS appends a commit record to the log.</li><li>It forces the log, including that commit record, to stable storage before it answers the client.</li><li>If a crash follows, the data pages may be stale or missing entirely, but the log holds every after-image of the committed transaction.</li><li>Recovery replays those after-images during redo, so the commit survives.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A) &mdash; force the log at commit, redo the pages later.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) Pages in the buffer pool vanish with the buffer pool; buffering is what makes redo necessary.</li><li>(C) Holding locks is isolation, not durability, and locks are meaningless after a crash.</li><li>(D) A backup taken after each commit is neither required nor how any real system works.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Believing the data page must be on disk at commit. It need not be; the log record must be.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Durability is a promise about the log at commit time, not about the data file.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U4-Q28", "subject": "databases", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "transactions-concurrency", "subtopic": "acid-properties", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-31", "text": "A buffer manager follows the steal and force policy: an updated page may be written to disk before its transaction commits, and all of a transaction’s pages are forced to disk before its commit is acknowledged. Which one of the following is TRUE about the recovery work this policy can require?", "options": ["Recovery may need both undo and redo.", "Recovery may need redo, but never undo.", "Recovery needs neither undo nor redo.", "Recovery may need undo, but never redo."], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The two buffer-policy questions decide recovery independently. <em>Steal</em> asks whether an uncommitted transaction&#8217;s page can reach disk, and if it can, that page must be undone when the transaction aborts. <em>Force</em> asks whether a committed transaction&#8217;s pages are on disk at commit, and if they are, nothing has to be redone.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Steal is allowed, so a page holding an uncommitted update can be on disk when the system crashes.</li><li>That page must be rolled back to its before-image, so undo is required.</li><li>Force is required, so every page of a committed transaction was already written before the commit was acknowledged.</li><li>A committed transaction therefore leaves no work for redo, so redo is never required.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (D) &mdash; undo yes, redo no.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) Redo would be needed only if a committed transaction could leave stale pages on disk, which force rules out.</li><li>(B) This is the answer for the opposite policy, no-steal plus no-force.</li><li>(C) Steal alone is enough to demand undo.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Treating steal and force as one setting. They are two independent decisions: steal decides undo, force decides redo.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> steal &rArr; undo needed; no-force &rArr; redo needed. Here steal and force together give undo without redo.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U4-Q29", "subject": "databases", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "transactions-concurrency", "subtopic": "acid-properties", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-31", "text": "Which of the following statements about the ACID properties and the way a DBMS implements them are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["Write-ahead logging &mdash; the log record for an update reaches stable storage before the data page it describes &mdash; is what makes undo possible after a crash.", "A transaction whose commit record is on stable storage can still be lost after a crash, because its updated pages may have been only in the buffer pool.", "Isolation is normally enforced by a concurrency-control protocol such as two-phase locking or timestamp ordering, rather than by running transactions one at a time.", "Durability requires the effects of a committed transaction to survive a failure that wipes out the contents of main memory."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (C) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Each ACID letter maps to a mechanism: atomicity and durability to the log, isolation to a concurrency-control protocol, consistency to the programmer&#8217;s invariants.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) is true. Undo restores a before-image, and that before-image is only available if the log record reached stable storage first. That ordering is the write-ahead rule.</li><li>(B) is false. If the commit record is durable, redo replays the after-images and the transaction survives; the missing data pages are exactly what redo is for.</li><li>(C) is true. Serial execution is the definition of the ideal, not the implementation; 2PL, timestamp ordering and validation are how real systems approximate it.</li><li>(D) is true. That is the definition of durability: the loss of volatile memory must not lose a committed transaction.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (C) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reading (B) as true because the pages are missing. Missing pages are recoverable from the log; only a missing commit record is fatal.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Log first, then pages &mdash; and a durable commit record is enough on its own.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U4-Q30", "subject": "databases", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "transactions-concurrency", "subtopic": "acid-properties", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-31", "text": "A system uses the deferred-update technique, so no update reaches the database until its transaction has committed. The log on stable storage reads, in order: ⟨T1 start⟩, ⟨T1 write A⟩, ⟨T1 commit⟩, ⟨T2 start⟩, ⟨T2 write B⟩, ⟨T3 start⟩, ⟨T3 write C⟩, ⟨T3 commit⟩, and then the machine crashes. Under deferred-update (redo-only) recovery, how many transactions are redone?(Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "2", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Under deferred update no uncommitted update ever reaches the database, so nothing needs undoing; recovery is redo-only, and only transactions with a commit record in the log are redone.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Scan the log and list the transactions that carry a commit record: <code class=\"mm\">T1</code> and <code class=\"mm\">T3</code>.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">T2</code> has a start record and a write record but no commit record, so it never committed and its write never reached the database. It is simply ignored.</li><li>Redo replays the after-images of <code class=\"mm\">T1</code> and <code class=\"mm\">T3</code>.</li><li>Number of transactions redone = 2.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 2</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Counting <code class=\"mm\">T2</code> because it appears in the log, or counting data items instead of transactions. The question asks for transactions redone, and an uncommitted transaction contributes none.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Redo-only recovery redoes exactly the transactions with a commit record.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U4-Q31", "subject": "databases", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "transactions-concurrency", "subtopic": "schedules-serializability-conflict-view", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-31", "text": "Two operations conflict when they belong to different transactions, access the same data item, and at least one of them is a write. Which one of the following pairs of operations does NOT conflict?", "options": ["r1(X) and w2(X)", "w1(X) and w2(X)", "r1(X) and r2(X)", "w1(X) and r2(X)"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A conflict needs three things at once: different transactions, the same data item, and at least one write. Two reads on the same item fail the third condition, because neither operation can change what the other sees.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) <code class=\"mm\">r1(X)</code> and <code class=\"mm\">w2(X)</code>: same item, different transactions, one write. Conflict (read&ndash;write).</li><li>(B) <code class=\"mm\">w1(X)</code> and <code class=\"mm\">w2(X)</code>: two writes on the same item. Conflict (write&ndash;write).</li><li>(C) <code class=\"mm\">r1(X)</code> and <code class=\"mm\">r2(X)</code>: two reads on the same item. Neither changes the value, so their order cannot matter. No conflict.</li><li>(D) <code class=\"mm\">w1(X)</code> and <code class=\"mm\">r2(X)</code>: one write. Conflict (write&ndash;read).</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C) &mdash; two reads never conflict.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b> Each of (A), (B) and (D) contains a write on <code class=\"mm\">X</code> by a different transaction, so swapping the two operations could change a value that someone reads.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Believing that any two operations on the same item conflict. Read&ndash;read is the one combination that is always harmless.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Conflicts come in three shapes: R&ndash;W, W&ndash;R and W&ndash;W. R&ndash;R is not one of them.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U4-Q32", "subject": "databases", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "transactions-concurrency", "subtopic": "schedules-serializability-conflict-view", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-31", "text": "Consider the schedule S = r1(X); w2(X); r3(X); w1(Y); w3(Y) of three transactions over data items X and Y. Which one of the following is TRUE?", "options": ["S is conflict serializable, and it is conflict equivalent to the serial order T1, T2, T3.", "S is conflict serializable, and it is conflict equivalent to the serial order T2, T1, T3.", "S is not conflict serializable, because the precedence graph contains the cycle T1 &rarr; T2 &rarr; T1.", "S is conflict serializable, and it is conflict equivalent to the serial order T1, T3, T2."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Draw the precedence graph: an edge <code class=\"mm\">Ti &rarr; Tj</code> for every pair of conflicting operations in which <code class=\"mm\">Ti</code> comes first. The schedule is conflict serializable exactly when that graph is acyclic, and any topological order of it is an equivalent serial order.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">r1(X)</code> before <code class=\"mm\">w2(X)</code>: edge T1 &rarr; T2.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">w2(X)</code> before <code class=\"mm\">r3(X)</code>: edge T2 &rarr; T3.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">w1(Y)</code> before <code class=\"mm\">w3(Y)</code>: edge T1 &rarr; T3.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">r1(X)</code> and <code class=\"mm\">r3(X)</code> are both reads, so they add no edge; the two writes are on different items, so they add none either.</li><li>The graph is T1 &rarr; T2 &rarr; T3 with the shortcut T1 &rarr; T3. It is acyclic, and the only topological order is T1, T2, T3.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A) &mdash; acyclic, equivalent to T1, T2, T3.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) The edge T1 &rarr; T2 forbids putting T2 first.</li><li>(C) There is no edge T2 &rarr; T1 anywhere: the only operations T2 and T1 share are on <code class=\"mm\">X</code>, and there <code class=\"mm\">r1(X)</code> precedes <code class=\"mm\">w2(X)</code>.</li><li>(D) The edge T2 &rarr; T3 forbids putting T3 before T2.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Counting <code class=\"mm\">r1(X)</code> and <code class=\"mm\">r3(X)</code> as a conflict and inventing an edge T1 &rarr; T3 (or T3 &rarr; T1) that the schedule does not justify.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Read the graph off the conflicting pairs only, then topologically sort it.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U4-Q33", "subject": "databases", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "transactions-concurrency", "subtopic": "schedules-serializability-conflict-view", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-31", "text": "Which of the following statements about view serializability are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["Every view-serializable schedule is conflict serializable.", "Every conflict-serializable schedule is view serializable.", "Deciding whether a given schedule is view serializable is NP-complete.", "A schedule that contains a blind write can be view serializable without being conflict serializable."], "answer_display": "Options (B), (C) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> View equivalence is weaker than conflict equivalence. Two schedules are view equivalent if they have the same reads-from relation and the same final write on every item; they are conflict equivalent only if one can be turned into the other by swapping adjacent non-conflicting operations.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) is false. The inclusion runs the other way: conflict serializability implies view serializability, not the reverse.</li><li>(B) is true. If a schedule is conflict equivalent to a serial schedule, it reads the same values from the same transactions and has the same final writes, so it is also view equivalent to it.</li><li>(C) is true. View serializability has no polynomial test; the decision problem is NP-complete, which is why practical schedulers settle for conflict serializability.</li><li>(D) is true. Blind writes are the extra freedom view equivalence allows: the writer does not read, so its position relative to other writes can matter less than conflict equivalence assumes.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (B), (C) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming the two notions coincide because they coincide on schedules without blind writes. That is exactly the case where the difference disappears.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Conflict serializable &sub; view serializable &sub; serializable, and only the first has an efficient test.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U4-Q34", "subject": "databases", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "transactions-concurrency", "subtopic": "schedules-serializability-conflict-view", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-31", "text": "Consider the schedule S = r1(X); w2(X); w1(X); w3(X) of three transactions over the single data item X, where every write stores a distinct value. Which one of the following is TRUE?", "options": ["S is conflict serializable but not view serializable.", "S is view serializable but not conflict serializable.", "S is both conflict serializable and view serializable.", "S is neither conflict serializable nor view serializable."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Test the two notions separately. The precedence graph settles conflict serializability. For view serializability, compare the reads-from relation and the final write with those of a serial schedule.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Conflicts on <code class=\"mm\">X</code>: <code class=\"mm\">r1(X)</code> before <code class=\"mm\">w2(X)</code> gives T1 &rarr; T2.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">w2(X)</code> before <code class=\"mm\">w1(X)</code> gives T2 &rarr; T1. The graph already contains the cycle T1 &rarr; T2 &rarr; T1, so S is not conflict serializable.</li><li>Now look at what S actually does: <code class=\"mm\">T1</code> reads the initial value of <code class=\"mm\">X</code> (no write precedes <code class=\"mm\">r1(X)</code>), and the last write is <code class=\"mm\">w3(X)</code>.</li><li>The serial schedule T1, T2, T3 gives exactly the same two facts: <code class=\"mm\">T1</code> reads the initial value, and <code class=\"mm\">T3</code> writes last. <code class=\"mm\">T2</code> and <code class=\"mm\">T3</code> are blind writers, so nothing they read can differ.</li><li>Hence S is view equivalent to the serial order T1, T2, T3, and is view serializable.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) &mdash; view serializable, not conflict serializable.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) and (C) fail because the cycle T1 &rarr; T2 &rarr; T1 is real: two writes to <code class=\"mm\">X</code> by different transactions are in the order <code class=\"mm\">w2</code> then <code class=\"mm\">w1</code>, which is the reverse of the order <code class=\"mm\">r1</code> then <code class=\"mm\">w2</code>.</li><li>(D) fails because of step 4: the serial order T1, T2, T3 reproduces every read and the final write.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Stopping at the precedence graph. A cycle rules out conflict serializability, not serializability itself; a blind write is precisely the case where the extra freedom matters.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Blind writes are the reason view serializability is strictly weaker than conflict serializability.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U4-Q35", "subject": "databases", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "transactions-concurrency", "subtopic": "schedules-serializability-conflict-view", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-31", "text": "Consider the schedule S = r1(X); w2(X); r2(Y); w3(Y); w1(Y) of three transactions over data items X and Y. How many edges does the precedence (serialization) graph of S contain?(Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "4", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The precedence graph has one edge <code class=\"mm\">Ti &rarr; Tj</code> for each pair of conflicting operations in which <code class=\"mm\">Ti</code> executes first. Several conflicting pairs between the same two transactions still give a single edge.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>On <code class=\"mm\">X</code>: <code class=\"mm\">r1(X)</code> precedes <code class=\"mm\">w2(X)</code>, so edge T1 &rarr; T2.</li><li>On <code class=\"mm\">Y</code>: <code class=\"mm\">r2(Y)</code> precedes <code class=\"mm\">w3(Y)</code>, so edge T2 &rarr; T3.</li><li>On <code class=\"mm\">Y</code>: <code class=\"mm\">r2(Y)</code> precedes <code class=\"mm\">w1(Y)</code>, so edge T2 &rarr; T1.</li><li>On <code class=\"mm\">Y</code>: <code class=\"mm\">w3(Y)</code> precedes <code class=\"mm\">w1(Y)</code>, so edge T3 &rarr; T1.</li><li>Distinct edges: T1 &rarr; T2, T2 &rarr; T3, T2 &rarr; T1 and T3 &rarr; T1, that is 4.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 4</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Counting conflicting operation pairs instead of distinct edges, which double-counts the T2 &rarr; T1 pair, or forgetting the write&ndash;write pair <code class=\"mm\">w3(Y), w1(Y)</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> List the conflicting pairs, then collapse them into distinct transaction edges. (The graph has the cycle T1 &rarr; T2 &rarr; T1, so <code class=\"mm\">S</code> is not conflict serializable &mdash; but the edge count is what was asked.)</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U4-Q36", "subject": "databases", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "transactions-concurrency", "subtopic": "recoverability-cascadeless", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-31", "text": "A schedule is NOT recoverable when there are transactions Ti and Tj such that Tj reads a data item written by Ti, and", "options": ["Ti commits before Tj commits.", "Tj aborts after Ti has committed.", "both transactions read the item before either of them writes it.", "Tj commits before Ti commits."], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Recoverability is about being able to undo a transaction that others have read from. If the reader commits first and the writer then aborts, the reader&#8217;s commit rests on a value that never existed, and there is no way to take it back.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">Tj</code> reads a value written by <code class=\"mm\">Ti</code>, so <code class=\"mm\">Tj</code> depends on <code class=\"mm\">Ti</code>.</li><li>A recoverable schedule must delay <code class=\"mm\">Tj</code>&#8217;s commit until <code class=\"mm\">Ti</code> has committed.</li><li>If <code class=\"mm\">Tj</code> commits first and <code class=\"mm\">Ti</code> then aborts, <code class=\"mm\">Tj</code> has already published a value derived from a transaction that was rolled back.</li><li>That is exactly the definition of a non-recoverable schedule.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (D) &mdash; the reader commits before the writer.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) is the recoverable case: the writer finishes first, so a rollback can still cascade to the reader.</li><li>(B) is harmless: an aborted reader leaves nothing behind.</li><li>(C) describes a read&ndash;read pair, which is not a dependency at all.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Confusing recoverability with \"no dirty reads\". A schedule can have dirty reads and still be recoverable, as long as the readers wait for the writer to finish.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Reader commits first, writer aborts later &rArr; non-recoverable.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U4-Q37", "subject": "databases", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "transactions-concurrency", "subtopic": "recoverability-cascadeless", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-31", "text": "Which one of the following statements about a strict schedule is TRUE?", "options": ["Every strict schedule is cascadeless, and therefore recoverable as well.", "A strict schedule can still be non-recoverable when two transactions interleave their writes.", "Strictness is a property of the locking protocol alone; no schedule can be strict unless locks are used.", "A strict schedule can never contain a blind write."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A strict schedule forbids a transaction from reading <em>or writing</em> an item until the last transaction that wrote it has committed or aborted. Cascadelessness forbids only the read part. So strict is the stronger condition, and the implications run one way.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Strict forbids both dirty reads and dirty writes.</li><li>Cascadeless forbids only dirty reads, so every strict schedule is cascadeless.</li><li>Cascadeless forbids every dependency on an uncommitted value, so the commit of a reader can never precede the commit of its writer; every cascadeless schedule is recoverable.</li><li>Chaining the two gives: strict &rArr; cascadeless &rArr; recoverable.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) Strictness is defined on the schedule, and it already rules out the dependency that non-recoverability needs.</li><li>(C) Locks are one way to enforce strictness, not the definition of it; the definition talks only about when reads and writes may occur.</li><li>(D) A blind write is a write without a read; it is perfectly compatible with strictness, which constrains the <em>order</em> of writes, not their presence.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Treating strict and cascadeless as synonyms, or believing the implication runs both ways.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> strict &sub; cascadeless &sub; recoverable &sub; all schedules.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U4-Q38", "subject": "databases", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "transactions-concurrency", "subtopic": "recoverability-cascadeless", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-31", "text": "Consider the schedule S = r1(X); w1(X); r2(X); w2(Y); c2; a1, in which T1 finally aborts. Which one of the following is TRUE?", "options": ["S is recoverable, because rolling back T1 leaves T2&#8217;s commit valid.", "S is cascadeless, because T2 wrote only Y, which T1 never touched.", "S is not recoverable, because T2 read X from T1 and committed before T1 terminated.", "S is strict, because T1&#8217;s abort has no effect on the value of Y."], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Check the dependency first, then the commit order. <code class=\"mm\">T2</code> read a value written by <code class=\"mm\">T1</code>, so <code class=\"mm\">T2</code> must not commit until <code class=\"mm\">T1</code> has committed. A schedule that breaks that rule cannot be repaired by any later action.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">r2(X)</code> follows <code class=\"mm\">w1(X)</code>, so <code class=\"mm\">T2</code> reads from <code class=\"mm\">T1</code> &mdash; a dirty read, since <code class=\"mm\">T1</code> has not committed.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">c2</code> comes before <code class=\"mm\">a1</code>, so <code class=\"mm\">T2</code> commits while <code class=\"mm\">T1</code> is still unresolved.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">T1</code> then aborts, so the value of <code class=\"mm\">X</code> that <code class=\"mm\">T2</code> used is rolled back.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">T2</code>&#8217;s committed effect, which was derived from that value, can no longer be undone. The schedule is not recoverable.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) Recoverability is decided by the commit order of reader and writer, and here it is the wrong way round.</li><li>(B) Cascadelessness forbids exactly the dirty read in step 1, regardless of which item was written later.</li><li>(D) Strictness requires that no other transaction read or write <code class=\"mm\">X</code> before <code class=\"mm\">T1</code> finished; <code class=\"mm\">r2(X)</code> breaks that.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Thinking the writer&#8217;s abort is enough to fix things. Once the reader has committed, a cascading rollback is impossible.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> A dirty read plus a reader that commits first is the textbook non-recoverable pattern.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U4-Q39", "subject": "databases", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "transactions-concurrency", "subtopic": "recoverability-cascadeless", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-31", "text": "Which of the following statements are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["Every strict schedule is cascadeless.", "Every cascadeless schedule is recoverable.", "Every recoverable schedule is cascadeless.", "A schedule in which no transaction reads uncommitted data is necessarily conflict serializable."], "answer_display": "Options (A) and (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Three nested classes: strict schedules inside cascadeless schedules inside recoverable schedules. Serializability is a different axis altogether &mdash; it is about the order of conflicting operations, not about when commits happen.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) is true. Strict forbids dirty reads and dirty writes; cascadeless forbids dirty reads. The stronger condition implies the weaker.</li><li>(B) is true. With no dirty read there is no dependency on an uncommitted value, so every reader commits after its writer and the schedule is recoverable.</li><li>(C) is false. A recoverable schedule may still contain a dirty read, provided the reader commits only after the writer. It then needs cascading rollback but stays recoverable.</li><li>(D) is false. Cascadelessness says nothing about the order of conflicting operations between transactions that never share uncommitted data, so a cascadeless schedule can still be non-serializable.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A) and (B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Merging the recoverability family with the serializability family. A schedule can be serializable but non-recoverable, and recoverable but non-serializable.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Recoverability and serializability answer different questions and neither implies the other.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U4-Q40", "subject": "databases", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "transactions-concurrency", "subtopic": "recoverability-cascadeless", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-31", "text": "Consider the schedule S = r1(X); w1(X); r2(X); r1(Y); w2(Y), and suppose T1 aborts. Under cascading rollback, what is the total number of transactions that are rolled back, counting T1 itself?(Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "2", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Cascading rollback propagates along dirty reads: if <code class=\"mm\">Tj</code> read an item from <code class=\"mm\">Ti</code> and <code class=\"mm\">Ti</code> rolls back, <code class=\"mm\">Tj</code> must roll back too, and so on transitively.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">T1</code> aborts: rollback count = 1.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">r2(X)</code> read <code class=\"mm\">X</code> after <code class=\"mm\">w1(X)</code>, so <code class=\"mm\">T2</code> depends on <code class=\"mm\">T1</code>.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">T2</code> must therefore roll back as well: rollback count = 2.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">T2</code> wrote only <code class=\"mm\">Y</code> and no third transaction read it, so the cascade stops.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 2</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Counting only <code class=\"mm\">T1</code>, or adding <code class=\"mm\">T1</code> twice because it both writes and aborts.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Follow the reads-from edges out of the aborted transaction; each reachable transaction rolls back once.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U4-Q41", "subject": "databases", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "transactions-concurrency", "subtopic": "two-phase-locking-2pl-strict-rigorous", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-31", "text": "Under strict two-phase locking, when may a transaction release an exclusive lock that it holds?", "options": ["As soon as it has finished using the item, even if it has not yet committed.", "Only when it commits or aborts.", "At the end of its growing phase, provided that all its other locks are already held.", "Whenever the lock manager decides to grant a conflicting request."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Strict 2PL keeps every exclusive lock until the end of the transaction, which is what stops anyone from reading or overwriting an uncommitted value. That is also why strict 2PL removes cascading rollback.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>2PL allows a growing phase, in which locks are acquired, followed by a shrinking phase, in which they are released.</li><li>Strict 2PL adds one restriction: no exclusive lock may be released before the transaction commits or aborts.</li><li>The shrinking phase of a strict transaction therefore coincides with its termination.</li><li>Releasing earlier would let another transaction read or overwrite a value that might still be rolled back.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) &mdash; at commit or abort only.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) That is plain lock-based execution, which permits dirty reads.</li><li>(C) Releasing at the end of the growing phase is basic 2PL, not strict 2PL.</li><li>(D) Locks are released by their holder, never by the lock manager on someone else&#8217;s behalf.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Thinking the shrinking phase must start before commit. In strict 2PL it does not start at all until the transaction ends.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Strict 2PL = 2PL + hold exclusive locks to the end.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U4-Q42", "subject": "databases", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "transactions-concurrency", "subtopic": "two-phase-locking-2pl-strict-rigorous", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-31", "text": "Which one of the following protocols guarantees both conflict serializability and freedom from cascading rollback?", "options": ["Basic two-phase locking.", "Conservative two-phase locking.", "Two-phase locking with lock upgrades but without the strictness rule.", "Strict two-phase locking."], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Serializability and recoverability are separate guarantees. 2PL in any form gives conflict serializability; only the strict form also keeps exclusive locks to the end and so prevents dirty reads.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Basic 2PL: conflict serializable, but locks may be released before commit, so cascading rollback is possible.</li><li>Conservative 2PL: deadlock-free and conflict serializable, but it says nothing about when locks are released.</li><li>Strict 2PL: 2PL plus hold-exclusive-locks-to-the-end, so no transaction can read an uncommitted value.</li><li>No dirty read means no cascading rollback; 2PL means conflict serializable. Both guarantees together belong to strict 2PL.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b> (A) and (C) permit early release, and (B) is silent about release timing; all three can therefore allow cascading rollback.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Believing 2PL alone is enough. Serializability does not stop a reader from reading uncommitted data.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Conflict serializability &larr; 2PL; no cascading rollback &larr; strictness. Strict 2PL supplies both.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U4-Q43", "subject": "databases", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "transactions-concurrency", "subtopic": "two-phase-locking-2pl-strict-rigorous", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-31", "text": "Which of the following statements about two-phase locking and its variants are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["Rigorous two-phase locking holds every lock, shared as well as exclusive, until the transaction commits or aborts, so it is a special case of strict two-phase locking.", "Conservative two-phase locking acquires all of a transaction&#8217;s locks before it starts, which removes deadlock at the cost of less concurrency.", "Basic two-phase locking guarantees freedom from deadlock.", "Strict two-phase locking guarantees conflict-serializable schedules."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Keep the guarantees apart: 2PL buys conflict serializability, strictness buys recoverability without cascades, and only the conservative variant buys deadlock freedom &mdash; by paying for it with concurrency.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) is true. Rigorous 2PL releases all locks together at commit; since it also holds exclusive locks to the end, it satisfies the strict rule and is the stronger special case.</li><li>(B) is true. If every lock is obtained up front, a transaction never waits while holding a lock, so a cycle cannot form. The price is that locks are held while they are still unused.</li><li>(C) is false. Basic 2PL is deadlock-prone: <code class=\"mm\">T1</code> can hold <code class=\"mm\">X</code> and wait for <code class=\"mm\">Y</code> while <code class=\"mm\">T2</code> holds <code class=\"mm\">Y</code> and waits for <code class=\"mm\">X</code>.</li><li>(D) is true. That is the theorem behind 2PL: any schedule produced by a 2PL-compliant set of transactions is conflict serializable.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming the growing and shrinking phases make deadlock impossible. They only fix the order of acquisition and release; two transactions can still each hold what the other wants.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> 2PL &rArr; serializable; strict &rArr; no cascades; conservative &rArr; no deadlock.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U4-Q44", "subject": "databases", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "transactions-concurrency", "subtopic": "two-phase-locking-2pl-strict-rigorous", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-31", "text": "Under two-phase locking, which one of the following is TRUE about changing the mode of a lock that the transaction already holds?", "options": ["An upgrade from shared to exclusive must happen in the growing phase, and a downgrade from exclusive to shared may only happen in the shrinking phase.", "An upgrade and a downgrade may both happen in either phase.", "An upgrade must happen in the shrinking phase and a downgrade in the growing phase.", "Lock conversions are not permitted in two-phase locking at all."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> An upgrade asks for more privilege, which is an acquisition, so it belongs to the growing phase. A downgrade gives privilege back, which is a release, so it belongs to the shrinking phase.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The growing phase may only add locks or strengthen them.</li><li>An upgrade <code class=\"mm\">S(X) &rarr; X(X)</code> strengthens a lock, so it must occur while the transaction is still growing.</li><li>The shrinking phase may only drop locks or weaken them.</li><li>A downgrade <code class=\"mm\">X(X) &rarr; S(X)</code> weakens a lock, so it can occur only after the growing phase has ended.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b> (B) would let a transaction strengthen a lock after it had begun releasing others, which is exactly what 2PL forbids. (C) inverts the two phases. (D) is false: upgrades are standard practice and are what let a transaction read an item and then write it.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Treating an upgrade as \"not a new lock request\". It is one, and it must respect the phase rule &mdash; and it can also deadlock two transactions that hold shared locks and both try to upgrade.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Strengthen while growing, weaken while shrinking.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U4-Q45", "subject": "databases", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "transactions-concurrency", "subtopic": "two-phase-locking-2pl-strict-rigorous", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-31", "text": "The wait-for graph of a system holds six transactions and seven edges, and contains exactly two disjoint cycles, one of length 3 and one of length 2. Under deadlock detection with abort-based victim selection, what is the minimum number of transactions that must be aborted to make the graph acyclic?(Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "2", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A deadlock is a cycle in the wait-for graph, and aborting one transaction on a cycle breaks that cycle. Disjoint cycles need one victim each, because no single transaction lies on both.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The graph has two cycles and they are disjoint, so they share no transaction.</li><li>One abort on the 3-cycle removes all three of its edges and leaves the 2-cycle untouched.</li><li>The 2-cycle still needs its own victim.</li><li>Two aborts therefore suffice, and one cannot: a single victim lies on at most one of the two cycles.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 2</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Taking the length of a cycle as the number of victims, or the total edge count. Only the number of <em>disjoint</em> cycles matters.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Minimum victims = number of disjoint cycles in the wait-for graph.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U4-Q46", "subject": "databases", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "transactions-concurrency", "subtopic": "timestamp-ordering-and-deadlocks", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-31", "text": "In timestamp-based concurrency control, the timestamp of a transaction is normally assigned", "options": ["when the transaction issues its first write.", "when the transaction commits.", "when the transaction starts.", "by the order in which its operations happen to reach the scheduler."], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Timestamp ordering enforces the serial order in which transactions <em>started</em>. The timestamp must therefore be fixed at start time and must never change, or the protocol would have no fixed serial order to enforce.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The scheduler assigns <code class=\"mm\">TS(T)</code> when <code class=\"mm\">T</code> begins, typically from a counter or the system clock.</li><li>Every data item carries the largest timestamp of any transaction that has read it (<code class=\"mm\">RTS(X)</code>) and written it (<code class=\"mm\">WTS(X)</code>).</li><li>Each operation is checked against those values, and a transaction whose timestamp is too old is rejected.</li><li>Because the timestamp is fixed at start, the accepted operations always reproduce the serial order of the start times.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C) &mdash; at start.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) The first write may come long after the transaction began and would not define a consistent order.</li><li>(B) A commit-time timestamp would order transactions by completion, which is not known in advance and cannot be enforced online.</li><li>(D) Arrival order at the scheduler is what timestamp ordering replaces, because arrival order can differ from the order in which operations were logically issued.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Confusing the timestamp with a lock request time. It is the start time of the transaction, assigned once.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> One timestamp per transaction, fixed at start.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U4-Q47", "subject": "databases", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "transactions-concurrency", "subtopic": "timestamp-ordering-and-deadlocks", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-31", "text": "Which one of the following concurrency-control schemes is free from deadlock?", "options": ["Basic two-phase locking.", "Basic timestamp ordering.", "Strict two-phase locking.", "Two-phase locking with lock upgrades."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Deadlock needs a cycle of transactions each waiting for something another holds. A protocol in which no transaction ever waits cannot form such a cycle.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Timestamp ordering never blocks a request: it either executes the operation or rejects the transaction.</li><li>With no waiting there is no wait-for graph to contain a cycle, so no deadlock.</li><li>All three 2PL variants in the options do block: a transaction waits for a lock held by another.</li><li>Lock upgrades make the waiting worse, since two transactions holding shared locks can each wait for the other to release.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) &mdash; basic timestamp ordering.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b> (A), (C) and (D) all allow a transaction to wait while holding locks, which is the raw material of deadlock; strictness and upgrades change the details, not the possibility.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Believing strictness or conservativeness of the <em>strict</em> kind removes deadlock. Only the conservative (pre-claiming) variant does that, and it is not among the options.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> No waiting, no deadlock &mdash; the trade is that timestamp ordering aborts instead.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U4-Q48", "subject": "databases", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "transactions-concurrency", "subtopic": "timestamp-ordering-and-deadlocks", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-31", "text": "Under the wait–die scheme, transaction Ti requests a lock held by transaction Tj, and TS(Ti) < TS(Tj), so Ti is the older of the two. What does the scheme do?", "options": ["Ti waits for Tj to release the lock; a younger requester facing an older holder is rolled back instead.", "Ti is rolled back at once, and Tj waits for Ti to restart.", "Both transactions are rolled back, so that the deadlock cannot form.", "Ti waits, and Tj is rolled back the next time it requests a lock held by Ti."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Wait&ndash;die removes deadlock by never letting a younger transaction wait for an older one. The older transaction waits; the younger one dies (rolls back) and restarts with its original timestamp.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">TS(Ti) &lt; TS(Tj)</code> means <code class=\"mm\">Ti</code> is older.</li><li>An older requester is allowed to wait, so <code class=\"mm\">Ti</code> blocks until <code class=\"mm\">Tj</code> releases.</li><li>Had the requester been younger (<code class=\"mm\">TS(Ti) &gt; TS(Tj)</code>), it would have been rolled back immediately rather than allowed to wait.</li><li>Since waits always run from older to younger, a cycle of waits is impossible, so the scheme is deadlock-free.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) describes wound&ndash;wait, where the older transaction wounds the younger holder.</li><li>(C) is not part of either scheme; only one transaction is ever a victim.</li><li>(D) mixes the two schemes and would reintroduce cycles.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Swapping wait&ndash;die with wound&ndash;wait. In wait&ndash;die the older waits and the younger dies; in wound&ndash;wait the older wounds (rolls back) the younger.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Older waits, younger dies &mdash; and a restarted transaction keeps its old timestamp, which is why it eventually wins.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U4-Q49", "subject": "databases", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "transactions-concurrency", "subtopic": "timestamp-ordering-and-deadlocks", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-31", "text": "Which of the following statements about timestamp ordering are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["Basic timestamp ordering is deadlock-free because a transaction never waits for a lock; a conflicting operation is rejected instead.", "Basic timestamp ordering can roll a transaction back even when the schedule it would have produced was serializable.", "Thomas&#8217;s write rule ignores an obsolete write instead of rejecting it, and so admits some schedules that basic timestamp ordering rejects.", "Basic timestamp ordering on its own guarantees that every schedule it produces is recoverable."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Timestamp ordering buys deadlock freedom by rejecting operations instead of waiting. The price is unnecessary aborts, which Thomas&#8217;s write rule partly recovers, and the protocol says nothing about commit order, so recoverability needs a separate rule.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) is true. Requests are checked against <code class=\"mm\">RTS</code> and <code class=\"mm\">WTS</code> and either executed or refused; nothing blocks, so no cycle can form.</li><li>(B) is true. The checks are local to one data item, so a transaction can be aborted even though the schedule as a whole would have been serializable.</li><li>(C) is true. If <code class=\"mm\">w(X)</code> arrives with <code class=\"mm\">TS(T) &lt; WTS(X)</code>, its value would be overwritten at once and can be ignored rather than causing a rollback, which admits more schedules.</li><li>(D) is false. Basic timestamp ordering allows a transaction to read a value written by an uncommitted transaction, so a schedule it accepts can be non-recoverable unless the reads are additionally deferred.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming a serializable protocol is automatically recoverable. Serializability orders operations; recoverability orders commits, and the two are independent.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Timestamp ordering: no deadlock, extra aborts, no built-in recoverability.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U4-Q50", "subject": "databases", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "transactions-concurrency", "subtopic": "timestamp-ordering-and-deadlocks", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-31", "text": "The read and write timestamps of both X and Y are 0, and TS(T1) = 10, TS(T2) = 20, TS(T3) = 30. The schedule is r1(X); w3(X); r2(X); w2(Y); r1(Y); w3(Y). Using basic timestamp ordering, how many of the six operations are rejected?(Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "2", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Each operation is checked against the item&#8217;s timestamps: a read is rejected if <code class=\"mm\">TS(T) &lt; WTS(X)</code>, and a write is rejected if <code class=\"mm\">TS(T) &lt; RTS(X)</code> or <code class=\"mm\">TS(T) &lt; WTS(X)</code>. A rejected operation aborts its transaction; the checks for the other operations still follow the same rule.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">r1(X)</code>: <code class=\"mm\">10 &ge; WTS(X) = 0</code>, accepted. Now <code class=\"mm\">RTS(X) = 10</code>.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">w3(X)</code>: <code class=\"mm\">30 &ge; RTS(X) = 10</code> and <code class=\"mm\">30 &ge; WTS(X) = 0</code>, accepted. Now <code class=\"mm\">WTS(X) = 30</code>.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">r2(X)</code>: <code class=\"mm\">20 &lt; WTS(X) = 30</code>, so <code class=\"mm\">T2</code> would read a value written by a younger transaction. Rejected (1).</li><li><code class=\"mm\">w2(Y)</code>: <code class=\"mm\">20 &ge; RTS(Y) = 0</code> and <code class=\"mm\">20 &ge; WTS(Y) = 0</code>, accepted. Now <code class=\"mm\">WTS(Y) = 20</code>.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">r1(Y)</code>: <code class=\"mm\">10 &lt; WTS(Y) = 20</code>, rejected (2).</li><li><code class=\"mm\">w3(Y)</code>: <code class=\"mm\">30 &ge; RTS(Y) = 0</code> and <code class=\"mm\">30 &ge; WTS(Y) = 20</code>, accepted. Now <code class=\"mm\">WTS(Y) = 30</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 2</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Checking a read only against <code class=\"mm\">WTS</code> but forgetting to update <code class=\"mm\">RTS</code>, or checking a write against <code class=\"mm\">WTS</code> only. Both timestamps must be maintained on every accepted operation.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Older operations that arrive late are the ones rejected: here two reads by older transactions, both of them too late.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U5-Q01", "subject": "databases", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "indexing-storage", "subtopic": "primary-secondary-clustering-indexes", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "A file of 30,000 records is stored in 1,000 blocks and is physically sorted on its search key. A primary index is built on that key. The index stores one entry for each", "options": ["record in the file.", "block in the file.", "distinct value of the search key.", "page of the index itself."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A primary index is a <b>sparse</b> index on the ordering key field of a file that is physically sorted on that key. Because the file is sorted, one entry per block is enough: the entry carries the key of the first record in the block together with a pointer to that block.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The file is sorted on the search key, so the records of one block occupy a contiguous run of key values and the block's first key identifies the whole block.</li><li>A single index entry per block therefore locates any key in that block after one extra read of the block.</li><li>With 30,000 records in 1,000 blocks the primary index has <b>1,000</b> entries.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) — one entry per block</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) One entry per record is a <i>dense</i> index; the ordering already makes those extra entries redundant for a primary index.</li><li>(C) One entry per distinct value describes a clustering index on a non-key field, or a dense secondary index on a key field.</li><li>(D) A page of the index is a unit inside the index, not a unit of the indexed file.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Treating the primary index as a dense index. The physical ordering is the whole reason the index may be sparse; drop the ordering and the same question has a different answer.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Primary index = ordered key + sparse = one entry per block. When a stem says “sorted on the key”, expect a block-level count, not a record-level one.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U5-Q02", "subject": "databases", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "indexing-storage", "subtopic": "primary-secondary-clustering-indexes", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "A file of 30,000 records in 1,000 blocks is sorted on a non-key field  dept  that has 500 distinct values. A clustering index on  dept  has how many entries?", "options": ["500", "1,000", "30,000", "1"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A clustering index is built on a <b>non-key</b> field that orders the file. Several records may share a value, so the index holds one entry per distinct value, pointing at the first block that holds that value.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The file is sorted on <code class=\"mm\">dept</code>, not on a key, so equal values sit together but a value may span many records and several blocks.</li><li>The index therefore cannot be per record (too many) and cannot be per block (a value may span blocks, and one block may hold two values).</li><li>It is per <b>distinct value</b> of the ordering field: 500 distinct values give 500 entries.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A) — 500 entries</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) 1,000 is the block count, which is the entry count of a primary index on the key, not of a clustering index on a non-key field.</li><li>(C) 30,000 is the record count, the entry count of a dense secondary index.</li><li>(D) A single entry would only be right if the field had one distinct value.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming every index on the ordering field is block-level. That holds only when the field is a key; with duplicates the entry count collapses to the number of distinct values.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Three counts, three indexes: per block for a primary index, per distinct value for a clustering index, per record for a dense secondary index.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U5-Q03", "subject": "databases", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "indexing-storage", "subtopic": "primary-secondary-clustering-indexes", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "A file has 50,000 records with 10 records per block, and is sorted on its search key. A single-level primary index uses entries of a 10-byte key plus a 6-byte block pointer, and index blocks are 1024 bytes. How many blocks does the primary index occupy? (Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "79", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A primary index has one entry per data block. Its size in blocks follows from the number of entries that fit in one index block, which is the block size divided by the entry size.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Data blocks = 50,000 ÷ 10 = <b>5,000</b>, so the primary index has 5,000 entries.</li><li>One entry = 10 + 6 = <b>16 bytes</b>, so an index block holds ⌊1024 ÷ 16⌋ = <b>64</b> entries.</li><li>Blocks needed = ⌈5,000 ÷ 64⌉ = ⌈78.125⌉ = <b>79</b>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 79 (an integer number of blocks; the last block is partly empty but is still allocated)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Using ⌊5000 ÷ 64⌋ = 78 and forgetting the partial final block. Allocation is in whole blocks, so the count always rounds up.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Index sizing is two steps: floor for entries per block, ceil for blocks. Reversing the two roundings is the commonest arithmetic slip in this topic.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U5-Q04", "subject": "databases", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "indexing-storage", "subtopic": "primary-secondary-clustering-indexes", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "Which of the following can place  all  records that share a value of the indexed field in one contiguous run of blocks?", "options": ["A dense secondary index on a non-ordering field.", "A clustering index on a non-key ordering field.", "A hash index on the search key.", "A B+ tree index on a non-ordering field."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Contiguity of equal values is a property of the <b>physical file order</b>, not of the index structure. Only an index on the field the file is sorted by can promise it.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>A clustering index is defined on a non-key field that orders the file, so all records with one value of that field are stored next to each other.</li><li>Every other index here is built on a field that does <b>not</b> order the file, so equal values may be scattered over the whole file.</li><li>Hence only the clustering index guarantees the contiguous run.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) — a clustering index on a non-key ordering field</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) A dense secondary index is by definition on a field that does not order the file; its entries point to scattered records.</li><li>(C) A hash index sends a key to a bucket, which says nothing about where equal values of a different field sit.</li><li>(D) A B+ tree orders its own leaves by key, not the data file; the records themselves stay wherever they were written.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reading “index on a field” as “file sorted on that field”. An index and the file it indexes are ordered independently, and only the clustering index ties them together.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Ask what orders the file. If the answer is the indexed field, the index is primary (key) or clustering (non-key); otherwise it is secondary and nothing is contiguous.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U5-Q05", "subject": "databases", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "indexing-storage", "subtopic": "primary-secondary-clustering-indexes", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "Which of the following statements about single-level indexes are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["A primary index is a sparse index built on the ordering key field of the file.", "A clustering index can have several records under one index entry, because the indexed field is not a key.", "A secondary index can be built only on a candidate key of the file.", "An index on a field that does not order the file must be dense, with one entry for every record."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Indexes are classified on two axes: the field they are built on (key or non-key) and whether that field orders the file. The four combinations are the primary, clustering and secondary indexes.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) True by definition: the file is sorted on the key, so the index needs only the first key of each block.</li><li>(B) True: the indexed field is non-key, so one value may cover many records and one entry points at the first of them.</li><li>(C) False. A secondary index may be built on any field; on a non-key field it is dense and keeps several records per entry.</li><li>(D) True: with no ordering to exploit, the index cannot skip records, so it must carry an entry for each one.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (D)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(C) is the only false statement: “secondary” means “not on the ordering field”, not “on a key only”.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reading “secondary” as a synonym for “on a non-key field”, or as “on a key only”. It names the field's relation to the file order, and nothing else.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Label an index by answering two questions: is the field a key, and does the file's order follow it? Those two bits generate all the standard properties.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U5-Q06", "subject": "databases", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "indexing-storage", "subtopic": "primary-secondary-clustering-indexes", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "A file has 5,000 data blocks and a primary index whose entries are 16 bytes, stored in 1024-byte blocks. The index is made multilevel, repeatedly indexing the previous level, until a single top block remains. How many index blocks does the whole multilevel structure contain?", "options": ["79", "81", "82", "5,000"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A multilevel index is a sparse index over the previous level, so each level is sized by the same rule: entries divided by entries-per-block, rounded up. Stop when a level fits in one block.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Entries per index block = ⌊1024 ÷ 16⌋ = <b>64</b>.</li><li>Level 1 over 5,000 data blocks: ⌈5,000 ÷ 64⌉ = <b>79</b> blocks.</li><li>Level 2 over 79 entries: ⌈79 ÷ 64⌉ = <b>2</b> blocks.</li><li>Level 3 over 2 entries: ⌈2 ÷ 64⌉ = <b>1</b> block — a single top block, so stop.</li><li>Total = 79 + 2 + 1 = <b>82</b> blocks.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C) — 82 blocks</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) 79 counts only the first level and ignores the levels built above it.</li><li>(B) 81 is 79 + 2, i.e. the top block is forgotten.</li><li>(D) 5,000 is the number of data blocks, not of index blocks.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Stopping one level too early, or adding only the first level. The structure is complete only when the topmost level is a single block.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Size each level separately and add them; the loop ends at the first level whose block count is 1, and that level counts too.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U5-Q07", "subject": "databases", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "indexing-storage", "subtopic": "dense-vs-sparse-indexing", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "Which index can decide whether the value 47 occurs in the indexed field  without reading the data file at all ?", "options": ["A sparse index on the ordering key.", "A dense index on the search key.", "A primary index on the ordering key.", "None — every index must read the data file to answer an existence query."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A dense index holds an entry for every search-key value present in the file, so the index alone decides existence. A sparse index holds entries for only some values, so a missing value proves nothing.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>In a dense index, every value that occurs in the file appears as a key in the index.</li><li>If 47 is absent from the dense index, it is absent from the file; if it is present, it occurs.</li><li>In a sparse index the entry for 47 may simply not have been stored, even though a record with 47 exists, so the index cannot answer the question alone.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) — a dense index</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) A sparse index omits most values, so absence from the index is not absence from the file.</li><li>(C) A primary index is sparse by construction.</li><li>(D) False: a dense index is exactly the structure that answers an existence query from the index alone.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Believing every index answers existence queries. A sparse index answers “where should I look”, not “does it exist”.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Dense means complete: one entry per value, so membership tests are index-only. Sparse means navigational: you land on a block and then look.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U5-Q08", "subject": "databases", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "indexing-storage", "subtopic": "dense-vs-sparse-indexing", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "A file of 20,000 records is stored with 8 records per block and is sorted on the search key. A sparse index stores one entry per block. How many entries does the index have?", "options": ["2,500", "20,000", "2,000", "8"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A sparse index on the ordering key keeps one entry per block, so its size is the block count, not the record count.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Blocks in the file = 20,000 ÷ 8 = <b>2,500</b>.</li><li>A sparse index on the ordering key keeps one entry per block, so it has 2,500 entries rather than the 20,000 a dense index would need.</li><li>Each entry carries the key of the block's first record and a pointer to the block.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A) — 2,500 entries</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) 20,000 is the record count, which is the size of a dense index, not of a sparse one.</li><li>(C) 2,000 comes from dividing the records by 10 instead of by the blocking factor of 8.</li><li>(D) 8 is the blocking factor itself, not a count of entries.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Multiplying by the blocking factor instead of dividing, or answering with the record count out of habit because most index questions are about dense indexes.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> For a sparse index, convert records to blocks first; the index lives at block granularity.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U5-Q09", "subject": "databases", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "indexing-storage", "subtopic": "dense-vs-sparse-indexing", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "Compared with a dense index on the same ordered file, a sparse index on the ordering key", "options": ["has fewer entries and may need a scan of the located block to reach the record.", "has more entries but avoids scanning the block.", "has the same number of entries and the same search cost.", "cannot be used in a multilevel structure."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Sparsity trades index size against a final in-block scan. The sparse index is smaller because it skips records; the price is that the entry points at a block, not at a record.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>A dense index has one entry per record; a sparse index has one entry per block, so it has far fewer entries and occupies fewer blocks.</li><li>A sparse entry identifies the block that must contain the key, so the search finishes with a scan inside that block.</li><li>Hence: smaller index, one extra in-block scan.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) A sparse index has fewer entries, not more.</li><li>(C) The entry counts differ by the blocking factor, so the costs cannot be identical.</li><li>(D) False: multilevel indexes are normally built as sparse structures over the level below.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming sparsity is free. It buys index size with a block scan; a dense index buys direct record access with index size.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Every index question is a size-versus-access trade. Say which side the stem is rewarding before choosing.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U5-Q10", "subject": "databases", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "indexing-storage", "subtopic": "dense-vs-sparse-indexing", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "Which of the following are TRUE of dense and sparse indexes? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["A sparse index on a search key can be built only when the file is ordered on that key.", "A dense index on a search key can be built on a file stored in any order.", "A dense index always occupies fewer blocks than a sparse index on the same file.", "A secondary index on a non-key field that does not order the file must be dense."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Sparsity is possible only when the file's order lets the index skip records. Without that order the index must be dense to be usable at all.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) True: skipping a record is safe only if the ordering guarantees that the record's key lies between the entry's key and the next entry's key.</li><li>(B) True: a dense index carries an entry per record, so it does not depend on where the records sit.</li><li>(C) False: dense means more entries, so it occupies more blocks, not fewer.</li><li>(D) True: with no ordering there is nothing to skip, so every record needs an entry.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (D)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(C) is the only false statement; it inverts the size relationship.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Believing a dense index is the “better” structure in every respect. It is the only possible structure when the file is unordered, and the more expensive one when it is ordered.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Ordering is the permission slip for sparsity. No ordering, no sparsity, and the index is dense.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U5-Q11", "subject": "databases", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "indexing-storage", "subtopic": "dense-vs-sparse-indexing", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "A sparse index is built over 10,000 data blocks. Each index entry is 12 bytes and index blocks hold 1024 bytes. The index is made multilevel until a single top block remains. How many index blocks are there in total?", "options": ["118", "120", "121", "122"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Same rule as any multilevel index: entries per block by floor, blocks per level by ceil, stop at the level that fits in one block, then sum the levels.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Entries per index block = ⌊1024 ÷ 12⌋ = <b>85</b>.</li><li>Level 1 over 10,000 blocks: ⌈10,000 ÷ 85⌉ = ⌈117.65⌉ = <b>118</b> blocks.</li><li>Level 2 over 118 entries: ⌈118 ÷ 85⌉ = ⌈1.39⌉ = <b>2</b> blocks.</li><li>Level 3 over 2 entries: ⌈2 ÷ 85⌉ = <b>1</b> block — stop.</li><li>Total = 118 + 2 + 1 = <b>121</b>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C) — 121 blocks</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) 118 stops at the first level.</li><li>(B) 120 is 118 + 2 and omits the top block.</li><li>(D) 122 over-counts by one, typically from rounding 117.65 up twice.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Rounding the entries-per-block value up instead of down. A block that holds 85 full entries plus a fragment of a 86th holds 85 entries.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Floor for capacity, ceil for blocks. Write both roundings explicitly before adding the levels.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U5-Q12", "subject": "databases", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "indexing-storage", "subtopic": "dense-vs-sparse-indexing", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "A file has 40,000 records. A dense index is built on its key with one entry per record, each entry 14 bytes, in index blocks of 1024 bytes. How many blocks does the dense index occupy? (Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "548", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A dense index has one entry per record, so the entry count is the record count; the block count is then entries divided by entries-per-block, rounded up.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Dense entries = <b>40,000</b>.</li><li>Entries per block = ⌊1024 ÷ 14⌋ = ⌈73.14⌋ = <b>73</b>.</li><li>Blocks = ⌈40,000 ÷ 73⌉ = ⌈547.9⌉ = <b>548</b>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 548 (an integer; 73 × 547 = 39,931 < 40,000, so 547 blocks are not enough)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Rounding 547.9 down to 547. A dense index must store every entry, so the last, partly filled block is still a block.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Check the rounding with a multiplication: if blocks × entries-per-block < entries, one more block is needed.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U5-Q13", "subject": "databases", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "indexing-storage", "subtopic": "b-trees-and-b-plus-trees-order-capacity", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "In a B+ tree index, the record pointers that lead to the actual data records appear", "options": ["in every node of the tree.", "only in the leaf nodes.", "only in the root node.", "only in the internal nodes."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A B+ tree separates navigation from retrieval: internal nodes hold only keys and child pointers, and every data pointer lives in a leaf.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Internal nodes guide the search downward using separator keys; they store no record pointers.</li><li>Leaves hold the keys together with the pointers to the records (or to the record blocks).</li><li>Because all data pointers are at one level, every search costs the same number of block accesses.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) — only in the leaf nodes</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) That describes a B tree, where data pointers may also sit in internal nodes.</li><li>(C) The root holds at most a few separator keys and child pointers.</li><li>(D) Internal nodes carry keys and child pointers only, which is exactly what keeps the fan-out high.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Confusing the B tree and the B+ tree. In a B tree a data pointer can appear in any node; in a B+ tree the data pointers are confined to the leaves.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> B tree: keys and data pointers everywhere. B+ tree: keys everywhere, data pointers only at the bottom, leaves chained for ranges.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U5-Q14", "subject": "databases", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "indexing-storage", "subtopic": "b-trees-and-b-plus-trees-order-capacity", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "A B+ tree internal node holds  p  tree pointers and  p − 1  search keys. The block size is 1024 bytes, a key is 10 bytes and a pointer is 6 bytes. What is the largest  p  that fits in one internal node? (Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "64", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The order of a B+ tree is limited by how many pointer-plus-key pairs fit in a block. An internal node pays for p pointers and p − 1 keys.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Space used = 6p + 10(p − 1) = 16p − 10 bytes.</li><li>Require 16p − 10 ≤ 1024, so 16p ≤ 1034 and p ≤ 64.625.</li><li>p is an integer, so the largest value is <b>64</b>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 64 (the value of p, not the byte count)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Forgetting the p − 1 and writing 16p ≤ 1024, which gives 64 as well here but fails on other numbers; or rounding 64.625 up to 65, which does not fit.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Write the node's byte equation first, then floor. The p − 1 key count is what distinguishes an internal node from a leaf.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U5-Q15", "subject": "databases", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "indexing-storage", "subtopic": "b-trees-and-b-plus-trees-order-capacity", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "In the same B+ tree, a leaf node holds up to  p  keys,  p  record pointers, and one extra pointer to the next leaf. Keys are 10 bytes, pointers 6 bytes, and a block is 1024 bytes. The largest number of keys in a leaf is", "options": ["63", "64", "62", "85"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A leaf pays for p keys, p record pointers, and one additional sibling pointer that internal nodes do not have. That extra pointer is what makes the leaf capacity differ from the internal fan-out.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Space used = 10p + 6p + 6 = 16p + 6 bytes.</li><li>Require 16p + 6 ≤ 1024, so 16p ≤ 1018 and p ≤ 63.625.</li><li>The largest integer is <b>63</b>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A) — 63 keys</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) 64 is the internal-node fan-out; the leaf's sibling pointer costs one slot, so a leaf holds one fewer.</li><li>(C) 62 looks like a safe round-down of 63.625 taken one step too far; the true floor is 63.</li><li>(D) 85 ignores the record pointers and the sibling pointer entirely.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reusing the internal-node answer. The leaf has one extra pointer and no p − 1 discount, so its capacity is a separate calculation.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Internal node: p pointers, p − 1 keys. Leaf: p keys, p record pointers, 1 sibling pointer. Two equations, two capacities.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U5-Q16", "subject": "databases", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "indexing-storage", "subtopic": "b-trees-and-b-plus-trees-order-capacity", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "A B+ tree has three levels: a root, one level of internal nodes, and leaves. Every internal node including the root holds at most 64 tree pointers, and every leaf holds at most 63 records. What is the largest number of records the tree can index? (Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "258048", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Capacity is a product along the path: each internal node multiplies the number of children, and each leaf contributes its record capacity.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The root has at most 64 children, so there are at most 64 internal nodes on the middle level.</li><li>Each of those has at most 64 children, so there are at most 64 × 64 = <b>4,096</b> leaves.</li><li>Each leaf holds at most 63 records, so the tree indexes at most 4,096 × 63 = <b>258,048</b> records.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 258,048 records</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Multiplying 64 × 64 × 64, which uses the pointer count as if it were the leaf record count. Leaves store records, not pointers, and their capacity is 63.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Count leaves first, then multiply by leaf capacity. Internal levels contribute fan-out; only leaves contribute records.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U5-Q17", "subject": "databases", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "indexing-storage", "subtopic": "b-trees-and-b-plus-trees-order-capacity", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "A B-tree has order  p = 5 , meaning a node holds at most 5 tree pointers. The maximum number of keys in one node is", "options": ["3", "4", "5", "6"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> In a B-tree of order p a node has at most p children and, between those children, at most p − 1 keys: k<sub>1</sub> P<sub>1</sub> k<sub>2</sub> P<sub>2</sub> … P<sub>p−1</sub> k<sub>p−1</sub> P<sub>p</sub>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>A node is laid out as k<sub>1</sub> P<sub>1</sub> k<sub>2</sub> P<sub>2</sub> … P<sub>p−1</sub> k<sub>p−1</sub> P<sub>p</sub>: every child pointer after the first is preceded by one key.</li><li>So the key count is always one less than the pointer count.</li><li>With p = 5 pointers the maximum key count is 5 − 1 = <b>4</b>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) — 4 keys</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) 3 is a slip from halving the order, ⌈5/2⌉, which has nothing to do with the key count.</li><li>(C) 5 is the pointer count, which is the order, not the key count.</li><li>(D) 6 exceeds the pointer count, which is impossible in a B-tree node.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Quoting the order as the key capacity. The order counts children; keys are always one fewer.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Order p → at most p children and p − 1 keys. Both B trees and B+ tree internal nodes obey this.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U5-Q18", "subject": "databases", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "indexing-storage", "subtopic": "b-trees-and-b-plus-trees-order-capacity", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "Which of the following are TRUE of B+ trees? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["All leaves are at the same level.", "Internal nodes contain only keys and pointers, never data records.", "Because the leaves are linked in sequence, range queries are efficient.", "An internal node of order p can hold at most p keys."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A B+ tree is a balanced search structure whose internal nodes exist only to route searches, with all data at the leaf level and the leaves chained together.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) True: every leaf is at the same depth, which is what keeps every search the same length.</li><li>(B) True: internal nodes carry separator keys and child pointers only.</li><li>(C) True: once a leaf is reached, a range scan walks the sibling chain instead of re-descending the tree.</li><li>(D) False: an internal node of order p holds at most p − 1 keys, because it has p children.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (C)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(D) is the only false statement; it uses the pointer count as the key count.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Accepting “p keys” for an internal node. The order counts children, so keys are p − 1.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Balance, separator-only internal nodes and linked leaves are the three defining features of a B+ tree; the p versus p − 1 distinction follows from the child-pointer structure.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U5-Q19", "subject": "databases", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "indexing-storage", "subtopic": "b-trees-and-b-plus-trees-order-capacity", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "A B+ tree has order  p = 5 : every node holds at most 5 tree pointers, a non-root internal node holds at least ⌈p/2⌉ = 3 pointers, and a non-root leaf holds between ⌈(p − 1)/2⌉ = 2 and p − 1 = 4 keys. Which statements are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["A non-root internal node holds at least 2 keys.", "A non-root internal node holds at most 4 keys.", "A non-root leaf holds at least 2 keys.", "A non-root internal node can hold 5 keys."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Occupancy bounds follow from the child-pointer bounds: keys are always one fewer than children, and leaves obey their own bound given in the stem.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) True: at least 3 pointers means at least 3 − 1 = 2 keys.</li><li>(B) True: at most 5 pointers means at most 5 − 1 = 4 keys.</li><li>(C) True: the stem states the leaf minimum is 2 keys.</li><li>(D) False: 5 keys would need 6 pointers, and the node holds at most 5.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (C)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(D) is the only false statement; it confuses the key bound with the pointer bound.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Applying the pointer bounds directly to keys. Every internal-node statement has to be shifted down by one.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Fix the pointer bounds from p, then subtract one for every key statement. Leaves are separate: they have no child pointers, only record pointers.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U5-Q20", "subject": "databases", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "indexing-storage", "subtopic": "b-trees-and-b-plus-trees-order-capacity", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "A B+ tree indexes 1,000,000 records. A leaf holds at most 63 records and an internal node at most 64 pointers. Assuming no buffering of any node, how many block accesses does a point search need?", "options": ["3", "4", "5", "6"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The cost of a B+ tree search is the height of the tree in nodes: one access per level from the root down to a leaf.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Leaves needed = ⌈1,000,000 ÷ 63⌉ = <b>15,874</b>.</li><li>Level above = ⌈15,874 ÷ 64⌉ = ⌈248.03⌉ = <b>249</b> nodes.</li><li>Next level = ⌈249 ÷ 64⌉ = <b>4</b> nodes.</li><li>Next level = ⌈4 ÷ 64⌉ = <b>1</b> node, the root — stop.</li><li>Levels = root, 4, 249, 15,874 leaves = <b>4</b> levels, so 4 block accesses.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) — 4 block accesses</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) 3 under-counts: three levels cover only 64 × 64 × 63 ≈ 258,048 records.</li><li>(C) and (D) over-count by adding levels the fan-out does not require.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Counting only the leaf access, or assuming one access per 64 records. The count is the number of tree levels, and a million records need only four.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Build the tree bottom-up: leaves first, then divide by the fan-out until one root remains. The number of divisions plus one is the search cost.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U5-Q21", "subject": "databases", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "indexing-storage", "subtopic": "static-and-dynamic-hashing", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "In static hashing, a bucket that receives more records than it can hold is handled by", "options": ["doubling the number of buckets and rehashing every record.", "chaining the excess records in an overflow bucket.", "moving the whole file to a second hash area.", "rejecting the insert."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Static hashing fixes the number of buckets when the file is created. Overflow is therefore handled inside the fixed structure, normally by chaining overflow buckets to the full bucket.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The bucket address is h(k) and the bucket set never grows, so a full bucket cannot be split.</li><li>The standard remedy is a linked chain of overflow buckets attached to the full bucket.</li><li>The main bucket is read first; the chain is scanned only if the record is not found there, so overflow degrades search time without changing the address function.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) — overflow chaining</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) Doubling the bucket count is what <i>dynamic</i> hashing does; in static hashing the count is fixed at creation.</li><li>(C) Moving the file is not a collision strategy at all.</li><li>(D) A hash file is expected to absorb overflow, not refuse inserts.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Describing extendible or linear hashing when the stem says static. The whole point of “static” is that the bucket array never changes size.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Static hashing: fixed buckets, overflow chains, performance that decays as the file grows. Dynamic hashing exists to remove that decay.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U5-Q22", "subject": "databases", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "indexing-storage", "subtopic": "static-and-dynamic-hashing", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "A static hash file has 10 buckets, each able to hold 3 records. Keys 1 to 40 are inserted with the hash function  h(k) = k mod 10 . How many records end up in overflow buckets? (Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "10", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> With a modulo hash the keys spread evenly over the buckets by residue class; overflow is the excess above bucket capacity in each class.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Keys with <code class=\"mm\">k mod 10 = r</code> for r = 0, 1, …, 9 are the keys r, r + 10, r + 20, r + 30 (with 10, 20, 30, 40 for r = 0).</li><li>Each residue class holds exactly <b>4</b> of the keys 1–40, so every one of the 10 buckets receives 4 records.</li><li>Each bucket holds 3, so each sends <b>1</b> record to overflow: 10 buckets × 1 = <b>10</b>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 10 records in overflow buckets</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Dividing 40 by 10 and concluding there is no overflow, or forgetting the residue class r = 0, which contains 10, 20, 30 and 40.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Enumerate residue classes before counting. Every class here has the same multiplicity, which is what makes the arithmetic a single multiplication.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U5-Q23", "subject": "databases", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "indexing-storage", "subtopic": "static-and-dynamic-hashing", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "In extendible hashing, the directory doubles in size when", "options": ["any bucket overflows.", "a bucket whose local depth equals the global depth overflows.", "the load factor of the file exceeds one half.", "a record is deleted from a bucket."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Extendible hashing keeps a directory of 2<sup>d</sup> entries, where d is the global depth. A bucket has its own local depth; only a bucket that is already using all d bits must force the directory to grow.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>A bucket with local depth < global depth is shared by several directory entries, so it can be split without touching the directory.</li><li>A bucket with local depth = global depth has no spare directory bit, so splitting it requires one more bit for the whole file.</li><li>Hence the directory doubles exactly when such a bucket overflows: global depth d becomes d + 1 and the directory size 2<sup>d</sup> becomes 2<sup>d+1</sup>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) A bucket whose local depth is smaller than the global depth splits without any directory change.</li><li>(C) Extendible hashing reacts to bucket overflow, not to a global load-factor threshold; that is closer to linear hashing's trigger.</li><li>(D) Deletions may allow buckets to merge, but they never double the directory.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Thinking every overflow doubles the directory. Most overflows are absorbed by splitting a shared bucket; only the deepest buckets force growth.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Compare the local and global depths before predicting a directory change. Equal depths mean doubling; a smaller local depth means a plain split.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U5-Q24", "subject": "databases", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "indexing-storage", "subtopic": "static-and-dynamic-hashing", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "Which of the following are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["In static hashing the number of buckets is fixed when the file is created.", "Linear hashing grows the file one bucket at a time and needs no directory.", "Extendible hashing keeps a directory of pointers that may double in size.", "Extendible hashing never needs overflow buckets."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The three hash organisations differ in how they accommodate growth: static hashing does not, linear hashing adds buckets one at a time, extendible hashing adds directory bits.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) True: the bucket array of a static hash file is chosen once and never resized, so growth shows up as overflow chains.</li><li>(B) True: linear hashing adds the next bucket in sequence and uses a split pointer rather than a directory.</li><li>(C) True: the directory of 2<sup>d</sup> entries doubles when the global depth increases.</li><li>(D) False: a bucket can still overflow when its local depth is below the global depth, and overflow chains are still permitted.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (C)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(D) is the only false statement. Extendible hashing reduces overflow, it does not abolish it.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reading “dynamic” as “no overflow ever”. Dynamic schemes bound the damage; they do not remove the need for overflow handling.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Learn the growth mechanism of each scheme: none (static), one bucket (linear), one bit of directory (extendible). Everything else follows.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U5-Q25", "subject": "databases", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "indexing-storage", "subtopic": "static-and-dynamic-hashing", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "An extendible hash table has global depth 2, so its directory holds 4 entries. A record is inserted into bucket  A , whose local depth is 2, and  A  overflows. A split is performed. After the split, the global depth and the directory size are", "options": ["2 and 4.", "3 and 8.", "3 and 4.", "4 and 16."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The global depth d fixes the directory size at 2<sup>d</sup>. A bucket can be split without touching the directory only if its local depth is less than d; equality forces a doubling.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Bucket A has local depth 2 and the global depth is 2, so A already uses every directory bit.</li><li>There is no spare bit with which to distinguish two halves of A, so the global depth must increase to 3.</li><li>The directory size becomes 2<sup>3</sup> = <b>8</b> entries, and A's two halves get local depth 3.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) — global depth 3 and 8 directory entries</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) The directory cannot stay at 4, because A has no bit left to split on.</li><li>(C) Global depth 3 forces 2<sup>3</sup> = 8 entries; a directory of 4 would contradict the new depth.</li><li>(D) One split raises the depth by exactly one, not by two.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Splitting the bucket and leaving the directory alone. That is only legal when the local depth is strictly below the global depth.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> One split raises the global depth by at most one. Check local against global first: equal means double the directory, smaller means split in place.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U5-Q26", "subject": "databases", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "indexing-storage", "subtopic": "primary-secondary-clustering-indexes", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "A file of employee records is physically ordered on the non-key field  dept . An index is built on  dept  so that all records of one department are reachable through one index entry. This index is a", "options": ["clustering index", "primary index", "dense secondary index", "hash index"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The three single-level index types are named by <i>what the file is ordered on</i> and by whether the indexed field is a key. A <b>primary index</b> is built on the field that orders the file when that field is the key; a <b>clustering index</b> is built on the field that orders the file when that field is <i>not</i> a key; a <b>secondary index</b> is built on a field that does not order the file at all.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">dept</code> orders the file, so the index is not secondary.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">dept</code> is not a key — many records share a value — so the index is not primary.</li><li>An index on the ordering non-key field is a clustering index, with one entry per distinct value.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A) — clustering index</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) A primary index is on the ordering field only when that field is the key; here the field has duplicates, so it is a clustering index.</li><li>(C) A dense secondary index is used when the file is <i>not</i> ordered on the indexed field.</li><li>(D) A hash index places records by a computed bucket address and destroys any physical ordering.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Calling any index on the ordering field a primary index. The word \"primary\" refers to the ordering <i>key</i>; once the ordering field is not a key the correct name is clustering.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Ordering + key → primary; ordering + non-key → clustering; not ordering → secondary (and then it must be dense).</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U5-Q27", "subject": "databases", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "indexing-storage", "subtopic": "primary-secondary-clustering-indexes", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "A file of 12,000 records occupies 800 blocks and is ordered on the non-key field  city , which has 120 distinct values. A clustering index on  city  holds how many entries?", "options": ["800", "120", "12,000", "12"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A clustering index has <b>one entry per distinct value</b> of the indexed field. Each entry carries that value together with a pointer to the first block of the run of blocks that hold the records of that value.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The index is on a non-key ordering field, so it is a clustering index.</li><li>A clustering index does not need one entry per block: all blocks of one value are contiguous, so one pointer reaches the whole run.</li><li>The number of entries is therefore the number of distinct values, 120.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) — 120 entries</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) 800 entries is the rule for a <i>primary</i> index, one entry per block; a clustering index needs only one entry per value.</li><li>(C) 12,000 entries is a <i>dense</i> index, one entry per record — never possible here, since the field is not a key.</li><li>(D) 12 is the number of blocks per distinct value on average, not the entry count.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Applying the primary-index rule \"one entry per block\" to every index on an ordering field.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Entry count: primary index = blocks; clustering index = distinct values; dense secondary index = records (or distinct values with a bucket of pointers).</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U5-Q28", "subject": "databases", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "indexing-storage", "subtopic": "primary-secondary-clustering-indexes", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "A secondary index is built on a field that neither orders the file nor is a key, so many records share each value. The standard organisation for such an index is", "options": ["one entry per record, each entry storing the whole record", "one entry per data block, holding the first key of that block", "one entry per distinct value, whose pointer leads to a block of record pointers", "no index at all, because a non-key field cannot be indexed"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> On a field that does not order the file, an index cannot skip records, so every record must be reachable from it. When the field is also not a key, storing one entry per record would repeat the same value many times, so the standard secondary index keeps <b>one entry per distinct value</b> and makes that entry point to a <b>block of record pointers</b>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The field is not the ordering field, so a sparse index is impossible — the file order cannot cover unindexed values.</li><li>The field is not a key, so a value repeats. Repeating the value in one entry per record wastes index space.</li><li>The usual fix is an extra level: one index entry per distinct value, pointing at a block that holds the pointers of all records with that value.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C) — one entry per distinct value pointing to a block of record pointers</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) An entry never stores the whole record; an index entry stores a search value and a pointer.</li><li>(B) One entry per block with the block's first key is the rule for a <i>primary</i> index, which requires the file to be ordered on the indexed field.</li><li>(D) Non-key fields are indexed routinely; that is exactly what a secondary index is for.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming a secondary index always has one entry per record. That is true for a secondary index on a key; with heavy duplication the distinct-value organisation with pointer blocks is the standard one.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Dense is forced by the absence of ordering; distinct-value entries plus pointer blocks are how the index stays compact when values repeat.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U5-Q29", "subject": "databases", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "indexing-storage", "subtopic": "primary-secondary-clustering-indexes", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "A file has 100,000 records with 20 records per block and is ordered on its key. A primary index has one entry per data block; each entry is 20 bytes and an index block holds 2048 bytes. The index is made multilevel, repeatedly indexing the previous level, until the top level fits in a single block. How many blocks does the whole multilevel index contain?", "options": ["50", "52", "5,001", "51"], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A multilevel index treats each level as an ordered file and indexes it, so the entry count of one level is the <i>block</i> count of the level below. The total is the sum of the levels, including the single top block.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Data blocks = 100,000 ÷ 20 = <b>5,000</b>, so the first level has 5,000 entries.</li><li>Entries per index block = ⌊2048 ÷ 20⌋ = <b>102</b>.</li><li>First level = ⌈5,000 ÷ 102⌉ = ⌈49.02⌉ = <b>50</b> blocks.</li><li>Second level = ⌈50 ÷ 102⌉ = <b>1</b> block, so the structure stops here.</li><li>Total = 50 + 1 = <b>51</b> blocks.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (D) — 51 blocks</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) 50 counts only the first level and forgets the top block that indexes it.</li><li>(B) 52 invents a level: 50 already fits in one block, so there is no third level.</li><li>(C) 5,001 adds one to the number of data blocks; the index is far smaller than the file.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Summing the levels but forgetting the top block, or adding a level that is not needed because the previous level already fits in one block.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Total multilevel blocks = ⌈N/b⌉ + ⌈⌈N/b⌉/b⌉ + … , stopping as soon as a level equals 1.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U5-Q30", "subject": "databases", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "indexing-storage", "subtopic": "primary-secondary-clustering-indexes", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "Which of the following statements about single-level indexes on a file of records are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["A clustering index is built on a non-key field that orders the file.", "A file may have more than one primary index at a time.", "A secondary index on a field that does not order the file must reach every record, so it cannot be sparse.", "A primary index entry stores the key of the first record in a block together with a pointer to that block."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (C) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The index types are defined by the field they are built on and by that field's relation to the file order. \"Primary\" is tied to the single ordering <i>key</i>; sparsity is possible only where the physical order covers the records between two index entries.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) TRUE — a clustering index is precisely the index on the field that orders the file when that field is not a key.</li><li>(B) FALSE — a file has exactly one ordering key, so it has at most one primary index.</li><li>(C) TRUE — without ordering, an index entry cannot cover the records between entries, so every record must be reachable, i.e. the index is dense (possibly with pointer blocks).</li><li>(D) TRUE — that is the definition of a sparse primary-index entry.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (C) and (D)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) Two primary indexes would need two different physical orderings of the same file.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Believing a file can carry several primary indexes, or that a secondary index may be sparse.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> One ordering key → at most one primary index. No ordering → no sparsity.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U5-Q31", "subject": "databases", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "indexing-storage", "subtopic": "primary-secondary-clustering-indexes", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "A dense secondary index is built on the search key of a file of 10,000 records. Each index entry holds a 12-byte key and a 6-byte block pointer, and index blocks are 1024 bytes. How many blocks does the index occupy? (Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "179", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A dense index on a key has one entry per record. The number of blocks follows from how many whole entries fit in one block, and the last partly filled block is still allocated.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>A dense index on the key has <b>10,000</b> entries.</li><li>One entry = 12 + 6 = <b>18 bytes</b>, so one block holds ⌊1024 ÷ 18⌋ = <b>56</b> entries.</li><li>Blocks = ⌈10,000 ÷ 56⌉ = ⌈178.57⌉ = <b>179</b>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 179 (an integer number of blocks; the final block is partly empty but is still allocated)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Rounding down to 178 and dropping the partial final block, or forgetting that the pointer also occupies space in the entry.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Always ceil the block count: ⌈entries ÷ entries-per-block⌉.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U5-Q32", "subject": "databases", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "indexing-storage", "subtopic": "dense-vs-sparse-indexing", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "A sparse index on the search key of a file can be built only when", "options": ["the file is physically ordered on that key", "the key values are unique", "the file occupies at most one block", "the index is made multilevel"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Sparsity means that some search-key values have no index entry. That is safe only if the file order guarantees that a missing value lies between the values of two neighbouring entries, which requires the file to be ordered on the indexed field.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>A sparse entry is a \"starting point\": it locates the block from which a scan begins.</li><li>The scan only finds the record if all values between two entries lie in the blocks that follow the first entry — that is exactly what physical ordering guarantees.</li><li>Without ordering, an unindexed value could sit anywhere in the file, so a sparse index would miss it.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A) — the file must be physically ordered on that key</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) Uniqueness is not needed: a sparse index on an ordered non-key field is a clustering index.</li><li>(C) A one-block file needs no index at all; the condition is about ordering, not size.</li><li>(D) Multilevel indexes are built on top of an existing first-level index and do not create the ability to be sparse.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Thinking sparsity depends on the key being unique. It depends on the <i>file order</i>, which is why a clustering index on an ordered non-key field is also sparse.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Sparsity ⇔ the indexed field orders the file.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U5-Q33", "subject": "databases", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "indexing-storage", "subtopic": "dense-vs-sparse-indexing", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "A file of 45,000 records is stored with 9 records per block and is ordered on its search key. A sparse index stores one entry per block. How many entries does the index have?", "options": ["45,000", "5,000", "9", "4,500"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A sparse index on the ordering key keeps one entry per <b>data block</b>, because the block's first key identifies every record in that block.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Blocks = 45,000 ÷ 9 = <b>5,000</b>.</li><li>One entry per block gives 5,000 entries.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) — 5,000 entries</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) 45,000 is the record count, which would be a dense index.</li><li>(C) 9 is the number of records per block, not a count of entries.</li><li>(D) 4,500 divides by 10 rather than by the 9 records per block.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Dividing by a round number instead of by the stated records-per-block.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Sparse entries on an ordering key = number of data blocks = ⌈records ÷ records-per-block⌉.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U5-Q34", "subject": "databases", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "indexing-storage", "subtopic": "dense-vs-sparse-indexing", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "For a successful point search on the ordering key, a sparse index and a dense index on the same key both cost one index search plus one data-block read. The sparse index needs no extra block read because", "options": ["a sparse index never needs to read the data file", "the sparse index stores the full record in each entry", "the extra scan happens inside the data block that was already read", "the dense index needs two data-block reads"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A sparse entry points at the block whose key range contains the search value. That block has to be read anyway, and the remaining scan takes place inside it, so no additional I/O is added.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The index search ends at the largest entry whose key is ≤ the target.</li><li>That entry supplies the address of the data block where the target must lie.</li><li>Reading that block is the one data-block access the search needs; scanning it costs CPU time, not another block access.</li><li>A dense index reaches the same block through an exact entry, so its cost is identical.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C) — the scan is absorbed by the data block that is read anyway</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) Every index search ends with a data-block read; no index avoids it.</li><li>(B) An index entry stores a search value and a pointer, never the full record.</li><li>(D) A dense index needs exactly one data-block read, not two.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Adding an extra block access for the in-block scan. The scan is a CPU cost inside a block that the search reads regardless.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Sparse and dense indexes have the same I/O for a point search; sparsity buys <i>index size</i>, not search time.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U5-Q35", "subject": "databases", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "indexing-storage", "subtopic": "dense-vs-sparse-indexing", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "A file of 60,000 records is stored with 15 records per block and is ordered on its key. A dense index on the key uses 10-byte keys and 6-byte pointers in 1024-byte blocks. How many blocks does the dense index occupy?", "options": ["937", "63", "4,000", "938"], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A dense index on a key holds one entry per record, so its block count is ⌈records ÷ entries-per-block⌉. Compare that with the sparse index on the same file, which holds one entry per block.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>A dense index has one entry per record = <b>60,000</b> entries.</li><li>One entry = 10 + 6 = <b>16 bytes</b>, so one index block holds ⌊1024 ÷ 16⌋ = <b>64</b> entries.</li><li>Dense blocks = ⌈60,000 ÷ 64⌉ = ⌈937.5⌉ = <b>938</b>.</li><li>For contrast the sparse index would need 60,000 ÷ 15 = 4,000 entries, i.e. ⌈4,000 ÷ 64⌉ = 63 blocks.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (D) — 938 blocks</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) 937 truncates 937.5 and loses the partly filled final block.</li><li>(B) 63 is the size of the <i>sparse</i> index on the same file, one entry per block.</li><li>(C) 4,000 is the sparse index's entry count, not the dense index's block count.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Confusing entry counts with block counts, or truncating instead of taking the ceiling.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Dense index entries = records; sparse index entries = blocks. Divide each by entries-per-block and ceil.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U5-Q36", "subject": "databases", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "indexing-storage", "subtopic": "dense-vs-sparse-indexing", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "Which of the following are TRUE of dense and sparse indexes? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["A sparse index holds fewer entries than a dense index on the same ordered file.", "A dense index can be built on a file stored in any order.", "A sparse index always answers an existence query without reading the data file.", "A sparse index on a non-ordering field is impossible."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Density is about whether every search value has an entry; sparsity is about whether the file order lets the index skip records. Ordering is the resource that sparsity spends.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) TRUE — a sparse index drops entries that the file order makes redundant, so it has fewer of them (and therefore fewer blocks).</li><li>(B) TRUE — a dense index does not rely on the file order at all; that is why it is the only option on an unordered file.</li><li>(C) FALSE — an existence query on a sparse index must still read the candidate block to see whether the value is actually present.</li><li>(D) TRUE — with no ordering, an unindexed value could be anywhere in the file.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (D)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(C) Sparsity means some values have no entry, so absence from the index is not absence from the file; the block must be read.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Treating a sparse index as a complete membership test. It is not: it narrows the search to one block, nothing more.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Dense → membership decidable from the index. Sparse → always one confirming block read.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U5-Q37", "subject": "databases", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "indexing-storage", "subtopic": "dense-vs-sparse-indexing", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "A file of 30,000 records is stored with 12 records per block and is ordered on its search key. A sparse index has one entry per block; each entry is 12 bytes and index blocks are 1024 bytes. How many blocks does the index occupy? (Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "30", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A sparse index on the ordering key has one entry per data block. Its own block count then follows from the entry size and the block size.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Data blocks = 30,000 ÷ 12 = <b>2,500</b>, so the index has 2,500 entries.</li><li>One block holds ⌊1024 ÷ 12⌋ = <b>85</b> entries.</li><li>Blocks = ⌈2,500 ÷ 85⌉ = ⌈29.41⌉ = <b>30</b>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 30 (the last block is partly empty but is still allocated)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Taking ⌊2,500 ÷ 85⌋ = 29 and dropping the partial final block.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Two ceilings are hidden here: one for data blocks and one for index blocks.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U5-Q38", "subject": "databases", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "indexing-storage", "subtopic": "b-trees-and-b-plus-trees-order-capacity", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "A B+ tree internal node holds  p  tree pointers and  p − 1  search keys. The block size is 1024 bytes, a key is 10 bytes and a pointer is 8 bytes. The largest  p  that fits in one internal node is", "options": ["57", "58", "64", "68"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The order of a B+ tree is fixed by the block size: one node holds <code class=\"mm\">p</code> pointers and <code class=\"mm\">p − 1</code> keys, and the total must fit inside one block.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Space = 8p + 10(p − 1) = 18p − 10 bytes.</li><li>Require 18p − 10 ≤ 1024, so 18p ≤ 1034 and p ≤ 57.44.</li><li>The largest integer is <b>57</b>; check: 8(57) + 10(56) = 456 + 560 = 1016 ≤ 1024, while p = 58 needs 464 + 570 = 1034 > 1024.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A) — p = 57</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) p = 58 needs 1034 bytes, ten bytes more than the block holds.</li><li>(C) 64 is the answer for a 10-byte key with a 6-byte pointer, not an 8-byte pointer.</li><li>(D) 68 ignores the key bytes entirely.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Forgetting that a node with p pointers has p − 1 keys, or dividing the block size by the pointer size alone.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Solve the inequality p·ptr + (p − 1)·key ≤ block, then floor.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U5-Q39", "subject": "databases", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "indexing-storage", "subtopic": "b-trees-and-b-plus-trees-order-capacity", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "A B+ tree has order  p = 7 , so every node holds at most 7 tree pointers. A non-root internal node must hold at least ⌈p/2⌉ = 4 pointers. The minimum number of keys in such a node is", "options": ["2", "3", "4", "6"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> In every B+ tree node the number of keys is one less than the number of tree pointers. Occupancy bounds therefore shift by exactly one when translated from pointers to keys.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Minimum pointers = ⌈7/2⌉ = 4.</li><li>Keys = pointers − 1 = <b>3</b>.</li><li>For symmetry the maximum is p − 1 = 6 keys, from 7 pointers.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) — 3 keys</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) 2 keys corresponds to 3 pointers, below the ⌈p/2⌉ = 4 floor.</li><li>(C) 4 is the pointer floor, not the key floor.</li><li>(D) 6 is the maximum number of keys, not the minimum.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Quoting the pointer bound as the key bound; a node with k keys has k + 1 pointers.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> keys = pointers − 1, always, in every internal node of a B-tree or B+ tree.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U5-Q40", "subject": "databases", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "indexing-storage", "subtopic": "b-trees-and-b-plus-trees-order-capacity", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "Which one of the following is a difference between a B-tree and a B+ tree of the same order?", "options": ["A B-tree links its leaves in sequence, while a B+ tree does not.", "A B+ tree allows its nodes to be under-full, while a B-tree does not.", "A B+ tree stores records only in its leaves, while a B-tree may store records in internal nodes.", "A B-tree can index only primary keys, while a B+ tree can index only secondary keys."], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> In a B-tree every node carries keys <i>and</i> data (or data pointers), so a search may end at an internal node. In a B+ tree internal nodes carry only keys and pointers; all data pointers sit in the leaves, which are chained together.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>A B-tree node = keys + data pointers; a search stops as soon as the key is found in any node.</li><li>A B+ tree node = keys + tree pointers only; every search runs down to a leaf, and the leaves hold all record pointers.</li><li>Because the leaves form a linked list, range queries walk the leaf level sequentially — a B-tree must re-traverse the tree instead.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C) — data lives only in the leaves of a B+ tree</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) It is the B+ tree, not the B-tree, that links its leaves.</li><li>(B) Both structures enforce minimum occupancy; under-full nodes are merged in either.</li><li>(D) Either structure can serve as a primary, clustering or secondary index.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reversing the leaf-link property. The chained leaves belong to the B+ tree, and that chain is what makes range scans cheap.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> B+ tree: all data at the leaf level, leaves linked, every search costs the full height. B-tree: data anywhere, search may stop early.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U5-Q41", "subject": "databases", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "indexing-storage", "subtopic": "b-trees-and-b-plus-trees-order-capacity", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "Which of the following are TRUE of a B+ tree of order  p ? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["An internal node holds at most p − 1 keys.", "A leaf node holds at most p keys.", "A non-root internal node holds at least ⌈p/2⌉ tree pointers.", "All leaves are at the same level, so the tree is balanced."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (C) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Order p fixes the maximum number of tree pointers in a node at p. Keys are one fewer, and the minimum occupancy of a non-root node is half the maximum, rounded up.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) TRUE — p pointers admit at most p − 1 keys.</li><li>(B) FALSE — a leaf holds at most p − 1 keys (or p − 1 record values); p would require p + 1 pointers, one of which is the sibling link.</li><li>(C) TRUE — that is the occupancy floor that keeps the tree at least half full.</li><li>(D) TRUE — every leaf is at the same depth, which is what makes every search cost the same number of block accesses.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (C) and (D)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) Including the next-leaf pointer, a full leaf holds p − 1 keys, p record pointers and one sibling pointer.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Applying the internal-node capacity p − 1 keys to the leaves without accounting for the extra next-leaf pointer, or the reverse.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Internal node: p pointers, p − 1 keys. Leaf: p − 1 keys and p record pointers, plus one sibling pointer.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U5-Q42", "subject": "databases", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "indexing-storage", "subtopic": "b-trees-and-b-plus-trees-order-capacity", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "Consider a B+ tree used as an index on a file of 1,000,000 records. Which of the following are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["Increasing the order p reduces the height of the tree.", "A range query is answered by locating the first leaf and then following the leaf sibling pointers.", "With no node buffered, a point search costs one block access per level of the tree.", "Deleting a record can never reduce the number of levels in the tree."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The height of a B+ tree is what a search pays for, and the height falls as the fan-out rises. The linked leaf level is what makes a range query cost one descent plus a sequential walk.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) TRUE — with a larger p each node covers more keys, so fewer levels are needed for the same record count.</li><li>(B) TRUE — the sibling chain visits every leaf in key order, so the range is read sequentially.</li><li>(C) TRUE — the root, each internal level and the leaf are each one block, so the cost equals the height.</li><li>(D) FALSE — an under-full node is merged, and if the root loses its last key the root is removed and the height drops by one.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (C)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(D) Merging on deletion can empty the root; the tree then shrinks by a level.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Treating the height of a B+ tree as fixed. It grows on splits and shrinks on merges.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Height drives search cost; leaf links drive range cost; both change with insertions and deletions.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U5-Q43", "subject": "databases", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "indexing-storage", "subtopic": "b-trees-and-b-plus-trees-order-capacity", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "A B+ tree has order  p = 5  and three levels: a root, one level of internal nodes, and leaves. A non-root internal node holds at least ⌈p/2⌉ = 3 pointers, a non-root leaf holds at least ⌈(p − 1)/2⌉ = 2 records, and the root holds at least 2 pointers. What is the  smallest  number of records such a tree can index?", "options": ["6", "18", "24", "12"], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A minimum-capacity count multiplies the smallest fan-out at every level. The root is special: it needs only two children, not ⌈p/2⌉.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Root: at least 2 pointers → <b>2</b> children at the internal level.</li><li>Each of those 2 internal nodes: at least 3 pointers → 2 × 3 = <b>6</b> leaves.</li><li>Each of those 6 leaves: at least 2 records → 6 × 2 = <b>12</b> records.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (D) — 12 records</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) 6 counts the leaves, not the records they hold.</li><li>(B) 18 applies ⌈p/2⌉ = 3 to the root as well; the root may have as few as 2 children.</li><li>(C) 24 uses the maximum fan-out at some level instead of the minimum.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Applying the ⌈p/2⌉ occupancy floor to the root. The root is exempt: with a single key it has two children.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Minimum records = (root fan-out) × ⌈p/2⌉^(h−2) × ⌈(p−1)/2⌉, with the root counted as 2.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U5-Q44", "subject": "databases", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "indexing-storage", "subtopic": "b-trees-and-b-plus-trees-order-capacity", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "A B-tree of order  p  stores at most  p  tree pointers and  p − 1  keys in every node. The block size is 4096 bytes, a key is 16 bytes and a pointer is 8 bytes. What is the largest order  p  that fits in one node? (Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "171", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Node capacity is the largest p satisfying p·pointer + (p − 1)·key ≤ block size. It is an inequality, so the answer is a floor, never a rounding.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Space per node = 8p + 16(p − 1) = 24p − 16 bytes.</li><li>Require 24p − 16 ≤ 4096, so 24p ≤ 4112 and p ≤ 171.33.</li><li>Largest integer p = <b>171</b>; check: 8(171) + 16(170) = 1368 + 2720 = 4088 ≤ 4096, while p = 172 needs 1376 + 2736 = 4112 > 4096.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 171 (the order, an integer; 4088 of the 4096 bytes are used)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Using p keys instead of p − 1, or rounding 171.33 up to 172 — which overflows the block by 16 bytes.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Write the inequality first, solve for p, floor it, then check both p and p + 1.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U5-Q45", "subject": "databases", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "indexing-storage", "subtopic": "static-and-dynamic-hashing", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "In extendible hashing with global depth  d , the number of directory entries is", "options": ["2d", "d", "the number of buckets", "twice the number of buckets"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The directory is indexed by the first <code class=\"mm\">d</code> bits of the hash value, where <code class=\"mm\">d</code> is the global depth, so it has one entry per bit pattern — <code class=\"mm\">2<sup>d</sup></code> of them.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Global depth d means the directory is addressed by d hash bits.</li><li>The number of distinct d-bit patterns is 2^d.</li><li>Several directory entries may point at the same bucket when that bucket's local depth is less than d.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A) — 2^d entries</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) d is the number of bits used, not the number of entries.</li><li>(C) The directory is usually larger than the bucket count; that is the whole point of local depths.</li><li>(D) There is no fixed ratio of 2 between directory entries and buckets.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Equating directory size with bucket count. A bucket of local depth less than the global depth is shared by 2^(d − local depth) directory entries.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> directory entries = 2^global depth; entries per bucket = 2^(global − local).</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U5-Q46", "subject": "databases", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "indexing-storage", "subtopic": "static-and-dynamic-hashing", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "An extendible hash table has global depth 3 and a bucket  B  with local depth 2. The number of directory entries that point to  B  is", "options": ["1", "2", "4", "8"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A bucket of local depth ℓ is addressed by ℓ bits, while the directory is addressed by d ≥ ℓ bits. The 2^(d − ℓ) directory entries that share the same ℓ low-order bits all point at that bucket.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Global depth d = 3, local depth ℓ = 2.</li><li>Entries sharing bucket B = 2^(d − ℓ) = 2^(3 − 2) = <b>2</b>.</li><li>Those two directory entries differ only in the most significant bit used by the directory.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) — 2 directory entries</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) 1 entry per bucket happens only when the local depth equals the global depth.</li><li>(C) 4 = 2^(3 − 1) would be the count for a bucket of local depth 1.</li><li>(D) 8 is the whole directory.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reading the local depth as a count of directory entries instead of as a number of hash bits.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Pointers to a bucket = 2^(global depth − local depth).</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U5-Q47", "subject": "databases", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "indexing-storage", "subtopic": "static-and-dynamic-hashing", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "Which of the following are TRUE of  static  hashing? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["A bucket overflow in a static hash file is resolved by doubling the number of buckets.", "The number of buckets is fixed when the file is created.", "Records that do not fit in their home bucket are kept in overflow buckets.", "As the file grows, the average overflow chain grows and the search cost rises."], "answer_display": "Options (B), (C) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Static hashing allocates a fixed bucket array up front. Overflow is absorbed by chaining, so the file can grow without limit but the chains grow with it, and the constant expected search cost of hashing is lost.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) FALSE — doubling the bucket array and rehashing everything is precisely what static hashing does <i>not</i> do; that is the dynamic-hashing remedy.</li><li>(B) TRUE — the bucket count is chosen at file creation from the expected file size.</li><li>(C) TRUE — overflow buckets chained to the home bucket hold the excess records.</li><li>(D) TRUE — as the number of records per bucket exceeds the bucket capacity, the chains lengthen and the average number of block accesses per search rises.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (B), (C) and (D)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) Rehashing the whole file is what linear and extendible hashing add; static hashing cannot do it cheaply.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Mixing static hashing with dynamic hashing. Static = fixed buckets + overflow chains; dynamic = grow the file, no long chains.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Static hashing trades unbounded chain growth for a fixed, predictable bucket array.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U5-Q48", "subject": "databases", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "indexing-storage", "subtopic": "static-and-dynamic-hashing", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "An extendible hash table has global depth 3, so its directory has 8 entries. Bucket  C  has local depth 1. How many directory entries point to bucket  C ? (Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "4", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The number of directory entries sharing one bucket is 2 raised to the difference between the global depth and that bucket's local depth.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Global depth d = 3, local depth ℓ = 1.</li><li>Pointers = 2^(d − ℓ) = 2^(3 − 1) = 2² = <b>4</b>.</li><li>Bucket C is addressed by one hash bit, so four of the eight directory entries — the ones agreeing in that bit — point at it.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 4 directory entries</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering 2 (using 2^ℓ) or 8 (the whole directory). The exponent is the <i>difference</i> of the two depths.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Pointers to a bucket = 2^(global depth − local depth); the exponents subtract.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U5-Q49", "subject": "databases", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "indexing-storage", "subtopic": "static-and-dynamic-hashing", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "A linear hash file starts with 8 buckets. A split is performed after every 4 insertions: one new bucket is appended to the file and the split pointer advances to the next bucket. After 40 insertions, how many buckets does the file have? (Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "18", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Linear hashing grows the file one bucket at a time, driven by a load threshold rather than by a directory. The number of buckets is the initial count plus the number of splits performed.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Splits = 40 ÷ 4 = <b>10</b>.</li><li>Buckets = 8 + 10 = <b>18</b>.</li><li>Because splits are triggered one at a time in round-robin order, no bucket ever needs a chain as long as it would in a static file.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 18 buckets</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Multiplying the insertions by the split rate instead of dividing, or forgetting to add the initial 8 buckets.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Linear hashing: buckets = initial buckets + number of splits; growth is one bucket at a time and needs no directory.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U5-Q50", "subject": "databases", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "indexing-storage", "subtopic": "static-and-dynamic-hashing", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-01", "text": "An extendible hash table has global depth 2, so its directory has 4 entries, and every bucket currently has local depth 2. A record is inserted into one bucket and that bucket overflows. After the split of that bucket, the global depth and the number of directory entries are", "options": ["2 and 4", "2 and 5", "3 and 8", "3 and 4"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A bucket can be split without touching the directory only if its local depth is below the global depth. When the two are equal there is no spare directory entry, so the directory must double first.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The overflowing bucket has local depth 2, equal to the global depth 2.</li><li>A split would need two directory entries per hash prefix, but only one currently points at this bucket.</li><li>So the directory doubles: global depth 2 → <b>3</b>, entries 4 → <b>8</b>.</li><li>Each old bucket is now referenced by two entries, and the overflowing bucket is split into two, each with local depth 3.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C) — global depth 3 and 8 directory entries</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) A split is impossible while local depth equals global depth; the directory must grow first.</li><li>(B) A directory is a power-of-two array indexed by hash bits; it cannot grow by one entry.</li><li>(D) Global depth 3 forces 2³ = 8 entries, not 4.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Splitting the bucket without doubling the directory when local depth already equals global depth. That is exactly the condition that triggers the doubling.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Directory doubles ⇔ the overflowing bucket's local depth equals the global depth; otherwise only that bucket's local depth increases.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U1B-Q01", "subject": "databases", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "er-relational-algebra", "subtopic": "er-diagrams-cardinality-weak-entities", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "In an entity&ndash;relationship diagram, the identifying relationship of a weak entity set is drawn as", "options": ["a double rectangle.", "a double diamond.", "a single diamond drawn with a dashed outline.", "a double ellipse."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Chen notation gives a weak entity set and its identifying relationship two different symbols. The <b>weak entity set</b> is a double rectangle; the <b>identifying relationship</b> through which it is identified is a double diamond. The partial key is underlined with a dashed line and the weak set's total participation in that relationship is shown by a double line.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>A weak entity set has no key attribute of its own. It is identified only by the pair (owner entity key, partial key).</li><li>The relationship that supplies the owner key is therefore not an ordinary relationship: it carries the identification, so it is drawn with the double outline.</li><li>The double outline on a <i>diamond</i> is the identifying relationship; on a <i>rectangle</i> it is the weak entity set itself.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) &mdash; a double diamond</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) A double rectangle is the weak entity set, not the relationship that identifies it.</li><li>(C) Chen notation has no dashed diamond; dashes are used under a partial key, not around a diamond.</li><li>(D) A double ellipse is a multivalued attribute.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Confusing the symbol for the weak entity set with the symbol for its identifying relationship. Both are double outlined, but the shape decides the meaning.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Shape says what the object is (rectangle = set, diamond = relationship, ellipse = attribute); the double outline says it is the weak or identifying one.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U1B-Q02", "subject": "databases", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "er-relational-algebra", "subtopic": "er-diagrams-cardinality-weak-entities", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "In an entity&ndash;relationship diagram, a  double line  joining an entity set to a relationship set expresses", "options": ["partial participation of that entity set in the relationship.", "a many-to-many cardinality ratio.", "total participation of that entity set in the relationship.", "that the entity set is weak."], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Participation is drawn on the edge between an entity set and a relationship set. A single line is partial participation (some entities take part); a double line is <b>total participation</b> (every entity of the set takes part in at least one relationship instance).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Cardinality (1:1, 1:N, M:N) is written on the edge as a number or letter, not as a line style.</li><li>Line multiplicity on the edge encodes participation: single = partial, double = total.</li><li>So a double line between an entity set and a relationship set means every entity of that set must participate.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C) &mdash; total participation</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) Partial participation is the single line.</li><li>(B) Cardinality is a separate annotation (1, N, M) placed on the edge.</li><li>(D) A weak entity set is marked by a double rectangle around the set, not by the edge.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reading the double line as a cardinality. Cardinality counts how many; participation says whether every entity must appear.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Double edge = every entity participates. Numbers on the edge = how many participate.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U1B-Q03", "subject": "databases", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "er-relational-algebra", "subtopic": "er-diagrams-cardinality-weak-entities", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "An ER schema has entity sets  Department  and  Employee  in a 1:N relationship: one department has many employees, and every employee belongs to exactly one department. Translating the schema to relations, what is the minimum number of relation schemas?", "options": ["2", "1", "3", "4"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A 1:N relationship needs no relation of its own. Each entity on the N side is associated with at most one entity on the 1 side, so the key of the 1 side can be carried into the N side as a foreign key. The relationship is absorbed; only the two entity sets become relations.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Every entity set becomes a relation, so <code class=\"mm\">Department</code> and <code class=\"mm\">Employee</code> already give two schemas.</li><li>For 1:N, put the key of the 1 side (<code class=\"mm\">dept_id</code>) into the relation of the N side as a foreign key: <code class=\"mm\">Employee(emp_id, name, dept_id)</code>.</li><li>That foreign key holds the whole relationship, so no third schema is needed. Total = 2.</li><li>Because the participation of <code class=\"mm\">Employee</code> is total, the foreign key is NOT NULL &mdash; still one column, not one relation.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A) &mdash; 2 relation schemas</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) A single relation would force employee data to repeat once per department row, which is the redundancy normalisation removes.</li><li>(C) A third relation is needed only for an M:N relationship, where one row cannot hold both keys without duplication.</li><li>(D) Four schemas would arise from mapping every relationship and every multivalued attribute separately, which this schema does not contain.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Adding a relation for the relationship out of habit. A 1:N relationship is always absorbed into the N side; only M:N relationships and multivalued attributes need a relation of their own.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> 1:N costs nothing extra. 1:1 can be absorbed into either side. M:N costs one relation.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U1B-Q04", "subject": "databases", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "er-relational-algebra", "subtopic": "er-diagrams-cardinality-weak-entities", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "An ER schema contains 3 strong entity sets, 1 weak entity set whose identifying relationship links it to one of the strong sets, and 1 many-to-many relationship set between two of the strong sets. The weak entity set has one multivalued attribute. What is the minimum number of relation schemas in the relational translation? (Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "5", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Count by rule, not by entity: every strong entity set is one relation; a weak entity set is one relation that also carries its owner's key; an M:N relationship set is one relation; a multivalued attribute is one relation; an identifying relationship and any 1:N or 1:1 relationship add nothing.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>3 strong entity sets &rarr; 3 relations.</li><li>1 weak entity set &rarr; 1 relation, keyed by (owner key, partial key). Its identifying relationship is absorbed into that relation and adds 0.</li><li>1 M:N relationship set &rarr; 1 relation holding the two participating keys plus any descriptive attributes.</li><li>1 multivalued attribute &rarr; 1 relation keyed by (owner key, attribute value).</li><li>Total = 3 + 1 + 1 + 1 = 5.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 5 relation schemas</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Two traps: counting the identifying relationship as a relation of its own (it is absorbed by the weak entity's relation), and forgetting that a multivalued attribute needs its own relation because a single cell cannot hold a set of values.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Relations = strong sets + weak sets + M:N relationships + multivalued attributes. Nothing else.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U1B-Q05", "subject": "databases", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "er-relational-algebra", "subtopic": "er-diagrams-cardinality-weak-entities", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "Which of the following statements about translating an ER schema into relations are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["A composite attribute is mapped to one separate relation for each of its components.", "A multivalued attribute is mapped to its own relation, keyed by the owning entity's key together with the attribute value.", "A ternary relationship is equivalent to three binary relationships and may therefore be dropped.", "A 1:N relationship can be represented without a separate relation, by placing the key of the 1 side into the relation of the N side."], "answer_display": "Options (B) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Each ER construct has exactly one standard mapping. Composite attributes are flattened into one column per component, never split into relations. Multivalued attributes cannot be flattened (a cell holds one value), so they become a relation keyed by owner key plus value. A 1:N relationship is absorbed as a foreign key on the N side. A ternary relationship is <i>not</i> three binaries.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(B) is the standard multivalued-attribute rule: <code class=\"mm\">Phone(emp_id, phone_no)</code> for a multivalued <code class=\"mm\">Phone</code> of <code class=\"mm\">Employee</code>.</li><li>(D) is the standard 1:N rule: each N-side entity has at most one 1-side partner, so one foreign key column suffices.</li><li>(A) is false: components of a composite attribute become ordinary columns of the same relation.</li><li>(C) is false: a ternary relationship can carry a constraint no pair of binaries can express, so it may lose information.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (B) and (D)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) Splitting a composite attribute into relations multiplies joins for no gain; its components are simply columns.</li><li>(C) Ternary and three-binary schemas admit different sets of instances, so they are not equivalent.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Treating composite and multivalued attributes as if they behaved the same way. Composite = flattened columns; multivalued = separate relation.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Flatten what fits in a cell, add a relation for what does not.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U1B-Q06", "subject": "databases", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "er-relational-algebra", "subtopic": "er-diagrams-cardinality-weak-entities", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "A ternary relationship set  R  associates entity sets  A ,  B  and  C . Which of the following statements is TRUE?", "options": ["R can always be replaced by three binary relationships without losing any information.", "The participation and cardinality constraints of R cannot in general be derived from the three pairwise binary relationships between A, B and C.", "R never needs a relation of its own provided at least one of A, B or C participates totally.", "R is legal only when all three of A, B and C are weak entity sets."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A ternary relationship is a set of triples. Three binary relationships are three sets of pairs, and a set of triples is not recoverable from its three pairwise projections: the projections lose the correlation between the three participants. That is why a ternary relationship must be modelled as a single construct.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>A triple (a, b, c) in R projects to (a, b), (b, c) and (a, c) in the three binaries.</li><li>Different ternary instances can share all three projections &mdash; the classic supply example, where the three binaries admit triples that the ternary constraint forbids.</li><li>So neither the instance nor the constraints of R are determined by the pairwise relationships. (B) follows.</li><li>R becomes a relation whenever its cardinality is many-to-many in some direction; total participation alone does not remove that need.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) &mdash; ternary constraints are not derivable from pairwise ones</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) Replacing R by three binaries can create spurious triples on a join &mdash; a lossy change.</li><li>(C) Absorption is decided by cardinality, not by participation.</li><li>(D) Weakness of the participants is unrelated to the arity of the relationship.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming arity is cosmetic. A ternary relationship encodes a correlation that no collection of binary relationships carries.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> A relationship of degree n is a set of n-tuples; its pairwise projections lose information whenever n &ge; 3.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U1B-Q07", "subject": "databases", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "er-relational-algebra", "subtopic": "er-diagrams-cardinality-weak-entities", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "In an ER diagram, an attribute name underlined with a  dashed  line denotes", "options": ["a derived attribute.", "a multivalued attribute.", "the primary key of a strong entity set.", "a partial key (discriminator) of a weak entity set."], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Underlining is about identification. A solid underline marks the primary key of a strong entity set. A dashed underline marks a <b>partial key</b>: an attribute that distinguishes weak entities belonging to the <i>same</i> owner but does not identify them globally.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>A weak entity set has no key of its own, so it cannot carry a solid underline.</li><li>Within one owner entity, a discriminator such as <code class=\"mm\">dependent_name</code> separates the weak entities. That attribute is the partial key.</li><li>Chen notation distinguishes it from a full key by using a dashed underline.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (D) &mdash; a partial key (discriminator)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) A derived attribute is drawn with a dashed <i>ellipse</i>, not a dashed underline.</li><li>(B) A multivalued attribute is drawn with a double ellipse.</li><li>(C) The primary key of a strong entity set is a solid underline.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Confusing the dashed ellipse (derived) with the dashed underline (partial key). The shape marks the attribute's kind; the line under it marks its identifying role.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Solid underline = key; dashed underline = partial key; double ellipse = multivalued; dashed ellipse = derived.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U1B-Q08", "subject": "databases", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "er-relational-algebra", "subtopic": "relational-model-constraints", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "A foreign key constraint on attribute  R.D  referencing the key  S.B  enforces that", "options": ["the values of R.D must be a candidate key of R.", "no tuple of S may be updated while R exists.", "every non-null value of R.D appears as a value of S.B.", "the values of R.D can never be null."], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A foreign key is a referential integrity constraint: it restricts the <i>values</i> of the referencing attribute, not their number or nullability. Each non-null value must match some value of the referenced key. A null foreign key is allowed and means &lsquo;no reference&rsquo;.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Referential integrity says: <code class=\"mm\">∀ t ∈ R : t[D] = NULL ∨ ∃ s ∈ S : s[B] = t[D]</code>.</li><li>The referenced side must be a key (primary or unique), which is why at most one tuple of <code class=\"mm\">S</code> can match a given value.</li><li>Nulls are exempt from the check, so a foreign key column may be null unless it is separately declared NOT NULL.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C) &mdash; every non-null value must appear in the referenced key</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) A foreign key is usually <i>not</i> unique in <code class=\"mm\">R</code>; many tuples may reference the same row of <code class=\"mm\">S</code>.</li><li>(B) Updating <code class=\"mm\">S</code> is controlled by the declared action (RESTRICT, CASCADE, SET NULL), not forbidden outright.</li><li>(D) Nullability is a separate NOT NULL constraint; a foreign key alone permits nulls.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Believing a foreign key must be unique or non-null. Uniqueness belongs to the referenced key; the referencing column may repeat values and may be null.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Foreign key = values must exist on the other side, or be NULL. Nothing more.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U1B-Q09", "subject": "databases", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "er-relational-algebra", "subtopic": "relational-model-constraints", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "A relation schema  R(A, B, C, D)  has exactly one declared key, the primary key  A . How many superkeys does  R  have? (Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "8", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A superkey is any attribute set that contains a candidate key. Once <code class=\"mm\">A</code> is in the set the set determines every attribute, so the superkeys are exactly the subsets of <code class=\"mm\">{A, B, C, D}</code> that contain <code class=\"mm\">A</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Fix <code class=\"mm\">A</code> in the set. The remaining attributes <code class=\"mm\">B, C, D</code> may each be present or absent, independently.</li><li>Number of such sets = <code class=\"mm\">2³</code> = 8.</li><li>They are <code class=\"mm\">A, AB, AC, AD, ABC, ABD, ACD, ABCD</code> &mdash; all superkeys.</li><li>Only <code class=\"mm\">A</code> is minimal, so <code class=\"mm\">R</code> has one candidate key but eight superkeys.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 8 superkeys</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reporting 1, the number of candidate keys. A superkey need not be minimal; only the candidate keys are.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> With n attributes and one key of size k, the superkey count is <code class=\"mm\">2^(n−k)</code>.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U1B-Q10", "subject": "databases", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "er-relational-algebra", "subtopic": "relational-model-constraints", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "In the relational model, a null value represents", "options": ["the number zero.", "a value that is unknown or not applicable.", "an empty string.", "a value that violates the primary key."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Null is a marker for the <i>absence</i> of a value, not a value itself. It stands for either &lsquo;unknown&rsquo; or &lsquo;does not apply&rsquo;, which is why comparisons with null yield UNKNOWN rather than TRUE or FALSE.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Zero and the empty string are ordinary values: <code class=\"mm\">0 = 0</code> is TRUE and <code class=\"mm\">'' = ''</code> is TRUE.</li><li>Null is not equal to itself in SQL: <code class=\"mm\">NULL = NULL</code> evaluates to UNKNOWN, so a row is kept by a WHERE clause only when the predicate is TRUE.</li><li>Hence null encodes missing or inapplicable information, not a particular value.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) &mdash; unknown or not applicable</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) Zero is a legitimate number and compares equal to itself.</li><li>(C) The empty string is a legitimate string value of length 0.</li><li>(D) A primary key forbids nulls; null does not itself violate anything.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Treating null as a third ordinary value and expecting <code class=\"mm\">NULL = NULL</code> to be TRUE, or expecting <code class=\"mm\">COUNT(col)</code> to include nulls.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Null means no value. Three-valued logic follows from that, and every aggregate except <code class=\"mm\">COUNT(*)</code> skips nulls.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U1B-Q11", "subject": "databases", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "er-relational-algebra", "subtopic": "relational-model-constraints", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "A relation schema  R(A, B, C, D)  has exactly two candidate keys,  AB  and  CD . How many superkeys does  R  have? (Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "7", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A superkey is any attribute set containing at least one candidate key. With two candidate keys the superkeys are the union of the supersets of each key, and the two families overlap in the supersets of their union.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Supersets of <code class=\"mm\">AB</code> inside <code class=\"mm\">{A,B,C,D}</code>: <code class=\"mm\">AB, ABC, ABD, ABCD</code> &mdash; 4 sets (add any subset of <code class=\"mm\">{C,D}</code>).</li><li>Supersets of <code class=\"mm\">CD</code>: <code class=\"mm\">CD, ACD, BCD, ABCD</code> &mdash; 4 sets (add any subset of <code class=\"mm\">{A,B}</code>).</li><li>Intersection = supersets of <code class=\"mm\">ABCD</code> = the single set <code class=\"mm\">ABCD</code>.</li><li>By inclusion&ndash;exclusion: 4 + 4 &minus; 1 = 7.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 7 superkeys</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Adding 4 + 4 = 8 and double counting <code class=\"mm\">ABCD</code>, which is a superkey of both keys at once.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Superkeys = union over candidate keys of their supersets; subtract the overlap with inclusion&ndash;exclusion.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U1B-Q12", "subject": "databases", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "er-relational-algebra", "subtopic": "relational-model-constraints", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "Which of the following statements about SQL integrity constraints are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["A PRIMARY KEY declaration implies both UNIQUE and NOT NULL.", "A CHECK constraint may contain a subquery that reads another table.", "Deleting a referenced tuple always fails, whatever referential action was declared.", "A foreign key column may hold NULL even though the referenced key can never be null."], "answer_display": "Options (A) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Each constraint has a precise scope. PRIMARY KEY = UNIQUE + NOT NULL on the key columns. CHECK is row-local in standard SQL: it may not query other tables. Referential actions (RESTRICT, CASCADE, SET NULL, SET DEFAULT) decide what a delete of a referenced row does. A foreign key value is checked only when it is non-null.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) is true by definition: a primary key is unique and non-null; the pair of constraints is implied.</li><li>(D) is true: referential integrity is a conditional check, and NULL satisfies it vacuously. The referenced key being NOT NULL does not propagate to the referencing column.</li><li>(B) is false: standard SQL forbids subqueries in CHECK, precisely so that a row's validity does not depend on other tables. (Some products relax this with triggers.)</li><li>(C) is false: with ON DELETE CASCADE the delete succeeds and the referencing rows are deleted; with SET NULL they are nulled.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A) and (D)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) A cross-table CHECK would make constraint checking depend on rows outside the modified table, which the standard excludes.</li><li>(C) RESTRICT and NO ACTION are only two of four standard referential actions.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming a foreign key inherits NOT NULL from the key it references, and assuming every delete of a referenced row is refused.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> PK = UNIQUE + NOT NULL. FK is conditional on non-null. CHECK is row-local. The delete behaviour is whatever the referential action says.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U1B-Q13", "subject": "databases", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "er-relational-algebra", "subtopic": "relational-model-constraints", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "A relation  R(A, B, C)  has candidate keys  AB  and  C . Which of the following attribute sets is a superkey but  not  a candidate key?", "options": ["AB", "C", "ABC", "A"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A superkey determines every attribute; a candidate key is a superkey with no proper subset that is also a superkey. <code class=\"mm\">ABC</code> determines everything (it contains the key <code class=\"mm\">C</code>) but is not minimal, so it is a superkey only.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">AB</code> and <code class=\"mm\">C</code> are given as candidate keys.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">ABC</code> contains <code class=\"mm\">C</code>, so it determines every attribute &rarr; superkey. It has the proper subset <code class=\"mm\">C</code> which is already a key &rarr; not minimal &rarr; not a candidate key.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">A</code> alone determines nothing beyond itself, so it is not a superkey at all.</li><li>Hence exactly one listed set is a superkey but not a candidate key.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C) &mdash; <code class=\"mm\">ABC</code></span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) <code class=\"mm\">AB</code> is a candidate key by hypothesis.</li><li>(B) <code class=\"mm\">C</code> is a candidate key by hypothesis.</li><li>(D) <code class=\"mm\">A</code> is not a superkey, so it is neither.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reading &lsquo;superkey&rsquo; as &lsquo;any set of attributes that looks complete&rsquo;. The test is determination plus minimality, and adding attributes never destroys the superkey property but always destroys minimality.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Every candidate key is a superkey; a superkey is a candidate key only when no proper subset of it is one.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U1B-Q14", "subject": "databases", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "er-relational-algebra", "subtopic": "relational-algebra-select-project-join-division", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "Which relational algebra expression returns exactly those tuples that appear in both  R  and  S , where both are union-compatible?", "options": ["R − S", "R ⋈ S", "R × S", "R ∩ S"], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Set intersection over union-compatible relations: a tuple is in <code class=\"mm\">R ∩ S</code> exactly when it is in both. It is not a primitive &mdash; it is definable as <code class=\"mm\">R − (R − S)</code> &mdash; but it is the operator that means &lsquo;in both&rsquo;.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">R − S</code> keeps tuples of <code class=\"mm\">R</code> that are absent from <code class=\"mm\">S</code>: the opposite of what is asked.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">R ⋈ S</code> is a natural join: it concatenates matching tuples, so its schema is wider than either input.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">R × S</code> is the Cartesian product: |R|·|S| tuples.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">R ∩ S</code> keeps the tuples common to both, with the same schema &mdash; exactly the requirement.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (D) &mdash; <code class=\"mm\">R ∩ S</code></span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) Difference removes the common tuples.</li><li>(B) A natural join returns combined rows, not common rows.</li><li>(C) A Cartesian product pairs every tuple with every tuple.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Using <code class=\"mm\">R ⋈ S</code> when the question asks for common tuples. A join combines; an intersection filters.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> &cap; keeps whole tuples present in both. &cap; = <code class=\"mm\">R − (R − S)</code>.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U1B-Q15", "subject": "databases", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "er-relational-algebra", "subtopic": "relational-algebra-select-project-join-division", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "Let  R(A, B)  = {(1, x), (1, y), (2, x), (3, y)} and  S(B)  = {x, y}. What is  R ÷ S ?", "options": ["{2}", "{1}", "{1, 2}", "&empty;"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Division answers &lsquo;for all&rsquo;: <code class=\"mm\">R ÷ S</code> = { a | for every b in S, (a, b) is in R }. An <code class=\"mm\">a</code> survives only if it is paired with <i>every</i> value in <code class=\"mm\">S</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Values of <code class=\"mm\">S</code> are x and y, so a survivor must appear with both.</li><li>a = 1: (1, x) and (1, y) are both in <code class=\"mm\">R</code> &rarr; survives.</li><li>a = 2: only (2, x) &mdash; y is missing &rarr; rejected.</li><li>a = 3: only (3, y) &mdash; x is missing &rarr; rejected.</li><li>Result = {1}, a one-attribute relation over <code class=\"mm\">A</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) &mdash; {1}</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) {2} is the <code class=\"mm\">a</code> that appears with x only.</li><li>(C) {1, 2} would be the answer if <code class=\"mm\">S</code> were {x} alone.</li><li>(D) &empty; would need every <code class=\"mm\">a</code> to miss some value of <code class=\"mm\">S</code>.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Computing the projection <code class=\"mm\">π_A(R)</code> = {1, 2, 3} and stopping there, or answering for <code class=\"mm\">S = {x}</code>. Division is a universal test, not a membership test.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> <code class=\"mm\">R ÷ S = π_A(R) − π_A( (π_A(R) × S) − R )</code> &mdash; drop the <code class=\"mm\">a</code> that is missing some <code class=\"mm\">b</code>.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U1B-Q16", "subject": "databases", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "er-relational-algebra", "subtopic": "relational-algebra-select-project-join-division", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "Relation  R(A, B)  holds 5 tuples and relation  S(B, C)  holds 4 tuples. Attribute  B  is a candidate key of  S . What is the  maximum  possible number of tuples in the natural join  R ⋈ S ? (Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "5", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The size of a natural join is governed by how many tuples of <code class=\"mm\">S</code> each tuple of <code class=\"mm\">R</code> can match. Because <code class=\"mm\">B</code> is a candidate key of <code class=\"mm\">S</code>, a given <code class=\"mm\">B</code> value occurs at most once there, so each <code class=\"mm\">R</code> tuple matches at most one <code class=\"mm\">S</code> tuple.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Matching is on the common attribute <code class=\"mm\">B</code>.</li><li>Key of <code class=\"mm\">S</code> &rarr; at most one <code class=\"mm\">S</code> tuple per <code class=\"mm\">B</code> value.</li><li>So each of the 5 tuples of <code class=\"mm\">R</code> contributes at most 1 joined tuple.</li><li>Maximum = 5 × 1 = 5, attained when every <code class=\"mm\">B</code> value in <code class=\"mm\">R</code> exists in <code class=\"mm\">S</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: At most 5 tuples</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Multiplying 5 × 4 = 20, which is the size of a Cartesian product, or the size of the join when <code class=\"mm\">B</code> is <i>not</i> a key of <code class=\"mm\">S</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Join size = |R| × (matches per R tuple). A key on the other side caps the multiplier at 1.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U1B-Q17", "subject": "databases", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "er-relational-algebra", "subtopic": "relational-algebra-select-project-join-division", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "Which of the following pairs of relational algebra expressions are equivalent for all relations of the appropriate schemas? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["π_A(R ⋈ S) and π_A(R) ⋈ π_A(S)", "σ_{A&gt;5}(σ_{B&lt;3}(R)) and σ_{B&lt;3}(σ_{A&gt;5}(R))", "R ⋈ S and S ⋈ R", "σ_{A=1}(R ∪ S) and σ_{A=1}(R) ∪ σ_{A=1}(S)"], "answer_display": "Options (B), (C) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Selection commutes with itself and distributes over union and difference; natural join is commutative and associative. Projection does <i>not</i> distribute over join, because projecting each side first can discard the attribute the join needs or create pairs that never coexisted.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(B) Nested selections on independent predicates can be applied in any order &mdash; both give <code class=\"mm\">σ_{A&gt;5 ∧ B&lt;3}(R)</code>.</li><li>(C) Natural join matches on the common attributes symmetrically, so the result set is the same; only the column order differs.</li><li>(D) Selection distributes over union: filtering a union equals the union of the filters.</li><li>(A) fails: dropping the join attribute before joining leaves nothing to match on (and the right side may even be ill-formed).</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (B), (C) and (D)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) Projection does not distribute over join. Counterexample: <code class=\"mm\">R(A,B) = {(1,2)}</code>, <code class=\"mm\">S(B,C) = {(2,3),(4,5)}</code>; the left side gives {1}, while <code class=\"mm\">π_A(R) ⋈ π_A(S)</code> needs a common attribute that no longer exists.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming every operator distributes over every other. Projection loses information, so it rarely commutes with join.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Push selections down freely; push projections down only when they keep the join attributes.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U1B-Q18", "subject": "databases", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "er-relational-algebra", "subtopic": "relational-algebra-select-project-join-division", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "Which of the following queries can  not  be expressed in the basic relational algebra  {σ, π, ×, ∪, −, ρ} ?", "options": ["the transitive closure of a binary relation", "R ÷ S", "R ∩ S", "a theta join R ⋈_θ S"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The basic algebra is first-order: every query it can write has a fixed, finite number of join and selection steps. Transitive closure needs a number of steps that depends on the <i>data</i>, which no fixed expression can supply.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(B) Division is definable: <code class=\"mm\">R ÷ S = π_A(R) − π_A((π_A(R) × S) − R)</code>.</li><li>(C) Intersection is definable: <code class=\"mm\">R ∩ S = R − (R − S)</code>.</li><li>(D) A theta join is definable: <code class=\"mm\">σ_θ(R × S)</code>.</li><li>(A) Transitive closure of <code class=\"mm\">R</code> requires iterating the join until a fixed point is reached. The iteration count is unbounded in the data, so no single expression of the basic algebra computes it &mdash; this is the standard proof that the algebra is not Turing-complete.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A) &mdash; transitive closure</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) Division is a derived operator, not a primitive.</li><li>(C) Intersection is a derived operator.</li><li>(D) Theta join is a derived operator, <code class=\"mm\">σ_θ</code> applied to a product.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Believing division is the &lsquo;hard&rsquo; operator that escapes the algebra. Division is expressible; recursion is not.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Fixed expressions cannot express unbounded iteration. Closure, aggregation and recursion all sit outside the basic algebra.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U1B-Q19", "subject": "databases", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "er-relational-algebra", "subtopic": "relational-algebra-select-project-join-division", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "Let  R(A, B)  and  S(B, C)  have exactly one common attribute,  B . Which expression computes the natural join  R ⋈ S ?", "options": ["σ_{R.B = S.B}(R × S)", "π_{A,C}(σ_{R.B = S.B}(R × S))", "π_{A,B,C}(σ_{R.B = S.B}(R × S))", "R × S"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Natural join = Cartesian product, then keep rows whose common attributes agree, then project away the duplicate copy of those attributes. Because the duplicate <code class=\"mm\">B</code> column is removed, the result schema is <code class=\"mm\">(A, B, C)</code> with one <code class=\"mm\">B</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Product: schema <code class=\"mm\">(R.A, R.B, S.B, S.C)</code>.</li><li>Equijoin condition on the common attribute: <code class=\"mm\">σ_{R.B = S.B}</code> keeps only agreeing rows.</li><li>Project onto <code class=\"mm\">{A, B, C}</code> to collapse the two identical <code class=\"mm\">B</code> columns into one.</li><li>That is exactly <code class=\"mm\">R ⋈ S</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C) &mdash; <code class=\"mm\">π_{A,B,C}(σ_{R.B=S.B}(R × S))</code></span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) Keeps both copies of <code class=\"mm\">B</code>, so the schema is not that of the natural join.</li><li>(B) Drops <code class=\"mm\">B</code> entirely &mdash; a projection of the join, not the join.</li><li>(D) No match condition at all: the Cartesian product.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Forgetting the final projection, which is what makes the join <i>natural</i> rather than an equijoin that keeps both copies of the common attribute.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Natural join = product &rarr; equality filter &rarr; drop the duplicate column.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U1B-Q20", "subject": "databases", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "er-relational-algebra", "subtopic": "tuple-and-domain-relational-calculus", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "The tuple relational calculus query  { t | t ∈ R ∧ t[A] > 5 }  is equivalent to which relational algebra expression?", "options": ["π_A(σ_{A>5}(R))", "σ_{A>5}(R)", "R ⋈ σ_{A>5}(R)", "σ_{A=5}(R)"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The calculus formula names a range relation (<code class=\"mm\">t ∈ R</code>) and a condition on its attributes <code class=\"mm\">(t[A] > 5)</code>. That is a pure filter: the tuple variable ranges over <code class=\"mm\">R</code> and the result keeps whole tuples that satisfy the condition. A filter is a selection.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Restrict the tuple variable to <code class=\"mm\">R</code> &rarr; the result is a subset of <code class=\"mm\">R</code>.</li><li>Keep whole tuples &rarr; no attribute is dropped, so no projection.</li><li>The condition is on one attribute &rarr; a selection <code class=\"mm\">σ_{A>5}(R)</code>.</li><li>No second relation appears, so no join.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) &mdash; <code class=\"mm\">σ_{A>5}(R)</code></span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) Projecting onto <code class=\"mm\">A</code> discards <code class=\"mm\">B</code>, which the calculus query does not.</li><li>(C) A self-join is not implied by ranging over one relation.</li><li>(D) The predicate is <code class=\"mm\">></code>, not <code class=\"mm\">=</code>.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Projecting because the condition mentions <code class=\"mm\">A</code>. A condition on an attribute filters tuples; it does not restrict the output schema.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> A range atom <code class=\"mm\">t ∈ R</code> plus a predicate is a selection; adding <code class=\"mm\">∃</code> over another relation is a join.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U1B-Q21", "subject": "databases", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "er-relational-algebra", "subtopic": "tuple-and-domain-relational-calculus", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "Domain relational calculus differs from tuple relational calculus in that its variables range over", "options": ["relations rather than attributes.", "the database schema.", "the set of all keys.", "individual attribute domains (values) rather than whole tuples."], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Tuple calculus binds a variable to an entire tuple: <code class=\"mm\">{ t | t ∈ R ∧ … }</code>. Domain calculus binds one variable per column: <code class=\"mm\">{ ⟨a, b⟩ | ⟨a, b⟩ ∈ R ∧ … }</code>, so each variable ranges over an attribute's domain. The two calculi have the same expressive power.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>In TRC a variable <code class=\"mm\">t</code> is a tuple and attributes are accessed as <code class=\"mm\">t[A]</code>.</li><li>In DRC there is one variable per attribute position, each drawn from that attribute's domain.</li><li>Formulas are built from membership atoms <code class=\"mm\">⟨a₁,…,aₙ⟩ ∈ R</code> and comparison atoms over the domain variables.</li><li>Hence the difference is the unit of quantification: tuple versus value.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (D) &mdash; attribute domains (values)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) Relations are the range in neither calculus; both name them in membership atoms.</li><li>(B) The schema is not quantified over in either.</li><li>(C) Keys are not a range for either.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming the domain calculus is weaker because it quantifies over single values. With the same safety restriction the two are equivalent.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> TRC quantifies over tuples, DRC over values; both are first-order and equally expressive.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U1B-Q22", "subject": "databases", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "er-relational-algebra", "subtopic": "tuple-and-domain-relational-calculus", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "Which of the following statements about relational calculus are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["Tuple relational calculus and domain relational calculus have the same expressive power.", "Every syntactically valid calculus expression denotes a finite relation, so no safety restriction is needed.", "A calculus query states what to retrieve rather than how to retrieve it.", "Relational calculus can express the transitive closure of a binary relation."], "answer_display": "Options (A) and (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The calculus is a declarative first-order language. TRC and DRC are inter-translatable, so they have identical power, and both are exactly as powerful as the basic relational algebra once safety is imposed. Safety is what keeps answers finite; without it, expressions such as <code class=\"mm\">{ t | ¬(t ∈ R) }</code> denote infinite sets.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) is the standard equivalence: each calculus translates into the other, and both equal the algebra on safe expressions.</li><li>(C) is the defining property of a declarative language: the formula constrains the answer, and the engine chooses the plan.</li><li>(B) is false: the complement of a relation is infinite, so the formula alone does not determine a finite answer.</li><li>(D) is false: closure needs unbounded iteration, which a fixed first-order formula cannot express.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A) and (C)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) Unsafe expressions such as <code class=\"mm\">{ t | ¬(t ∈ R) }</code> range over the whole universe of tuples, so safety is essential, not decorative.</li><li>(D) Closure is not first-order expressible, exactly as in the algebra.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Thinking the calculus is more powerful than the algebra because it looks like logic. It is not: it is the same first-order power, restricted to safe expressions.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> TRC = DRC = safe calculus = basic algebra. Closure and aggregation lie outside all of them.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U1B-Q23", "subject": "databases", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "er-relational-algebra", "subtopic": "tuple-and-domain-relational-calculus", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "Let  R(A, B)  = {(1, x), (1, y), (2, x), (2, y), (3, x)} and  S(B)  = {x, y}. Consider the tuple calculus query  { ⟨a⟩ | ∀b ( S(b) → R(a, b) ) } . How many tuples does the result contain? (Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "2", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A universally quantified implication <code class=\"mm\">∀b (S(b) → R(a,b))</code> holds for an <code class=\"mm\">a</code> only when that <code class=\"mm\">a</code> is paired in <code class=\"mm\">R</code> with <i>every</i> value that occurs in <code class=\"mm\">S</code>. This is the calculus form of division.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Values in <code class=\"mm\">S</code> are x and y, so a qualifying <code class=\"mm\">a</code> needs both (a, x) and (a, y) in <code class=\"mm\">R</code>.</li><li>a = 1: (1, x) and (1, y) present &rarr; qualifies.</li><li>a = 2: (2, x) and (2, y) present &rarr; qualifies.</li><li>a = 3: only (3, x); the implication fails at b = y &rarr; rejected.</li><li>Result = {⟨1⟩, ⟨2⟩}, two tuples.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 2 tuples</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reading the implication as existential and answering 3, or reading <code class=\"mm\">S(b)</code> as ranging over all of <code class=\"mm\">R</code>'s <code class=\"mm\">B</code> values. The implication is vacuously true only for <code class=\"mm\">b</code> outside <code class=\"mm\">S</code>, so the binding values come from <code class=\"mm\">S</code> alone.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> <code class=\"mm\">∀b (S(b) → R(a,b))</code> is division by <code class=\"mm\">S</code>: keep <code class=\"mm\">a</code> only if it is paired with every <code class=\"mm\">b</code> in <code class=\"mm\">S</code>.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U1B-Q24", "subject": "databases", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "er-relational-algebra", "subtopic": "tuple-and-domain-relational-calculus", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "Which of the following relational calculus expressions is  not  safe?", "options": ["{ t | ¬(t ∈ R) }", "{ t | t ∈ R ∧ t[A] = 5 }", "{ t | t ∈ R ∧ ¬∃s ∈ S ( s[B] = t[A] ) }", "{ t | ∃s ∈ S ( t[A] = s[B] ∧ t[C] = s[C] ) }"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Safety requires that every value in the answer come from the constants of the expression or from the relations it names. <code class=\"mm\">{ t | ¬(t ∈ R) }</code> quantifies over the entire universe of tuples, whose complement of <code class=\"mm\">R</code> is infinite, so it is unsafe.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) The answer is every tuple not in <code class=\"mm\">R</code> &mdash; infinitely many, and drawn from no named relation. Unsafe.</li><li>(B) Ranges over <code class=\"mm\">R</code> and filters, so all values come from <code class=\"mm\">R</code> and the constant 5. Safe.</li><li>(C) Ranges over <code class=\"mm\">R</code> with a negated existential over <code class=\"mm\">S</code>; the free variable still ranges over <code class=\"mm\">R</code>. Safe.</li><li>(D) Free variable <code class=\"mm\">t</code> is constrained by an existential over <code class=\"mm\">S</code>, which bounds every value it can take. Safe.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A) &mdash; <code class=\"mm\">{ t | ¬(t ∈ R) }</code></span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B), (C) and (D) each bind their free tuple variable to a named relation, which is exactly the condition that keeps the answer finite.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming a negated atom is automatically unsafe. Negation is fine when the variable is still bounded by a positive membership atom; here it is not.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Safety = every free variable bounded by a named relation, and every quantified variable too. A bare negated atom breaks it.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U1B-Q25", "subject": "databases", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "er-relational-algebra", "subtopic": "tuple-and-domain-relational-calculus", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "Which of the following statements are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["Aggregate functions such as COUNT and SUM can be expressed in the basic relational algebra.", "Relational algebra and safe relational calculus have the same expressive power.", "Division can be expressed using only σ, π, × and −.", "The transitive closure of a binary relation is expressible in relational calculus."], "answer_display": "Options (B) and (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The classical result is that basic relational algebra, safe tuple calculus and safe domain calculus all define the same class of queries. Division is inside that class; aggregation and recursion are outside it.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(B) is the fundamental equivalence theorem for the relational model.</li><li>(C) holds because <code class=\"mm\">R ÷ S = π_A(R) − π_A((π_A(R) × S) − R)</code> uses only those four operators.</li><li>(A) is false: COUNT and SUM collapse many tuples into one value, which no expression built from set operations and projections can do &mdash; the algebra is tuple-preserving, aggregation is not.</li><li>(D) is false: closure needs unbounded iteration, and the calculus is first-order.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (B) and (C)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) Aggregation is not first-order definable; it is added to the algebra as a separate operator.</li><li>(D) A first-order formula has a fixed number of quantifiers, so it cannot iterate to a fixed point.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming the calculus can do anything the algebra can plus more. Both stop at the same first-order boundary.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Algebra = safe calculus. Aggregation and recursion are extensions bolted on afterwards.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U2-Q51", "subject": "databases", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "sql-querying", "subtopic": "ddl-dml-dcl", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "A table is created as  student(roll INT PRIMARY KEY, email VARCHAR(40) UNIQUE) . Two rows are inserted with  email  left out of the column list, so it takes its default, NULL. What happens?", "options": ["Only the first row is stored; the second is rejected because two NULLs in email are duplicates.", "Both rows are stored: a UNIQUE column may hold any number of NULLs, while a PRIMARY KEY column may hold none.", "Neither row is stored, because a NULL duplicates every other NULL.", "Both rows are stored, and a PRIMARY KEY column would likewise accept several NULLs."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A UNIQUE constraint is a statement about pairs of <i>values</i>, and SQL compares values with three-valued logic: <code class=\"mm\">NULL = NULL</code> is UNKNOWN, not TRUE. A duplicate is therefore a pair for which equality is TRUE, and a pair of NULLs never is. A PRIMARY KEY is different: the standard forbids NULL in a primary-key column outright.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Row 1 gets <code class=\"mm\">email = NULL</code>. The constraint is checked against no earlier row, so nothing is violated.</li><li>Row 2 also gets <code class=\"mm\">email = NULL</code>. The check compares it with row 1 and evaluates <code class=\"mm\">NULL = NULL</code>, which is UNKNOWN.</li><li>A UNIQUE constraint is violated only when some pair of rows makes the comparison TRUE, so UNKNOWN passes and row 2 is stored.</li><li>Had <code class=\"mm\">email</code> been the PRIMARY KEY, the first insert would already have failed: a primary key column is implicitly <code class=\"mm\">NOT NULL</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: both rows are stored &mdash; option B.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) treats two NULLs as equal; SQL never does.</li><li>(C) makes the same mistake and then applies it to the first row as well.</li><li>(D) confuses UNIQUE with PRIMARY KEY: only UNIQUE tolerates NULLs, and only because UNKNOWN is not TRUE.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reading UNIQUE as \"at most one NULL\". The rule is \"no two rows may compare equal\", and NULLs never compare equal &mdash; which is exactly why a UNIQUE column can collect any number of NULLs while a PRIMARY KEY column can hold none.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U2-Q52", "subject": "databases", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "sql-querying", "subtopic": "ddl-dml-dcl", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "Which single statement removes every row of  enroll  while keeping the table and its definition, and cannot be given a  WHERE  clause?", "options": ["DELETE FROM enroll; &mdash; it removes every row, but it does accept a WHERE clause.", "DROP TABLE enroll; &mdash; it removes the rows and the definition together.", "TRUNCATE TABLE enroll;", "ALTER TABLE enroll DROP COLUMN marks; &mdash; it keeps the rows and drops one column."], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Three statements empty a table and only one of them matches the description. <code class=\"mm\">DELETE</code> is DML: it removes rows one at a time, honours a <code class=\"mm\">WHERE</code> clause and can be rolled back. <code class=\"mm\">TRUNCATE</code> is DDL: it deallocates the table's storage and re-creates it empty, so it takes no <code class=\"mm\">WHERE</code> clause and no row triggers fire for the removed rows. <code class=\"mm\">DROP</code> removes the definition as well.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">DELETE FROM enroll;</code> keeps the definition and empties the table, but it accepts <code class=\"mm\">WHERE marks &lt; 40</code>, so it fails the \"cannot be given a WHERE clause\" part.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">DROP TABLE enroll;</code> deletes the rows and the table itself, so the definition is lost.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">TRUNCATE TABLE enroll;</code> keeps the table and its definition and empties it; its grammar has no <code class=\"mm\">WHERE</code> clause at all.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">ALTER TABLE ... DROP COLUMN</code> removes a column, not the rows.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: <code class=\"mm\">TRUNCATE TABLE enroll;</code> &mdash; option C.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) empties the table but is DML and does take a WHERE clause.</li><li>(B) destroys the definition, so the table no longer exists.</li><li>(D) changes the schema of the rows rather than removing them.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> <code class=\"mm\">TRUNCATE</code> looks like a row operation, so it is often filed under DML. It is a DDL statement: it does not scan rows and cannot be filtered, which is why it is much faster than an unqualified <code class=\"mm\">DELETE</code> and why it cannot be rolled back in every product.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U2-Q53", "subject": "databases", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "sql-querying", "subtopic": "ddl-dml-dcl", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "The table is created as CREATE TABLE t(a INT, b INT, CONSTRAINT c1 CHECK (a <= b)); and then  INSERT INTO t VALUES (5, NULL);  is executed. What happens?", "options": ["The row is rejected, because 5 &lt;= NULL is UNKNOWN rather than TRUE.", "The row is rejected, because a column named in a CHECK constraint may not hold NULL.", "The row is accepted, and a &lt;= b is treated as TRUE whenever b is NULL.", "The row is accepted: a CHECK constraint is violated only when its condition is FALSE, and UNKNOWN is not FALSE."], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A <code class=\"mm\">CHECK</code> constraint rejects a row only when its condition evaluates to FALSE. SQL uses three-valued logic, so a condition over a NULL operand can be UNKNOWN, and UNKNOWN is accepted &mdash; the row is neither proven good nor proven bad.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Evaluate <code class=\"mm\">a &lt;= b</code> on the new row: <code class=\"mm\">b</code> is NULL, so the comparison gives UNKNOWN.</li><li>The constraint tests \"is the condition FALSE?\". UNKNOWN is not FALSE, so the test fails and the row is inserted.</li><li>The row now holds <code class=\"mm\">(5, NULL)</code>, and a later <code class=\"mm\">SELECT</code> will show it.</li><li>The constraint is not violated silently: any future update that makes the condition FALSE is rejected.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: the row is accepted, because UNKNOWN is not FALSE &mdash; option D.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) correctly computes UNKNOWN and then wrongly treats it as a rejection.</li><li>(B) invents a rule: a CHECK constraint says nothing about nullability, which is what NOT NULL is for.</li><li>(C) reaches the right verdict for the wrong reason: UNKNOWN is not \"treated as TRUE\", it simply does not trigger the violation test.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reading a CHECK constraint as \"the condition must be TRUE\". It is \"the condition must not be FALSE\", so every row with a NULL in a checked column slips through &mdash; the standard hole in CHECK-based validation.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U2-Q54", "subject": "databases", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "sql-querying", "subtopic": "ddl-dml-dcl", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "User  u1  has been granted  SELECT  on  student   WITH GRANT OPTION  and  INSERT  on  student  without it, and holds no other privilege on that table.  u1  now issues four statements, in this order: GRANT SELECT ON student TO u2;\nGRANT INSERT ON student TO u3;\nGRANT SELECT ON student TO u4 WITH GRANT OPTION;\nGRANT DELETE ON student TO u5; How many of the four statements succeed?", "options": [], "answer_display": "3", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A user may grant only a privilege it actually holds, and may pass on the <code class=\"mm\">WITH GRANT OPTION</code> right only for a privilege it holds with that option. A privilege the grantor lacks cannot be granted at all.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Statement 1 needs <code class=\"mm\">SELECT</code> on <code class=\"mm\">student</code>: <code class=\"mm\">u1</code> has it, so it succeeds.</li><li>Statement 2 needs <code class=\"mm\">INSERT</code>: <code class=\"mm\">u1</code> has it (without grant option, which is all a plain grant requires), so it succeeds.</li><li>Statement 3 needs <code class=\"mm\">SELECT</code> <code class=\"mm\">WITH GRANT OPTION</code>: <code class=\"mm\">u1</code> holds exactly that, so it succeeds.</li><li>Statement 4 needs <code class=\"mm\">DELETE</code>, which <code class=\"mm\">u1</code> was never granted; the statement is rejected for insufficient privilege.</li><li>Three of the four statements succeed.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 3</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Granting a privilege the grantor does not hold, in the belief that any user may hand out any privilege. Only the object owner (or a user with <code class=\"mm\">WITH GRANT OPTION</code> on that privilege) can grant it.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U2-Q55", "subject": "databases", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "sql-querying", "subtopic": "ddl-dml-dcl", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "Which of the following statements about DROP CASCADE, ALTER TABLE and REVOKE CASCADE are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["DROP TABLE t CASCADE also drops the views and constraints that depend on t.", "Adding a NOT NULL column with no default to a table that already holds rows fails, because the existing rows would have to receive NULL.", "A column declared INT DEFAULT 5 NOT NULL accepts an explicit NULL in an INSERT and then falls back to the default.", "REVOKE ... CASCADE withdraws the privilege from the revokee and from everyone to whom the revokee passed it on."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B), (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Four separate rules are being tested: the <code class=\"mm\">CASCADE</code>/<code class=\"mm\">RESTRICT</code> choice on <code class=\"mm\">DROP</code>, what a new <code class=\"mm\">NOT NULL</code> column means for rows that already exist, the difference between an omitted column and an explicit NULL, and the cascading of a <code class=\"mm\">REVOKE</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Option (A): <code class=\"mm\">DROP TABLE ... CASCADE</code> is the standard form that removes the object together with everything that depends on it; <code class=\"mm\">RESTRICT</code> is the form that fails instead. TRUE.</li><li>Option (B): an added column takes its default, and with no default the value is NULL; NULL in a <code class=\"mm\">NOT NULL</code> column violates the constraint, so the whole <code class=\"mm\">ALTER TABLE</code> is rejected. TRUE.</li><li>Option (C): the default applies only when the column is left out. An explicit NULL is a value, and it collides with <code class=\"mm\">NOT NULL</code> at once. FALSE.</li><li>Option (D): <code class=\"mm\">REVOKE ... CASCADE</code> follows the grant tree down, so privileges the revokee granted are withdrawn too; <code class=\"mm\">RESTRICT</code> refuses when such dependent grants exist. TRUE.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: options A, B and D are TRUE; option C is FALSE.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(C) is the trap: DEFAULT is a fallback for an omitted column, not a filter that rewrites an explicit NULL.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming that <code class=\"mm\">DEFAULT</code> rescues an explicit NULL. It does not &mdash; the default is applied only when the column is absent from the column list, so <code class=\"mm\">DEFAULT 5 NOT NULL</code> rejects <code class=\"mm\">NULL</code> outright.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U2-Q56", "subject": "databases", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "sql-querying", "subtopic": "nested-and-correlated-subqueries", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "The query SELECT * FROM student\nWHERE roll IN (SELECT roll FROM enroll WHERE cid = 'C9'); is run while  enroll  holds no row with  cid = 'C9' . What does it return?", "options": ["No rows.", "Every row of student.", "Every student whose roll is NULL.", "An error, because the subquery returns an empty table."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> <code class=\"mm\">x IN (S)</code> is shorthand for <code class=\"mm\">x = ANY (S)</code>, a disjunction over the members of <code class=\"mm\">S</code>. An empty <code class=\"mm\">S</code> makes that a disjunction over nothing, and an empty disjunction is FALSE &mdash; never TRUE, and never an error.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The subquery returns the empty set, because no enrollment has <code class=\"mm\">cid = 'C9'</code>.</li><li>For each student the condition becomes <code class=\"mm\">roll = ANY (empty)</code>, which is FALSE.</li><li>No row satisfies a FALSE condition, so the result is empty.</li><li>An empty result is a legal answer, not a run-time error.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: no rows &mdash; option A.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) confuses an empty subquery with an absent condition.</li><li>(C) confuses it with a NULL comparison: no comparison is ever made here.</li><li>(D) an empty subquery is perfectly legal; only a scalar subquery used where one value is expected raises a cardinality error.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reading <code class=\"mm\">IN</code> as \"matches everything when the list is empty\". The dual fact is worth memorising with it: <code class=\"mm\">NOT IN (empty)</code> is TRUE for every row, which is why the empty-set cases are the ones to check first.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U2-Q57", "subject": "databases", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "sql-querying", "subtopic": "nested-and-correlated-subqueries", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "The query SELECT s.roll, (SELECT e.cid FROM enroll e WHERE e.roll = s.roll)\nFROM student s; is run, and student 13 has three rows in  enroll . Which statement is TRUE?", "options": ["Student 13 appears three times in the result, once for each cid.", "Student 13 appears once, carrying the first cid that the storage engine finds.", "The statement fails at run time, because a subquery used where a single value is expected may return at most one row.", "Student 13 appears once with NULL in the second column."], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A subquery in the select list occupies a position that holds one value per outer row, so it is a <i>scalar</i> subquery. SQL requires it to return at most one row; a second row is a cardinality violation, and the standard makes it an error rather than a silent truncation.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>For each outer row the correlated subquery runs with <code class=\"mm\">s.roll</code> bound to that student.</li><li>For students with no enrollment it returns zero rows: a scalar subquery with no row yields NULL, which is legal.</li><li>For student 13 it returns three rows, and one value cannot hold three rows.</li><li>The statement therefore fails at run time with a cardinality violation; no result is produced at all.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: the statement fails at run time &mdash; option C.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) describes what a join or a lateral subquery would do; a scalar subquery cannot multiply rows.</li><li>(B) assumes an arbitrary pick, which the standard does not permit.</li><li>(D) is the empty-result rule, which applies to zero rows, not to three.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Believing that a select-list subquery quietly takes the first row. Zero rows give NULL, one row gives the value, two or more rows abort the statement &mdash; and the fix is an aggregate such as <code class=\"mm\">MIN(cid)</code> or a join, not a rewrite of the outer query.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U2-Q58", "subject": "databases", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "sql-querying", "subtopic": "nested-and-correlated-subqueries", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "With the data below, what does the query return? course:  C1, C2, C3\nenroll:  (11,C1) (11,C2) (11,C3) (12,C1) (12,C2)\n         (13,C1) (13,C2) (13,C3)  SELECT DISTINCT e.roll\nFROM enroll e\nWHERE NOT EXISTS (\n        SELECT * FROM course c\n        WHERE NOT EXISTS (\n                SELECT * FROM enroll e2\n                WHERE e2.roll = e.roll AND e2.cid = c.cid)); ", "options": ["Only 11.", "11 and 13.", "All three students, 11, 12 and 13.", "Only 13."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Two nested <code class=\"mm\">NOT EXISTS</code> express relational division: \"there is no course for which this student has no enrollment\". The outer negation ranges over students, the inner one over courses, and the innermost query is the only place the two are correlated together.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The inner-most query asks: does student <code class=\"mm\">e.roll</code> have a row for course <code class=\"mm\">c.cid</code>?</li><li>The middle query asks: is there some course with no such row?</li><li>The outer <code class=\"mm\">NOT EXISTS</code> keeps only the students for whom that middle query is empty.</li><li>Student 11 covers C1, C2 and C3, so the middle query is empty: kept.</li><li>Student 12 has no C3, so C3 makes the middle query non-empty: dropped.</li><li>Student 13 covers C1, C2 and C3: kept.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 11 and 13 &mdash; option B.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) would be right if C3 were missing from student 13's enrollments.</li><li>(C) includes student 12, who lacks C3.</li><li>(D) drops student 11, who does cover every course.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Writing division as a single <code class=\"mm\">NOT EXISTS</code>, or as <code class=\"mm\">COUNT(DISTINCT cid) = (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM course)</code> &mdash; the count form silently breaks when the enrollment table can hold duplicates, whereas the double negation never does.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U2-Q59", "subject": "databases", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "sql-querying", "subtopic": "nested-and-correlated-subqueries", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "The table  student(roll, dept, marks)  holds (1,CSE,90)  (2,CSE,70)  (3,ECE,95)  (4,ECE,70)  (5,MECH,60) and the query SELECT COUNT(*)\nFROM student\nWHERE marks >= ALL (SELECT marks FROM student WHERE dept = 'CSE'); is run. What value does it return?", "options": [], "answer_display": "2", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> <code class=\"mm\">x >= ALL (S)</code> holds when <code class=\"mm\">x</code> is at least as large as every member of <code class=\"mm\">S</code>, which is the same as <code class=\"mm\">x >= MAX(S)</code>. The subquery here is not correlated: it is evaluated once, over the CSE rows only.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The subquery returns the CSE marks: <code class=\"mm\">{90, 70}</code>.</li><li>The largest of those is 90, so the predicate is <code class=\"mm\">marks &gt;= 90</code>.</li><li>The outer scan is over all five rows: 90 passes, 70 fails, 95 passes, 70 fails, 60 fails.</li><li>Two rows satisfy it, so <code class=\"mm\">COUNT(*)</code> is 2.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 2</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Comparing each row only with the CSE rows of the same department, or assuming <code class=\"mm\">>= ALL</code> means \"greater than the average\". It means \"at least the maximum\", so the department filter on the inner query does not restrict the outer one.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U2-Q60", "subject": "databases", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "sql-querying", "subtopic": "nested-and-correlated-subqueries", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "Which of the following statements about  ANY ,  ALL  and  NOT IN  are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["x &gt; ALL (S) is TRUE when S is empty.", "x &gt; ANY (S) is TRUE when S is empty.", "x NOT IN (S) is not TRUE when S contains a NULL, even for an x that matches no row of S.", "x = ANY (S) means the same as x IN (S)."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (C), (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> <code class=\"mm\">ANY</code> is a disjunction over <code class=\"mm\">S</code> and <code class=\"mm\">ALL</code> is a conjunction over <code class=\"mm\">S</code>. A disjunction over nothing is FALSE; a conjunction over nothing is TRUE. That single asymmetry settles the empty-set cases, and three-valued logic settles the NULL case.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Option (A): <code class=\"mm\">x &gt; ALL (empty)</code> is a conjunction over no members, hence vacuously TRUE. TRUE.</li><li>Option (B): <code class=\"mm\">x &gt; ANY (empty)</code> is a disjunction over no members, hence FALSE for every <code class=\"mm\">x</code>. FALSE.</li><li>Option (C): <code class=\"mm\">x NOT IN (S)</code> is <code class=\"mm\">NOT (x = ANY (S))</code>. If <code class=\"mm\">S</code> contains a NULL, the disjunction is UNKNOWN rather than FALSE whenever <code class=\"mm\">x</code> matches no other row, and <code class=\"mm\">NOT UNKNOWN</code> is UNKNOWN, which is not TRUE. TRUE.</li><li>Option (D): <code class=\"mm\">IN</code> is defined as <code class=\"mm\">= ANY</code>, so the two are the same predicate. TRUE.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: options A, C and D are TRUE; option B is FALSE.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) reverses the empty-set rule: it is ANY, not ALL, that is FALSE over an empty set.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming <code class=\"mm\">NOT IN</code> and <code class=\"mm\">NOT EXISTS</code> agree. They diverge exactly when the subquery holds a NULL: <code class=\"mm\">NOT IN</code> collapses to UNKNOWN for every row, while <code class=\"mm\">NOT EXISTS</code> still answers correctly.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U2-Q61", "subject": "databases", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "sql-querying", "subtopic": "aggregate-functions-group-by-having", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "Table  t(v)  holds the three rows  10 ,  20  and  NULL . What does SELECT SUM(v), COUNT(v), AVG(v) FROM t; return?", "options": ["30, 2, 15", "30, 3, 10", "30, 3, 15", "NULL, 2, 15"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Every aggregate except <code class=\"mm\">COUNT(*)</code> ignores NULL inputs. So <code class=\"mm\">SUM</code> adds the non-NULL values, <code class=\"mm\">COUNT(v)</code> counts the non-NULL values, and <code class=\"mm\">AVG</code> divides the sum by that same non-NULL count &mdash; not by the number of rows.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">SUM(v)</code> = 10 + 20 = 30, because the NULL contributes nothing.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">COUNT(v)</code> counts only non-NULL values, so it is 2.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">AVG(v)</code> = 30 / 2 = 15, not 30 / 3.</li><li>The answer is 30, 2, 15.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: <code class=\"mm\">30</code>, <code class=\"mm\">2</code>, <code class=\"mm\">15</code> &mdash; option A.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) divides by the row count, which is the classic AVG error.</li><li>(C) counts the NULL as a value but still divides by 2, which is internally inconsistent.</li><li>(D) believes a NULL poisons SUM; SUM skips it entirely.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Computing <code class=\"mm\">AVG</code> as <code class=\"mm\">SUM/COUNT(*)</code>. It is <code class=\"mm\">SUM/COUNT(col)</code>, which is why a table of NULLs makes <code class=\"mm\">AVG</code> return NULL rather than 0 &mdash; and why <code class=\"mm\">SUM/COUNT(*)</code> silently understates the average.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U2-Q62", "subject": "databases", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "sql-querying", "subtopic": "aggregate-functions-group-by-having", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "Which of the following queries is valid in standard SQL?", "options": ["SELECT dept, roll FROM student GROUP BY dept;", "SELECT dept FROM student WHERE COUNT(*) &gt; 2 GROUP BY dept;", "SELECT dept, COUNT(roll) FROM student GROUP BY COUNT(roll);", "SELECT dept, COUNT(*) FROM student GROUP BY dept HAVING COUNT(*) &gt; 2;"], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A grouped query may select only grouping columns and aggregates of other columns; <code class=\"mm\">WHERE</code> runs before grouping and so cannot contain an aggregate; <code class=\"mm\">GROUP BY</code> must name columns, not aggregate results; and <code class=\"mm\">HAVING</code> runs after grouping and is exactly where an aggregate condition belongs.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Option (A): <code class=\"mm\">roll</code> is neither grouped nor aggregated, so the query is rejected.</li><li>Option (B): <code class=\"mm\">COUNT(*)</code> appears in <code class=\"mm\">WHERE</code>, which is evaluated before the groups exist, so it is rejected.</li><li>Option (C): the grouping expression is an aggregate, which is not a column of the input, so it is rejected.</li><li>Option (D): <code class=\"mm\">dept</code> is grouped, <code class=\"mm\">COUNT(*)</code> is an aggregate, and the aggregate condition sits in <code class=\"mm\">HAVING</code>. Valid.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: option D is the only valid query.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) leaves a bare column outside the grouping.</li><li>(B) puts an aggregate in WHERE, where no group exists yet.</li><li>(C) groups by an aggregate instead of by a column.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Thinking a bare column is fine as long as the rows \"happen\" to agree. Some products tolerate it when the column is functionally dependent on the grouping key, but the standard does not, and GATE follows the standard.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U2-Q63", "subject": "databases", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "sql-querying", "subtopic": "aggregate-functions-group-by-having", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "Table  t(a, b)  holds (1,1)  (1,1)  (1,2)  (2,NULL)  (2,NULL)  (NULL,3) and the query SELECT a, b, COUNT(*) FROM t GROUP BY a, b; is run. Which statement about its result is TRUE?", "options": ["It returns 3 rows, because rows whose b is NULL are dropped before grouping.", "It returns 5 rows, because each NULL in b starts a group of its own.", "It returns 4 rows, and the group with a = 2, b = NULL has a count of 2.", "It returns 6 rows, one group for every row of t."], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> <code class=\"mm\">GROUP BY</code> partitions rows by the <i>values</i> of the grouping expressions, and NULL is a value for this purpose: all NULLs fall into one partition, because grouping uses \"not distinct from\" rather than equality. Every aggregate except <code class=\"mm\">COUNT(*)</code> still ignores NULL inputs, but grouping itself never discards a row.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Group by the pair <code class=\"mm\">(a, b)</code> and list the distinct pairs: <code class=\"mm\">(1,1)</code>, <code class=\"mm\">(1,2)</code>, <code class=\"mm\">(2,NULL)</code>, <code class=\"mm\">(NULL,3)</code>.</li><li>That is 4 groups, so the query returns 4 rows.</li><li>Counts: <code class=\"mm\">(1,1)</code> &rarr; 2, <code class=\"mm\">(1,2)</code> &rarr; 1, <code class=\"mm\">(2,NULL)</code> &rarr; 2, <code class=\"mm\">(NULL,3)</code> &rarr; 1.</li><li>No row is dropped: the two rows with <code class=\"mm\">b = NULL</code> are one group, not two and not zero.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 4 rows, with <code class=\"mm\">(2, NULL)</code> counted as 2 &mdash; option C.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) applies the aggregate rule (ignore NULL) to grouping, where it does not hold.</li><li>(B) applies equality to NULLs; grouping uses \"not distinct from\", so all NULLs merge.</li><li>(D) confuses grouping with a row-by-row listing.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> <code class=\"mm\">COUNT(*)</code> and <code class=\"mm\">COUNT(b)</code> disagree inside the NULL group: <code class=\"mm\">COUNT(*)</code> reports 2 while <code class=\"mm\">COUNT(b)</code> reports 0. Both are correct, and the difference is the whole point of keeping the two forms apart.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U2-Q64", "subject": "databases", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "sql-querying", "subtopic": "aggregate-functions-group-by-having", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "Table  t(g, v)  holds (A,5)  (A,7)  (B,4)  (C,9)  (C,2)  (D,NULL) and the query SELECT COUNT(*)\nFROM (SELECT g FROM t GROUP BY g HAVING SUM(v) > 10) x; is run. What value does it return?", "options": [], "answer_display": "2", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> <code class=\"mm\">HAVING</code> filters whole groups after <code class=\"mm\">SUM</code> has been computed, so the question is how many groups have a sum above 10. <code class=\"mm\">SUM</code> ignores NULL, and a group whose only value is NULL sums to NULL &mdash; which fails the test, because NULL &gt; 10 is UNKNOWN.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Group A: 5 + 7 = 12, and 12 &gt; 10 is TRUE, so the group survives.</li><li>Group B: 4, and 4 &gt; 10 is FALSE, so the group is dropped.</li><li>Group C: 9 + 2 = 11, and 11 &gt; 10 is TRUE, so the group survives.</li><li>Group D: the only value is NULL, so <code class=\"mm\">SUM(v)</code> is NULL and NULL &gt; 10 is UNKNOWN; the group is dropped.</li><li>Two groups survive, so the outer <code class=\"mm\">COUNT(*)</code> is 2.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 2</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Counting group D as passing, or counting group B because 4 is a small number. The two facts to keep are that <code class=\"mm\">SUM</code> of all-NULL is NULL and that a group failing the <code class=\"mm\">HAVING</code> test on UNKNOWN is dropped, exactly as a row failing a <code class=\"mm\">WHERE</code> test is.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U2-Q65", "subject": "databases", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "sql-querying", "subtopic": "aggregate-functions-group-by-having", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "Which of the following statements about  COUNT ,  AVG  and an empty group are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["COUNT(*) counts the rows of a group, while COUNT(c) counts only the rows in which c is not NULL.", "AVG(c) equals SUM(c) / COUNT(*) even when c contains NULLs.", "SELECT COUNT(*) FROM t WHERE 1 = 0; returns no row at all.", "With no GROUP BY and no qualifying row, SELECT COUNT(*), SUM(v) FROM t; still returns one row, holding 0 and NULL."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> An aggregate query without <code class=\"mm\">GROUP BY</code> produces exactly one group &mdash; the whole table, even when it is empty &mdash; and returns exactly one row. Within a group, <code class=\"mm\">COUNT(*)</code> counts rows while every other aggregate, including <code class=\"mm\">COUNT(c)</code>, skips NULL inputs.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Option (A): that is the definition of the two forms. TRUE.</li><li>Option (B): <code class=\"mm\">AVG</code> divides by <code class=\"mm\">COUNT(c)</code>, the number of non-NULL values, so the identity fails as soon as a NULL is present. FALSE.</li><li>Option (C): the query has no <code class=\"mm\">GROUP BY</code>, so it is one group over zero rows and returns one row holding 0. FALSE.</li><li>Option (D): <code class=\"mm\">COUNT(*)</code> over an empty group is 0 and <code class=\"mm\">SUM</code> over it is NULL, both in a single row. TRUE.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: options A and D are TRUE; options B and C are FALSE.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) is the AVG trap; (C) confuses an empty result set with an empty group.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Expecting <code class=\"mm\">COUNT(*)</code> to return no row when nothing matches. It returns the number 0, which is why <code class=\"mm\">SELECT COUNT(*) ... </code> is safe to test for emptiness while a query returning rows is not.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U2-Q66", "subject": "databases", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "sql-querying", "subtopic": "joins-outer-joins", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "The data is student: 11, 12, 13, 14\nenroll:  (11,C1) (11,C2) (12,C1) (12,C3) (13,C1) and the query SELECT COUNT(*)\nFROM student s LEFT JOIN enroll e ON e.roll = s.roll; is run. What value does it return?", "options": ["4", "6", "7", "9"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A left outer join keeps every row of the left table. A left row with <code class=\"mm\">k</code> matching rows on the right contributes <code class=\"mm\">k</code> output rows; a left row with none contributes exactly one row padded with NULLs.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Student 11 has two enrollments, so it contributes 2 rows.</li><li>Student 12 has two enrollments, so it contributes 2 rows.</li><li>Student 13 has one enrollment, so it contributes 1 row.</li><li>Student 14 has none, so it contributes 1 NULL-padded row.</li><li>2 + 2 + 1 + 1 = 6.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 6 &mdash; option B.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) counts students instead of result rows.</li><li>(C) adds an extra row as if one student matched twice.</li><li>(D) is the size of the cross product.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Counting only the matched rows (5) and forgetting that the unmatched left row still produces a row. The join never loses a left row &mdash; that is the whole difference from an inner join.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U2-Q67", "subject": "databases", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "sql-querying", "subtopic": "joins-outer-joins", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "The query SELECT s.*\nFROM student s LEFT JOIN enroll e ON e.roll = s.roll\nWHERE e.roll IS NULL; returns", "options": ["every student, with NULLs wherever the enrollment is missing.", "the students who have at least one enrollment.", "no rows at all, because e.roll IS NULL can never be TRUE in a join.", "exactly the students who have no row in enroll."], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> In a left outer join, the NULL padding marks precisely the left rows that found no partner. Testing a right-hand column for NULL therefore selects the unmatched left rows &mdash; the anti-join idiom.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Matched students get a real <code class=\"mm\">e.roll</code> from the right table.</li><li>Only the unmatched left rows are padded with NULLs.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">WHERE e.roll IS NULL</code> keeps exactly those padded rows.</li><li>The result is the students with no enrollment: an anti-join written as an outer join.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: exactly the students with no row in enroll &mdash; option D.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) is the query without the WHERE clause.</li><li>(B) inverts the test, which would need <code class=\"mm\">e.roll IS NOT NULL</code>.</li><li>(C) forgets the NULL padding that the outer join exists to produce.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Using <code class=\"mm\">e.roll = NULL</code> instead of <code class=\"mm\">e.roll IS NULL</code>. Equality with NULL is UNKNOWN, so the query would return no rows &mdash; the single most common NULL mistake in SQL.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U2-Q68", "subject": "databases", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "sql-querying", "subtopic": "joins-outer-joins", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "In  emp(eno, name, salary, mgr) ,  mgr  references  emp.eno . Which query returns the names of the employees who earn more than their own manager?", "options": ["SELECT e.name FROM emp e JOIN emp m ON e.mgr = m.eno WHERE e.salary &gt; m.salary;", "SELECT e.name FROM emp e JOIN emp m ON e.mgr = m.eno WHERE m.salary &gt; e.salary;", "SELECT e.name FROM emp e JOIN emp m ON e.eno = m.mgr WHERE e.salary &gt; m.salary;", "SELECT e.name FROM emp e, emp m WHERE e.salary &gt; m.salary;"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A self-join needs two roles for one table: one alias plays the employee and the other plays the manager. The join condition must bind the employee's <code class=\"mm\">mgr</code> to the manager's <code class=\"mm\">eno</code>, and the filter must compare the employee's salary with the manager's.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Alias <code class=\"mm\">e</code> for the employee row and <code class=\"mm\">m</code> for the manager row.</li><li>Bind them with <code class=\"mm\">e.mgr = m.eno</code>: the manager of <code class=\"mm\">e</code> is the row <code class=\"mm\">m</code>.</li><li>Keep the pair when <code class=\"mm\">e.salary &gt; m.salary</code>, that is, when the employee out-earns the manager.</li><li>Project <code class=\"mm\">e.name</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: option A.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) keeps the pairs where the manager out-earns the employee, which is the reverse question.</li><li>(C) swaps the join columns, so <code class=\"mm\">e</code> becomes the manager and the answer lists managers who out-earn a subordinate.</li><li>(D) has no join condition at all, so it compares every employee with every other employee.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Forgetting that the top manager's <code class=\"mm\">mgr</code> is NULL. An inner self-join drops that row silently &mdash; correct here, since the top manager has no manager &mdash; while a left self-join would keep it with a NULL salary and the comparison would be UNKNOWN.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U2-Q69", "subject": "databases", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "sql-querying", "subtopic": "joins-outer-joins", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "Table  A(k)  holds  1, 2, 3  and table  B(k)  holds  2, 3, 4 . What does SELECT COUNT(*) FROM A FULL OUTER JOIN B ON A.k = B.k; return?", "options": [], "answer_display": "4", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A full outer join is the union of the left and right outer joins: every matched pair appears once, every unmatched left row appears once with NULLs on the right, and every unmatched right row appears once with NULLs on the left.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Matches: <code class=\"mm\">k = 2</code> and <code class=\"mm\">k = 3</code>, so 2 rows.</li><li>Left rows with no match: <code class=\"mm\">k = 1</code>, so 1 row padded with NULL.</li><li>Right rows with no match: <code class=\"mm\">k = 4</code>, so 1 row padded with NULL.</li><li>2 + 1 + 1 = 4 rows.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 4</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Returning 2, the inner-join count, or 9, the cross product. The full outer join keeps both sides' unmatched rows, so it is neither.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U2-Q70", "subject": "databases", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "sql-querying", "subtopic": "joins-outer-joins", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "Which of the following statements about  ON  and  WHERE  in an outer join, and about  CROSS  and  NATURAL  joins, are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["In a LEFT JOIN, a predicate on the right-hand table written in the WHERE clause can discard the unmatched left rows and so turn the join into an inner join.", "The same predicate written in the ON clause keeps the unmatched left rows, with NULLs on the right.", "A CROSS JOIN B produces as many rows as A INNER JOIN B ON TRUE, even when one of the two tables is empty.", "A NATURAL JOIN B joins on every column that the two tables have in common and keeps both copies of each such column in the result."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B), (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> In an outer join the <code class=\"mm\">ON</code> clause decides only which rows pair up; the <code class=\"mm\">WHERE</code> clause is applied afterwards to the joined result and knows nothing about the padding. A predicate that rejects NULLs on the right therefore removes the padded rows, which is what turns the outer join back into an inner one.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Option (A): <code class=\"mm\">WHERE b.x &gt; 5</code> is UNKNOWN on a padded row, so the padded row is dropped; only matched pairs survive. TRUE.</li><li>Option (B): the same predicate in <code class=\"mm\">ON</code> merely prevents the pairing, so the left row is still emitted, padded. TRUE.</li><li>Option (C): both produce the full cartesian product, of size <code class=\"mm\">|A| &times; |B|</code>, which is 0 when either table is empty. TRUE.</li><li>Option (D): <code class=\"mm\">NATURAL JOIN</code> does join on the common columns, but it keeps only one copy of each, unlike <code class=\"mm\">JOIN ... USING</code> with <code class=\"mm\">SELECT *</code> in some products and unlike a plain join. FALSE.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: options A, B and C are TRUE; option D is FALSE.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(D) is the trap: the join columns are merged into one, not duplicated.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Moving a predicate from <code class=\"mm\">ON</code> to <code class=\"mm\">WHERE</code> in an outer join and expecting the same answer. For an inner join the two are interchangeable; for an outer join they are not, and that is the difference the exam tests.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U2-Q71", "subject": "databases", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "sql-querying", "subtopic": "views-triggers-constraints", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "Which statement about  CREATE VIEW v AS SELECT ...  is TRUE?", "options": ["It stores the query text; the rows are produced from the base tables each time v is referenced.", "It stores a copy of the rows, so a later change to a base table is invisible through v.", "It creates a new base table that has to be filled by INSERT.", "It stores only the column names, and the query is re-read from the original statement text at every use of v."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A view is a named query, not a copy of data. The catalogue keeps the defining query, and each reference to the view is expanded into that query over the current base tables, so the view is always as fresh as they are.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">CREATE VIEW</code> records the query and the column names in the catalogue.</li><li>A later <code class=\"mm\">SELECT ... FROM v</code> is rewritten against the base tables and executed then.</li><li>Because no rows are stored, every change to the base tables shows through immediately.</li><li>A materialised view is the variant that does store rows, and it is a different object.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: option A.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) describes a materialised view or a snapshot.</li><li>(C) describes <code class=\"mm\">CREATE TABLE ... AS SELECT</code>.</li><li>(D) drops the stored definition, without which the view name could not be resolved at all.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Thinking a view caches its rows for speed. A plain view costs the same as running its query, which is why a chain of views can be much slower than the equivalent single query.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U2-Q72", "subject": "databases", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "sql-querying", "subtopic": "views-triggers-constraints", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "Which statement about a row-level  BEFORE UPDATE  trigger is TRUE in standard SQL?", "options": ["It fires after the row has been written, so assigning to NEW.col there has no effect.", "It fires before the row is written, and assigning to NEW.col changes the value that is stored.", "It fires once per statement, whatever the number of rows the statement matches.", "It cannot refer to OLD and NEW in the same trigger body."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A row-level <code class=\"mm\">BEFORE</code> trigger runs once for each affected row, before the change is applied. Its <code class=\"mm\">NEW</code> row is writable, so the trigger can adjust the values that will be stored &mdash; the standard mechanism for derived columns, audit stamps and value normalisation.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Row-level means once per affected row; statement-level means once per statement, so option C describes the other kind.</li><li>BEFORE means the row has not been written yet, and <code class=\"mm\">NEW</code> is the proposed row.</li><li>Assigning to <code class=\"mm\">NEW.col</code> in the body replaces the proposed value before it is stored.</li><li>Both <code class=\"mm\">OLD</code> and <code class=\"mm\">NEW</code> are visible in an update trigger, so option D is wrong.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: option B.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) describes an <code class=\"mm\">AFTER</code> trigger, where the row is already stored.</li><li>(C) describes a statement-level trigger.</li><li>(D) is false: an update trigger sees both the old and the new row.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Trying to modify <code class=\"mm\">NEW</code> in an <code class=\"mm\">AFTER</code> trigger. There the row is already written, so the assignment either has no effect or is rejected outright.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U2-Q73", "subject": "databases", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "sql-querying", "subtopic": "views-triggers-constraints", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "In standard SQL, which constraints may be declared  DEFERRABLE INITIALLY DEFERRED , so that they are checked when the transaction commits rather than at the end of each statement?", "options": ["NOT NULL only.", "CHECK only.", "PRIMARY KEY, UNIQUE and FOREIGN KEY, but not NOT NULL or CHECK.", "Every constraint, NOT NULL and CHECK included."], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Deferral exists so that a transaction may pass through states that violate a constraint temporarily, which only makes sense for the uniqueness and referential constraints. The standard allows <code class=\"mm\">DEFERRABLE</code> on <code class=\"mm\">PRIMARY KEY</code>, <code class=\"mm\">UNIQUE</code> and <code class=\"mm\">FOREIGN KEY</code> constraints; <code class=\"mm\">NOT NULL</code> and <code class=\"mm\">CHECK</code> are always checked immediately.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Uniqueness and referential constraints describe relationships between rows, and a multi-statement transaction can legitimately break them in the middle.</li><li>The standard therefore lets those three be <code class=\"mm\">DEFERRABLE</code>, with <code class=\"mm\">INITIALLY DEFERRED</code> postponing the check to commit time.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">NOT NULL</code> and <code class=\"mm\">CHECK</code> constrain a single row in isolation, so deferring them would have no purpose and the standard does not allow it.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: option C.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) and (B) name exactly the two constraint kinds that cannot be deferred.</li><li>(D) over-generalises: the standard is explicit that column-level checks are immediate.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming deferral is a general switch. A cyclic foreign key is the classic case that needs it, and that is the case the standard designed it for.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U2-Q74", "subject": "databases", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "sql-querying", "subtopic": "views-triggers-constraints", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "A row-level  AFTER UPDATE  trigger is declared on  enroll . The statement  UPDATE enroll SET marks = marks + 5 WHERE cid = 'C1';  matches 7 rows. How many times does the trigger fire?", "options": [], "answer_display": "7", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The firing count is decided by the trigger's granularity, not by the statement's form. A row-level trigger fires once per affected row; a statement-level trigger fires once per statement regardless of how many rows it touched.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The trigger is declared <code class=\"mm\">FOR EACH ROW</code>, that is row-level.</li><li>The <code class=\"mm\">UPDATE</code> matches and changes 7 rows.</li><li>A row-level trigger fires once for each of them: 7 firings.</li><li>Had it been statement-level, the answer would be 1.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 7</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering 1 by thinking of the trigger as attached to the statement. Granularity is the whole question here, and the same statement would fire a statement-level trigger exactly once.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U2-Q75", "subject": "databases", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "sql-querying", "subtopic": "views-triggers-constraints", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-02", "text": "Which of the following statements about foreign-key targets,  CREATE OR REPLACE VIEW  and assertions are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["A foreign key must reference the PRIMARY KEY of the parent table; referencing a UNIQUE column is not permitted.", "CREATE OR REPLACE VIEW may add new columns at the end of the view, but may not drop or rename the existing ones.", "An SQL assertion (CREATE ASSERTION) is a schema-level constraint whose condition may reference several tables.", "A CHECK constraint may be declared on a view, and then it restricts the rows inserted through that view."], "answer_display": "Options (B), (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Three separate rules are in play: what a foreign key may point at, what <code class=\"mm\">CREATE OR REPLACE</code> is allowed to change, and which mechanism restricts inserts through a view.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Option (A): the referenced columns need only be <code class=\"mm\">UNIQUE</code> (a primary key qualifies because it is unique). FALSE.</li><li>Option (B): replacement may append columns, but existing columns must keep their names, order and types, because dependent objects are bound to them. TRUE.</li><li>Option (C): an assertion is a constraint at schema level, checked over the whole database and free to span several tables. TRUE.</li><li>Option (D): constraints are declared on base tables; the way to restrict what may be inserted through a view is <code class=\"mm\">WITH CHECK OPTION</code> on the view. FALSE.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: options B and C are TRUE; options A and D are FALSE.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) is the commonest foreign-key myth: any unique key will do.</li><li>(D) confuses <code class=\"mm\">CHECK</code> with <code class=\"mm\">WITH CHECK OPTION</code>.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Believing a foreign key must name the primary key. The standard requires the referenced columns to be covered by a unique constraint, which is why a foreign key onto a <code class=\"mm\">UNIQUE</code> column is legal.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U3-Q51", "subject": "databases", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "normalization", "subtopic": "functional-dependencies-armstrong-axioms", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-03", "text": "Relation <code class=\"mm\">R(A, B, C)</code> satisfies <code class=\"mm\">F = {A &rarr; B}</code>. Which one of the following functional dependencies is in <code class=\"mm\">F&#8314;</code>?", "options": ["<code class=\"mm\">B &rarr; A</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">AC &rarr; B</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">C &rarr; A</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">AB &rarr; C</code>"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A dependency belongs to <code class=\"mm\">F&#8314;</code> exactly when the right-hand side is inside the closure of the left-hand side. Closures in <code class=\"mm\">F = {A &rarr; B}</code>: <code class=\"mm\">A&#8314; = {A, B}</code>, <code class=\"mm\">B&#8314; = {B}</code>, <code class=\"mm\">C&#8314; = {C}</code>, <code class=\"mm\">AC&#8314; = {A, B, C}</code>, <code class=\"mm\">AB&#8314; = {A, B}</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The only dependency given is <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; B</code>.</li><li>Augmenting it with <code class=\"mm\">C</code> gives <code class=\"mm\">AC &rarr; BC</code>; decomposition then gives <code class=\"mm\">AC &rarr; B</code>.</li><li>So <code class=\"mm\">AC &rarr; B</code> is in <code class=\"mm\">F&#8314;</code>.</li><li>The other three need <code class=\"mm\">B&#8314; &#8839; {A}</code>, <code class=\"mm\">C&#8314; &#8839; {A}</code> and <code class=\"mm\">AB&#8314; &#8839; {C}</code>, and none of those closures contains the required attribute.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) &mdash; <code class=\"mm\">AC &rarr; B</code> is in <code class=\"mm\">F&#8314;</code>.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) reverses the arrow: <code class=\"mm\">B&#8314; = {B}</code> does not contain <code class=\"mm\">A</code>.</li><li>(C) invents a determinant: <code class=\"mm\">C</code> determines nothing here.</li><li>(D) needs <code class=\"mm\">C</code> in <code class=\"mm\">AB&#8314;</code>, and <code class=\"mm\">C</code> is determined by no dependency.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Augmentation adds attributes to the left-hand side only. It never lets you drop part of a left-hand side, and it never lets you reverse an arrow.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U3-Q52", "subject": "databases", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "normalization", "subtopic": "functional-dependencies-armstrong-axioms", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-03", "text": "Relation <code class=\"mm\">R(A, B, C, D)</code> satisfies <code class=\"mm\">F = {AB &rarr; C, C &rarr; D}</code>. Which one of the following functional dependencies is <b>NOT</b> in <code class=\"mm\">F&#8314;</code>?", "options": ["<code class=\"mm\">AB &rarr; D</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">C &rarr; D</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; C</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">ABC &rarr; D</code>"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> To test membership in <code class=\"mm\">F&#8314;</code> compute the closure of the left-hand side under <code class=\"mm\">F</code> and check whether the right-hand side is inside it.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">A&#8314; = {A}</code>: no dependency applies, so <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; C</code> is not derivable.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">(AB)&#8314; = {A, B, C, D}</code> because <code class=\"mm\">AB &rarr; C</code> then <code class=\"mm\">C &rarr; D</code>, so <code class=\"mm\">AB &rarr; D</code> holds.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">C &rarr; D</code> is given outright.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">(ABC)&#8314; = {A, B, C, D}</code>, so <code class=\"mm\">ABC &rarr; D</code> holds.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C) &mdash; <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; C</code> is the only listed dependency that is not in <code class=\"mm\">F&#8314;</code>.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A), (B) and (D) all follow: <code class=\"mm\">AB</code> and <code class=\"mm\">ABC</code> determine everything, and <code class=\"mm\">C &rarr; D</code> is in <code class=\"mm\">F</code>.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> A composite left-hand side is not decomposable. <code class=\"mm\">AB &rarr; C</code> says nothing about <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; C</code>; the two are different determinants.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U3-Q53", "subject": "databases", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "normalization", "subtopic": "functional-dependencies-armstrong-axioms", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-03", "text": "Which of the following inference rules for functional dependencies are valid? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["If <code class=\"mm\">X &rarr; Y</code> and <code class=\"mm\">X &rarr; Z</code> then <code class=\"mm\">X &rarr; YZ</code>.", "If <code class=\"mm\">XY &rarr; Z</code> then <code class=\"mm\">X &rarr; Z</code>.", "If <code class=\"mm\">X &rarr; Y</code> then <code class=\"mm\">XZ &rarr; YZ</code>.", "If <code class=\"mm\">X &rarr; Y</code> and <code class=\"mm\">WY &rarr; Z</code> then <code class=\"mm\">XW &rarr; Z</code>."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (C) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The three rules (A), (C) and (D) are the union, augmentation and pseudotransitivity rules, all of which are derived from Armstrong's reflexivity, augmentation and transitivity and are therefore sound.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) is the union rule: <code class=\"mm\">X&#8314;</code> contains both <code class=\"mm\">Y</code> and <code class=\"mm\">Z</code>, hence <code class=\"mm\">YZ</code>.</li><li>(C) is augmentation: if equal <code class=\"mm\">X</code> values force equal <code class=\"mm\">Y</code> values, then equal <code class=\"mm\">XZ</code> values (which agree on <code class=\"mm\">X</code>) force equal <code class=\"mm\">YZ</code> values.</li><li>(D) is pseudotransitivity: augment <code class=\"mm\">X &rarr; Y</code> with <code class=\"mm\">W</code> to get <code class=\"mm\">XW &rarr; WY</code>, then apply transitivity with <code class=\"mm\">WY &rarr; Z</code>.</li><li>(B) is invalid: it drops <code class=\"mm\">Y</code> from a left-hand side that genuinely needs it.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (C) and (D) are valid; option (B) is not.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) fails on <code class=\"mm\">R(A, B, C)</code> with <code class=\"mm\">F = {AB &rarr; C}</code>: <code class=\"mm\">AB &rarr; C</code> holds, yet <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; C</code> does not, because <code class=\"mm\">A&#8314; = {A}</code>.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Every valid rule either adds attributes to a left-hand side or combines two dependencies that share a determinant. No rule removes an attribute from a left-hand side.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U3-Q54", "subject": "databases", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "normalization", "subtopic": "functional-dependencies-armstrong-axioms", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-03", "text": "Relation <code class=\"mm\">R(A, B, C, D)</code> satisfies <code class=\"mm\">F = {A &rarr; BC, B &rarr; C, A &rarr; B, AB &rarr; C}</code>. How many functional dependencies are there in a minimal cover of <code class=\"mm\">F</code>?<br/>(Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "2", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A minimal cover has every right-hand side a single attribute, no redundant attribute on any left-hand side, and no dependency derivable from the others.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Split every right-hand side: <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; B</code>, <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; C</code>, <code class=\"mm\">B &rarr; C</code>, <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; B</code> (a duplicate), <code class=\"mm\">AB &rarr; C</code>.</li><li>No left-hand side has a redundant attribute: dropping <code class=\"mm\">A</code> from <code class=\"mm\">AB &rarr; C</code> would leave <code class=\"mm\">B &rarr; C</code>, which is already present.</li><li>Prune redundant dependencies. <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; C</code> is implied by <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; B</code> and <code class=\"mm\">B &rarr; C</code>, so drop it. <code class=\"mm\">AB &rarr; C</code> is implied by the same pair, so drop it.</li><li>What remains is <code class=\"mm\">{A &rarr; B, B &rarr; C}</code>: <code class=\"mm\">A&#8314; = {A, B, C}</code> without the second dependency, so neither survivor is redundant.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 2 &mdash; the minimal cover is <code class=\"mm\">{A &rarr; B, B &rarr; C}</code>.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> The duplicate <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; B</code> and the composite right-hand side <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; BC</code> are the two traps. A minimal cover counts distinct single-attribute dependencies, not the lines written in <code class=\"mm\">F</code>.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U3-Q55", "subject": "databases", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "normalization", "subtopic": "functional-dependencies-armstrong-axioms", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-03", "text": "<code class=\"mm\">F = {A &rarr; B, AB &rarr; C, C &rarr; A}</code> is a set of dependencies on <code class=\"mm\">R(A, B, C)</code>. Which one of the following sets of dependencies is equivalent to <code class=\"mm\">F</code>?", "options": ["<code class=\"mm\">{A &rarr; B, C &rarr; A, B &rarr; C}</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">{A &rarr; B, C &rarr; A, A &rarr; C}</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">{A &rarr; B, C &rarr; A}</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">{A &rarr; B, AB &rarr; C, B &rarr; C}</code>"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Two sets are equivalent when each implies every dependency of the other, that is when they have the same closures for every attribute set. It is not enough to check one direction.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Closures under <code class=\"mm\">F</code>: <code class=\"mm\">A&#8314; = {A, B, C}</code>, <code class=\"mm\">C&#8314; = {C, A, B}</code>, <code class=\"mm\">B&#8314; = {B}</code>.</li><li>Closures under option (B), <code class=\"mm\">G = {A &rarr; B, C &rarr; A, A &rarr; C}</code>: <code class=\"mm\">A&#8314; = {A, B, C}</code>, <code class=\"mm\">C&#8314; = {C, A, B}</code>, <code class=\"mm\">B&#8314; = {B}</code> &mdash; identical to those of <code class=\"mm\">F</code>.</li><li>So every dependency of <code class=\"mm\">F</code> follows from <code class=\"mm\">G</code> and vice versa: the two sets are equivalent.</li><li>The three distractors each lose a closure: <code class=\"mm\">B &rarr; C</code> in (A) would need <code class=\"mm\">B&#8314; &#8839; {C}</code>, <code class=\"mm\">AB &rarr; C</code> in (C) needs <code class=\"mm\">(AB)&#8314; &#8839; {C}</code> which fails when only <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; B</code> and <code class=\"mm\">C &rarr; A</code> are given, and <code class=\"mm\">C &rarr; A</code> in (D) needs <code class=\"mm\">C&#8314; &#8839; {A}</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) &mdash; <code class=\"mm\">{A &rarr; B, C &rarr; A, A &rarr; C}</code> is equivalent to <code class=\"mm\">F</code>.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) adds <code class=\"mm\">B &rarr; C</code>, which is not in <code class=\"mm\">F&#8314;</code> because <code class=\"mm\">B&#8314; = {B}</code>.</li><li>(C) drops the ability to derive <code class=\"mm\">AB &rarr; C</code>: with only <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; B</code> and <code class=\"mm\">C &rarr; A</code>, <code class=\"mm\">(AB)&#8314; = {A, B}</code>.</li><li>(D) drops <code class=\"mm\">C &rarr; A</code>: <code class=\"mm\">C&#8314; = {C}</code> there, so <code class=\"mm\">C &rarr; A</code> is not derivable.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Checking only that <code class=\"mm\">F</code> implies <code class=\"mm\">G</code> is the standard error. Equivalence needs both directions, and the fastest check is the set of closures of the single attributes.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U3-Q56", "subject": "databases", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "normalization", "subtopic": "closure-of-attribute-sets-keys", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-03", "text": "Relation <code class=\"mm\">R(A, B, C, D)</code> satisfies <code class=\"mm\">F = {A &rarr; B, C &rarr; D}</code>. Which one of the following is a candidate key of <code class=\"mm\">R</code>?", "options": ["<code class=\"mm\">{A, B}</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">{A, C}</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">{A, D}</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">{B, C}</code>"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A candidate key is a minimal set whose closure is the whole schema. <code class=\"mm\">A</code> and <code class=\"mm\">C</code> appear on no right-hand side, so every key must contain both; the only question is whether anything else is needed.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">(AC)&#8314; = {A, C} &cup; {B} &cup; {D} = {A, B, C, D}</code> using <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; B</code> then <code class=\"mm\">C &rarr; D</code>.</li><li>Neither <code class=\"mm\">A</code> alone (<code class=\"mm\">A&#8314; = {A, B}</code>) nor <code class=\"mm\">C</code> alone (<code class=\"mm\">C&#8314; = {C, D}</code>) determines all four attributes, so <code class=\"mm\">{A, C}</code> is minimal.</li><li>The other pairs fail: <code class=\"mm\">(AB)&#8314; = {A, B}</code>, <code class=\"mm\">(AD)&#8314; = {A, B, D}</code>, <code class=\"mm\">(BC)&#8314; = {B, C, D}</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) &mdash; <code class=\"mm\">{A, C}</code> is the only candidate key of <code class=\"mm\">R</code>.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A), (C) and (D) each omit <code class=\"mm\">C</code> or <code class=\"mm\">A</code>, and neither attribute is determined by any dependency, so no superset that omits them can be a key.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Adding an attribute that is already determined, such as <code class=\"mm\">B</code> or <code class=\"mm\">D</code>, keeps a superkey a superkey but destroys minimality. A candidate key contains only attributes that are not themselves determined.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U3-Q57", "subject": "databases", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "normalization", "subtopic": "closure-of-attribute-sets-keys", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-03", "text": "Relation <code class=\"mm\">R(A, B, C, D, E)</code> satisfies <code class=\"mm\">F = {AB &rarr; C, CD &rarr; E}</code>. Which of the following sets of attributes must be present in every candidate key of <code class=\"mm\">R</code>?", "options": ["<code class=\"mm\">{C}</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">{E}</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">{A, B, D}</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">{A, C}</code>"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> An attribute that never appears on the right-hand side of a dependency can never be derived, so every candidate key must contain it. Here <code class=\"mm\">A</code>, <code class=\"mm\">B</code> and <code class=\"mm\">D</code> appear on no right-hand side.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Attributes on no right-hand side: <code class=\"mm\">A, B, D</code>. Every key therefore contains <code class=\"mm\">{A, B, D}</code>.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">(ABD)&#8314; = {A, B, D} &cup; {C} &cup; {E} = {A, B, C, D, E}</code>: <code class=\"mm\">AB &rarr; C</code> adds <code class=\"mm\">C</code>, then <code class=\"mm\">CD &rarr; E</code> adds <code class=\"mm\">E</code>.</li><li>So <code class=\"mm\">{A, B, D}</code> is already the unique candidate key, and it is exactly the set that must be present in every key.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">C</code> and <code class=\"mm\">E</code> are determined attributes: adding either to <code class=\"mm\">{A, B, D}</code> only makes a superkey.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C) &mdash; <code class=\"mm\">{A, B, D}</code> must be present in every candidate key of <code class=\"mm\">R</code>.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) and (B) name determined attributes, which need not be in a key at all.</li><li>(D) omits <code class=\"mm\">D</code>, which is never determined, so <code class=\"mm\">{A, C}</code> cannot be a key.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Determined attributes are the ones you may leave out. The attribute that looks least important &mdash; here <code class=\"mm\">D</code>, which never appears on the right &mdash; is the one a key cannot do without.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U3-Q58", "subject": "databases", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "normalization", "subtopic": "closure-of-attribute-sets-keys", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-03", "text": "Relation <code class=\"mm\">R(A, B, C, D)</code> satisfies <code class=\"mm\">F = {A &rarr; B, BC &rarr; D, D &rarr; A}</code>. How many candidate keys does <code class=\"mm\">R</code> have?<br/>(Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "3", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Enumerate the attribute pairs, take closures, and keep only those that reach all four attributes; a pair is a candidate key exactly when neither of its members alone does.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">A&#8314; = {A, B}</code>, <code class=\"mm\">B&#8314; = {B}</code>, <code class=\"mm\">C&#8314; = {C}</code>, <code class=\"mm\">D&#8314; = {D, A, B}</code>: no single attribute is a key.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">(AC)&#8314; = {A, C} &cup; {B} &cup; {D} = {A, B, C, D}</code> &mdash; a key.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">(BC)&#8314; = {B, C} &cup; {D} &cup; {A} = {A, B, C, D}</code> &mdash; a key.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">(CD)&#8314; = {C, D} &cup; {A} &cup; {B} = {A, B, C, D}</code> &mdash; a key.</li><li>The remaining pairs fall short: <code class=\"mm\">(AB)&#8314; = {A, B}</code>, <code class=\"mm\">(AD)&#8314; = {A, B, D}</code>, <code class=\"mm\">(BD)&#8314; = {B, D, A}</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 3 &mdash; the candidate keys are <code class=\"mm\">{A, C}</code>, <code class=\"mm\">{B, C}</code> and <code class=\"mm\">{C, D}</code>.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> The cycle <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; B</code>, <code class=\"mm\">BC &rarr; D</code>, <code class=\"mm\">D &rarr; A</code> lets any of <code class=\"mm\">A</code>, <code class=\"mm\">B</code> or <code class=\"mm\">D</code> stand in for the others once <code class=\"mm\">C</code> is present, which is why three different pairs all reach the full schema.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U3-Q59", "subject": "databases", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "normalization", "subtopic": "closure-of-attribute-sets-keys", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-03", "text": "Relation <code class=\"mm\">R(A, B, C, D)</code> satisfies <code class=\"mm\">F = {A &rarr; BC, D &rarr; A}</code>. Which of the following statements are <b>TRUE</b>? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["<code class=\"mm\">D</code> is the only candidate key of <code class=\"mm\">R</code>.", "<code class=\"mm\">A</code> is a prime attribute of <code class=\"mm\">R</code>.", "<code class=\"mm\">R</code> has 8 superkeys.", "<code class=\"mm\">{A, D}</code> is a candidate key of <code class=\"mm\">R</code>."], "answer_display": "Options (A) and (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Prime means \"belongs to some candidate key\". Superkeys are all sets that contain a candidate key.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">D&#8314; = {D, A, B, C} = {A, B, C, D}</code> using <code class=\"mm\">D &rarr; A</code> then <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; BC</code>, so <code class=\"mm\">D</code> is a key.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">A&#8314; = {A, B, C}</code> misses <code class=\"mm\">D</code>, and no other single attribute determines all four, so <code class=\"mm\">{D}</code> is the unique candidate key.</li><li>Therefore <code class=\"mm\">D</code> is the only prime attribute and <code class=\"mm\">A</code> is non-prime.</li><li>Superkeys are the supersets of <code class=\"mm\">{D}</code>: choose freely among <code class=\"mm\">A, B, C</code> &rarr; <code class=\"mm\">2&#179; = 8</code> superkeys.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">{A, D}</code> is one of those eight, but it is not minimal, so it is a superkey and not a candidate key.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A) and (C) are TRUE; options (B) and (D) are FALSE.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) fails because <code class=\"mm\">A</code> is in no candidate key: the only key is <code class=\"mm\">{D}</code>.</li><li>(D) fails because <code class=\"mm\">{A, D}</code> properly contains the key <code class=\"mm\">{D}</code>.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> A relation with one attribute that determines everything has a single candidate key of size one, so exactly one attribute is prime. Counting superkeys by <code class=\"mm\">2&#8319;&#8315;&#185;</code> is valid only when the key is unique and of size one.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U3-Q60", "subject": "databases", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "normalization", "subtopic": "closure-of-attribute-sets-keys", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-03", "text": "Relation <code class=\"mm\">R(A, B, C, D)</code> has exactly two candidate keys, <code class=\"mm\">{A, C}</code> and <code class=\"mm\">{B, D}</code>. How many superkeys does <code class=\"mm\">R</code> have?", "options": ["5", "6", "7", "8"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A set is a superkey exactly when it contains at least one candidate key. Count the supersets of each key and subtract the sets counted twice, which are the supersets of the union of both keys.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Supersets of <code class=\"mm\">{A, C}</code>: choose freely among <code class=\"mm\">B</code> and <code class=\"mm\">D</code> &rarr; <code class=\"mm\">2&#178; = 4</code>.</li><li>Supersets of <code class=\"mm\">{B, D}</code>: choose freely among <code class=\"mm\">A</code> and <code class=\"mm\">C</code> &rarr; <code class=\"mm\">2&#178; = 4</code>.</li><li>Sets counted twice are those containing <code class=\"mm\">{A, B, C, D}</code>, that is only the full set &rarr; <code class=\"mm\">1</code>.</li><li>Total <code class=\"mm\">4 + 4 &minus; 1 = 7</code>: <code class=\"mm\">AC, ABC, ACD, ABCD, BD, ABD, BCD</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C) &mdash; 7 superkeys.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>5 and 6 undercount by subtracting too much; 8 is <code class=\"mm\">2&#179;</code>, the count that would be right only if one attribute were forced into every key.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> With two overlapping keys the inclusion&ndash;exclusion step is compulsory. Adding the two counts gives 8 and double-counts <code class=\"mm\">{A, B, C, D}</code>.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U3-Q61", "subject": "databases", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "normalization", "subtopic": "1nf-2nf-3nf-bcnf", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-03", "text": "Which one of the following normal forms permits a non-trivial functional dependency <code class=\"mm\">X &rarr; A</code> in which <code class=\"mm\">X</code> is not a superkey, provided <code class=\"mm\">A</code> is a prime attribute?", "options": ["1NF", "2NF", "3NF", "BCNF"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> 3NF is the disjunction \"for every non-trivial <code class=\"mm\">X &rarr; A</code>, either <code class=\"mm\">X</code> is a superkey or <code class=\"mm\">A</code> is prime\". BCNF drops the second arm and keeps only the superkey condition.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>1NF asks only that attribute values be atomic; it says nothing about dependencies.</li><li>2NF forbids partial dependencies of non-prime attributes on a candidate key, but permits a transitive dependency of a prime attribute.</li><li>3NF allows exactly the case in the question: <code class=\"mm\">X</code> not a superkey, <code class=\"mm\">A</code> prime.</li><li>BCNF requires <code class=\"mm\">X</code> to be a superkey for every non-trivial dependency, so it does not permit this case.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C) &mdash; 3NF.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) and (B) are weaker conditions that do not describe the superkey-or-prime disjunction at all.</li><li>(D) is the one normal form that forbids the case outright.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> The single difference between 3NF and BCNF is the prime-attribute escape clause. Every relation in BCNF is in 3NF, and the relations that separate the two classes are exactly those with an <code class=\"mm\">X &rarr; A</code> where <code class=\"mm\">A</code> is prime and <code class=\"mm\">X</code> is not a superkey.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U3-Q62", "subject": "databases", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "normalization", "subtopic": "1nf-2nf-3nf-bcnf", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-03", "text": "Relation <code class=\"mm\">R(A, B, C)</code> has the single candidate key <code class=\"mm\">{A}</code> and satisfies <code class=\"mm\">B &rarr; C</code>. Which one of the following is <b>TRUE</b>?", "options": ["<code class=\"mm\">R</code> is in BCNF.", "<code class=\"mm\">R</code> is in 3NF but not in BCNF.", "<code class=\"mm\">R</code> is in 2NF but not in 3NF.", "<code class=\"mm\">R</code> is not in 1NF."], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Test each form in order. With <code class=\"mm\">{A}</code> the only key, <code class=\"mm\">A</code> is the only prime attribute and <code class=\"mm\">B</code>, <code class=\"mm\">C</code> are non-prime.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>2NF: a partial dependency would need a proper subset of a key to determine a non-prime attribute. The only key has size one and has no non-empty proper subset, so 2NF holds.</li><li>3NF: <code class=\"mm\">B &rarr; C</code> is non-trivial, <code class=\"mm\">B</code> is not a superkey (<code class=\"mm\">B&#8314; = {B, C}</code>) and <code class=\"mm\">C</code> is non-prime. The disjunction fails, so 3NF does not hold.</li><li>BCNF fails for the same dependency, so it cannot be in BCNF either.</li><li>Atomic attributes give 1NF.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C) &mdash; <code class=\"mm\">R</code> is in 2NF but not in 3NF.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) and (B) fail because <code class=\"mm\">B</code> is not a superkey, which breaks both BCNF and 3NF.</li><li>(D) fails because 1NF is about atomic values only, and nothing here suggests a repeating group.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> A single-attribute key guarantees 2NF and nothing more. It removes the possibility of a partial dependency but leaves transitive dependencies between non-prime attributes completely untouched.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U3-Q63", "subject": "databases", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "normalization", "subtopic": "1nf-2nf-3nf-bcnf", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-03", "text": "Relation <code class=\"mm\">R(A, B, C, D, E)</code> satisfies <code class=\"mm\">F = {AB &rarr; C, C &rarr; D, D &rarr; E}</code>. How many of the five attributes of <code class=\"mm\">R</code> are non-prime?<br/>(Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "3", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Find every candidate key first; an attribute is prime exactly when it belongs to at least one candidate key.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">A</code> and <code class=\"mm\">B</code> appear on no right-hand side, so both are in every key.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">(AB)&#8314; = {A, B} &cup; {C} &cup; {D} &cup; {E} = {A, B, C, D, E}</code>, so <code class=\"mm\">{A, B}</code> is a key and is minimal.</li><li>No other attribute is forced into a key, so <code class=\"mm\">{A, B}</code> is the unique candidate key.</li><li>Prime attributes: <code class=\"mm\">A</code> and <code class=\"mm\">B</code>. Non-prime: <code class=\"mm\">C</code>, <code class=\"mm\">D</code>, <code class=\"mm\">E</code> &rarr; 3.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 3 &mdash; <code class=\"mm\">C</code>, <code class=\"mm\">D</code> and <code class=\"mm\">E</code> are non-prime.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> The chain <code class=\"mm\">AB &rarr; C &rarr; D &rarr; E</code> makes the three trailing attributes look structural, but a determined attribute is never part of a minimal key, so none of them is prime.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U3-Q64", "subject": "databases", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "normalization", "subtopic": "1nf-2nf-3nf-bcnf", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-03", "text": "Which of the following statements about third normal form are <b>TRUE</b>? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["In a 3NF relation no non-prime attribute depends transitively on a candidate key.", "Every relation whose only candidate key consists of a single attribute is in 3NF.", "A relation can be in 3NF without being in 2NF.", "If every attribute of a relation is prime, the relation is in 3NF."], "answer_display": "Options (A) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> 3NF is equivalent to two familiar descriptions: no transitive dependency of a non-prime attribute on a key, and the superkey-or-prime disjunction on every non-trivial dependency.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) is the classical statement of 3NF and is exactly equivalent to the disjunction.</li><li>(B) is false: <code class=\"mm\">R(A, B, C)</code> with key <code class=\"mm\">{A}</code> and <code class=\"mm\">B &rarr; C</code> has a single-attribute key yet violates 3NF, because <code class=\"mm\">B</code> is not a superkey and <code class=\"mm\">C</code> is non-prime.</li><li>(C) is false: 3NF is stronger than 2NF, so every 3NF relation is in 2NF.</li><li>(D) is true: if every attribute is prime then the right-hand side of every non-trivial dependency is prime, so the disjunction holds for every dependency and 3NF is satisfied automatically.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A) and (D) are TRUE; options (B) and (C) are FALSE.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) confuses 2NF with 3NF: a one-attribute key kills partial dependencies but not transitive ones.</li><li>(C) reverses the containment, which runs 3NF &sub; 2NF &sub; 1NF.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Normal forms nest downwards: BCNF implies 3NF, 3NF implies 2NF, 2NF implies 1NF. Any statement that a weaker form implies a stronger one is automatically false.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U3-Q65", "subject": "databases", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "normalization", "subtopic": "1nf-2nf-3nf-bcnf", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-03", "text": "Which one of the following relations is in 3NF but <b>NOT</b> in BCNF?", "options": ["<code class=\"mm\">R(A, B, C)</code> with <code class=\"mm\">F = {AB &rarr; C, C &rarr; B}</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">R(A, B, C)</code> with <code class=\"mm\">F = {A &rarr; B, B &rarr; C}</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">R(A, B, C, D)</code> with <code class=\"mm\">F = {A &rarr; B, BC &rarr; D}</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">R(A, B, C)</code> with <code class=\"mm\">F = {A &rarr; B, B &rarr; A, A &rarr; C}</code>"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Compute the candidate keys, mark the prime attributes, then check each dependency against the 3NF disjunction and the BCNF superkey condition.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A): keys <code class=\"mm\">{A, B}</code> and <code class=\"mm\">{A, C}</code>; all three attributes are prime. <code class=\"mm\">C &rarr; B</code> has a non-superkey left side but a prime right side, so 3NF holds and BCNF fails.</li><li>(B): key <code class=\"mm\">{A}</code>; <code class=\"mm\">B &rarr; C</code> has a non-superkey left side and a non-prime right side, so 3NF already fails.</li><li>(C): key <code class=\"mm\">{A, C}</code>; <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; B</code> is a partial dependency of the non-prime <code class=\"mm\">B</code> on part of the key, so even 2NF fails.</li><li>(D): keys <code class=\"mm\">{A}</code> and <code class=\"mm\">{B}</code>; every left-hand side is a superkey, so the relation is in BCNF.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A) &mdash; <code class=\"mm\">R(A, B, C)</code> with <code class=\"mm\">F = {AB &rarr; C, C &rarr; B}</code> is in 3NF but not in BCNF.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) is only in 2NF, so it is not a 3NF relation at all.</li><li>(C) is only in 1NF, because <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; B</code> is a partial dependency.</li><li>(D) is in BCNF, which is stronger than what the question asks for.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> This is the textbook witness that 3NF is strictly weaker than BCNF. It needs an overlapping pair of candidate keys and a dependency whose left side is not a superkey but whose right side is prime.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U3-Q66", "subject": "databases", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "normalization", "subtopic": "lossless-join-dependency-preservation", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-03", "text": "Relation <code class=\"mm\">R(A, B, C, D)</code> satisfies <code class=\"mm\">F = {B &rarr; D}</code> and is decomposed into <code class=\"mm\">R&#8321;(A, B, C)</code> and <code class=\"mm\">R&#8322;(B, D)</code>. Which one of the following is <b>TRUE</b>?", "options": ["The decomposition is lossless, because <code class=\"mm\">B &rarr; D</code> holds and <code class=\"mm\">B</code> determines all of <code class=\"mm\">R&#8322;</code>.", "The decomposition is lossy, because <code class=\"mm\">B</code> is not a key of <code class=\"mm\">R</code>.", "The decomposition is lossless only if <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; B</code> also holds.", "The decomposition is lossy, because <code class=\"mm\">R&#8321; &cap; R&#8322; = {B}</code> is not empty."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> For a decomposition into two relations the test is exact: it is lossless if and only if the common attributes functionally determine at least one of the two parts.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">R&#8321; &cap; R&#8322; = {B}</code>.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">B&#8314; = {B, D}</code>, which is exactly the attribute set of <code class=\"mm\">R&#8322;</code>.</li><li>So the intersection determines one part and the decomposition is lossless; no join can create spurious tuples.</li><li>Note that losslessness does not require <code class=\"mm\">B</code> to be a key of the original <code class=\"mm\">R</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A) &mdash; the decomposition is lossless.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) applies the wrong criterion: being a key of <code class=\"mm\">R</code> is sufficient for losslessness, not necessary.</li><li>(C) adds a condition the two-way test does not need.</li><li>(D) states the opposite of the truth: a non-empty intersection is what makes the test applicable at all.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> The common attribute must determine one whole part, not merely be non-empty. Losslessness is about the intersection's closure covering <code class=\"mm\">R&#8321;</code> or <code class=\"mm\">R&#8322;</code>.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U3-Q67", "subject": "databases", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "normalization", "subtopic": "lossless-join-dependency-preservation", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-03", "text": "Relation <code class=\"mm\">R(A, B, C, D)</code> satisfies <code class=\"mm\">F = {A &rarr; B, B &rarr; C, C &rarr; D, D &rarr; A}</code> and is decomposed into <code class=\"mm\">R&#8321;(A, B)</code> and <code class=\"mm\">R&#8322;(C, D)</code>. How many of the four dependencies in <code class=\"mm\">F</code> are preserved by this decomposition?<br/>(Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "2", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A dependency is preserved when it can be derived from the union of the dependencies that hold inside the individual parts. Compute the projection of <code class=\"mm\">F</code> onto each part first.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>On <code class=\"mm\">R&#8321;(A, B)</code>: <code class=\"mm\">A&#8314; = {A, B}</code> and <code class=\"mm\">B&#8314; = {B, C, D, A}</code>, so the projection contains <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; B</code> and <code class=\"mm\">B &rarr; A</code>.</li><li>On <code class=\"mm\">R&#8322;(C, D)</code>: <code class=\"mm\">C&#8314; = {C, D, A, B}</code> and <code class=\"mm\">D&#8314; = {D, A, B, C}</code>, so the projection contains <code class=\"mm\">C &rarr; D</code> and <code class=\"mm\">D &rarr; C</code>.</li><li>The union of the two projections can derive <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; B</code> and <code class=\"mm\">C &rarr; D</code> but nothing that crosses the two parts.</li><li>So <code class=\"mm\">B &rarr; C</code> and <code class=\"mm\">D &rarr; A</code> are lost: 2 of the 4 dependencies survive.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 2 &mdash; <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; B</code> and <code class=\"mm\">C &rarr; D</code> are preserved; <code class=\"mm\">B &rarr; C</code> and <code class=\"mm\">D &rarr; A</code> are not.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> This decomposition is also lossy, since <code class=\"mm\">R&#8321; &cap; R&#8322;</code> is empty. Losslessness and dependency preservation are independent properties, and a decomposition can fail either one while satisfying the other.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U3-Q68", "subject": "databases", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "normalization", "subtopic": "lossless-join-dependency-preservation", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-03", "text": "Relation <code class=\"mm\">R(A, B, C, D)</code> satisfies <code class=\"mm\">F = {A &rarr; B, B &rarr; C, C &rarr; D}</code> and is decomposed into <code class=\"mm\">R&#8321;(A, B)</code>, <code class=\"mm\">R&#8322;(B, C)</code> and <code class=\"mm\">R&#8323;(C, D)</code>. Which one of the following is <b>TRUE</b>?", "options": ["The decomposition is both lossless and dependency preserving.", "The decomposition is lossless but not dependency preserving.", "The decomposition is dependency preserving but not lossless.", "The decomposition is neither lossless nor dependency preserving."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Check the two properties separately. Dependency preservation looks at whether each dependency lives inside some part; losslessness for a chain of parts is checked by joining them one at a time.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Preservation: <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; B</code> lies inside <code class=\"mm\">R&#8321;</code>, <code class=\"mm\">B &rarr; C</code> inside <code class=\"mm\">R&#8322;</code>, <code class=\"mm\">C &rarr; D</code> inside <code class=\"mm\">R&#8323;</code>. Every dependency is preserved.</li><li>Losslessness of <code class=\"mm\">R&#8321;</code> and <code class=\"mm\">R&#8322;</code>: they share <code class=\"mm\">B</code> and <code class=\"mm\">B&#8314; = {B, C, D} &#8839; R&#8322;</code>, so the join is lossless and yields <code class=\"mm\">(A, B, C)</code>.</li><li>Losslessness of <code class=\"mm\">(A, B, C)</code> and <code class=\"mm\">R&#8323;(C, D)</code>: they share <code class=\"mm\">C</code> and <code class=\"mm\">C&#8314; = {C, D} = R&#8323;</code>, so the join is lossless.</li><li>Both properties hold.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A) &mdash; the decomposition is both lossless and dependency preserving.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B), (C) and (D) each deny a property that the two tests above confirm.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> A chain of relations linked by a key-to-foreign-key style dependency is the standard example of a decomposition that is good on both counts. Do not assume that a multi-way decomposition must be lossy: apply the test pairwise and merge.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U3-Q69", "subject": "databases", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "normalization", "subtopic": "lossless-join-dependency-preservation", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-03", "text": "Which of the following statements about lossless-join decompositions are <b>TRUE</b>? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["The tableau (chase) test is a necessary and sufficient test for losslessness.", "A decomposition of <code class=\"mm\">R</code> is lossless if and only if at least one of the parts is a superkey of <code class=\"mm\">R</code>.", "A decomposition of <code class=\"mm\">R</code> into two relations <code class=\"mm\">R&#8321;</code> and <code class=\"mm\">R&#8322;</code> is lossless if and only if <code class=\"mm\">R&#8321; &cap; R&#8322;</code> functionally determines <code class=\"mm\">R&#8321;</code> or <code class=\"mm\">R&#8322;</code>.", "Any decomposition into two relations that share at least one attribute is lossless."], "answer_display": "Options (A) and (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The chase is the general decision procedure; for two parts it collapses to a simple closure test.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) is true: the chase terminates and produces a row of all distinguished variables exactly when the join is lossless.</li><li>(B) is false: a part being a superkey of <code class=\"mm\">R</code> is sufficient for losslessness but not necessary. A three-way chain such as <code class=\"mm\">R&#8321;(A, B)</code>, <code class=\"mm\">R&#8322;(B, C)</code>, <code class=\"mm\">R&#8323;(C, D)</code> can be lossless with no part a superkey of <code class=\"mm\">R</code>.</li><li>(C) is true and is the two-relation form of the chase.</li><li>(D) is false: sharing an attribute only makes the test applicable. <code class=\"mm\">R&#8321;(A, B)</code> and <code class=\"mm\">R&#8322;(B, C)</code> with no dependency at all is lossy.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A) and (C) are TRUE; options (B) and (D) are FALSE.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) turns a sufficient condition into an if-and-only-if and fails on multi-way decompositions.</li><li>(D) mistakes a precondition of the test for a guarantee of its outcome.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Sufficient is not necessary. A superkey part is the easy way to be lossless, not the only way, and the chase exists precisely because the easy test is incomplete.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U3-Q70", "subject": "databases", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "normalization", "subtopic": "lossless-join-dependency-preservation", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-03", "text": "Relation <code class=\"mm\">R(A, B, C)</code> satisfies <code class=\"mm\">F = {A &rarr; B, B &rarr; C}</code>. Which one of the following decompositions is lossless but <b>NOT</b> dependency preserving?", "options": ["<code class=\"mm\">R&#8321;(A, B)</code> and <code class=\"mm\">R&#8322;(A, C)</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">R&#8321;(A, B)</code> and <code class=\"mm\">R&#8322;(B, C)</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">R&#8321;(A, C)</code> and <code class=\"mm\">R&#8322;(B, C)</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">R(A, B, C)</code> kept as a single relation"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Test losslessness with the intersection closure, then test preservation by asking whether each dependency can be derived from the union of the projections.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A): <code class=\"mm\">R&#8321; &cap; R&#8322; = {A}</code> and <code class=\"mm\">A&#8314; = {A, B, C} &#8839; R&#8321;</code>, so lossless. The projections give <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr; B</code> only; <code class=\"mm\">B&#8314;</code> inside the union is <code class=\"mm\">{B}</code>, so <code class=\"mm\">B &rarr; C</code> is lost. Lossless, not preserving.</li><li>(B): <code class=\"mm\">R&#8321; &cap; R&#8322; = {B}</code> and <code class=\"mm\">B&#8314; = {B, C} &#8839; R&#8322;</code>, so lossless; <code class=\"mm\">B &rarr; C</code> lies inside <code class=\"mm\">R&#8322;</code>, so both dependencies are preserved.</li><li>(C): <code class=\"mm\">R&#8321; &cap; R&#8322; = {C}</code> and <code class=\"mm\">C&#8314; = {C}</code>, which covers neither part, so the join is lossy.</li><li>(D) is the trivial single-relation decomposition, lossless and preserving by definition.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A) &mdash; <code class=\"mm\">R&#8321;(A, B)</code> and <code class=\"mm\">R&#8322;(A, C)</code> is lossless but not dependency preserving.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) is lossless and dependency preserving, so it satisfies too much.</li><li>(C) is lossy, so it fails the first half of the question.</li><li>(D) is both properties trivially.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> This pair of relations is the standard counterexample to \"BCNF decomposition is always dependency preserving\". The lost dependency is the one whose left-hand side straddles both parts.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U3-Q71", "subject": "databases", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "normalization", "subtopic": "multivalued-dependencies-4nf", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-03", "text": "Relation <code class=\"mm\">R(A, B, C, D)</code> satisfies the non-trivial multivalued dependency <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr;&rarr; B</code>. Which one of the following is <b>TRUE</b>?", "options": ["<code class=\"mm\">R</code> is automatically in 4NF.", "<code class=\"mm\">R</code> is in 4NF only if <code class=\"mm\">A</code> is a superkey of <code class=\"mm\">R</code>.", "<code class=\"mm\">R</code> is in 4NF only if <code class=\"mm\">B</code> is a superkey of <code class=\"mm\">R</code>.", "<code class=\"mm\">R</code> is in 4NF only if <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr;&rarr; B</code> is trivial."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> 4NF: for every non-trivial multivalued dependency <code class=\"mm\">X &rarr;&rarr; Y</code> that holds, <code class=\"mm\">X</code> must be a superkey. The condition is on the left-hand side only.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The dependency given, <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr;&rarr; B</code>, is stated to be non-trivial, so it must be tested against the 4NF condition.</li><li>The condition requires <code class=\"mm\">A</code>, the left-hand side, to be a superkey.</li><li>If <code class=\"mm\">A</code> is a superkey the dependency is harmless and 4NF can hold; if <code class=\"mm\">A</code> is not a superkey the relation is not in 4NF.</li><li>Nothing is required of <code class=\"mm\">B</code>, and the dependency being non-trivial is exactly the situation the condition is written for.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) &mdash; <code class=\"mm\">R</code> is in 4NF only if <code class=\"mm\">A</code> is a superkey of <code class=\"mm\">R</code>.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) is false: a non-trivial multivalued dependency whose left side is not a superkey is precisely what 4NF forbids.</li><li>(C) checks the right-hand side, which the definition never mentions.</li><li>(D) is backwards: trivial multivalued dependencies are exempt, not required.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> The left-hand side is the determinant in 4NF just as it is in BCNF. Checking the right-hand side, or assuming any multivalued dependency destroys 4NF, are the two usual errors.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U3-Q72", "subject": "databases", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "normalization", "subtopic": "multivalued-dependencies-4nf", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-03", "text": "Which one of the following relations is in 4NF?", "options": ["<code class=\"mm\">R(A, B, C)</code> with candidate key <code class=\"mm\">{A, B}</code> and the non-trivial multivalued dependency <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr;&rarr; C</code>.", "<code class=\"mm\">R(A, B, C)</code> with candidate key <code class=\"mm\">{A}</code> and the multivalued dependency <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr;&rarr; B</code>.", "<code class=\"mm\">R(A, B, C, D)</code> with candidate key <code class=\"mm\">{A, B, C, D}</code> and the non-trivial multivalued dependency <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr;&rarr; B</code>.", "<code class=\"mm\">R(A, B, C)</code> with candidate key <code class=\"mm\">{A, B}</code> and the non-trivial multivalued dependency <code class=\"mm\">B &rarr;&rarr; C</code>."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A relation is in 4NF when every non-trivial multivalued dependency that holds has a superkey on its left. A dependency is trivial when <code class=\"mm\">Y &#8838; X</code> or <code class=\"mm\">X &cup; Y</code> is the whole schema.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A): <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr;&rarr; C</code> is non-trivial and <code class=\"mm\">A</code> is not a superkey (the key is <code class=\"mm\">{A, B}</code>), so not 4NF.</li><li>(B): <code class=\"mm\">A</code> is the candidate key and therefore a superkey, so the dependency raises no 4NF violation. The relation is in 4NF.</li><li>(C): <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr;&rarr; B</code> is non-trivial because <code class=\"mm\">A &cup; B</code> is not all four attributes, and <code class=\"mm\">A</code> is not a superkey, so not 4NF.</li><li>(D): <code class=\"mm\">B &rarr;&rarr; C</code> is non-trivial and <code class=\"mm\">B</code> is not a superkey, so not 4NF.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) &mdash; <code class=\"mm\">R(A, B, C)</code> with candidate key <code class=\"mm\">{A}</code> and <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr;&rarr; B</code> is in 4NF.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) and (D) each hold a non-trivial multivalued dependency whose left side is not a superkey.</li><li>(C) does the same: a candidate key made of all four attributes makes the dependency non-trivial without making <code class=\"mm\">A</code> a superkey.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> A relation can hold a multivalued dependency and still be in 4NF, provided the left side is a superkey. The presence of a multivalued dependency is not itself a violation.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U3-Q73", "subject": "databases", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "normalization", "subtopic": "multivalued-dependencies-4nf", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-03", "text": "Relation <code class=\"mm\">R(A, B, C, D, E)</code> satisfies the multivalued dependencies <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr;&rarr; B</code>, <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr;&rarr; C</code> and <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr;&rarr; D</code>, and no other constraint. Applying the 4NF decomposition repeatedly until no non-trivial multivalued dependency with a non-superkey left-hand side remains, how many relations result?<br/>(Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "4", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Each non-trivial <code class=\"mm\">X &rarr;&rarr; Y</code> is removed by splitting into <code class=\"mm\">(X &cup; Y)</code> and <code class=\"mm\">(X &cup; (R &minus; X &minus; Y))</code>. Repeat on each part, because the remaining dependencies may still bite inside the larger piece.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">A &rarr;&rarr; B</code> splits <code class=\"mm\">R</code> into <code class=\"mm\">(A, B)</code> and <code class=\"mm\">(A, C, D, E)</code>. The first part has no dependency left, so it is final.</li><li>Inside <code class=\"mm\">(A, C, D, E)</code>, <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr;&rarr; C</code> is still non-trivial, so split into <code class=\"mm\">(A, C)</code> and <code class=\"mm\">(A, D, E)</code>.</li><li>Inside <code class=\"mm\">(A, D, E)</code>, <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr;&rarr; D</code> is non-trivial, so split into <code class=\"mm\">(A, D)</code> and <code class=\"mm\">(A, E)</code>.</li><li>No part retains a non-trivial dependency with a non-superkey left side, so the process stops with four relations: <code class=\"mm\">(A, B)</code>, <code class=\"mm\">(A, C)</code>, <code class=\"mm\">(A, D)</code>, <code class=\"mm\">(A, E)</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 4 &mdash; <code class=\"mm\">(A, B)</code>, <code class=\"mm\">(A, C)</code>, <code class=\"mm\">(A, D)</code> and <code class=\"mm\">(A, E)</code>.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> A single split is not enough: after removing <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr;&rarr; B</code> the other two dependencies are still non-trivial inside the remainder, and each must be removed in turn. The final count is one relation per right-hand side plus the leftover attributes.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U3-Q74", "subject": "databases", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "normalization", "subtopic": "multivalued-dependencies-4nf", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-03", "text": "Which of the following statements about multivalued dependencies and 4NF are <b>TRUE</b>? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["If <code class=\"mm\">X &rarr;&rarr; Y</code> and <code class=\"mm\">X &rarr;&rarr; Z</code> both hold, then <code class=\"mm\">X &rarr;&rarr; YZ</code> holds.", "If <code class=\"mm\">X &rarr;&rarr; Y</code> holds, then <code class=\"mm\">X &rarr;&rarr; Y &minus; Z</code> holds for every set <code class=\"mm\">Z</code> of attributes.", "In a relation that is in 4NF, every multivalued dependency that holds is trivial.", "A non-trivial multivalued dependency whose left-hand side is a superkey may hold in a relation that is in 4NF."], "answer_display": "Options (A) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The multivalued inference rules mirror Armstrong's axioms: complementation, augmentation and transitivity, plus the derived union, intersection and difference rules that require a shared determinant.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) is the union rule for multivalued dependencies and is sound: agreement on <code class=\"mm\">X</code> already forces the <code class=\"mm\">Y</code> and <code class=\"mm\">Z</code> sets to vary independently, so their union varies independently too.</li><li>(B) is unsound: the difference rule needs <code class=\"mm\">X &rarr;&rarr; Z</code> as well, and without it <code class=\"mm\">Y &minus; Z</code> need not be independent of the rest.</li><li>(C) is false: 4NF exempts only non-trivial dependencies whose left side is not a superkey, so a trivial one or one with a superkey left side may hold freely.</li><li>(D) is true and is the whole point of the exemption: the 4NF test is applied only to non-trivial dependencies with a non-superkey left side.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A) and (D) are TRUE; options (B) and (C) are FALSE.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) omits the second determinant that the difference rule requires.</li><li>(C) overstates 4NF: the normal form bans a particular class of multivalued dependency, not all of them.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Every derived rule for multivalued dependencies that removes attributes needs two dependencies with the same left-hand side. A single multivalued dependency licenses complementation, not arbitrary subtraction.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U3-Q75", "subject": "databases", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "normalization", "subtopic": "multivalued-dependencies-4nf", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-03", "text": "Relation <code class=\"mm\">R(A, B, C, D)</code> satisfies both <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr;&rarr; BC</code> and <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr;&rarr; BD</code>. Which one of the following multivalued dependencies must hold in every such relation?", "options": ["<code class=\"mm\">A &rarr;&rarr; B</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">B &rarr;&rarr; A</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">C &rarr;&rarr; A</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">D &rarr;&rarr; B</code>"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Two multivalued dependencies with the same left-hand side can be combined. The intersection rule gives <code class=\"mm\">X &rarr;&rarr; Y &cap; Z</code> and the difference rule gives <code class=\"mm\">X &rarr;&rarr; Y &minus; Z</code> and <code class=\"mm\">X &rarr;&rarr; Z &minus; Y</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Write the two given dependencies with the same determinant: <code class=\"mm\">Y = BC</code> and <code class=\"mm\">Z = BD</code>.</li><li>Intersection: <code class=\"mm\">Y &cap; Z = {B}</code>, so <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr;&rarr; B</code> holds.</li><li>For completeness the difference rule also gives <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr;&rarr; C</code> and <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr;&rarr; D</code>, and complementation then gives <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr;&rarr; D</code> again.</li><li>None of the other options follows: no rule turns <code class=\"mm\">B</code>, <code class=\"mm\">C</code> or <code class=\"mm\">D</code> into a determinant, because neither given dependency has any of them on the left.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A) &mdash; <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr;&rarr; B</code> must hold.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B), (C) and (D) all move an attribute from the right-hand side to the left. Multivalued inference rules never do that; they only combine right-hand sides that share a determinant.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> The right way to read <code class=\"mm\">A &rarr;&rarr; BC</code> is \"for a fixed <code class=\"mm\">A</code>, the <code class=\"mm\">B, C</code> values are independent of the <code class=\"mm\">D</code> values\". Two such statements with the same fixed <code class=\"mm\">A</code> can be intersected or subtracted, but the determinant stays <code class=\"mm\">A</code>.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U4-Q51", "subject": "databases", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "transactions-concurrency", "subtopic": "acid-properties", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-03", "text": "Transaction <code class=\"mm\">T1</code> updates account <code class=\"mm\">A</code> and has not yet committed. Transaction <code class=\"mm\">T2</code> reads the new value of <code class=\"mm\">A</code>, and <code class=\"mm\">T1</code> then aborts. Which ACID property does this situation violate?", "options": ["Atomicity, because <code class=\"mm\">T1</code>'s update is never undone.", "Consistency, because the sum of the balances changed.", "Durability, because <code class=\"mm\">T1</code>'s update did not survive.", "Isolation, because an uncommitted value became visible to another transaction."], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Isolation requires that concurrently executing transactions do not see each other's uncommitted work. A read of a value written by a transaction that has not committed is a <b>dirty read</b>, and it is the isolation property that forbids it.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">T1</code> has written <code class=\"mm\">A</code> but has not committed, so its value is provisional.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">T2</code> reads that provisional value. Nothing in the schedule stops it, because the DBMS has not enforced isolation.</li><li>When <code class=\"mm\">T1</code> aborts, the value <code class=\"mm\">T2</code> read never existed, so <code class=\"mm\">T2</code>'s computation is built on a value the database never held.</li><li>The property that rules this out is isolation. Atomicity, durability and consistency are all untouched here: the abort itself is clean.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (D) &mdash; isolation.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) Atomicity is about undoing <code class=\"mm\">T1</code> completely. The abort does exactly that, so atomicity is respected.</li><li>(B) The balances are restored when <code class=\"mm\">T1</code> is rolled back, so no invariant is permanently broken.</li><li>(C) Durability concerns committed work surviving a crash. <code class=\"mm\">T1</code> never committed, so there is nothing to preserve.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> A dirty read is often blamed on atomicity because it ends with an abort. The abort is atomicity working correctly; the defect is that the value was visible before the abort, which is isolation.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Dirty read, non-repeatable read and phantom read are all isolation failures; a torn or partially applied transaction is an atomicity failure.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U4-Q52", "subject": "databases", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "transactions-concurrency", "subtopic": "acid-properties", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-03", "text": "Under write-ahead logging, the log record describing an update must reach stable storage before the corresponding data page is written to disk. Which guarantee does this rule protect?", "options": ["It keeps the undo information on stable storage, so an uncommitted update already written to disk can be rolled back after a crash.", "It guarantees that no two transactions can deadlock.", "It makes every schedule conflict serializable.", "It removes the need for checkpoints."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Write-ahead logging (WAL) is the ordering rule <i>log first, data page second</i>. With a steal policy an uncommitted page may already be on disk when the system crashes, and only a log record written earlier can say what the old value was.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Take the steal case: <code class=\"mm\">T1</code> modifies page <code class=\"mm\">P</code>, the buffer manager writes <code class=\"mm\">P</code> out, and then <code class=\"mm\">T1</code> aborts or the system crashes.</li><li>To undo, recovery must know the before-image of <code class=\"mm\">P</code>. That information exists only in the log.</li><li>If the data page had been allowed to reach disk before its log record, the before-image would be lost and the update could not be undone, breaking atomicity.</li><li>The same ordering also supports redo: a committed update whose page never reached disk is replayed from the log.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A) &mdash; it preserves the undo information that makes an uncommitted on-disk update reversible.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) Deadlock is a locking phenomenon. The log ordering says nothing about who waits for whom.</li><li>(C) Serializability comes from the concurrency-control protocol (2PL, timestamp ordering), not from the order in which log and data pages are written.</li><li>(D) Checkpoints exist to bound the amount of log that recovery must scan; WAL does not remove them.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> WAL is often remembered only as a redo rule. Its undo half is what makes steal policies safe, and it is the half that questions usually test.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Faster method.</b> If the option mentions locks, deadlock or serializability, it is not a logging guarantee. WAL is about recoverability only.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> WAL = undo information before the page, redo information before the commit is acknowledged.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U4-Q53", "subject": "databases", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "transactions-concurrency", "subtopic": "acid-properties", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-03", "text": "In a recovery subsystem, which pair of ACID properties is served by the undo and the redo components respectively?", "options": ["Durability by undo, atomicity by redo.", "Atomicity by undo, durability by redo.", "Isolation by undo, consistency by redo.", "Consistency by undo, isolation by redo."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Undo removes the effects of transactions that did not commit; redo re-applies the effects of transactions that did commit but whose pages never reached disk. The first is atomicity, the second is durability.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>A transaction that started but did not commit must leave no trace. Its already-written pages are reverted with the before-images, which is <b>undo</b>, and that is <b>atomicity</b>.</li><li>A transaction that committed must survive. Its after-images are replayed, which is <b>redo</b>, and that is <b>durability</b>.</li><li>Neither operation concerns visibility between concurrent transactions (isolation) or invariant checking (consistency).</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) &mdash; undo serves atomicity, redo serves durability.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) Reverses the two.</li><li>(C) and (D) attach recovery to isolation and consistency, which are enforced by concurrency control and by constraints, not by undo and redo.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> The commonest slip is to swap them: undo feels like it protects committed work because it acts on disk. It acts on uncommitted work; redo is the one that protects commits.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Undo = atomicity, redo = durability, both driven from the log.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U4-Q54", "subject": "databases", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "transactions-concurrency", "subtopic": "acid-properties", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-03", "text": "Which of the following statements about the ACID properties are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["Shadow paging can provide atomicity and durability without writing a log.", "A DBMS can guarantee consistency (the C of ACID) by enforcing its declared constraints alone, with no help from the application's logic.", "Choosing an isolation level weaker than serializable relaxes only the I of ACID; atomicity and durability are still enforced.", "A transaction that has written some of its pages to disk without committing must be undone at restart, and that undo is the atomicity property at work."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (C) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Each ACID letter has its own enforcement mechanism: atomicity and durability through logging or shadow paging, isolation through the concurrency-control protocol, and consistency through constraints plus correct application logic.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) is TRUE. Shadow paging writes a modified page to a free page and switches the page table only at commit, so a crash leaves either the old or the new state. No log is needed.</li><li>(B) is FALSE. Declared constraints catch only what can be written down as a constraint; a transfer that debits one account and never credits the other can still satisfy every declared constraint unless the transaction itself is written correctly.</li><li>(C) is TRUE. Isolation levels such as read committed trade visibility for throughput, but the log is still written, so committed work survives and aborted work is removed.</li><li>(D) is TRUE. That is precisely the steal case that undo exists for.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (C) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Consistency is the one property the DBMS cannot supply on its own. It is the application that must preserve invariants across statements; the DBMS only rejects violations it was told about.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> A, C and D hold; B overstates what constraints can do.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U4-Q55", "subject": "databases", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "transactions-concurrency", "subtopic": "acid-properties", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-03", "text": "A system uses steal / no-force buffer management with write-ahead logging. The log on stable storage reads, in order: <code class=\"mm\">&#10216;T1 start&#10217;, &#10216;T1 write X&#10217;, &#10216;T2 start&#10217;, &#10216;T2 write Y&#10217;, &#10216;T1 commit&#10217;, &#10216;T3 start&#10217;, &#10216;T3 write Z&#10217;, &#10216;T2 write W&#10217;, &#10216;T2 commit&#10217;, &#10216;T4 start&#10217;, &#10216;T4 write V&#10217;, &#10216;T3 write U&#10217;</code> and then the system crashes. How many write operations must be undone during recovery? (Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "3", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Recovery partitions the log into a redo set (transactions with a commit record) and an undo set (transactions that started but never committed). Only the undo set is rolled back, and the count asked for is of write records, not of transactions.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Scan the log for commit records: <code class=\"mm\">T1</code> and <code class=\"mm\">T2</code> committed.</li><li>Everything else that has a start record without a commit record is in the undo set: <code class=\"mm\">T3</code> and <code class=\"mm\">T4</code>.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">T3</code> wrote <code class=\"mm\">Z</code> and <code class=\"mm\">U</code>; <code class=\"mm\">T4</code> wrote <code class=\"mm\">V</code>.</li><li>That is 2 + 1 = 3 write records to undo. The writes of <code class=\"mm\">T1</code> (<code class=\"mm\">X</code>) and <code class=\"mm\">T2</code> (<code class=\"mm\">Y</code>, <code class=\"mm\">W</code>) are redone if their pages are missing, never undone.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 3</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Two traps. First, counting transactions (2) instead of write records (3). Second, undoing <code class=\"mm\">T2</code>'s write of <code class=\"mm\">W</code> because it appears after <code class=\"mm\">T3</code> started: a commit record anywhere in the log protects every earlier write of that transaction.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Faster method.</b> Mark each transaction C or U first, then count write records of the U transactions only.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Undo set = started but no commit record; count write records, not transactions.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U4-Q56", "subject": "databases", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "transactions-concurrency", "subtopic": "schedules-serializability-conflict-view", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-03", "text": "Consider the schedule <code class=\"mm\">S = r1(X); r2(X); w2(X); r1(Y); w1(Y)</code>. Which one of the following is TRUE?", "options": ["<code class=\"mm\">S</code> is not conflict serializable, because <code class=\"mm\">T2</code> reads <code class=\"mm\">X</code> before <code class=\"mm\">T1</code> has finished.", "<code class=\"mm\">S</code> is conflict serializable and conflict-equivalent to the serial schedule <code class=\"mm\">T1; T2</code>.", "<code class=\"mm\">S</code> is conflict serializable and conflict-equivalent to the serial schedule <code class=\"mm\">T2; T1</code>.", "The precedence graph of <code class=\"mm\">S</code> contains the cycle <code class=\"mm\">T1 &rarr; T2 &rarr; T1</code>."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Overlapping in time is not a conflict. Build the precedence graph from conflicting pairs only, and the schedule is conflict serializable exactly when that graph is acyclic.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">r1(X)</code> and <code class=\"mm\">r2(X)</code> both read <code class=\"mm\">X</code>: two reads never conflict, so no edge.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">r1(X)</code> precedes <code class=\"mm\">w2(X)</code>: a read before a write on the same item from different transactions conflicts, giving the edge <code class=\"mm\">T1 &rarr; T2</code>.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">w2(X)</code> and <code class=\"mm\">r1(Y)</code>, <code class=\"mm\">w1(Y)</code> touch different items, so no further edge. <code class=\"mm\">r1(Y)</code> and <code class=\"mm\">w1(Y)</code> belong to the same transaction, which is not a conflict.</li><li>The graph is the single edge <code class=\"mm\">T1 &rarr; T2</code>: acyclic, so <code class=\"mm\">S</code> is conflict serializable and equivalent to <code class=\"mm\">T1; T2</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) &mdash; conflict serializable, equivalent to <code class=\"mm\">T1; T2</code>.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) Concurrency alone is not disqualifying; only a cycle is.</li><li>(C) The only edge runs <code class=\"mm\">T1 &rarr; T2</code>, so the equivalent serial order is <code class=\"mm\">T1</code> first.</li><li>(D) One edge cannot form a cycle.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Students often add an edge for every pair of operations from different transactions. Reads do not conflict with reads, and operations on different items never conflict.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Faster method.</b> Write the operations of each item in order, and add an edge only where a write is separated from another transaction's operation on that item.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Edge = two operations, different transactions, same item, at least one write, and the earlier one comes first.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U4-Q57", "subject": "databases", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "transactions-concurrency", "subtopic": "schedules-serializability-conflict-view", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-03", "text": "Two schedules are view equivalent when three conditions hold. Which one of the following is NOT one of those three conditions?", "options": ["Every read in one schedule reads the value produced by the same write as the corresponding read in the other schedule.", "For every data item, the transaction that performs the last write in one schedule also performs the last write in the other.", "The two schedules perform the same number of read and write operations on each data item.", "A read that sees the initial value of an item in one schedule also sees the initial value in the other."], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> View equivalence is defined by <i>what each read sees</i> and <i>who writes last</i>, never by how many operations there are. Its three conditions are: same initial reads, same read-from relation, same final write.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Condition 1: if a transaction reads the initial value of an item in the first schedule, it must read the initial value in the second. That is option (D).</li><li>Condition 2: if a transaction reads a value written by another transaction in the first schedule, it must read that same writer's value in the second. That is option (A).</li><li>Condition 3: the last write on each item must be performed by the same transaction in both schedules. That is option (B).</li><li>Counting operations is not part of the definition: two view-equivalent schedules may even differ in the number of writes, as long as no read observes the difference.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C) &mdash; matching operation counts is not a view-equivalence condition.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A), (B) and (D) are exactly the three defining conditions.</li><li>(C) describes a syntactic similarity that view equivalence deliberately does not require.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Because conflict equivalence is checked by counting conflicting pairs, it is tempting to think view equivalence also counts operations. It compares observations, not operation tallies.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> View equivalence: same initial reads, same read-from, same final writes. Nothing else.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U4-Q58", "subject": "databases", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "transactions-concurrency", "subtopic": "schedules-serializability-conflict-view", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-03", "text": "In the precedence (serialization) graph of a schedule <code class=\"mm\">S</code>, a directed edge <code class=\"mm\">Ti &rarr; Tj</code> is drawn exactly when", "options": ["<code class=\"mm\">Ti</code> starts before <code class=\"mm\">Tj</code> in <code class=\"mm\">S</code>.", "<code class=\"mm\">Ti</code> and <code class=\"mm\">Tj</code> both access at least one common data item.", "<code class=\"mm\">Ti</code> commits before <code class=\"mm\">Tj</code>.", "some operation of <code class=\"mm\">Ti</code> precedes and conflicts with some operation of <code class=\"mm\">Tj</code> in <code class=\"mm\">S</code>."], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The precedence graph encodes only the orders that the schedule is forced to respect. Those orders come from conflicting pairs, not from arrival, overlap or commit times.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Two operations conflict when they belong to different transactions, touch the same data item, and at least one of them is a write.</li><li>Each such pair, in the order it occurs, forces one transaction to behave as if it had run before the other.</li><li>The edge <code class=\"mm\">Ti &rarr; Tj</code> records exactly that forced order.</li><li>Start times, shared items without a write, and commit times create no such obligation, so none of them produces an edge.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) Two transactions may start in one order and still be serializable in the other.</li><li>(B) Shared access to a read-only item creates no conflict at all.</li><li>(C) Commit order matters for recoverability, not for the precedence graph.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Commit order is a recoverability notion. Mixing it into the precedence graph is the most common way to get the wrong set of edges.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Precedence graph edges come from conflicts; cycles mean not conflict serializable.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U4-Q59", "subject": "databases", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "transactions-concurrency", "subtopic": "schedules-serializability-conflict-view", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-03", "text": "Which of the following statements about testing and comparing serializability are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["Testing a schedule for conflict serializability takes time polynomial in the number of operations.", "Testing a schedule for view serializability is NP-complete.", "A schedule whose precedence graph contains a cycle can never be view serializable.", "Blind writes are what allow a schedule to be view serializable while failing conflict serializability."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Conflict serializability is decidable by a cycle test on a graph built in polynomial time. View serializability is a strictly weaker but computationally harder property, and the gap between the two classes is created by blind writes.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) is TRUE. Build the graph from the operation list and run a cycle test; both steps are polynomial.</li><li>(B) is TRUE. Deciding view serializability is NP-complete; that is why practical systems enforce conflict serializability instead.</li><li>(C) is FALSE. The classic example <code class=\"mm\">r1(X); w2(X); w1(X)</code> has the cycle <code class=\"mm\">T1 &rarr; T2 &rarr; T1</code>, yet it is view equivalent to <code class=\"mm\">T2; T1</code> because <code class=\"mm\">T2</code>'s write is blind, that is, nobody reads it before <code class=\"mm\">T1</code> overwrites it.</li><li>(D) is TRUE. Without a blind write, any view-serializable schedule is also conflict serializable.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> The implication runs one way only: conflict serializable &rArr; view serializable. The converse fails, and (C) is the false statement that assumes it does not.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Cycle in the graph rules out conflict serializability, never view serializability.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U4-Q60", "subject": "databases", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "transactions-concurrency", "subtopic": "schedules-serializability-conflict-view", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-03", "text": "Consider <code class=\"mm\">S = r1(X); r2(X); w1(Y); w3(Y); r2(Y); w3(X); w1(X)</code>. Call an ordered pair of operations <code class=\"mm\">(op_i, op_j)</code> with <code class=\"mm\">i &lt; j</code> a <i>conflict</i> when the two operations belong to different transactions, touch the same data item, and at least one of them is a write. How many conflicting ordered pairs does <code class=\"mm\">S</code> contain? (Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "7", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A conflict is a pair of operations, not a pair of transactions. Enumerate the operations of one data item at a time and count the pairs that differ in transaction and include a write.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Operations on <code class=\"mm\">X</code>, in order: <code class=\"mm\">r1(X)</code> at position 1, <code class=\"mm\">r2(X)</code> at 2, <code class=\"mm\">w3(X)</code> at 6, <code class=\"mm\">w1(X)</code> at 7.</li><li>Pairs on <code class=\"mm\">X</code>: (1,2) is two reads, no conflict. (1,6) conflicts, (2,6) conflicts, (2,7) conflicts, (6,7) conflicts. (1,7) is <code class=\"mm\">T1</code> with itself, so no conflict. That is 4 pairs.</li><li>Operations on <code class=\"mm\">Y</code>, in order: <code class=\"mm\">w1(Y)</code> at 3, <code class=\"mm\">w3(Y)</code> at 4, <code class=\"mm\">r2(Y)</code> at 5. All three pairs contain a write and use different transactions, so all 3 conflict.</li><li>Total 4 + 3 = 7.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 7</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Two traps. Counting pairs of transactions instead of pairs of operations gives 3. Forgetting that a read followed by a write on the same item from another transaction is a conflict drops the pair <code class=\"mm\">(r1(X), w3(X))</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Faster method.</b> For an item with k operations of which r are reads, the conflicting pairs are all pairs minus the same-transaction pairs minus the read-read pairs.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Conflicts are counted per ordered pair of operations on the same item.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U4-Q61", "subject": "databases", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "transactions-concurrency", "subtopic": "recoverability-cascadeless", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-03", "text": "Which one of the following schedules is recoverable?", "options": ["<code class=\"mm\">r1(X); w1(X); r2(X); c2; c1</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">r1(X); w1(X); r2(X); c1; c2</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">r1(X); w1(X); r2(X); w2(X); c2; a1</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">w1(X); r2(X); c2; w1(Y); c1</code>"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Recoverability asks one question of every read-from edge: does the writer commit before the reader commits? If any reader commits first, the schedule is not recoverable.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Option (A): <code class=\"mm\">T2</code> reads <code class=\"mm\">X</code> written by <code class=\"mm\">T1</code> and commits at <code class=\"mm\">c2</code>, before <code class=\"mm\">T1</code>'s <code class=\"mm\">c1</code>. Not recoverable.</li><li>Option (B): the same read-from edge, but <code class=\"mm\">c1</code> now precedes <code class=\"mm\">c2</code>. Every reader commits after its writer, so the schedule is recoverable.</li><li>Option (C): <code class=\"mm\">T2</code> reads <code class=\"mm\">T1</code>'s value and commits, then <code class=\"mm\">T1</code> aborts. Not recoverable.</li><li>Option (D): <code class=\"mm\">T2</code> reads <code class=\"mm\">X</code> written by <code class=\"mm\">T1</code> and commits before <code class=\"mm\">T1</code>. Not recoverable.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) The reader commits before the writer, so a later abort of the writer would have to undo a committed transaction.</li><li>(C) The reader commits before the writer's outcome is known, and the writer then aborts.</li><li>(D) Same defect as (A): the writer's commit comes last.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Recoverability is about commit order on read-from edges, not about whether the schedule is serializable. All four schedules here are conflict serializable; only one is recoverable.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> For each read-from edge, the writer must commit first.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U4-Q62", "subject": "databases", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "transactions-concurrency", "subtopic": "recoverability-cascadeless", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-03", "text": "Which one of the following schedules is cascadeless but NOT strict?", "options": ["<code class=\"mm\">r1(X); w1(X); r2(X); c1; c2</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">r1(X); w1(X); c1; r2(X); w2(X); c2</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">r1(X); w1(X); w2(X); c1; c2</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">r1(X); w1(X); r2(X); c2; c1</code>"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Cascadeless forbids <i>reading</i> an item before its last writer commits. Strict forbids both reading <b>and writing</b> an item before its last writer commits. The gap between them is exactly a write that overwrites an uncommitted value.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Option (A): <code class=\"mm\">T2</code> reads <code class=\"mm\">X</code> before <code class=\"mm\">T1</code> commits, so it is not even cascadeless.</li><li>Option (B): <code class=\"mm\">T2</code> touches <code class=\"mm\">X</code> only after <code class=\"mm\">c1</code>, so both reads and writes are protected: it is strict.</li><li>Option (C): <code class=\"mm\">T2</code> writes <code class=\"mm\">X</code> while <code class=\"mm\">T1</code> is uncommitted, so it is not strict. But nothing ever reads an uncommitted value, so no abort can cascade: it is cascadeless.</li><li>Option (D): <code class=\"mm\">T2</code> reads uncommitted <code class=\"mm\">X</code> and commits first, so it is not recoverable at all.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C) &mdash; a blind overwrite of an uncommitted value.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) Violates cascadelessness.</li><li>(B) Is strict, the stronger class.</li><li>(D) Is not even recoverable.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> The three classes are often treated as one. They are nested: strict &sub; cascadeless &sub; recoverable. A schedule can sit in the middle band only by writing, never reading, an uncommitted item.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Reading uncommitted data is the cascadeless violation; writing over it is the strict violation.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U4-Q63", "subject": "databases", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "transactions-concurrency", "subtopic": "recoverability-cascadeless", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-03", "text": "A system uses the deferred-update (no-steal) technique, so no update reaches the database before its transaction commits. The schedules it produces are automatically", "options": ["conflict serializable but not necessarily recoverable.", "cascadeless but not necessarily recoverable.", "recoverable only if every transaction is short.", "strict, and therefore cascadeless and recoverable."], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> With no-steal, an uncommitted value never reaches the database, so no other transaction can read it or overwrite it. That is precisely the strict condition.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Under no-steal, a data item in the database always holds a committed value.</li><li>A read therefore never observes an uncommitted write, which gives cascadelessness.</li><li>A write never overwrites an uncommitted value either, which is the additional strict condition.</li><li>Since strict &sub; cascadeless &sub; recoverable, all three properties hold at once.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) and (B) both deny recoverability, which no-steal guarantees.</li><li>(C) makes recoverability depend on transaction length, which plays no part.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Deferred update is often confused with a concurrency-control protocol. It constrains buffer management only; serializability still has to come from locking or timestamp ordering.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> No-steal &rArr; strict &rArr; cascadeless &rArr; recoverable.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U4-Q64", "subject": "databases", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "transactions-concurrency", "subtopic": "recoverability-cascadeless", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-03", "text": "Which of the following statements about recoverable, cascadeless and strict schedules are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["Every strict schedule is cascadeless.", "Every cascadeless schedule is recoverable.", "A cascadeless schedule cannot suffer cascading rollback.", "Every recoverable schedule is cascadeless."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The three classes form a chain of strict inclusions: strict &sub; cascadeless &sub; recoverable. Each step up adds one restriction, so implications run downward only.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) is TRUE. Strict forbids reading and writing an uncommitted item; cascadeless forbids only reading it. The stronger condition implies the weaker one.</li><li>(B) is TRUE. If no transaction reads an uncommitted value, every reader reads from a committed writer, so every read-from edge already has the writer committing first.</li><li>(C) is TRUE. Cascading rollback is triggered by a read of a value that is later undone, and cascadelessness removes exactly those reads.</li><li>(D) is FALSE. Counterexample: <code class=\"mm\">r1(X); w1(X); r2(X); c1; c2</code>. <code class=\"mm\">T2</code> reads an uncommitted value, so the schedule is not cascadeless; but <code class=\"mm\">T1</code> commits before <code class=\"mm\">T2</code> does, so it is recoverable. Recoverability constrains commit order only; it never forbids the dirty read itself.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> The usual error is to reverse an inclusion, for example claiming every recoverable schedule is cascadeless. Recoverability only constrains commit order; it does not stop a read of an uncommitted value from happening in the first place.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> strict &sub; cascadeless &sub; recoverable, and implications run one way only.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U4-Q65", "subject": "databases", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "transactions-concurrency", "subtopic": "recoverability-cascadeless", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-03", "text": "Consider <code class=\"mm\">r1(X); w1(X); r2(X); w2(Y); r3(Y); w3(Z); r4(Z); w4(W); r5(W)</code>. No transaction has committed when <code class=\"mm\">T2</code> aborts. Under cascading rollback, how many transactions are rolled back in total, counting <code class=\"mm\">T2</code> itself? (Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "4", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Cascading rollback propagates along read-from edges, not along write edges and not backwards. A transaction is rolled back when it read a value produced by a transaction that is being rolled back.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">T2</code> aborts: that is 1.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">T3</code> read <code class=\"mm\">Y</code>, which <code class=\"mm\">T2</code> wrote, so <code class=\"mm\">T3</code> must also roll back: 2.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">T4</code> read <code class=\"mm\">Z</code>, which <code class=\"mm\">T3</code> wrote, so <code class=\"mm\">T4</code> rolls back: 3.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">T5</code> read <code class=\"mm\">W</code>, which <code class=\"mm\">T4</code> wrote, so <code class=\"mm\">T5</code> rolls back: 4.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">T1</code> wrote <code class=\"mm\">X</code>, which <code class=\"mm\">T2</code> read. Propagation runs from a rolled-back writer to its readers, so <code class=\"mm\">T1</code> is not dragged in by its own reader's abort.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 4</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Two traps. Following write edges instead of read-from edges, which suggests <code class=\"mm\">T1</code> should also go. And stopping after the first hop, which gives 2.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Faster method.</b> Draw arrows from writer to reader for every read, then close the set reachable from the aborted transaction.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Cascading rollback follows read-from edges forward from the aborting transaction.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U4-Q66", "subject": "databases", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "transactions-concurrency", "subtopic": "two-phase-locking-2pl-strict-rigorous", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-03", "text": "Which one of the following locking protocols is both conflict serializable and free from deadlock?", "options": ["Basic two-phase locking", "Strict two-phase locking", "Conservative (static) two-phase locking", "Rigorous two-phase locking"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Every two-phase locking variant is conflict serializable because the lock point orders the transactions. Deadlock freedom needs an extra rule: a transaction must not hold a lock while waiting for another, which is what acquiring all locks up front achieves.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Basic, strict and rigorous 2PL all allow a transaction to hold one lock while requesting another, so a cycle of waits is possible.</li><li>Conservative 2PL acquires every lock the transaction will need before it performs any operation.</li><li>If any lock is unavailable, it acquires none and waits holding nothing.</li><li>A transaction that holds nothing can never be part of a wait cycle, so no deadlock can form. Serializability comes from the ordinary two-phase discipline.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C) &mdash; conservative (static) two-phase locking.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) Basic 2PL is conflict serializable but deadlock-prone.</li><li>(B) and (D) add commit-time lock holding, which helps recovery but does nothing for deadlock.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Strict and rigorous are remembered as the safe variants, but the property they add is protection from cascading rollback, not deadlock freedom.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Deadlock-free locking must acquire everything before it holds anything.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U4-Q67", "subject": "databases", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "transactions-concurrency", "subtopic": "two-phase-locking-2pl-strict-rigorous", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-03", "text": "Which one of the following correctly distinguishes rigorous two-phase locking from strict two-phase locking?", "options": ["Strict 2PL holds only exclusive locks until commit; rigorous 2PL holds all locks, shared and exclusive, until commit.", "Rigorous 2PL holds only exclusive locks until commit; strict 2PL holds all locks until commit.", "Strict 2PL forbids lock upgrades; rigorous 2PL permits them.", "Rigorous 2PL has three phases; strict 2PL has only two."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Both variants delay the release of locks to the commit point, and both are conflict serializable and cascadeless. They differ in which locks are delayed: strict delays exclusive locks, rigorous delays every lock.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Strict 2PL: an exclusive lock is held until commit or abort; shared locks may be released earlier, at the end of the growing phase.</li><li>Rigorous 2PL: every lock, shared or exclusive, is held until commit or abort.</li><li>Rigorous is therefore the stronger of the two, and it is what most textbook treatments call the practical default.</li><li>Neither variant forbids upgrades, and both have exactly two phases.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) Swaps the two definitions.</li><li>(C) Lock upgrades are permitted in both, provided they happen in the growing phase.</li><li>(D) Both are two-phase protocols; the phase count is what gives 2PL its name.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Because rigorous is stronger, it is easy to assume it is the one that holds only exclusive locks. It is the other way round: strict is the weaker, exclusive-lock-only version.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Strict delays X-locks; rigorous delays both S- and X-locks.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U4-Q68", "subject": "databases", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "transactions-concurrency", "subtopic": "two-phase-locking-2pl-strict-rigorous", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-03", "text": "The lock point of a transaction under two-phase locking is", "options": ["the moment at which it holds every lock it will ever need, so that only the shrinking phase remains.", "the moment at which it acquires its first lock.", "the moment at which it releases its last lock.", "the moment at which it commits."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A 2PL transaction grows its lock set and then shrinks it. The lock point is the boundary: after it, no new lock may be acquired. Serializability comes from the fact that the lock points of any two transactions cannot interleave in a conflicting way.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>In the growing phase the transaction may acquire locks and must release none.</li><li>At the lock point it holds all the locks it will ever need.</li><li>In the shrinking phase it may release locks and must acquire none.</li><li>The lock point is therefore the pivot of the whole protocol, not its first or last lock operation.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) The first lock opens the growing phase; the lock point comes later.</li><li>(C) The last release ends the shrinking phase.</li><li>(D) Commit happens after the shrinking phase, and under strict 2PL it coincides with the last release.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> The lock point is often placed at the first lock or at commit. It is neither: it is where the transaction stops growing.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Grow to the lock point, then shrink and nothing else.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U4-Q69", "subject": "databases", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "transactions-concurrency", "subtopic": "two-phase-locking-2pl-strict-rigorous", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-03", "text": "Which of the following statements about locking are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["Two transactions may hold shared locks on the same data item at the same time.", "An exclusive lock is compatible with a shared lock held by another transaction.", "Under two-phase locking, a transaction that has entered its shrinking phase and then needs a new lock must abort and restart.", "A transaction that has released a lock may re-acquire a lock on the same item later, provided no other transaction currently holds it."], "answer_display": "Options (A) and (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Lock compatibility is decided by the mode pair: S with S is compatible, and any pair involving X is not. The two-phase rule is a statement about the transaction's whole history, so a re-acquisition after a release always breaks it, whoever else holds the item.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) is TRUE. Shared locks are read-only, and many readers may hold S on the same item.</li><li>(B) is FALSE. An exclusive lock conflicts with every other lock on the item, shared or exclusive.</li><li>(C) is TRUE. Acquiring a lock after releasing one would create a second growing phase, so the protocol is violated; the standard remedy is to abort and restart the transaction.</li><li>(D) is FALSE. Availability of the item is irrelevant: the two-phase discipline is broken by the transaction's own history, not by contention.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A) and (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Option (D) is the standard trap. Whether another transaction holds the item has nothing to do with the two-phase rule; the rule counts the transaction's own acquisitions after its first release.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> S-S compatible; X conflicts with everything; no lock after any release.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U4-Q70", "subject": "databases", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "transactions-concurrency", "subtopic": "two-phase-locking-2pl-strict-rigorous", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-03", "text": "Four transactions perform the sequences below, where <code class=\"mm\">LS</code> is a shared lock, <code class=\"mm\">LX</code> an exclusive lock, <code class=\"mm\">U</code> an unlock and <code class=\"mm\">C</code> the commit point.<br/><code class=\"mm\">T1: LX(X), LX(Y), U(X), C, U(Y)</code><br/><code class=\"mm\">T2: LX(X), LX(Y), C, U(X), U(Y)</code><br/><code class=\"mm\">T3: LS(X), LX(X), C, U(X)</code><br/><code class=\"mm\">T4: LX(X), U(X), LX(Y), U(Y), C</code><br/>How many of the four obey strict two-phase locking? (Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "2", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Strict 2PL requires two things at once: no lock is acquired after any lock has been released (the two-phase rule), and no exclusive lock is released before the commit point.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">T1</code>: <code class=\"mm\">U(X)</code> comes before <code class=\"mm\">C</code>, so an exclusive lock is released early. Fails strict.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">T2</code>: grows through both locks, commits, then releases. Obeys strict.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">T3</code>: upgrades <code class=\"mm\">S</code> to <code class=\"mm\">X</code> while still growing, then commits before releasing. An upgrade is an acquisition, so it must occur in the growing phase, and it does. Obeys strict.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">T4</code>: releases <code class=\"mm\">X</code> and then acquires <code class=\"mm\">Y</code>, which breaks the two-phase rule as well as releasing an exclusive lock early. Fails strict.</li><li>Two of the four obey: <code class=\"mm\">T2</code> and <code class=\"mm\">T3</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 2</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Two traps. Treating the <code class=\"mm\">LS</code> to <code class=\"mm\">LX</code> upgrade in <code class=\"mm\">T3</code> as a violation, when an upgrade inside the growing phase is legal. And counting <code class=\"mm\">T1</code> as strict because it never re-acquires a lock, when the early <code class=\"mm\">U(X)</code> already disqualifies it.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Strict = two-phase plus hold every X-lock until commit.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U4-Q71", "subject": "databases", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "transactions-concurrency", "subtopic": "timestamp-ordering-and-deadlocks", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-03", "text": "Under basic timestamp ordering, a write <code class=\"mm\">w(X)</code> by transaction <code class=\"mm\">T</code> is rejected. Which one of the following is a sufficient condition for that rejection?", "options": ["<code class=\"mm\">TS(T) &lt; RTS(X)</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">TS(T) &gt; WTS(X)</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">TS(T) &lt; TS</code> of every other transaction", "<code class=\"mm\">RTS(X) &lt; WTS(X)</code>"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Basic timestamp ordering rejects a write when it is too late in either respect: too late for the readers of the item, <code class=\"mm\">TS(T) &lt; RTS(X)</code>, or too late for its writers, <code class=\"mm\">TS(T) &lt; WTS(X)</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">RTS(X)</code> is the largest timestamp among the transactions that have read <code class=\"mm\">X</code>.</li><li>If <code class=\"mm\">TS(T) &lt; RTS(X)</code>, a younger transaction has already read a value of <code class=\"mm\">X</code> that <code class=\"mm\">T</code>'s write would now invalidate.</li><li>The only way to keep the read valid is to reject the write and abort <code class=\"mm\">T</code>.</li><li>The condition is sufficient on its own; it does not need the <code class=\"mm\">WTS</code> test to hold as well.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A) &mdash; <code class=\"mm\">TS(T) &lt; RTS(X)</code>.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) is the opposite of the write-timestamp test and is not a rejection condition at all.</li><li>(C) compares timestamps of transactions, but ordering is decided per data item, not globally.</li><li>(D) describes a state of the item, not a relationship between <code class=\"mm\">T</code> and the item.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Basic TO has two rejection tests, one against <code class=\"mm\">RTS</code> and one against <code class=\"mm\">WTS</code>. A question that asks for <i>a</i> sufficient condition is satisfied by either; do not reject (A) because it omits the other.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Faster method.</b> Younger writers must never rewrite what an older reader has already seen, and must never overwrite a younger write.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Write rejected if too late for readers (RTS) or too late for writers (WTS).</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U4-Q72", "subject": "databases", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "transactions-concurrency", "subtopic": "timestamp-ordering-and-deadlocks", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-03", "text": "Under the wound&ndash;wait deadlock-prevention scheme, transaction <code class=\"mm\">Ti</code> requests a lock held by <code class=\"mm\">Tj</code>, and <code class=\"mm\">TS(Ti) &gt; TS(Tj)</code>. Which one of the following happens?", "options": ["<code class=\"mm\">Ti</code> wounds <code class=\"mm\">Tj</code>, aborting it, because <code class=\"mm\">Ti</code> is the younger transaction.", "<code class=\"mm\">Ti</code> is aborted and restarted with a larger timestamp.", "<code class=\"mm\">Ti</code> waits, because it is the younger transaction.", "Both transactions are aborted, breaking a possible cycle."], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Wound&ndash;wait is older-favouring. The older transaction never waits: it <i>wounds</i> the younger holder. The younger transaction always waits. A larger timestamp means younger.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">TS(Ti) &gt; TS(Tj)</code>, so <code class=\"mm\">Tj</code> is the older transaction and <code class=\"mm\">Ti</code> the younger.</li><li>Wound&ndash;wait rule: an older requester wounds the younger holder; a younger requester waits for the older holder.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">Ti</code> is the younger requester, so it waits.</li><li>Because waits always run from younger to older, the wait-for graph cannot contain a cycle, so no deadlock forms.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C) &mdash; <code class=\"mm\">Ti</code> waits.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) Wounding is done by the older transaction, and here <code class=\"mm\">Ti</code> is the younger.</li><li>(B) Aborting the requester with a new timestamp is the wait&ndash;die behaviour for an older requester, not wound&ndash;wait.</li><li>(D) No cycle exists, so there is nothing to break.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> The two schemes are mirror images and are easily swapped. Wait&ndash;die: the older requester dies (aborts). Wound&ndash;wait: the older requester wounds the holder and proceeds.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Faster method.</b> Older = smaller timestamp. Older never waits in wound&ndash;wait, so whoever is younger waits.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Wound&ndash;wait: older wounds, younger waits. Wait&ndash;die: older dies, younger waits.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U4-Q73", "subject": "databases", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "transactions-concurrency", "subtopic": "timestamp-ordering-and-deadlocks", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-03", "text": "In timestamp ordering, <code class=\"mm\">RTS(X)</code> records", "options": ["the timestamp of the transaction that most recently wrote <code class=\"mm\">X</code>.", "the largest timestamp among all transactions that have read <code class=\"mm\">X</code>.", "the number of times <code class=\"mm\">X</code> has been read.", "the timestamp of the oldest transaction still holding a lock on <code class=\"mm\">X</code>."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Every data item carries two timestamps: <code class=\"mm\">RTS(X)</code>, the largest timestamp of any transaction that has read it, and <code class=\"mm\">WTS(X)</code>, the timestamp of the transaction that wrote it last.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>When <code class=\"mm\">T</code> reads <code class=\"mm\">X</code>, the scheduler sets <code class=\"mm\">RTS(X) = max(RTS(X), TS(T))</code>.</li><li>So <code class=\"mm\">RTS(X)</code> is always the largest timestamp of a reader, that is, the youngest reader so far.</li><li>It is compared against <code class=\"mm\">TS(T)</code> of a later write to decide whether that write would invalidate an existing read.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">WTS(X)</code> is the other field, and it tracks the last writer.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) &mdash; the largest timestamp among the readers of <code class=\"mm\">X</code>.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(A) describes <code class=\"mm\">WTS(X)</code>, not <code class=\"mm\">RTS(X)</code>.</li><li>(C) a count is never stored; only a maximum timestamp is.</li><li>(D) timestamp ordering uses no locks at all, so there is no lock holder to record.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> The two fields are frequently swapped. Remember that <b>R</b>TS belongs to <b>R</b>eaders and <b>W</b>TS to the <b>W</b>riter.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> RTS = youngest reader; WTS = last writer.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U4-Q74", "subject": "databases", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "transactions-concurrency", "subtopic": "timestamp-ordering-and-deadlocks", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-03", "text": "Which of the following statements about timestamp ordering are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["A transaction that is restarted after an abort is given a new, larger timestamp, so it is always treated as the youngest transaction.", "Basic timestamp ordering can force cascading rollback, because a read is never blocked and may see a value written by a transaction that has not yet committed.", "Thomas's write rule ignores a write that the basic rule would have rejected, and the transaction continues.", "Under Thomas's write rule a write is still rejected when <code class=\"mm\">TS(T) &lt; RTS(X)</code>."], "answer_display": "Options (B), (C) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Timestamps are fixed at the start of a transaction and survive a restart unchanged. Thomas's write rule relaxes only the write-timestamp test; the read-timestamp test still aborts the writer.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) is FALSE. A restarted transaction keeps its original timestamp. Giving it a new, larger one would let it jump the queue and would break the ordering the protocol depends on.</li><li>(B) is TRUE. Nothing blocks a read, so an uncommitted value can be read; if the writer then aborts, the reader must be rolled back too.</li><li>(C) is TRUE. That is exactly what Thomas's rule does: the obsolete write is skipped, not rejected.</li><li>(D) is TRUE. If a younger transaction has already read <code class=\"mm\">X</code>, writing <code class=\"mm\">X</code> with an older timestamp would invalidate that read, so the write is still rejected.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (B), (C) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Thomas's rule is often described as making timestamp ordering less strict across the board. It relaxes one test only, and option (A) is the other common error: restarts reuse the original timestamp.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Restart keeps the timestamp; Thomas relaxes WTS only, never RTS.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U4-Q75", "subject": "databases", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "transactions-concurrency", "subtopic": "timestamp-ordering-and-deadlocks", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-03", "text": "Transactions <code class=\"mm\">T1</code>, <code class=\"mm\">T2</code> and <code class=\"mm\">T3</code> have timestamps 10, 20 and 30. The read and write timestamps of <code class=\"mm\">X</code>, <code class=\"mm\">Y</code> and <code class=\"mm\">Z</code> all start at 0. Thomas's write rule is used for writes and the basic rule for reads, and the operations run in the order shown:<br/>1. <code class=\"mm\">w2(X)</code> &nbsp; 2. <code class=\"mm\">w1(X)</code> &nbsp; 3. <code class=\"mm\">r3(X)</code> &nbsp; 4. <code class=\"mm\">w1(Y)</code> &nbsp; 5. <code class=\"mm\">w3(Y)</code> &nbsp; 6. <code class=\"mm\">r2(X)</code> &nbsp; 7. <code class=\"mm\">w3(Z)</code> &nbsp; 8. <code class=\"mm\">w1(Z)</code> &nbsp; 9. <code class=\"mm\">r2(Y)</code> &nbsp; 10. <code class=\"mm\">r1(Y)</code><br/>How many of the ten operations are rejected, that is, cause their transaction to be aborted? (Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "2", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Three rules decide each operation. A read is rejected when <code class=\"mm\">TS(T) &lt; WTS(X)</code>. A write is rejected when <code class=\"mm\">TS(T) &lt; RTS(X)</code>. A write with <code class=\"mm\">TS(T) &lt; WTS(X)</code> but not below <code class=\"mm\">RTS(X)</code> is <i>ignored</i> under Thomas's rule, which is not a rejection.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>1. <code class=\"mm\">w2(X)</code>: <code class=\"mm\">TS = 20</code>, both timestamps 0, so it executes and <code class=\"mm\">WTS(X) = 20</code>.</li><li>2. <code class=\"mm\">w1(X)</code>: <code class=\"mm\">TS = 10 &lt; RTS(X) = 0</code> is false, but <code class=\"mm\">10 &lt; WTS(X) = 20</code>, so the write is ignored. Not a rejection.</li><li>3. <code class=\"mm\">r3(X)</code>: <code class=\"mm\">30 &ge; WTS(X) = 20</code>, executes and <code class=\"mm\">RTS(X) = 30</code>.</li><li>4. <code class=\"mm\">w1(Y)</code>: <code class=\"mm\">10 &ge; RTS(Y) = 0</code> and <code class=\"mm\">10 &ge; WTS(Y) = 0</code>, executes and <code class=\"mm\">WTS(Y) = 10</code>.</li><li>5. <code class=\"mm\">w3(Y)</code>: <code class=\"mm\">30 &ge; 0</code> and <code class=\"mm\">30 &ge; 10</code>, executes and <code class=\"mm\">WTS(Y) = 30</code>.</li><li>6. <code class=\"mm\">r2(X)</code>: <code class=\"mm\">TS = 20 &lt; WTS(X) = 20</code> is false, so it executes; <code class=\"mm\">RTS(X) = max(30, 20) = 30</code>.</li><li>7. <code class=\"mm\">w3(Z)</code>: both timestamps 0, executes and <code class=\"mm\">WTS(Z) = 30</code>.</li><li>8. <code class=\"mm\">w1(Z)</code>: <code class=\"mm\">10 &lt; RTS(Z) = 0</code> is false, <code class=\"mm\">10 &lt; WTS(Z) = 30</code> is true, so the write is ignored. Not a rejection.</li><li>9. <code class=\"mm\">r2(Y)</code>: <code class=\"mm\">20 &lt; WTS(Y) = 30</code>, so it is rejected and <code class=\"mm\">T2</code> aborts.</li><li>10. <code class=\"mm\">r1(Y)</code>: <code class=\"mm\">10 &lt; WTS(Y) = 30</code>, so it is rejected and <code class=\"mm\">T1</code> aborts.</li><li>Rejections: operations 9 and 10, that is 2. Operations 2 and 8 were ignored, not rejected.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 2</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> The whole difficulty is the difference between <i>ignore</i> and <i>reject</i>. Counting the two ignored writes gives 4; counting only the abort of the writer gives 1. Note also that operation 6 executes, because the test is strict inequality <code class=\"mm\">TS &lt; WTS</code>, not <code class=\"mm\">&le;</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Faster method.</b> Tabulate <code class=\"mm\">RTS</code> and <code class=\"mm\">WTS</code> per item and tick each operation I (ignored), R (rejected) or E (executed).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Thomas ignores stale writes; only a read that is too late, or a write that a reader has already overtaken, aborts the transaction.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U5-Q51", "subject": "databases", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "indexing-storage", "subtopic": "primary-secondary-clustering-indexes", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-04", "text": "A data file can carry at most one clustering index because:", "options": ["a clustering index must always be built on the primary key", "the records of a file can be physically ordered on at most one field", "a clustering index always needs overflow blocks", "SQL permits only one index per relation"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A clustering index is defined on a field by which the file is physically ordered (that field need not be a key). Since a file has exactly one physical order, it admits at most one clustering index.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>A clustering index is non-dense or dense but always on the ordering field.</li><li>A file's records can be sorted on one field only, so only one field can act as the ordering field.</li><li>Hence at most one clustering index per file, whatever the number of non-clustering (secondary) indexes.</li><li>SQL allows many indexes per table, so option D is a false reason.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Confusing 'one clustering index' with 'one index per relation'. A relation may have many secondary indexes; only the clustering one is limited to one.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U5-Q52", "subject": "databases", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "indexing-storage", "subtopic": "dense-vs-sparse-indexing", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-04", "text": "Which statement must be true of a <b>sparse</b> index?", "options": ["It has one entry for every record in the data file", "It is built on a field on which the data file is physically ordered", "It can be built on any non-ordering field of the file", "It always occupies more blocks than a dense index on the same field"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A sparse (non-dense) index keeps one entry per block, so a block can be located only if records of the searched value are known to be contiguous — that requires the file to be ordered on the index field.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Sparse index: an index entry exists only for the first record of each data block.</li><li>To reach the block holding a given key, the records must be sorted on that key, otherwise the key could be anywhere.</li><li>So a sparse index is possible only on an ordering field (primary index or clustering index).</li><li>Dense indexes, by contrast, may be built on any field, ordering or not.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming a sparse secondary index on an arbitrary field is possible. Without a physical ordering there is no block anchor for the missing keys.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U5-Q53", "subject": "databases", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "indexing-storage", "subtopic": "b-trees-and-b-plus-trees-order-capacity", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-04", "text": "In a B+ tree index, an internal (non-leaf) node stores:", "options": ["only search-key values and block pointers", "search-key values together with record pointers", "only record pointers", "complete data records"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A B+ tree keeps every data pointer in the leaves; internal nodes are a pure navigation structure of (key, child-pointer) pairs.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Leaf nodes hold (key, record-pointer) pairs and are chained in key order.</li><li>Internal nodes hold separator keys and child block pointers only.</li><li>This is exactly why internal fan-out is larger than in a B-tree of the same block size.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Carrying the B-tree layout over to the B+ tree and expecting record pointers in internal nodes.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U5-Q54", "subject": "databases", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "indexing-storage", "subtopic": "static-and-dynamic-hashing", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-04", "text": "The main structural drawback of static hashing is that:", "options": ["no hash function can be used", "the number of buckets is fixed in advance, so a growing file produces long overflow chains", "equality search is not supported", "the file must be kept sorted on the hash key"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> In static hashing the bucket count M is allocated once. As records are added the load factor rises and overflow chains lengthen, degrading search towards linear scan.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Bucket address = <code class=\"mm\">h(K) mod M</code>, with M fixed when the file is created.</li><li>Insertion into a full bucket goes to an overflow block chained to it.</li><li>A growing file therefore converts O(1) search into a chain walk.</li><li>Dynamic schemes (extendible, linear hashing) grow the bucket set instead.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Blaming the hash function. The problem is the fixed bucket count, not the function.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U5-Q55", "subject": "databases", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "indexing-storage", "subtopic": "dense-vs-sparse-indexing", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-04", "text": "A secondary index built on a <b>non-ordering</b> key field of a file must be:", "options": ["sparse", "dense", "a primary index", "impossible to construct"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Without a physical ordering there is no block anchor, so an index entry must exist for every search-key value.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>A sparse index is feasible only if records with equal keys are contiguous, i.e. the file is ordered on that field.</li><li>A non-ordering field destroys that contiguity, so every search-key value needs its own entry.</li><li>Therefore the index is dense (and, for duplicates, points to a list of record pointers).</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Thinking a secondary index is automatically sparse. Secondary indexes on non-ordering fields are dense.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U5-Q56", "subject": "databases", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "indexing-storage", "subtopic": "primary-secondary-clustering-indexes", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "GATE_PYQ_VARIATION", "date": "2026-11-04", "text": "A file has 30,000 records, and a disk block holds 30 records. A <b>sparse primary index</b> is built with 15-byte entries on blocks of 1024 bytes. How many blocks does the index occupy?", "options": ["15", "16", "68", "1000"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Sparse primary index: entries = data blocks; entries per index block = ⌊block size / entry size⌋; index blocks = ⌈entries / entries-per-block⌉.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Data blocks = <code class=\"mm\">30000 / 30 = 1000</code>.</li><li>Index entries (one per data block) = 1000.</li><li>Entries per index block = <code class=\"mm\">⌊1024 / 15⌋ = 68</code>.</li><li>Index blocks = <code class=\"mm\">⌈1000 / 68⌉ = ⌈14.71⌉ = 15</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 15 blocks — option A.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Computing <code class=\"mm\">⌈1024/15⌉ = 69</code> and then 15 by luck, or dividing 30000 by 15 and forgetting the sparse structure entirely. A sparse primary index never has one entry per record.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U5-Q57", "subject": "databases", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "indexing-storage", "subtopic": "dense-vs-sparse-indexing", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-04", "text": "The same file (30,000 records, 30 records per block) carries a <b>dense index on its key</b>. How many entries do the dense index and the sparse primary index hold respectively?", "options": ["30,000 and 1,000", "1,000 and 30,000", "30,000 and 30,000", "1,000 and 1,000"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A dense index on a key has one entry per record; a sparse primary index has one entry per data block.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Dense index on the key: 30,000 distinct key values → 30,000 entries.</li><li>Sparse primary index: one entry per block → <code class=\"mm\">30000/30 = 1000</code> entries.</li><li>So dense = 30,000 and sparse = 1,000.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 30,000 and 1,000 — option A.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming a dense index also collapses duplicates. For a non-key field a dense index has one entry per distinct value plus pointer lists; on a key field it is one entry per record.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U5-Q58", "subject": "databases", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "indexing-storage", "subtopic": "b-trees-and-b-plus-trees-order-capacity", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "GATE_PYQ_VARIATION", "date": "2026-11-04", "text": "In a B-tree of order 6 (a node holds at most 6 children), the minimum number of keys in a <b>non-root internal node</b> is:", "options": ["2", "3", "4", "5"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> For order m: minimum children of a non-root internal node = <code class=\"mm\">⌈m/2⌉</code>, hence minimum keys = <code class=\"mm\">⌈m/2⌉ − 1</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Minimum children = <code class=\"mm\">⌈6/2⌉ = 3</code>.</li><li>A node with c children holds c − 1 keys.</li><li>Minimum keys = <code class=\"mm\">3 − 1 = 2</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 2 keys — option A.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering <code class=\"mm\">⌈m/2⌉</code> (children) instead of keys, or applying the root's relaxed minimum (1 key) to internal nodes.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U5-Q59", "subject": "databases", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "indexing-storage", "subtopic": "static-and-dynamic-hashing", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-04", "text": "In extendible hashing, a bucket with local depth <code class=\"mm\">d</code> overflows while the global depth is <code class=\"mm\">D</code>. The directory is doubled only when:", "options": ["d &lt; D", "d = D", "d &gt; D", "the bucket holds two or more records"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Doubling the directory is needed only when the overflowing bucket is shared by exactly one directory entry, i.e. its local depth equals the global depth.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>A bucket with local depth d is pointed to by <code class=\"mm\">2^(D−d)</code> directory entries.</li><li>If <code class=\"mm\">d &lt; D</code> the directory already has spare prefixes: split the bucket and repoint those entries — no doubling.</li><li>If <code class=\"mm\">d = D</code> the bucket has a single directory entry, so a new prefix bit is required: double the directory and then split.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: <code class=\"mm\">d = D</code> — option B.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Doubling on every overflow. Doubling is conditional and happens only at <code class=\"mm\">d = D</code>.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U5-Q60", "subject": "databases", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "indexing-storage", "subtopic": "primary-secondary-clustering-indexes", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-04", "text": "Compared with a static multilevel index, the decisive advantage of a B+ tree index is that it:", "options": ["keeps the structure balanced under insertions and deletions by node splitting and merging, so no overflow chains accumulate", "needs no root node", "stores records only in internal nodes", "can be built only on the primary key"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A multilevel index is a static structure: insertions push overflow blocks onto the lowest level and the level count drifts. A B+ tree is a dynamic, height-balanced structure.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Multilevel index: built bottom-up over a fixed set of data blocks; new records go to overflow blocks chained to a block.</li><li>B+ tree: insertion into a full node splits it and propagates the separator upward, keeping all leaves at one depth.</li><li>Deletion merges or redistributes, so occupancy stays between the minimum and maximum.</li><li>The result is a bounded, predictable search cost.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Believing a multilevel index is also balanced. It is static; only the B-tree family self-balances.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U5-Q61", "subject": "databases", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "indexing-storage", "subtopic": "dense-vs-sparse-indexing", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-04", "text": "A sparse primary index occupies 10 blocks. Using binary search on the index and then one access to the data block, the <b>maximum</b> number of block accesses to fetch a record is:", "options": ["4", "5", "6", "11"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Binary search over b index blocks costs <code class=\"mm\">⌈log2 b⌉</code> accesses; the final data block adds one more.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Index blocks b = 10, so binary search needs <code class=\"mm\">⌈log2 10⌉ = 4</code> accesses.</li><li>The pointer found leads to one data block: +1 access.</li><li>Total = <code class=\"mm\">4 + 1 = 5</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 5 accesses — option B.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reporting <code class=\"mm\">⌈log2 10⌉ = 4</code> and forgetting the data-block access, or answering 11 by scanning the index linearly.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U5-Q62", "subject": "databases", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "indexing-storage", "subtopic": "b-trees-and-b-plus-trees-order-capacity", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "GATE_PYQ_VARIATION", "date": "2026-11-04", "text": "A B+ tree is built on a file with block size 2048 bytes, search key 10 bytes, block pointer 8 bytes and record pointer 6 bytes. The maximum number of record pointers that fit in one <b>leaf</b> node is:", "options": ["127", "114", "128", "204"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Leaf node cost: each entry is (key + record pointer) plus one block pointer to the next leaf.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Leaf entry size = <code class=\"mm\">10 + 6 = 16</code> bytes.</li><li>Reserve one block pointer (8 bytes) for the sibling link: usable = <code class=\"mm\">2048 − 8 = 2040</code>.</li><li>Number of entries = <code class=\"mm\">⌊2040 / 16⌋ = 127</code> (127.5 truncated).</li><li>Internal node for comparison: <code class=\"mm\">p·8 + (p−1)·10 ≤ 2048</code> gives <code class=\"mm\">p ≤ 114</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 127 record pointers — option A.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Using <code class=\"mm\">⌊2048/16⌋ = 128</code> and forgetting the 8-byte sibling pointer. Also mixing the leaf figure (127) with the internal fan-out (114).</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U5-Q63", "subject": "databases", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "indexing-storage", "subtopic": "static-and-dynamic-hashing", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-04", "text": "Which statement correctly separates <b>linear hashing</b> from extendible hashing?", "options": ["Linear hashing uses a split pointer and overflow chains instead of a directory, splitting buckets in a fixed round-robin order", "Linear hashing cannot handle insertions once the last bucket is full", "Linear hashing keeps a directory that doubles whenever any bucket overflows", "Linear hashing requires the file to be kept sorted on the hash key"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Linear hashing is directory-less: a split pointer walks the buckets in order, and the address computation uses one or two hash functions depending on the pointer position.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Extendible hashing: directory of <code class=\"mm\">2^D</code> entries; a bucket is split on demand when it overflows.</li><li>Linear hashing: no directory; bucket <code class=\"mm\">n</code> (the split pointer) is split next, chosen by a schedule rather than by which bucket overflowed.</li><li>Addressing: <code class=\"mm\">h0(K)</code> if <code class=\"mm\">h0(K) ≥ n</code>, else <code class=\"mm\">h1(K)</code> — one extra hash function while a round is in progress.</li><li>Because splits are not triggered by the overflowing bucket itself, overflow chains remain possible.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Attributing the directory to linear hashing. The directory belongs to extendible hashing.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U5-Q64", "subject": "databases", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "indexing-storage", "subtopic": "primary-secondary-clustering-indexes", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-04", "text": "An employee file is physically ordered on <code class=\"mm\">salary</code> and carries a clustering index on <code class=\"mm\">salary</code>. Which query gains the most from that index?", "options": ["<code class=\"mm\">SELECT * FROM Emp WHERE empid = 7</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">SELECT * FROM Emp WHERE salary BETWEEN 40000 AND 50000</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">SELECT COUNT(*) FROM Emp</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">SELECT * FROM Emp WHERE name = 'Rao'</code>"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A clustering index pays off when a query fetches a <b>range</b> of ordered values: the qualifying records sit in consecutive blocks, so the scan is sequential.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The file is ordered on salary, so all tuples with salary in [40000, 50000] occupy a contiguous block range.</li><li>The clustering index locates the first qualifying block in one probe.</li><li>The remaining blocks are read sequentially — near-optimal I/O for a range query.</li><li>A single equality on <code class=\"mm\">empid</code> or on <code class=\"mm\">name</code> uses a different (secondary or primary key) index; <code class=\"mm\">COUNT(*)</code> scans everything.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Choosing the equality query. Clustering rewards ranges; a unique equality needs only one block either way.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U5-Q65", "subject": "databases", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "indexing-storage", "subtopic": "b-trees-and-b-plus-trees-order-capacity", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "GATE_PYQ_VARIATION", "date": "2026-11-04", "text": "A B+ tree of order 5 (each internal node holds at most 5 pointers; each leaf holds at most 4 key–pointer entries) indexes 100 distinct keys. The <b>minimum</b> possible number of leaf nodes is:", "options": ["20", "25", "34", "50"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Fewest leaves means each leaf is packed to its maximum capacity of <code class=\"mm\">p − 1</code> entries.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Leaf capacity = <code class=\"mm\">p − 1 = 4</code> entries.</li><li>Minimum leaves = <code class=\"mm\">⌈100 / 4⌉ = 25</code>.</li><li>Check: 25 leaves × 4 entries = 100 keys exactly, so no rounding slack.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 25 leaves — option B.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Using the order 5 as the leaf capacity and answering 20. The leaf holds <code class=\"mm\">p − 1</code> entries, the internal node holds <code class=\"mm\">p</code> pointers.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U5-Q66", "subject": "databases", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "indexing-storage", "subtopic": "b-trees-and-b-plus-trees-order-capacity", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-04", "text": "Which of the following are true of a B+ tree index? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["Every search-key value appears in some leaf node", "Leaf nodes are chained together in key order", "Internal nodes contain only separator keys and block pointers", "Data records are stored in internal nodes"], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A B+ tree is a two-part structure: internal nodes for navigation, a linked list of leaves holding all keys and all record pointers.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>All keys are replicated into the leaves; internal nodes only guide the search (A, C).</li><li>The leaf level is a doubly or singly linked list, which makes range scans sequential (B).</li><li>Record pointers exist only at the leaf level, so D is false.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: A, B and C. D is false.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Importing the B-tree property that internal nodes carry data pointers. In a B+ tree they never do.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U5-Q67", "subject": "databases", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "indexing-storage", "subtopic": "dense-vs-sparse-indexing", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-04", "text": "Which statements about dense and sparse indexes are correct? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["A dense index contains an entry for every search-key value present in the file", "A dense index can be built on a non-ordering field", "A sparse index must be built on a field on which the file is ordered", "A dense index always occupies fewer blocks than a sparse index on the same field"], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Density is about entry count, not about ordering. Dense = one entry per search-key value; sparse = one entry per block, which forces an ordering field.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>By definition a dense index has an entry for every value, whether or not the file is ordered (A, B).</li><li>A sparse index can omit keys only if it can infer their block from a neighbour, which needs ordering (C).</li><li>A dense index has more entries and hence normally more blocks than a sparse one, so D is false.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: A, B and C. D is false.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Equating 'dense' with 'on a key field'. Density is independent of whether the field is a key or is ordered.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U5-Q68", "subject": "databases", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "indexing-storage", "subtopic": "static-and-dynamic-hashing", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-04", "text": "Which statements hold for extendible hashing? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["The directory has <code class=\"mm\">2^D</code> entries, where <code class=\"mm\">D</code> is the global depth", "A bucket's local depth never exceeds the global depth", "Every bucket split doubles the directory", "Two or more directory entries may point to the same bucket"], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Global depth sizes the directory; local depth records how many low-order bits distinguish a bucket. Buckets with <code class=\"mm\">d &lt; D</code> are shared by several directory slots.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Directory size = <code class=\"mm\">2^D</code> (A).</li><li>By construction <code class=\"mm\">d ≤ D</code>; a bucket with <code class=\"mm\">d = D</code> is pointed to by exactly one slot (B).</li><li>A split doubles the directory only when <code class=\"mm\">d = D</code>; otherwise the split merely repoints existing slots (so C is false).</li><li>Buckets with <code class=\"mm\">d &lt; D</code> are referenced by <code class=\"mm\">2^(D−d)</code> directory entries (D).</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: A, B and D. C is false.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Treating directory doubling as unconditional. It is conditional on the overflowing bucket's local depth.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U5-Q69", "subject": "databases", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "indexing-storage", "subtopic": "primary-secondary-clustering-indexes", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-11-04", "text": "Which statements about a <b>primary index</b> are correct? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["It is defined on the field by which the data file is ordered, and that field is a key", "It is a sparse index", "The number of index entries equals the number of blocks in the data file", "A file may have two primary indexes, one ascending and one descending"], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A primary index sits on the ordering key field and is sparse: one entry per block, holding that block's first key.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Ordering key field is the definition (A).</li><li>One entry per block makes it sparse (B, C).</li><li>Only one field can order the file, so only one primary index exists (D is false).</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: A, B and C. D is false.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Confusing the primary index with the primary key constraint. The index exists because of the physical ordering, not because of a declared key.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U5-Q70", "subject": "databases", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "indexing-storage", "subtopic": "b-trees-and-b-plus-trees-order-capacity", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-04", "text": "Which of the following comparisons between a B-tree and a B+ tree are correct? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["In a B-tree a successful search may terminate at an internal node", "For the same block size, a B+ tree node can hold more keys than a B-tree node", "Both B-tree and B+ tree are height balanced", "In a B+ tree, deletion never requires merging or redistribution"], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The B-tree stores data pointers at every level; the B+ tree pushes them all to the leaves, which frees space for more keys per internal node.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>B-tree: a record pointer sits beside each key, so a search can end in an internal node (A).</li><li>B+ tree internal nodes hold keys and block pointers only, so the fan-out for a fixed block size is larger (B).</li><li>Both maintain all leaves at the same depth — height balanced (C).</li><li>B+ tree deletion still merges or redistributes when a node falls below half full, so D is false.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: A, B and C. D is false.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming the B+ tree's larger fan-out also removes the need for merging. Occupancy rules are unchanged.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U5-Q71", "subject": "databases", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "indexing-storage", "subtopic": "dense-vs-sparse-indexing", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "GATE_PYQ_VARIATION", "date": "2026-11-04", "text": "A file stores 40,000 records with 25 records per block. A sparse primary index holds one entry per data block. How many index entries does it contain?<br/>(Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "1600", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A sparse primary index has exactly one entry per data block.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Data blocks = <code class=\"mm\">40000 / 25</code>.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">40000 / 25 = 1600</code>.</li><li>Entries = blocks = 1600.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 1600 entries (exact integer).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reporting 40,000 by assuming a dense index, or 1600 blocks instead of entries — here they coincide, so state the unit.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U5-Q72", "subject": "databases", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "indexing-storage", "subtopic": "b-trees-and-b-plus-trees-order-capacity", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "GATE_PYQ_VARIATION", "date": "2026-11-04", "text": "A B+ tree node occupies one block of 1024 bytes. Keys are 10 bytes and block pointers 6 bytes. What is the maximum number of block pointers (the order <code class=\"mm\">p</code>) in an internal node?<br/>(Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "64", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> An internal node with p pointers holds p − 1 keys: <code class=\"mm\">p·P + (p−1)·K ≤ B</code>, so <code class=\"mm\">p ≤ (B + K)/(P + K)</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">p·6 + (p−1)·10 ≤ 1024</code></li><li><code class=\"mm\">16p − 10 ≤ 1024</code> → <code class=\"mm\">16p ≤ 1034</code></li><li><code class=\"mm\">p ≤ 64.625</code> → <code class=\"mm\">p = 64</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 64 pointers (exact integer).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Using <code class=\"mm\">⌊1024/16⌋ = 64</code> by luck and then, on another block size, forgetting the <code class=\"mm\">−10</code> term. The closed form <code class=\"mm\">⌊(B+K)/(P+K)⌋</code> is safer.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U5-Q73", "subject": "databases", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "indexing-storage", "subtopic": "static-and-dynamic-hashing", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "GATE_PYQ_VARIATION", "date": "2026-11-04", "text": "An extendible hash file has bucket capacity 2 and initial global depth 1. Keys with hash values 0, 4, 1, 5, 2, 6, 3, 7, 8 are inserted in that order (hash values are used as the bit patterns, low-order bits first). What is the final <b>global depth</b>?<br/>(Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "3", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The global depth grows by one each time a bucket whose local depth equals the global depth overflows; a split alone, when <code class=\"mm\">d &lt; D</code>, does not double the directory.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Insert 0, 4 → bucket 0 full (<code class=\"mm\">[0,4]</code>); 1, 5 → bucket 1 full (<code class=\"mm\">[1,5]</code>). <code class=\"mm\">D = 1</code>.</li><li>Insert 2: bucket 0 overflows and its local depth (1) equals <code class=\"mm\">D</code> (1) → directory doubles, <code class=\"mm\">D = 2</code>. Split by bit 1: bucket 0 keeps <code class=\"mm\">[0,4]</code>, new bucket gets <code class=\"mm\">[2]</code>.</li><li>Insert 6 → <code class=\"mm\">[2,6]</code>; insert 3: bucket 1 overflows, local depth 1 &lt; <code class=\"mm\">D</code> = 2 → split without doubling; insert 7 → <code class=\"mm\">[3,7]</code>.</li><li>Insert 8: bucket 0 (<code class=\"mm\">[0,4]</code>) overflows, local depth 2 = <code class=\"mm\">D</code> = 2 → directory doubles again, <code class=\"mm\">D = 3</code>. Split by bit 2: bucket 0 keeps <code class=\"mm\">[0,8]</code>, new bucket gets <code class=\"mm\">[4]</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Global depth = 3 (directory of 8 entries; exact integer).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Counting every bucket split as a doubling. With nine insertions and four splits the answer would then be 4, which is wrong — only two splits occurred at <code class=\"mm\">d = D</code>.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U5-Q74", "subject": "databases", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "indexing-storage", "subtopic": "b-trees-and-b-plus-trees-order-capacity", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "GATE_PYQ_VARIATION", "date": "2026-11-04", "text": "A B-tree of order 5 (each node holds at most 5 children) has height 3, with the root at level 1. What is the maximum total number of keys the tree can hold?<br/>(Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "124", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Nodes at level i ≤ <code class=\"mm\">5^(i−1)</code>; each node holds at most <code class=\"mm\">m − 1 = 4</code> keys.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Maximum nodes: level 1 = 1, level 2 = 5, level 3 = 25 → total 31 nodes.</li><li>Maximum keys per node = <code class=\"mm\">m − 1 = 4</code>.</li><li>Total keys = <code class=\"mm\">31 × 4 = 124</code>.</li><li>Equivalently <code class=\"mm\">m^h − 1 = 5^3 − 1 = 124</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 124 keys (exact integer).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Using <code class=\"mm\">m − 1</code> children instead of <code class=\"mm\">m</code>, or counting height 3 as three levels of branching from the root's children. Verify with the closed form <code class=\"mm\">m^h − 1</code>.</p>"}, {"id": "DB-U5-Q75", "subject": "databases", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "indexing-storage", "subtopic": "primary-secondary-clustering-indexes", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-11-04", "text": "A multilevel sparse primary index is built over a file of 100,000 data blocks. Each index block can hold 204 entries. Counting every level, how many index blocks does the structure need?<br/>(Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "495", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A multilevel index is built bottom-up: the first level indexes the data blocks, each higher level indexes the level below, until a single block remains.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Level 1: <code class=\"mm\">⌈100000 / 204⌉ = ⌈490.20⌉ = 491</code> blocks.</li><li>Level 2: <code class=\"mm\">⌈491 / 204⌉ = ⌈2.41⌉ = 3</code> blocks.</li><li>Level 3: <code class=\"mm\">⌈3 / 204⌉ = 1</code> block — this is the top level, so the build stops.</li><li>Total = <code class=\"mm\">491 + 3 + 1 = 495</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 495 index blocks (exact integer).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Stopping after level 2 (494) or adding a further empty level. The build stops as soon as a level fits in one block; the top block is counted once.</p>"}];